ditional Chief Secretary to Government, Home, Prohibition and Excise Department & District Magistrate, Tirunelveli\n... Respondent(s) / State\n\nSTATUTORY PROVISIONS & ACTS REFERRED:\nTamil Nadu Act 14 of 1982 (Goondas Act), Constitution of India\n\nSECTIONS & CLAUSES UNDER CONSIDERATION:\nTamil Nadu Act 14 of 1982 Section 3, Article 21, Article 22(5)\n\nKEY LEGAL DOMAINS & TOPICS:\nHabeas Corpus, Preventive Detention, Goondas Act, Delay in Representation, Article 22(5)\n\n================================================================================\n                                   JUDGMENT\n================================================================================\n\n1. FACTUAL MATRIX:\nDetenu was detained as 'Goonda' under TN Act 14/1982. Representation submitted by wife was kept pending for 24 days without any explanation by the Detaining Authority.\n\n2. ISSUES FRAMED FOR ADJUDICATION:\n1. Whether unexplained delay in considering detenu representation vitiates preventive detention order under Article 22(5)?\n\n3. STATUTORY FRAMEWORK & PRECEDENTS:\nArticle 22(5) confers a constitutional right to have representation considered at the earliest opportunity as held in Rekha v. State of Tamil Nadu.\n\n4. RATIO DECIDENDI:\nUnexplained delay in considering representation of preventive detenu violates constitutional guarantee under Article 22(5), rendering detention illegal.\n\n5. FINAL DISPOSITION & ORDER:\nHabeas Corpus Petition Allowed; Detention order under Tamil Nadu Act 14 of 1982 quashed for unexplained 24-day delay in considering representation.\n\nDecreed and ordered accordingly.\n\nHon'ble Justice M.S. Ramesh & Justice M. Nirmal Kumar\nMadras High Court (Madurai Bench)\n[LEXGUARD CRYPTOGRAPHIC VERIFIED REPOSITORY RECORD]\n	3	t	HCMD010008922026	Judicial Precedent	\N	Single Bench	Kasthuri	The Additional Chief Secretary to Government & 2 Ors.	\N	\N	Civil Law	In this judgment under Constitutional Law, the Court ruled on the dispute between Kasthuri and 