 REFERRED:\nInformation Technology Act 2000, Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita 2023\n\nSECTIONS & PROVISIONS:\nIT Act Section 66D, IT Act Section 66E, BNS Section 308, BNS Section 318, BNSS Section 482\n\nKEY TOPICS & ISSUES:\nAI Deepfake, Cyber Extortion, Digital Impersonation, Forensic Data Recovery, Custodial Interrogation\n\n================================================================================\n                                   JUDGMENT\n================================================================================\n\n1. FACTS OF THE CASE:\nPetitioner created synthetic AI deepfake video of bank executive to extort commercial cryptocurrency payments.\n\n2. ISSUES FRAMED:\nWhether anticipatory bail can be granted where digital devices used for generative AI extortion remain unrecovered?\n\n3. LEGAL RATIO & FINDINGS:\nPre-arrest bail cannot be granted where digital forensics and neural network servers must be seized to trace extortion proceeds.\n\n4. FINAL ORDER:\nAnticipatory Bail Dismissed; AI deepfake extortion ring requires custodial forensic interrogation.\n\nOrdered and decreed accordingly.\n\nHon'ble Justice G. Jayachandran\nMadras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)\n[LEXGUARD REPOSITORY CRYPTOGRAPHIC SEAL]\n	3	t	HCMA010110292026	Criminal Original Petition (Bail)	\N	Single Bench	K. Vasanth	Inspector of Police, Cyber Crime Division	\N	\N	Civil Law	In this criminal original petition under Civil Law, the Court ruled on the dispute between K. Vasanth and Inspector of Police, Cyber Crime Division.	0	2026 LexGuard (Crim) D2FEE8	{"court": "Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)", "title": "K. Vasanth v. Inspector of Police, Cyber Crime Division", "case_id": "d2fee8f7-c26b-4a72-91d0-68e9a338e6d5", "case_no": "Crl.O.P. No. 11029 of 2026", "parties": "K. Vasanth (Accused)", "category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "petitioner": "K. Vasanth", "respondent": "Inspector of Police, Cyber Crime Division", "legal_domain": "Civil Law", "category_facts": {"category": "BAIL_SU