1074f881-7a42-4cb3-baa2-3754438a962b	NO. 11887 of 2026	Represented by Adv. - Manas Ranjan Baug v. STATE OF ODISHA	High Court of Orissa at Cuttack	2026	Hon'ble MR. JUSTICE ADITYA KUMAR MOHAPATRA	Represented by Adv. - Manas Ranjan Baug v. STATE OF ODISHA	[]	["Section 482"]	["civil & constitutional law", "ohc", "disposed"]	Judgment in NO. 11887 of 2026 delivered by Hon'ble MR. JUSTICE ADITYA KUMAR MOHAPATRA at High Court of Orissa at Cuttack. The proceedings concern Civil & Constitutional Law with respect to Section 482.	Disposed	storage/cases/OHC_2026_NO_11887_of_2026_1074f881.pdf	50893eb0326e5d8c9250edad242230072cb0e6d905466c54c1ee960576500bc1	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 14:51:50.3678+05:30	2026-10-25 14:51:50.367804+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.299657+05:30	23.10.2026 Order No. 01. 1. This matter is taken up through Hybrid Arrangement (Virtual /Physical Mode). 2. Heard learned counsel for the Petitioner and learned Additional Government Advocate. Perused the Anticipatory Bail Application as well as the documents annexed therein.	[]	Holding and principles affirmed in NO. 11887 of 2026 regarding Civil & Constitutional Law. The Court adjudicated upon Section 482.	[]	Disposed	[]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding Precedent)	IN THE HIGH COURT OF ORISSA AT CUTTACK\nABLAPL No.11887 of 2026\nCNR No. ODHC010796322026\nAmit Das ..... Petitioner\nRepresented by Adv. -\nManas Ranjan Baug\n-versus-\nSTATE OF ODISHA ..... Opposite Party\nRepresented by Adv. –\nMiss B.K. Sahu, AGA\nCORAM:\nMR. JUSTICE ADITYA KUMAR MOHAPATRA\nORDER\n23.10.2026\nOrder No.\n01. 1. This matter is taken up through Hybrid Arrangement (Virtual\n/Physical Mode).\n2. Heard learned counsel for the Petitioner and learned\nAdditional Government Advocate. Perused the Anticipatory Bail\nApplication as well as the documents annexed therein.\n3. By filing the present application under Section 482 of the\nB.N.S.S., 2023, the petitioner has prayed for his release on transit\nbail to appear before the jurisdictional Court and to move an\napplication for regular bail.\n4. Learned counsel for the Petitioner, at the outset, contended\nthat the present petitioner is arrayed as an accused in connection\nwith Tahasil P.S. Case No.370 dated 14.07.2026 of Nagpur City in\nthe State of Maharashtra.\nPage 1 of 2.\n\n5. Learned counsel for the petitioner further submitted that\nalthough the petitioner is ready and willing to appear before the\nlearned trial court and before the IO, however, he apprehends his\narrest by the concerned police. Learned counsel for the State, on the\nother hand, objected to the protection prayed for by the petitioner.\n6. Considering the submissions made by learned counsel\nappearing for both sides, this Court directs the petitioner to\nsurrender before the learned trial Court in siesin over the matter till\nthe end of November, 2026 and move an application for bail. Till\nsuch time, no coercive action shall be taken against the petitioner.\n7. With the aforesaid observation, the ABLAPL stands disposed\nof accordingly.\nUrgent certified copy of this order be granted as per rules.\n( A.K. Mohapatra)\nJudge\nSisir\nPage 2 of 2.	2	t	ODHC010796322026	Judicial Precedent	2026-10-23	Single Bench	Represented by Adv. - Manas Ranjan Baug	STATE OF ODISHA	\N	\N	Civil & Constitutional Law	In this matter, the High Court of Orissa at Cuttack adjudicated issues between Represented by Adv. - Manas Ranjan Baug and STATE OF ODISHA. The Court rendered a decision disposing of the application under statutory provisions.	0	2026 LexGuard (OHC) 1074F881	{"court": "High Court of Orissa at Cuttack", "title": "Represented by Adv. - Manas Ranjan Baug v. STATE OF ODISHA", "case_id": "1074f881-7a42-4cb3-baa2-3754438a962b", "case_no": "NO. 11887 of 2026", "parties": "Represented by Adv. - Manas Ranjan Baug v. STATE OF ODISHA", "category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "petitioner": "Represented by Adv. - Manas Ranjan Baug", "respondent": "STATE OF ODISHA", "legal_domain": "Civil & Constitutional Law", "category_facts": {"category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "surety_count": null, "custody_period": null, "application_type": "Anticipatory Bail", "bail_bond_amount": null, "trial_court_fine": null, "reason_for_relief": null, "reporting_condition": "appear before the jurisdictional Court and to move an", "trial_court_sentence": null, "trial_court_fine_paid": false}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "scenario_keywords": [], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Disposed", "disposition": "Disposed"}}
11111111-0002-4000-8000-000000000002	SLP (Crl.) No. 5191 of 2021	Satender Kumar Antil v. Central Bureau of Investigation & Another	Supreme Court of India	2022	Hon'ble Sanjay Kishan Kaul & M.M. Sundresh, JJ.	Satender Kumar Antil v. Central Bureau of Investigation & Another	["A CrPC", "Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973"]	["Section 41 A CrPC"]	["bail guidelines", "Section 41A notice", "non-arrest during investigation", "personal liberty", "undertrial prisoner"]	Landmark ruling laying down comprehensive categorization of offences (Categories A to D) and mandating grant of bail/release on appearance without custody if the accused cooperated during investigation.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_2022_SLP_Crl_No_5191_of_2021_11110002.pdf	387da21bd1cda969350fc3e0f4d0417a65d85d2f43c29720fd4a33c3807f61a0	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	\N	\N	2026-10-25 00:13:23.875119+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.301113+05:30	\N	[]	When an accused is not arrested during investigation and has cooperated with the investigating agency, on filing of the charge sheet under Section 170 CrPC, the court must issue summons or bailable warrant in the first instance. Accused persons under Category A offences (punishable up to 7 years) are entitled to be released on bail/bonds without mechanical custodial remand.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2022) 10 SCC 51]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2022) 10 SCC 51", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding Precedent)	IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA\nBAIL & CUSTODIAL JURISPRUDENCE JURISDICTION\nSLP (Crl.) No. 5191 of 2021\nCNR: SCIN010051912021\nCITATION: (2022) 10 SCC 51\n\nBEFORE:\nHon'ble Sanjay Kishan Kaul & M.M. Sundresh, JJ.\n\nSatender Kumar Antil v. Central Bureau of Investigation & Another\n\nPETITIONER: Satender Kumar Antil\nRESPONDENT: Central Bureau of Investigation & Another\nCOUNSEL FOR PETITIONER: Senior Advocate Sidharth Luthra\nCOUNSEL FOR RESPONDENT: Additional Solicitor General S.V. Raju\n\nJUDGMENT DATE: 11-07-2022\n\nSUMMARY & HEADNOTE:\nLandmark ruling laying down comprehensive categorization of offences (Categories A to D) and mandating grant of bail/release on appearance without custody if the accused cooperated during investigation.\n\nPLAIN-LANGUAGE EXPLANATION:\nIf an accused cooperated during police investigation and was not arrested before the chargesheet was filed, the court should grant bail or release them on personal bond without sending them to jail.\n\nSTATUTES AND PROVISIONS CONSIDERED:\nCode of Criminal Procedure, 1973, Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002\nSection 41 CrPC, Section 41A CrPC, Section 88 CrPC, Section 170 CrPC, Section 437 CrPC, Section 439 CrPC\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nWhen an accused is not arrested during investigation and has cooperated with the investigating agency, on filing of the charge sheet under Section 170 CrPC, the court must issue summons or bailable warrant in the first instance. Accused persons under Category A offences (punishable up to 7 years) are entitled to be released on bail/bonds without mechanical custodial remand.\n\nFINAL ORDER & DISPOSITION:\nComprehensive Bail Guidelines Affirmed. The petition is Directions Issued.\nPrecedential Status: Good Law (Binding Precedent).	1	f	SCIN010051912021	Special Leave Petition	2022-07-11	Division Bench (2 Judges)	Satender Kumar Antil	Central Bureau of Investigation & Another	Senior Advocate Sidharth Luthra	Additional Solicitor General S.V. Raju	Bail & Custodial Jurisprudence	If an accused cooperated during police investigation and was not arrested before the chargesheet was filed, the court should grant bail or release them on personal bond without sending them to jail.	5	(2022) 10 SCC 51	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Satender Kumar Antil v. Central Bureau of Investigation & Another", "case_id": "11111111-0002-4000-8000-000000000002", "case_no": "SLP (Crl.) No. 5191 of 2021", "parties": "Satender Kumar Antil v. Central Bureau of Investigation & Another", "category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "petitioner": "Satender Kumar Antil", "respondent": "Central Bureau of Investigation & Another", "legal_domain": "Bail & Custodial Jurisprudence", "category_facts": {"category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "surety_count": null, "custody_period": null, "application_type": "Regular Bail", "bail_bond_amount": null, "trial_court_fine": null, "reason_for_relief": null, "reporting_condition": null, "trial_court_sentence": null, "trial_court_fine_paid": false}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CRPC", "section": "41", "canonical": "Section 41 CRPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CRPC", "section": "41A", "canonical": "Section 41A CRPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CRPC", "section": "88", "canonical": "Section 88 CRPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CRPC", "section": "170", "canonical": "Section 170 CRPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CRPC", "section": "437", "canonical": "Section 437 CRPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CRPC", "section": "439", "canonical": "Section 439 CRPC", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["pre_trial_custody"], "scenario_keywords": ["remand"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Directions Issued", "disposition": "Directions Issued"}}
11111111-0006-4000-8000-000000000006	Criminal Appeal No. 1020 of 2010	Rangappa v. Sri Mohan	Supreme Court of India	2010	Hon'ble K.G. Balakrishnan, C.J., P. Sathasivam, J.M. Panchal, JJ.	Rangappa v. Sri Mohan	["NI Act", "Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881", "the Act"]	["Section 118 NI Act", "Section 138 NI Act", "Section 139 NI Act", "Section 139 the Act"]	["cheque dishonour", "Section 139 presumption", "legally enforceable debt", "preponderance of probabilities"]	Three-Judge Bench ruling holding that the statutory presumption mandated under Section 139 NI Act includes the existence of a legally enforceable debt, which can be rebutted on preponderance of probabilities.	Allowed	storage/cases/SC_2010_Crl_A_No_1020_of_2010_11110006.pdf	c7ded76b979b0098204d3abace3f71698b8c338929135e7fd63ecaa8009c5193	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	\N	\N	2026-10-25 00:13:23.951596+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.309298+05:30	\N	[]	The presumption mandated by Section 139 of the Act does indeed include the existence of a legally enforceable debt or liability. However, the standard of proof for rebutting the presumption is that of preponderance of probabilities. The accused can rely on the materials submitted by the complainant in order to raise such a defence and it is conceivable that in some cases the accused may not need to adduce evidence of his own.	[]	Allowed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2010) 11 SCC 441]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2010) 11 SCC 441", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding 3-Judge Precedent)	IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA\nNEGOTIABLE INSTRUMENTS & COMMERCIAL OFFENCES JURISDICTION\nCriminal Appeal No. 1020 of 2010\nCNR: SCIN010010202010\nCITATION: (2010) 11 SCC 441\n\nBEFORE:\nHon'ble K.G. Balakrishnan, C.J., P. Sathasivam, J.M. Panchal, JJ.\n\nRangappa v. Sri Mohan\n\nPETITIONER: Rangappa\nRESPONDENT: Sri Mohan\nCOUNSEL FOR PETITIONER: Advocate S.N. Bhat\nCOUNSEL FOR RESPONDENT: Advocate Anand Sanjay M. Nuli\n\nJUDGMENT DATE: 07-05-2010\n\nSUMMARY & HEADNOTE:\nThree-Judge Bench ruling holding that the statutory presumption mandated under Section 139 NI Act includes the existence of a legally enforceable debt, which can be rebutted on preponderance of probabilities.\n\nPLAIN-LANGUAGE EXPLANATION:\nIn cheque bounce cases, the court presumes the cheque was issued for a valid debt. The drawer does not need absolute proof to defend themselves, but only needs to raise a probable doubt.\n\nSTATUTES AND PROVISIONS CONSIDERED:\nNegotiable Instruments Act, 1881\nSection 138 NI Act, Section 139 NI Act, Section 118 NI Act\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nThe presumption mandated by Section 139 of the Act does indeed include the existence of a legally enforceable debt or liability. However, the standard of proof for rebutting the presumption is that of preponderance of probabilities. The accused can rely on the materials submitted by the complainant in order to raise such a defence and it is conceivable that in some cases the accused may not need to adduce evidence of his own.\n\nFINAL ORDER & DISPOSITION:\nConviction Set Aside under Section 138. The petition is Allowed.\nPrecedential Status: Good Law (Binding 3-Judge Precedent).	1	f	SCIN010010202010	Criminal Appeal	2010-05-07	Full Bench (3 Judges)	Rangappa	Sri Mohan	Advocate S.N. Bhat COUNSEL FOR RESPONDENT: Advocate Anand Sanjay M. Nuli	Advocate Anand Sanjay M. Nuli	Negotiable Instruments & Commercial Offences	In cheque bounce cases, the court presumes the cheque was issued for a valid debt. The drawer does not need absolute proof to defend themselves, but only needs to raise a probable doubt.	0	(2010) 11 SCC 441	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Rangappa v. Sri Mohan", "case_id": "11111111-0006-4000-8000-000000000006", "case_no": "Criminal Appeal No. 1020 of 2010", "parties": "Rangappa v. Sri Mohan", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Rangappa", "respondent": "Sri Mohan", "legal_domain": "Negotiable Instruments & Commercial Offences", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Criminal Appeal Against Conviction", "judicial_disposition": "Allowed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["financial_commercial"], "scenario_keywords": ["section 138", "negotiable instruments", "legally enforceable debt"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
11111111-0007-4000-8000-000000000007	SLP (Crl.) No. 8989 of 2010	Gian Singh v. State of Punjab and Another	Supreme Court of India	2012	Hon'ble R.M. Lodha, T.S. Thakur, Anil R. Dave, JJ.	Gian Singh v. State of Punjab and Another	["Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973", "Indian Penal Code, 1860", "the Code"]	["Section 320 the Code"]	["compromise quashing", "non-compoundable offence", "Section 482 CrPC", "amicable settlement", "private dispute"]	Three-Judge Bench upholding the inherent power of the High Court under Section 482 CrPC to quash criminal proceedings in non-compoundable offences having predominantly civil or personal character upon genuine compromise.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_2012_SLP_Crl_No_8989_of_2010_11110007.pdf	93fe51fd114f4b524deecb77fdfbb23068958ae5dd22e4a0732bb4d3d11827f3	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	\N	\N	2026-10-25 00:13:23.965456+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.311925+05:30	\N	[]	The power of the High Court in quashing a criminal proceeding or FIR or complaint in exercise of its inherent jurisdiction is distinct and different from the power given to a criminal court for compounding the offences under Section 320 of the Code. In offences arising from commercial, financial, mercantile, civil, partnership or such like transactions or the offences arising out of matrimony relating to dowry, etc. or family disputes where the wrong is basically private or personal and parties have resolved their dispute, the High Court may quash proceedings.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2012) 10 SCC 303]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2012) 10 SCC 303", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding 3-Judge Precedent)	IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA\nINHERENT POWERS & CRIMINAL SETTLEMENT JURISDICTION\nSLP (Crl.) No. 8989 of 2010\nCNR: SCIN010089892010\nCITATION: (2012) 10 SCC 303\n\nBEFORE:\nHon'ble R.M. Lodha, T.S. Thakur, Anil R. Dave, JJ.\n\nGian Singh v. State of Punjab and Another\n\nPETITIONER: Gian Singh\nRESPONDENT: State of Punjab and Another\nCOUNSEL FOR PETITIONER: Senior Advocate KTS Tulsi\nCOUNSEL FOR RESPONDENT: Advocate Kuldip Singh\n\nJUDGMENT DATE: 24-09-2012\n\nSUMMARY & HEADNOTE:\nThree-Judge Bench upholding the inherent power of the High Court under Section 482 CrPC to quash criminal proceedings in non-compoundable offences having predominantly civil or personal character upon genuine compromise.\n\nPLAIN-LANGUAGE EXPLANATION:\nIf both sides in a private or commercial dispute have settled their grievances peacefully, the High Court can quash the criminal case even if the offence is officially non-compoundable.\n\nSTATUTES AND PROVISIONS CONSIDERED:\nCode of Criminal Procedure, 1973, Indian Penal Code, 1860\nSection 482 CrPC, Section 320 CrPC\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nThe power of the High Court in quashing a criminal proceeding or FIR or complaint in exercise of its inherent jurisdiction is distinct and different from the power given to a criminal court for compounding the offences under Section 320 of the Code. In offences arising from commercial, financial, mercantile, civil, partnership or such like transactions or the offences arising out of matrimony relating to dowry, etc. or family disputes where the wrong is basically private or personal and parties have resolved their dispute, the High Court may quash proceedings.\n\nFINAL ORDER & DISPOSITION:\nQuashing on Amicable Compromise Affirmed. The petition is Clarified.\nPrecedential Status: Good Law (Binding 3-Judge Precedent).	1	f	SCIN010089892010	Special Leave Petition	2012-09-24	Full Bench (3 Judges)	Gian Singh	State of Punjab and Another	Senior Advocate KTS Tulsi COUNSEL FOR RESPONDENT: Advocate Kuldip Singh	Advocate Kuldip Singh	Inherent Powers & Criminal Settlement	If both sides in a private or commercial dispute have settled their grievances peacefully, the High Court can quash the criminal case even if the offence is officially non-compoundable.	0	(2012) 10 SCC 303	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Gian Singh v. State of Punjab and Another", "case_id": "11111111-0007-4000-8000-000000000007", "case_no": "SLP (Crl.) No. 8989 of 2010", "parties": "Gian Singh v. State of Punjab and Another", "category": "QUASHING", "petitioner": "Gian Singh", "respondent": "State of Punjab and Another", "legal_domain": "Inherent Powers & Criminal Settlement", "category_facts": {"category": "QUASHING", "civil_cloak": true, "quash_ground": "Purely civil transaction given criminal cloak", "compromise_affidavit": false, "bhajan_lal_principles": true, "proceedings_challenged": "First Information Report (FIR)"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CRPC", "section": "482", "canonical": "Section 482 CRPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CRPC", "section": "320", "canonical": "Section 320 CRPC", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "scenario_keywords": [], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Clarified", "disposition": "Clarified"}}
11111111-0008-4000-8000-000000000008	Writ Petition No. 231 of 1977	Maneka Gandhi v. Union of India and Another	Supreme Court of India	1978	Hon'ble M.H. Beg, C.J., Y.V. Chandrachud, P.N. Bhagwati, V.R. Krishna Iyer, N.L. Untwalia, S. Murtaza Fazal Ali, P.S. Kailasam, JJ.	Maneka Gandhi v. Union of India and Another	["Constitution of India", "Passports Act"]	["Section 10(3)(c) Passports Act"]	["right to travel", "Article 21 due process", "golden triangle", "natural justice", "audi alteram partem"]	Seven-Judge Bench ruling revolutionizing Article 21 by establishing that 'procedure established by law' must be just, fair, and reasonable, and not arbitrary or oppressive.	Allowed	storage/cases/SC_1978_Writ_Petition_No_231_of_1977_11110008.pdf	487bd6c41dd6e753e192571efaea4b752869e1675f7c262075f41966998c8b3b	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	\N	\N	2026-10-25 00:13:23.977628+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.3136+05:30	\N	[]	The principle of reasonableness, which legally as well as philosophically, is an essential element of equality or non-arbitrariness pervades Article 14 like a brooding omnipresence and the procedure contemplated by Article 21 must answer the test of reasonableness in order to be in conformity with Article 14. It must be 'right and just and fair' and not arbitrary, fanciful or oppressive.	[]	Allowed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [1978) 1 SCC 248]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "1978) 1 SCC 248", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Foundational 7-Judge Landmark)	IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA\nCONSTITUTIONAL LAW & FUNDAMENTAL RIGHTS JURISDICTION\nWrit Petition No. 231 of 1977\nCNR: SCIN010002311977\nCITATION: (1978) 1 SCC 248\n\nBEFORE:\nHon'ble M.H. Beg, C.J., Y.V. Chandrachud, P.N. Bhagwati, V.R. Krishna Iyer, N.L. Untwalia, S. Murtaza Fazal Ali, P.S. Kailasam, JJ.\n\nManeka Gandhi v. Union of India and Another\n\nPETITIONER: Maneka Gandhi\nRESPONDENT: Union of India and Another\nCOUNSEL FOR PETITIONER: Senior Advocate Fali S. Nariman\nCOUNSEL FOR RESPONDENT: Attorney General S.V. Gupte\n\nJUDGMENT DATE: 25-01-1978\n\nSUMMARY & HEADNOTE:\nSeven-Judge Bench ruling revolutionizing Article 21 by establishing that 'procedure established by law' must be just, fair, and reasonable, and not arbitrary or oppressive.\n\nPLAIN-LANGUAGE EXPLANATION:\nThe government cannot restrict a citizen's passport or personal liberty arbitrarily. The legal procedure must be completely fair, non-discriminatory, and respect natural justice.\n\nSTATUTES AND PROVISIONS CONSIDERED:\nConstitution of India, Passports Act, 1967\nArticle 21, Article 14, Article 19, Section 10(3)(c) Passports Act\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nThe principle of reasonableness, which legally as well as philosophically, is an essential element of equality or non-arbitrariness pervades Article 14 like a brooding omnipresence and the procedure contemplated by Article 21 must answer the test of reasonableness in order to be in conformity with Article 14. It must be 'right and just and fair' and not arbitrary, fanciful or oppressive.\n\nFINAL ORDER & DISPOSITION:\nPassport Impounding Quashed & Principles Affirmed. The petition is Allowed in Principle.\nPrecedential Status: Good Law (Foundational 7-Judge Landmark).	1	f	SCIN010002311977	Writ Petition	1978-01-25	Constitution Bench (7 Judges)	Maneka Gandhi	Union of India and Another	Senior Advocate Fali S. Nariman	Attorney General S.V. Gupte	Constitutional Law & Fundamental Rights	The government cannot restrict a citizen's passport or personal liberty arbitrarily. The legal procedure must be completely fair, non-discriminatory, and respect natural justice.	1	(1978) 1 SCC 248	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Maneka Gandhi v. Union of India and Another", "case_id": "11111111-0008-4000-8000-000000000008", "case_no": "Writ Petition No. 231 of 1977", "parties": "Maneka Gandhi v. Union of India and Another", "category": "QUASHING", "petitioner": "Maneka Gandhi", "respondent": "Union of India and Another", "legal_domain": "Constitutional Law & Fundamental Rights", "category_facts": {"category": "QUASHING", "civil_cloak": false, "quash_ground": "Absence of prima facie ingredients", "compromise_affidavit": false, "bhajan_lal_principles": true, "proceedings_challenged": "First Information Report (FIR)"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "21", "canonical": "Section 21 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "14", "canonical": "Section 14 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "19", "canonical": "Section 19 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["constitutional_fundamental"], "scenario_keywords": ["article 14", "article 21"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed in Principle", "disposition": "Allowed in Principle"}}
11111111-0009-4000-8000-000000000009	Writ Petition (Civil) No. 494 of 2012	Justice K.S. Puttaswamy (Retd.) v. Union of India and Others	Supreme Court of India	2017	9-Judge Constitution Bench (per J.S. Khehar, C.J., J. Chelameswar, S.A. Bobde, R.K. Agrawal, Rohinton Fali Nariman, A.M. Sapre, D.Y. Chandrachud, Sanjay Kishan Kaul, S. Abdul Nazeer, JJ.)	Justice K.S. Puttaswamy (Retd.) v. Union of India and Others	["Constitution of India"]	["Article 21", "Article 14", "Article 19", "Article 12"]	["right to privacy", "fundamental right", "informational privacy", "biometrics", "proportionality test"]	Unanimous Nine-Judge Constitution Bench holding that the Right to Privacy is a fundamental right protected intrinsically under Article 21 and Part III of the Constitution.	Allowed	storage/cases/SC_2017_WP_Civil_No_494_of_2012_11110009.pdf	6c2fdca30aa95de18df306898f6cc59885e7153a60aa974b51153b090325ab7c	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	\N	\N	2026-10-25 00:13:23.989084+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.315509+05:30	\N	[]	The right to privacy is protected as an intrinsic part of the right to life and personal liberty under Article 21 and as a part of the freedoms guaranteed by Part III of the Constitution. Privacy has both positive and negative contents. Any encroachment by the State must satisfy the three-fold test of legality, legitimate state aim, and proportionality.	[]	Allowed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2017) 10 SCC 1]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2017) 10 SCC 1", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Unanimous 9-Judge Benchmark)	IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA\nCONSTITUTIONAL LAW & DIGITAL RIGHTS JURISDICTION\nWrit Petition (Civil) No. 494 of 2012\nCNR: SCIN010004942012\nCITATION: (2017) 10 SCC 1\n\nBEFORE:\n9-Judge Constitution Bench (per J.S. Khehar, C.J., J. Chelameswar, S.A. Bobde, R.K. Agrawal, Rohinton Fali Nariman, A.M. Sapre, D.Y. Chandrachud, Sanjay Kishan Kaul, S. Abdul Nazeer, JJ.)\n\nJustice K.S. Puttaswamy (Retd.) and Another v. Union of India and Others\n\nPETITIONER: Justice K.S. Puttaswamy (Retd.)\nRESPONDENT: Union of India and Others\nCOUNSEL FOR PETITIONER: Senior Advocates Shyam Divan, Gopal Subramanium, Arvind Datar\nCOUNSEL FOR RESPONDENT: Attorney General K.K. Venugopal, ASG Tushar Mehta\n\nJUDGMENT DATE: 24-08-2017\n\nSUMMARY & HEADNOTE:\nUnanimous Nine-Judge Constitution Bench holding that the Right to Privacy is a fundamental right protected intrinsically under Article 21 and Part III of the Constitution.\n\nPLAIN-LANGUAGE EXPLANATION:\nEvery Indian citizen possesses an inherent fundamental right to privacy, shielding their bodily autonomy, personal choices, and digital data from unjustified surveillance.\n\nSTATUTES AND PROVISIONS CONSIDERED:\nConstitution of India, Information Technology Act, 2000\nArticle 21, Article 14, Article 19, Article 12\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nThe right to privacy is protected as an intrinsic part of the right to life and personal liberty under Article 21 and as a part of the freedoms guaranteed by Part III of the Constitution. Privacy has both positive and negative contents. Any encroachment by the State must satisfy the three-fold test of legality, legitimate state aim, and proportionality.\n\nFINAL ORDER & DISPOSITION:\nRight to Privacy Affirmed as Fundamental Right. The petition is Allowed.\nPrecedential Status: Good Law (Unanimous 9-Judge Benchmark).	1	f	SCIN010004942012	Writ Petition	2017-08-24	Constitution Bench (9 Judges)	Justice K.S. Puttaswamy (Retd.)	Union of India and Others	Senior Advocates Shyam Divan, Gopal Subramanium, Arvind Datar	Attorney General K.K. Venugopal, ASG Tushar Mehta	Constitutional Law & Digital Rights	Every Indian citizen possesses an inherent fundamental right to privacy, shielding their bodily autonomy, personal choices, and digital data from unjustified surveillance.	2	(2017) 10 SCC 1	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Justice K.S. Puttaswamy (Retd.) and Another v. Union of India and Others", "case_id": "11111111-0009-4000-8000-000000000009", "case_no": "Writ Petition (Civil) No. 494 of 2012", "parties": "Justice K.S. Puttaswamy (Retd.) v. Union of India and Others", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Justice K.S. Puttaswamy (Retd.)", "respondent": "Union of India and Others", "legal_domain": "Constitutional Law & Digital Rights", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Writ Petition / Criminal Appeal", "judicial_disposition": "Allowed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "21", "canonical": "Section 21 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "14", "canonical": "Section 14 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "19", "canonical": "Section 19 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "12", "canonical": "Section 12 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["constitutional_fundamental"], "scenario_keywords": ["article 14", "article 21"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
11111111-0010-4000-8000-000000000010	Criminal Appeal No. 335 of 1978	Gurbaksh Singh Sibbia and Others v. State of Punjab	Supreme Court of India	1980	Hon'ble Y.V. Chandrachud, C.J., P.N. Bhagwati, N.L. Untwalia, R.S. Pathak, O. Chinnappa Reddy, JJ.	Gurbaksh Singh Sibbia and Others v. State of Punjab	["Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973"]	["Section 438 CrPC", "Section 437 CrPC", "Article 21"]	["anticipatory bail", "judicial discretion", "personal liberty", "Section 438 CrPC", "freedom from custody"]	Five-Judge Constitution Bench holding that judicial discretion under Section 438 CrPC to grant anticipatory bail must remain wide and untrammeled by judicially created rigid conditions.	Allowed	storage/cases/SC_1980_Crl_A_No_335_of_1978_11110010.pdf	5c704fd7f887fd6f67095eb9ccd14da0083ed5bcd9964a630cba79584a050284	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	\N	\N	2026-10-25 00:13:23.999204+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.317768+05:30	\N	[]	Section 438 is a procedural provision which is concerned with the personal liberty of each individual citizen. It confers wide powers on the High Court and the Court of Session to grant anticipatory bail and that discretion, by its very nature, cannot be circumscribed by any rigid, iron formula or cast in a stone jacket.	[]	Allowed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [1980) 2 SCC 565]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "1980) 2 SCC 565", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (5-Judge Constitution Bench Precedent)	IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA\nANTICIPATORY BAIL JURISPRUDENCE JURISDICTION\nCriminal Appeal No. 335 of 1978\nCNR: SCIN010003351978\nCITATION: (1980) 2 SCC 565\n\nBEFORE:\nHon'ble Y.V. Chandrachud, C.J., P.N. Bhagwati, N.L. Untwalia, R.S. Pathak, O. Chinnappa Reddy, JJ.\n\nGurbaksh Singh Sibbia and Others v. State of Punjab\n\nPETITIONER: Gurbaksh Singh Sibbia and Others\nRESPONDENT: State of Punjab\nCOUNSEL FOR PETITIONER: Senior Advocate Soli J. Sorabjee\nCOUNSEL FOR RESPONDENT: Advocate General of Punjab\n\nJUDGMENT DATE: 09-04-1980\n\nSUMMARY & HEADNOTE:\nFive-Judge Constitution Bench holding that judicial discretion under Section 438 CrPC to grant anticipatory bail must remain wide and untrammeled by judicially created rigid conditions.\n\nPLAIN-LANGUAGE EXPLANATION:\nHigh Courts and Sessions Courts possess broad authority to grant anticipatory bail to protect citizens against unjustified arrests; courts should not place artificial limits on this power.\n\nSTATUTES AND PROVISIONS CONSIDERED:\nCode of Criminal Procedure, 1973\nSection 438 CrPC, Section 437 CrPC, Article 21\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nSection 438 is a procedural provision which is concerned with the personal liberty of each individual citizen. It confers wide powers on the High Court and the Court of Session to grant anticipatory bail and that discretion, by its very nature, cannot be circumscribed by any rigid, iron formula or cast in a stone jacket.\n\nFINAL ORDER & DISPOSITION:\nAnticipatory Bail Scope Expanded. The petition is Allowed.\nPrecedential Status: Good Law (5-Judge Constitution Bench Precedent).	1	f	SCIN010003351978	Criminal Appeal	1980-04-09	Constitution Bench (5 Judges)	Gurbaksh Singh Sibbia and Others	State of Punjab	Senior Advocate Soli J. Sorabjee COUNSEL FOR RESPONDENT: Advocate General of Punjab	Advocate General of Punjab	Anticipatory Bail Jurisprudence	High Courts and Sessions Courts possess broad authority to grant anticipatory bail to protect citizens against unjustified arrests; courts should not place artificial limits on this power.	0	(1980) 2 SCC 565	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Gurbaksh Singh Sibbia and Others v. State of Punjab", "case_id": "11111111-0010-4000-8000-000000000010", "case_no": "Criminal Appeal No. 335 of 1978", "parties": "Gurbaksh Singh Sibbia and Others v. State of Punjab", "category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "petitioner": "Gurbaksh Singh Sibbia and Others", "respondent": "State of Punjab", "legal_domain": "Anticipatory Bail Jurisprudence", "category_facts": {"category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "surety_count": null, "custody_period": null, "application_type": "Anticipatory Bail", "bail_bond_amount": null, "trial_court_fine": null, "reason_for_relief": null, "reporting_condition": null, "trial_court_sentence": null, "trial_court_fine_paid": false}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CRPC", "section": "438", "canonical": "Section 438 CRPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CRPC", "section": "437", "canonical": "Section 437 CRPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "21", "canonical": "Section 21 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["pre_trial_custody", "constitutional_fundamental"], "scenario_keywords": ["anticipatory bail", "article 21"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
11111111-0011-4000-8000-000000000011	Special Leave Petition (Crl.) Nos. 7281-7282 of 2017	Sushila Aggarwal and Others v. State (NCT of Delhi) and Another	Supreme Court of India	2020	Hon'ble Arun Mishra, Indira Banerjee, Vineet Saran, M.R. Shah, S. Ravindra Bhat, JJ.	Sushila Aggarwal and Others v. State (NCT of Delhi) and Another	["Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973"]	["Section 438 CrPC", "Section 173(2) CrPC"]	["duration of anticipatory bail", "chargesheet filing", "trial duration", "Section 438 CrPC", "liberty protection"]	Five-Judge Constitution Bench settling the law that anticipatory bail does not automatically terminate upon filing of the chargesheet and normally continues till the conclusion of trial.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_2020_SLP_Crl_Nos_7281_7282_of_2017_11110011.pdf	53b22ee216e6cfe358f7f01988617e10ea2c5025810632e2cecc074e0fd07949	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	\N	\N	2026-10-25 00:13:24.009849+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.32043+05:30	\N	[]	The protection granted to a person under Section 438 CrPC should not invariably be limited to a fixed period; it should inure in favour of the accused without any restriction on time and normally endure till the conclusion of trial. The mere filing of a charge sheet does not automatically compel the accused to surrender and apply for regular bail.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2020) 5 SCC 1]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2020) 5 SCC 1", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (5-Judge Constitution Bench)	IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA\nANTICIPATORY BAIL DURATION JURISDICTION\nSpecial Leave Petition (Crl.) Nos. 7281-7282 of 2017\nCNR: SCIN010072812017\nCITATION: (2020) 5 SCC 1\n\nBEFORE:\nHon'ble Arun Mishra, Indira Banerjee, Vineet Saran, M.R. Shah, S. Ravindra Bhat, JJ.\n\nSushila Aggarwal and Others v. State (NCT of Delhi) and Another\n\nPETITIONER: Sushila Aggarwal and Others\nRESPONDENT: State (NCT of Delhi) and Another\nCOUNSEL FOR PETITIONER: Senior Advocate Harin P. Raval\nCOUNSEL FOR RESPONDENT: Solicitor General Tushar Mehta\n\nJUDGMENT DATE: 29-01-2020\n\nSUMMARY & HEADNOTE:\nFive-Judge Constitution Bench settling the law that anticipatory bail does not automatically terminate upon filing of the chargesheet and normally continues till the conclusion of trial.\n\nPLAIN-LANGUAGE EXPLANATION:\nOnce a court grants anticipatory bail, that protection usually lasts throughout the entire criminal trial, not just until the police complete their investigation.\n\nSTATUTES AND PROVISIONS CONSIDERED:\nCode of Criminal Procedure, 1973\nSection 438 CrPC, Section 173(2) CrPC\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nThe protection granted to a person under Section 438 CrPC should not invariably be limited to a fixed period; it should inure in favour of the accused without any restriction on time and normally endure till the conclusion of trial. The mere filing of a charge sheet does not automatically compel the accused to surrender and apply for regular bail.\n\nFINAL ORDER & DISPOSITION:\nAnticipatory Bail Life Sustained Till End of Trial. The petition is Answered in Affirmative.\nPrecedential Status: Good Law (5-Judge Constitution Bench).	1	f	SCIN010072812017	Judicial Precedent	2020-01-29	Constitution Bench (5 Judges)	Sushila Aggarwal and Others	State (NCT of Delhi) and Another	Senior Advocate Harin P. Raval	Solicitor General Tushar Mehta	Anticipatory Bail Duration	Once a court grants anticipatory bail, that protection usually lasts throughout the entire criminal trial, not just until the police complete their investigation.	1	(2020) 5 SCC 1	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Sushila Aggarwal and Others v. State (NCT of Delhi) and Another", "case_id": "11111111-0011-4000-8000-000000000011", "case_no": "Special Leave Petition (Crl.) Nos. 7281-7282 of 2017", "parties": "Sushila Aggarwal and Others v. State (NCT of Delhi) and Another", "category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "petitioner": "Sushila Aggarwal and Others", "respondent": "State (NCT of Delhi) and Another", "legal_domain": "Anticipatory Bail Duration", "category_facts": {"category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "surety_count": null, "custody_period": null, "application_type": "Anticipatory Bail", "bail_bond_amount": null, "trial_court_fine": null, "reason_for_relief": null, "reporting_condition": null, "trial_court_sentence": null, "trial_court_fine_paid": false}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CRPC", "section": "438", "canonical": "Section 438 CRPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CRPC", "section": "173", "canonical": "Section 173 CRPC", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["pre_trial_custody"], "scenario_keywords": ["anticipatory bail", "regular bail"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Answered in Affirmative", "disposition": "Answered in Affirmative"}}
11111111-0012-4000-8000-000000000012	Writ Petition (Criminal) No. 167 of 2012	Shreya Singhal v. Union of India	Supreme Court of India	2015	Hon'ble J. Chelameswar & Rohinton Fali Nariman, JJ.	Shreya Singhal v. Union of India	["Constitution of India", "IT Act", "Information Technology Act"]	["Section 66A IT Act", "Section 66A Information Technology Act", "Section 79 IT Act"]	["Section 66A IT Act", "freedom of speech", "chilling effect", "vagueness doctrine", "online speech"]	Struck down Section 66A of the Information Technology Act in its entirety as unconstitutional for violating the fundamental right to free speech under Article 19(1)(a).	Allowed	storage/cases/SC_2015_WP_Crl_No_167_of_2012_11110012.pdf	4dbc10dfb95c53296804e4a3baf4910a174a19cc211eb8a2af32c876c25fd4be	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	\N	\N	2026-10-25 00:13:24.022657+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.322606+05:30	\N	[]	Section 66A of the Information Technology Act, 2000 is struck down in its entirety being violative of Article 19(1)(a) and not saved under Article 19(2). The section is cast so widely that virtually any opinion on any subject would be covered, creating an impermissible chilling effect on speech.	[]	Allowed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2015) 5 SCC 1]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2015) 5 SCC 1", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Landmark Free Speech Ruling)	IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA\nFREE SPEECH & CYBER JURISPRUDENCE JURISDICTION\nWrit Petition (Criminal) No. 167 of 2012\nCNR: SCIN010001672012\nCITATION: (2015) 5 SCC 1\n\nBEFORE:\nHon'ble J. Chelameswar & Rohinton Fali Nariman, JJ.\n\nShreya Singhal v. Union of India\n\nPETITIONER: Shreya Singhal\nRESPONDENT: Union of India\nCOUNSEL FOR PETITIONER: Senior Advocates Soli J. Sorabjee, Mukul Rohatgi\nCOUNSEL FOR RESPONDENT: Additional Solicitor General Tushar Mehta\n\nJUDGMENT DATE: 24-03-2015\n\nSUMMARY & HEADNOTE:\nStruck down Section 66A of the Information Technology Act in its entirety as unconstitutional for violating the fundamental right to free speech under Article 19(1)(a).\n\nPLAIN-LANGUAGE EXPLANATION:\nStruck down the law that allowed police to arrest citizens for writing offensive or annoying posts online, protecting democratic free speech on social media.\n\nSTATUTES AND PROVISIONS CONSIDERED:\nInformation Technology Act, 2000, Constitution of India\nSection 66A IT Act, Section 79 IT Act, Article 19(1)(a), Article 19(2)\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nSection 66A of the Information Technology Act, 2000 is struck down in its entirety being violative of Article 19(1)(a) and not saved under Article 19(2). The section is cast so widely that virtually any opinion on any subject would be covered, creating an impermissible chilling effect on speech.\n\nFINAL ORDER & DISPOSITION:\nSection 66A Declared Unconstitutional. The petition is Allowed.\nPrecedential Status: Good Law (Landmark Free Speech Ruling).	1	f	SCIN010001672012	Writ Petition	2015-03-24	Division Bench (2 Judges)	Shreya Singhal	Union of India	Senior Advocates Soli J. Sorabjee, Mukul Rohatgi	Additional Solicitor General Tushar Mehta	Free Speech & Cyber Jurisprudence	Struck down the law that allowed police to arrest citizens for writing offensive or annoying posts online, protecting democratic free speech on social media.	0	(2015) 5 SCC 1	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Shreya Singhal v. Union of India", "case_id": "11111111-0012-4000-8000-000000000012", "case_no": "Writ Petition (Criminal) No. 167 of 2012", "parties": "Shreya Singhal v. Union of India", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Shreya Singhal", "respondent": "Union of India", "legal_domain": "Free Speech & Cyber Jurisprudence", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Writ Petition / Criminal Appeal", "judicial_disposition": "Allowed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "19", "canonical": "Section 19 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "scenario_keywords": [], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
11111111-0013-4000-8000-000000000013	Writ Petition (Crl.) Nos. 666-70 of 1992	Vishaka and Others v. State of Rajasthan and Others	Supreme Court of India	1997	Hon'ble J.S. Verma, C.J., Sujata V. Manohar, B.N. Kirpal, JJ.	Vishaka and Others v. State of Rajasthan and Others	["Constitution of India"]	["Article 14", "Article 19(1)(g)", "Article 21", "Article 32"]	["Vishaka guidelines", "sexual harassment", "workplace equality", "Internal Complaints Committee", "Article 21"]	Framed binding judicial guidelines to prevent and address sexual harassment of working women at workplaces under Articles 14, 19, and 21, in the absence of enacted legislation.	Allowed	storage/cases/SC_1997_WP_Crl_Nos_666_70_of_1992_11110013.pdf	0d9a7699e9167742f0e03ea44b50d683734c831084b302068bcadf277c64aab8	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	\N	\N	2026-10-25 00:13:24.033756+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.324719+05:30	\N	[]	Each incident of sexual harassment of a woman at workplace results in violation of the fundamental rights of Gender Equality and the Right of Life and Liberty. In the absence of legislative enactments to provide for effective enforcement of the basic human right of gender equality, guidelines and norms are hereby specified for due observance at all workplaces.	[]	Allowed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [1997) 6 SCC 241]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "1997) 6 SCC 241", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Codified into POSH Act, 2013)	IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA\nGENDER JUSTICE & WORKPLACE RIGHTS JURISDICTION\nWrit Petition (Crl.) Nos. 666-70 of 1992\nCNR: SCIN010006661992\nCITATION: (1997) 6 SCC 241\n\nBEFORE:\nHon'ble J.S. Verma, C.J., Sujata V. Manohar, B.N. Kirpal, JJ.\n\nVishaka and Others v. State of Rajasthan and Others\n\nPETITIONER: Vishaka and Others\nRESPONDENT: State of Rajasthan and Others\nCOUNSEL FOR PETITIONER: Senior Advocate Meenakshi Arora\nCOUNSEL FOR RESPONDENT: Senior Advocate Altaf Ahmad\n\nJUDGMENT DATE: 13-08-1997\n\nSUMMARY & HEADNOTE:\nFramed binding judicial guidelines to prevent and address sexual harassment of working women at workplaces under Articles 14, 19, and 21, in the absence of enacted legislation.\n\nPLAIN-LANGUAGE EXPLANATION:\nMandated that every workplace must create an Internal Complaints Committee and take concrete preventive measures against sexual harassment.\n\nSTATUTES AND PROVISIONS CONSIDERED:\nConstitution of India, CEDAW\nArticle 14, Article 19(1)(g), Article 21, Article 32\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nEach incident of sexual harassment of a woman at workplace results in violation of the fundamental rights of Gender Equality and the Right of Life and Liberty. In the absence of legislative enactments to provide for effective enforcement of the basic human right of gender equality, guidelines and norms are hereby specified for due observance at all workplaces.\n\nFINAL ORDER & DISPOSITION:\nBinding Workplace Guidelines Enacted. The petition is Allowed.\nPrecedential Status: Good Law (Codified into POSH Act, 2013).	1	f	SCIN010006661992	Writ Petition	1997-08-13	Full Bench (3 Judges)	Vishaka and Others	State of Rajasthan and Others	Senior Advocate Meenakshi Arora COUNSEL FOR RESPONDENT: Senior Advocate Altaf Ahmad	Senior Advocate Altaf Ahmad	Gender Justice & Workplace Rights	Mandated that every workplace must create an Internal Complaints Committee and take concrete preventive measures against sexual harassment.	0	(1997) 6 SCC 241	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Vishaka and Others v. State of Rajasthan and Others", "case_id": "11111111-0013-4000-8000-000000000013", "case_no": "Writ Petition (Crl.) Nos. 666-70 of 1992", "parties": "Vishaka and Others v. State of Rajasthan and Others", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Vishaka and Others", "respondent": "State of Rajasthan and Others", "legal_domain": "Gender Justice & Workplace Rights", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Writ Petition / Criminal Appeal", "judicial_disposition": "Allowed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "14", "canonical": "Section 14 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "19", "canonical": "Section 19 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "21", "canonical": "Section 21 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "32", "canonical": "Section 32 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["constitutional_fundamental"], "scenario_keywords": ["article 32", "fundamental rights", "article 14", "article 21"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
11111111-0014-4000-8000-000000000014	Criminal Appeal No. 1340 of 2019	P. Chidambaram v. Directorate of Enforcement	Supreme Court of India	2019	Hon'ble R. Banumathi, A.S. Bopanna, Hrishikesh Roy, JJ.	P. Chidambaram v. Directorate of Enforcement	["Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973"]	["Section 45 PMLA", "Section 439 CrPC", "Section 3 PMLA"]	["bail in economic offences", "PMLA Section 45", "flight risk", "witness tampering", "pre-trial detention"]	Held that economic offences cannot be treated as an absolute class where bail must be denied automatically; the basic bail test (flight risk, witness tampering) remains operative.	Allowed	storage/cases/SC_2019_Crl_A_No_1340_of_2019_11110014.pdf	7300f6c5232ef6f9f9d56f32a77e4382d1cc331e57fc3826c88079b06606b36a	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	\N	\N	2026-10-25 00:13:24.045242+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.326264+05:30	\N	[]	Even if the allegation is one of grave economic offence, it is not a rule that bail should be denied in every such case. The gravity of the offence is to be gathered from the nature of the allegation and the severity of punishment. In determining bail, the court must consider whether there is flight risk, risk of tampering with evidence, or influencing witnesses.	[]	Allowed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2019) 9 SCC 24]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2019) 9 SCC 24", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Precedent on PMLA Bail)	IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA\nECONOMIC OFFENCES & BAIL STANDARDS JURISDICTION\nCriminal Appeal No. 1340 of 2019\nCNR: SCIN010013402019\nCITATION: (2019) 9 SCC 24\n\nBEFORE:\nHon'ble R. Banumathi, A.S. Bopanna, Hrishikesh Roy, JJ.\n\nP. Chidambaram v. Directorate of Enforcement\n\nPETITIONER: P. Chidambaram\nRESPONDENT: Directorate of Enforcement\nCOUNSEL FOR PETITIONER: Senior Advocates Kapil Sibal, Abhishek Manu Singhvi\nCOUNSEL FOR RESPONDENT: Solicitor General Tushar Mehta\n\nJUDGMENT DATE: 04-12-2019\n\nSUMMARY & HEADNOTE:\nHeld that economic offences cannot be treated as an absolute class where bail must be denied automatically; the basic bail test (flight risk, witness tampering) remains operative.\n\nPLAIN-LANGUAGE EXPLANATION:\nAccused persons in financial or economic cases cannot be denied bail perpetually as a form of pre-trial punishment if they do not pose a flight risk or tampering danger.\n\nSTATUTES AND PROVISIONS CONSIDERED:\nPrevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002, Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973\nSection 45 PMLA, Section 439 CrPC, Section 3 PMLA\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nEven if the allegation is one of grave economic offence, it is not a rule that bail should be denied in every such case. The gravity of the offence is to be gathered from the nature of the allegation and the severity of punishment. In determining bail, the court must consider whether there is flight risk, risk of tampering with evidence, or influencing witnesses.\n\nFINAL ORDER & DISPOSITION:\nRegular Bail Granted in INX Media Case. The petition is Allowed.\nPrecedential Status: Good Law (Precedent on PMLA Bail).	1	f	SCIN010013402019	Criminal Appeal	2019-12-04	Full Bench (3 Judges)	P. Chidambaram	Directorate of Enforcement	Senior Advocates Kapil Sibal, Abhishek Manu Singhvi	Solicitor General Tushar Mehta	Economic Offences & Bail Standards	Accused persons in financial or economic cases cannot be denied bail perpetually as a form of pre-trial punishment if they do not pose a flight risk or tampering danger.	0	(2019) 9 SCC 24	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "P. Chidambaram v. Directorate of Enforcement", "case_id": "11111111-0014-4000-8000-000000000014", "case_no": "Criminal Appeal No. 1340 of 2019", "parties": "P. Chidambaram v. Directorate of Enforcement", "category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "petitioner": "P. Chidambaram", "respondent": "Directorate of Enforcement", "legal_domain": "Economic Offences & Bail Standards", "category_facts": {"category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "surety_count": null, "custody_period": null, "application_type": "Regular Bail", "bail_bond_amount": null, "trial_court_fine": null, "reason_for_relief": null, "reporting_condition": null, "trial_court_sentence": null, "trial_court_fine_paid": false}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CRPC", "section": "439", "canonical": "Section 439 CRPC", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "scenario_keywords": [], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
