ions and constitutional safeguards warrants that the requested relief be allowed subject to the directions of the Court.	[]	Disposed	[]	https://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis	verified	Good Law	--- PAGE 1 ---\nW.P.(MD).No.17351 of 2018\nBEFORE THE MADURAI BENCH OF MADRAS HIGH COURT\nDATED : 09.09.2026\nCORAM:\nTHE HONOURABLE MR.JUSTICE RAJNISH PATHIYIL\nW.P.(MD).No.17351 of 2018\nand\nW.M.P(MD).No.15256 of 2018\nA. Johnson ... Petitioner\nVs\n1. The Managing Director,\nTamil Nadu State Transport Corporation (tirunelveli) Ltd.,\nVannarapettai,\nTirunelveli District.\n2. The General Manager,\nTamil Nadu State Transport Corporation (tirunelveli) Ltd.,\nNagercoil Region,\nKanyakumari District.\n3. The Branch Manager,\nTamil Nadu State Transport Corporation (tirunelveli) Ltd.,\nNagercoil Region,\nThiruvattar Branch,\nKanyakumari District. ... Respondents\n1/10\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\n--- PAGE 2 ---\nW.P.(MD).No.17351 of 2018\nPRAYER: Writ Petition filed under Article 226 of the Constitution of India,\npraying this Court to issue a Writ of Certiorarified Mandamus to call for the\nrecords on the file of 2nd respondent pertaining to its order bearing letter No.\n66/A2/TNSTC(Thili)/Nagar/2011 dated 03.04.2017 and to quash the same\nand consequently direct the respondents to promote the petitioner as Ticket\nExaminer on par with his immediate juniors with effect from the date of\npromotion granted to them.\nFor Petitioner : Mr.T.Jeen Joseph\nFor R1 to R3 : Mr.P.F.Ferlin Castro\nORDER\nThe relief sought for in the writ petition is to quash the impugned order\ndated 03.04.2017 bearing letter No.66/A2/TNSTC(Thili)/Nagar/2011 and\nconsequently, direct the respondents to promote the petitioner as Ticket\nExaminer.\n2. It is the case of the petitioner that he was employed through the\nDistrict Employment Office and was appointed in the year 1985 at\nKuzhithurai Branch in Kanyakumari District. He was regularized in service\nwith effect from 01.09.1986.\n2/10\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\n--- PAGE 3 ---\nW.P.(MD).No.17351 of 2018\n3. It is the further case of the petitioner that though he has put in 31\nyears of service, he has not been promoted. However, the persons who joined\nalong with the petitioner and some of his juniors were placed in the original\nseniority list and promoted to the post of Ticket Checking Inspector.\n4. It is the further case of the petitioner that he has an unblemished\nrecord of service and that no charges were framed against him and further, no\npunishment was imposed and that he has been victimized without any reason\nand consequently, he claims that he should promoted with effect on the date\nhis immediate junior namely R.Henry David (Employment No.1044) was\npromoted. He would contend that earlier he had sent a representation dated\n21.05.2016 as it did not evoke any response, he filed a writ petition in\nW.P(MD).No.11772 of 2016 and this Court vide order dated 01.12.2016 has\ndirected as follows:-\n“This writ petition has been filed, seeking a direction to the\nrespondents to promote the petitioner as Ticker Examiner on par with\nhis immediate juniors with effect from the date of promotion granted\nto them by considering the representation of the petitioner, dated\n21.05.2016.\n2. Heard the learned counsel for the petitioner and the learned\ncounsel for the respondents. By consent, the writ petition itself is\ntaken up for final disposal at the stage of admission.\n3/10\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\n--- PAGE 4 ---\nW.P.(MD).No.17351 of 2018\n3. According to the petitioner, the petitioner was appointed as\na Conductor on 1985 and his services were regularised with effect\nfrom 01.09.1996. Even though he has put in 31 years of service, the\nrespondent Corporation has not given any promotion to him.\nHowever, the other conductors who are juniors to the dated\npetitioner granted promotion as Ticket Examiners during June 2015.\nTherefore, the petitioner has given a representation dated 21.05.2016\nto the respondents 1 and 2. Since no order has been passed, the\npetitioner has filed this writ petition.\n4. The learned counsel appearing for the respondents would\nsubmit that it is agreeable for the second respondent to consider the\nrepresentation and to pass orders.\n5. Under such circumstances, the second respondent is directed\nto consider the representation of the petitioner dated 21.05.2016 and\nto pass appropriate orders, within a period of eight weeks from the\ndate of receipt of a copy of this order.\n6. The writ petition is disposed of with the above direction. No\ncosts. Consequently, W.M.P(MD).No.9031 of 2016 is closed.\n5. Consequently, the impugned order dated 03.04.2017 came to be\npassed and that is now put to challenge. The grounds urged are that the\nservices of the petitioner requires review at the end of the completion of\n6 years, 8 years and 10 years and that his review was delayed deliberately and\nhence, his promotion to the post of the Ticket Examiner has not been granted.\n4/10\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\n--- PAGE 5 ---\nW.P.(MD).No.17351 of 2018\n6. Per contra, learned counsel appearing for the respondents 1 to 3\nadverted to the counter dated 29.08.2026 and submitted that though the\npetitioner as well as the said R.Henry David (Employment No.1044) were\nappointed on the same date, i.e., 08.08.1985 and became permanent with\neffect from 01.09.1986, the petitioner was suspended from 19.07.1987 to\n17.08.1987 and the said suspension itself was treated as specific punishment.\nMoreover, the petitioner remained on leave on loss of pay from 18.08.1987 to\n20.10.1987 (Over a period of 64 days) and accordingly, the review which was\nsupposed to have taken place on 01.02.1992 (1st review) could not be granted\non that day and was granted subsequently, only on 01.02.1993 because of the\npunishment inflicted as well as the leave on account of loss of pay. Similarly,\nthe petitioner was also on leave without allowance during the 2nd and 3rd\nreviews and accordingly, the 3rd review came to be granted only on\n01.08.2008 and after the seniority list which was published on 17.05.2016\nconsequent upon the 3rd review, the petitioner was placed at No.61.\n5/10\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\n--- PAGE 6 ---\nW.P.(MD).No.17351 of 2018\n7. Paragraph 4 of the counter affidavit adverts to a table, which is\nextracted hereunder:-\nName & EDP No Date of daily Date of 1st Review 2nd Review 3rd Review Date of\npaid Monthly paid Promotion\nA.Johnson, 08.08.1985 01.09.1986 01.02.1993 01.05.2001 01.08.2008 13.07.2017\nEDP No.1040\nR.Henry David, 08.08.1985 01.09.1986 01.11.1991 01.11.1999 01.11.2009 30.06.2015\nEDP No.1044\nD.Bright Paul, 03.01.1985 03.01.1986 01.05.1991 01.01.2000 01.02.2008 15.05.2017\nEDP No.5621\nThe above table clearly shows that the immediate junior having EDP.No.1044\nwas granted the 1st review, 2nd review and 3rd review.\n8. It is further averred by the respondents that consequent upon the\nsettlement arrived between the management and the workmen under Section\n12(3) in the Industrial Disputes Act, 1974, the pattern hitherto being followed\nthat is 6 + 8 + 10 (1st review, 2nd review & 3rd review) has now been revised to\n(6 + 7 + 8 + 8 + 3) and that the petitioner himself has completed 21 years of\nqualifying service under the revised pattern as per the Settlement Dated\n22.01.2011 and the Juniors who the petitioners point out have been promoted\nearlier to the petitioner is only because of the fact that the 1st review, the 2nd\nreview and the promotions were all affected earlier. Thus, the petitioner\n6/10\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\n--- PAGE 7 ---\nW.P.(MD).No.17351 of 2018\ncannot claim parity on account of the promotions effected to the juniors as he\nwas issued with the punishment order and remained on leave without pay and\nthus, as and when vacancies arise for consideration and since the petitioners\nplaced at Sl.No.61, he would be considered for promotion.\n9. The counter affidavit filed by the respondents 1 to 3, referred to\nsupra, demonstrates that the petitioner's 1st review was conducted only on\n01.02.1993, in view of the fact that he was placed under suspension from\n09.07.1987 to 17.08.1987, for a period of 30 days, which was treated as a\npunishment.\n10. The petitioner also remained on leave without pay from 18.08.1987\nto 20.10.1987, for a period of 64 days. Therefore, the 1st review, which fell\ndue on 01.11.1991, could not be granted and was consequently granted only\non 01.02.1993. Thereafter, the 2nd review was conducted on 01.05.2001, and\nthe 3rd review was conducted on 01.08.2008. Thus, in effect, the delay in\nconducting the subsequent reviews was attributable to the postponement of\nthe 1st review on account of the punishment imposed on the petitioner for the\nmisconduct committed by him. The period of suspension was specifically\n7/10\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\n--- PAGE 8 ---\nW.P.(MD).No.17351 of 2018\ntreated as punishment, and the petitioner had also availed leave without any\naccumulated leave to his credit, which was consequently treated as leave\nwithout pay. Thus, the postponement of the 1st review was attributable to the\npetitioner's own conduct.\n11. In the above circumstances, I find that the petitioner has not made\nout a case warranting interference. Hence, the order dated 03.04.2017 bearing\nletter No.66/A2/TNSTC(Thili)/Nagar/2011, issued by the 2nd respondent does\nnot call for any interference.\n12. Accordingly, the writ petition stands dismissed. No costs.\nConsequently, connected miscellaneous petition is closed.\n09.09.2026\nNCC :yes/No\nIndex :yes/No\nInternet:yes/No\nrgm\n8/10\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\n--- PAGE 9 ---\nW.P.(MD).No.17351 of 2018\nTo\n1. The Managing Director,\nTamil Nadu State Transport Corporation (tirunelveli) Ltd.,\nVannarapettai,\nTirunelveli District.\n2. The General Manager,\nTamil Nadu State Transport Corporation (tirunelveli) Ltd.,\nNagercoil Region,\nKanyakumari District.\n3. The Branch Manager,\nTamil Nadu State Transport Corporation (tirunelveli) Ltd.,\nNagercoil Region,\nThiruvattar Branch,\nKanyakumari District.\n9/10\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\n--- PAGE 10 ---\nW.P.(MD).No.17351 of 2018\nRAJNISH PATHIYIL, J.\nrgm\nW.P.(MD).No.17351 of 2018\nand\nW.M.P(MD).No.15256 of 2018\n09.09.2026\n10/10\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis	10	t	HCMA010173512016	Writ Petition	2026-09-09	Single Bench	A. Johnson ... Petitioner	The Managing Director (W.P(MD).No.11772 of 2016)	Mr.T.Jeen Joseph	\N	Constitutional Law	In this writ petition under Criminal Law, the Court ruled on the dispute between and and The Managing Director (W.P(MD).No.11772 of 2016).	0	2016 LexGuard (Writ) 11F484	{"court": "Madras High Court", "title": "and v. The Managing Director (W.P(MD).No.11772 of 2016)", "case_id": "11f48441-0ffd-483b-b75b-2913675fff25", "case_no": "W.P.(MD).No.17351 of 2018", "parties": "and v. The Managing Director", "category": "NDPS", "petitioner": "and", "respondent": "The Managing Director (W.P(MD).No.11772 of 2016)", "legal_domain": "Constitutional Law", "category_facts": {"category": "NDPS", "custody_period": null, "contraband_type": "Contraband / Narcotics", "seized_quantity": null, "statutory_issue": "Bail / Pre-trial Release under NDPS Act", "quantity_category": "Intermediate Quantity", "section_37_embargo": false, "search_compliance_sec_50": "Search procedure evaluated"}, "activity_events": ["writ petition filed"], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "BNSS", "section": "483", "canonical": "Section 483 BNSS", "equivalents": ["Section 439 CrPC"]}], "activity_clusters": ["writ_intervention"], "scenario_clusters": ["constitutional_fundamental", "narcotics_contraband"], "scenario_keywords": ["writ of certiorari", "article 226", "ndps", "section 37 ndps"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
