P.J. Reddy, D.G. Palekar, H.R. Khanna, K.K. Mathew, M.H. Beg, S.N. Dwivedi, A.K. Mukherjea, Y.V. Chandrachud, JJ.\nDate: 1973-04-24\nCitation: (1973) 4 SCC 225\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nArticle 368 does not enable Parliament to alter the basic structure or framework of the Constitution. The constituent power of Parliament is plenary but subject to the inherent limitation that the Constitution's foundational pillars cannot be abrogated.\n\nSUMMARY:\nHistoric 13-judge bench ruling establishing the Basic Structure Doctrine: Parliament has wide powers to amend the Constitution under Article 368 but cannot alter or destroy its essential features or basic foundation.\n\nSTATUTES: Article 368, Article 13, Article 14, Article 19, Article 31\nDISPOSITION: Basic Structure Doctrine Enunciated	2	f	SCIN010001351970	Writ Petition	1973-04-24	Full Constitution Bench (13 Judges)	His Holiness Kesavananda Bharati Sripadagalvaru	State of Kerala & Another	Senior Advocate N.A. Palkhivala, F.S. Nariman	Attorney General Niren De, H.M. Seervai	Constitutional Law & Basic Structure	Parliament can amend most parts of the Constitution, but it cannot destroy the fundamental framework of democracy, rule of law, federalism, secularism, or judicial independence.	0	(1973) 4 SCC 225	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Kesavananda Bharati Sripadagalvaru v. State of Kerala and Another", "case_id": "22222222-0001-4000-8000-000000000001", "case_no": "Writ Petition (Civil) No. 135 of 1970", "parties": "His Holiness Kesavananda Bharati Sripadagalvaru v. State of Kerala & Another", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "His Holiness Kesavananda Bharati Sripadagalvaru", "respondent": "State of Kerala & Another", "legal_domain": "Constitutional Law & Basic Structure", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Writ Petition / Criminal Appeal", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "pro