22222222-0002-4000-8000-000000000002	Writ Petition No. 356 of 1977	Minerva Mills Ltd. & Others v. Union of India & Others	Supreme Court of India	1980	Hon'ble Y.V. Chandrachud, C.J., P.N. Bhagwati, A.C. Gupta, N.L. Untwalia, P.S. Kailasam, JJ.	Minerva Mills Ltd. & Others v. Union of India & Others	["Constitution of India", "Constitution (Forty-second Amendment) Act, 1976"]	["Article 368(4)", "Article 368(5)", "Article 31C", "Article 14", "Article 19"]	["Fundamental Rights vs DPSP", "judicial review", "amendment limits", "unlimited amending power void"]	Five-judge bench struck down clauses (4) and (5) of Article 368 inserted by 42nd Amendment, holding that conferring unlimited amending power on Parliament and ousting judicial review violates the Basic Structure.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_1980_WP_No_356_of_1977_22220002.pdf	9c69a3fa677eada8089270538463f40ca2d511e13ac6fcb9b39de17bf1c608f2	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:20:37.124894+05:30	2026-10-25 11:20:37.124899+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.343543+05:30	\N	[]	A limited amending power is one of the basic features of the Indian Constitution and therefore the limitations on that power cannot be destroyed. Indian Constitution is founded on the bedrock of the balance between Parts III and IV.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [1980) 3 SCC 625]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "1980) 3 SCC 625", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Constitution Bench Landmark)	Supreme Court of India\nWrit Petition No. 356 of 1977\nMinerva Mills Ltd. and Others v. Union of India and Others\nBench: Hon'ble Y.V. Chandrachud, C.J., P.N. Bhagwati, A.C. Gupta, N.L. Untwalia, P.S. Kailasam, JJ.\nDate: 1980-07-31\nCitation: (1980) 3 SCC 625\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nA limited amending power is one of the basic features of the Indian Constitution and therefore the limitations on that power cannot be destroyed. Indian Constitution is founded on the bedrock of the balance between Parts III and IV.\n\nSUMMARY:\nFive-judge bench struck down clauses (4) and (5) of Article 368 inserted by 42nd Amendment, holding that conferring unlimited amending power on Parliament and ousting judicial review violates the Basic Structure.\n\nSTATUTES: Article 368(4), Article 368(5), Article 31C, Article 14, Article 19\nDISPOSITION: Sections 4 & 55 of 42nd Amendment Struck Down	2	f	SCIN010003561977	Writ Petition	1980-07-31	Constitution Bench (5 Judges)	Minerva Mills Ltd. & Others	Union of India & Others	Senior Advocate N.A. Palkhivala	Attorney General L.N. Sinha	Constitutional Law & Fundamental Rights	The Constitution is founded on a fine balance between Fundamental Rights and Directive Principles. Parliament cannot make itself supreme over the Constitution or abolish the courts' power of judicial review.	0	(1980) 3 SCC 625	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Minerva Mills Ltd. and Others v. Union of India and Others", "case_id": "22222222-0002-4000-8000-000000000002", "case_no": "Writ Petition No. 356 of 1977", "parties": "Minerva Mills Ltd. & Others v. Union of India & Others", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Minerva Mills Ltd. & Others", "respondent": "Union of India & Others", "legal_domain": "Constitutional Law & Fundamental Rights", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Writ Petition / Criminal Appeal", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "368", "canonical": "Section 368 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "31C", "canonical": "Section 31C CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "14", "canonical": "Section 14 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "s