22222222-0003-4000-8000-000000000003	Writ Petition (Civil) No. 930 of 1990	Indra Sawhney & Others v. Union of India & Others	Supreme Court of India	1992	Hon'ble M.H. Kania, C.J., M.N. Venkatachaliah, S.R. Pandian, T.K. Thommen, A.M. Ahmadi, K. Singh, P.B. Sawant, R.M. Sahai, B.P. Jeevan Reddy, JJ.	Indra Sawhney & Others v. Union of India & Others	["Constitution of India"]	["Article 16(1)", "Article 16(4)", "Article 15(4)", "Article 14", "Article 340"]	["Mandal Commission", "50 percent ceiling", "creamy layer", "OBC reservation", "Article 16(4)"]	Nine-judge Constitution Bench upheld 27% reservation for Other Backward Classes (OBCs) subject to the exclusion of the 'creamy layer' and ruled that total reservations should not exceed 50% except in extraordinary situations.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_1992_WP_Civil_No_930_of_1990_22220003.pdf	8da74907c1979094d9ac97faf8101fbb98c35ac70fe52576f316f3cb358f980b	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:20:37.149417+05:30	2026-10-25 11:20:37.14942+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.344165+05:30	\N	[]	Reservation under Article 16(4) cannot exceed 50% of seats or posts save in extraordinary situations. The creamy layer among socially and educationally backward classes must be excluded from affirmative action benefits.	[]	Disposed	[]	\N	unverified	Good Law (9-Judge Constitution Bench Landmark)	Supreme Court of India\nWrit Petition (Civil) No. 930 of 1990\nIndra Sawhney and Others v. Union of India and Others\nBench: Hon'ble M.H. Kania, C.J., M.N. Venkatachaliah, S.R. Pandian, T.K. Thommen, A.M. Ahmadi, K. Singh, P.B. Sawant, R.M. Sahai, B.P. Jeevan Reddy, JJ.\nDate: 1992-11-16\nCitation: 1992 Supp (3) SCC 217\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nReservation under Article 16(4) cannot exceed 50% of seats or posts save in extraordinary situations. The creamy layer among socially and educationally backward classes must be excluded from affirmative action benefits.\n\nSUMMARY:\nNine-judge Constitution Bench upheld 27% reservation for Other Backward Classes (OBCs) subject to the exclusion of the 'creamy layer' and ruled that total reservations should not exceed 50% except in extraordinary situations.\n\nSTATUTES: Article 16(1), Article 16(4), Article 15(4), Article 14, Article 340\nDISPOSITION: Mandal Commission Upheld with 50% Ceiling	2	f	SCIN010009301990	Writ Petition	1992-11-16	Constitution Bench (9 Judges)	Indra Sawhney & Others	Union of India & Others	Senior Advocate K.K. Venugopal, N.A. Palkhivala	Attorney General G. Ramaswamy	Constitutional Law & Affirmative Action	OBC reservations up to 27% are constitutional, but wealthy/influential members (creamy layer) must be excluded. Total reservations cannot cross 50% under normal circumstances.	3	1992 Supp (3) SCC 217	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Indra Sawhney and Others v. Union of India and Others", "case_id": "22222222-0003-4000-8000-000000000003", "case_no": "Writ Petition (Civil) No. 930 of 1990", "parties": "Indra Sawhney & Others v. Union of India & Others", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Indra Sawhney & Others", "respondent": "Union of India & Others", "legal_domain": "Constitutional Law & Affirmative Action", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Writ Petition / Criminal Appeal", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "16", "canonical": "Section 16 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "15", "canonical": "Section 15 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "14", "canonical": "Section 14 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "340", "canonical": "Section 340 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["constitutional_fundamental"], "scenario_keywords": ["article 14"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Disposed with Directions", "disposition": "Disposed with Directions"}}
22222222-0004-4000-8000-000000000004	Civil Appeal No. 3645 of 1989	S.R. Bommai & Others v. Union of India & Others	Supreme Court of India	1994	Hon'ble S. Ratnavel Pandian, A.M. Ahmadi, J.S. Verma, P.B. Sawant, K. Ramaswamy, S.C. Agrawal, Y. Dayal, B.P. Jeevan Reddy, S.P. Bharucha, JJ.	S.R. Bommai & Others v. Union of India & Others	["Constitution of India"]	["Article 356", "Article 355", "Article 74(2)", "Article 174", "Article 226"]	["President's Rule", "Article 356", "floor test", "secularism as basic structure", "federalism"]	Nine-judge bench laid down stringent safeguards against arbitrary imposition of President's Rule under Article 356. Floor test in the Legislative Assembly is the only authentic method to prove majority.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_1994_CA_No_3645_of_1989_22220004.pdf	b33b9dfa61da0ea5d7f196e079370cab74a5e63685f060aa645692d831c89b43	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:20:37.174403+05:30	2026-10-25 11:20:37.174407+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.344684+05:30	\N	[]	Presidential proclamation under Article 356 is subject to judicial review on grounds of mala fides or irrelevant considerations. The Legislative Assembly cannot be dissolved until Parliament approves the proclamation under Article 356(3).	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [1994) 3 SCC 1]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "1994) 3 SCC 1", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (9-Judge Federalism Landmark)	Supreme Court of India\nCivil Appeal No. 3645 of 1989\nS.R. Bommai and Others v. Union of India and Others\nBench: Hon'ble S. Ratnavel Pandian, A.M. Ahmadi, J.S. Verma, P.B. Sawant, K. Ramaswamy, S.C. Agrawal, Y. Dayal, B.P. Jeevan Reddy, S.P. Bharucha, JJ.\nDate: 1994-03-11\nCitation: (1994) 3 SCC 1\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nPresidential proclamation under Article 356 is subject to judicial review on grounds of mala fides or irrelevant considerations. The Legislative Assembly cannot be dissolved until Parliament approves the proclamation under Article 356(3).\n\nSUMMARY:\nNine-judge bench laid down stringent safeguards against arbitrary imposition of President's Rule under Article 356. Floor test in the Legislative Assembly is the only authentic method to prove majority.\n\nSTATUTES: Article 356, Article 355, Article 74(2), Article 174, Article 226\nDISPOSITION: Guidelines on Article 356 and Assembly Dissolution Established	2	f	SCIN010036451989	Civil Appeal	1994-03-11	Constitution Bench (9 Judges)	S.R. Bommai & Others	Union of India & Others	Senior Advocate Soli J. Sorabjee, Ram Jethmalani	Attorney General Milon K. Banerji	Constitutional Law & Federalism	The Central Government cannot dismiss an elected state government arbitrarily. Majority must be tested on the floor of the Assembly, not in the Governor's office. Secularism and federalism are part of the Basic Structure.	0	(1994) 3 SCC 1	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "S.R. Bommai and Others v. Union of India and Others", "case_id": "22222222-0004-4000-8000-000000000004", "case_no": "Civil Appeal No. 3645 of 1989", "parties": "S.R. Bommai & Others v. Union of India & Others", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "S.R. Bommai & Others", "respondent": "Union of India & Others", "legal_domain": "Constitutional Law & Federalism", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Writ Petition / Criminal Appeal", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "356", "canonical": "Section 356 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "355", "canonical": "Section 355 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "74", "canonical": "Section 74 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "174", "canonical": "Section 174 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "226", "canonical": "Section 226 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["constitutional_fundamental"], "scenario_keywords": ["article 226"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed in Part", "disposition": "Allowed in Part"}}
22222222-0005-4000-8000-000000000005	Civil Appeal No. 1392 of 1975	Additional District Magistrate, Jabalpur v. Shivkant Shukla & Others	Supreme Court of India	1976	Hon'ble A.N. Ray, C.J., H.R. Khanna, M.H. Beg, Y.V. Chandrachud, P.N. Bhagwati, JJ.	Additional District Magistrate, Jabalpur v. Shivkant Shukla & Others	["Constitution of India"]	["Article 21", "Article 19", "Article 14", "Article 359", "Article 226"]	["Emergency", "Habeas Corpus", "suspension of Article 21", "Khanna dissent", "detention without trial"]	Majority held that during Emergency, citizens could not enforce Right to Life and Liberty under Article 21 via Habeas Corpus. Justice H.R. Khanna delivered historic lone dissent. Explicitly overruled by K.S. Puttaswamy (2017).	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_1976_CA_No_1392_of_1975_22220005.pdf	0083e4a67e055a071e26219d4b8b6937f4a96cdb553d99d553e15720ee572124	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:20:37.209353+05:30	2026-10-25 11:20:37.209358+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.345433+05:30	\N	[]	In view of Presidential Order under Article 359(1), no person has locus standi to move any writ petition under Article 226 for habeas corpus challenging legality of detention order. [OVERRULED by 9-Judge Bench in K.S. Puttaswamy (2017)].	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Justice K.S. Puttaswamy (Retd.) v. Union of India", "status": "Good Law (Unanimous 9-Judge Benchmark)", "case_id": "11111111-0009-4000-8000-000000000009", "citation": "(2017) 10 SCC 1", "ratio_key": "Fundamental right to privacy under Article 21", "treatment": "Followed / Relied Upon"}, {"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [1976) 2 SCC 521]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "1976) 2 SCC 521", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Overruled by Supreme Court (K.S. Puttaswamy)	Supreme Court of India\nCivil Appeal No. 1392 of 1975\nAdditional District Magistrate, Jabalpur v. Shivkant Shukla\nBench: Hon'ble A.N. Ray, C.J., H.R. Khanna, M.H. Beg, Y.V. Chandrachud, P.N. Bhagwati, JJ.\nDate: 1976-04-28\nCitation: (1976) 2 SCC 521\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nIn view of Presidential Order under Article 359(1), no person has locus standi to move any writ petition under Article 226 for habeas corpus challenging legality of detention order. [OVERRULED by 9-Judge Bench in K.S. Puttaswamy (2017)].\n\nSUMMARY:\nMajority held that during Emergency, citizens could not enforce Right to Life and Liberty under Article 21 via Habeas Corpus. Justice H.R. Khanna delivered historic lone dissent. Explicitly overruled by K.S. Puttaswamy (2017).\n\nSTATUTES: Article 21, Article 19, Article 14, Article 359, Article 226\nDISPOSITION: Habeas Corpus Petitions Barred (Overruled in 2017)	2	f	SCIN010013921975	Civil Appeal	1976-04-28	Constitution Bench (5 Judges)	Additional District Magistrate, Jabalpur	Shivkant Shukla & Others	Attorney General Niren De	Senior Advocate Shanti Bhushan, Soli Sorabjee	Habeas Corpus & Emergency Jurisprudence	Historic Emergency ruling that wrongly held no one could challenge illegal detention during Emergency. Justice Khanna bravely dissented. This decision was permanently overruled in the 2017 Right to Privacy judgment.	0	(1976) 2 SCC 521	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Additional District Magistrate, Jabalpur v. Shivkant Shukla", "case_id": "22222222-0005-4000-8000-000000000005", "case_no": "Civil Appeal No. 1392 of 1975", "parties": "Additional District Magistrate, Jabalpur v. Shivkant Shukla & Others", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Additional District Magistrate, Jabalpur", "respondent": "Shivkant Shukla & Others", "legal_domain": "Habeas Corpus & Emergency Jurisprudence", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Habeas Corpus (Preventive Detention Challenge)", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "21", "canonical": "Section 21 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "19", "canonical": "Section 19 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "14", "canonical": "Section 14 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "359", "canonical": "Section 359 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "226", "canonical": "Section 226 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["constitutional_fundamental"], "scenario_keywords": ["article 226", "article 14", "article 21"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Reversed", "disposition": "Reversed"}}
22222222-0007-4000-8000-000000000007	Writ Petition (Criminal) No. 76 of 2016	Navtej Singh Johar & Others v. Union of India through Ministry of Law and Justice	Supreme Court of India	2018	Hon'ble Dipak Misra, C.J., R.F. Nariman, A.M. Khanwilkar, D.Y. Chandrachud, Indu Malhotra, JJ.	Navtej Singh Johar & Others v. Union of India through Ministry of Law and Justice	["Indian Penal Code, 1860"]	["Section 377 IPC", "Article 14", "Article 15", "Article 19(1)(a)", "Article 21"]	["Section 377", "LGBTQ+ rights", "consensual adult relationships", "constitutional morality", "dignity and privacy"]	Five-judge Constitution Bench struck down Section 377 IPC insofar as it criminalized consensual sexual acts between adults in private, affirming constitutional morality over majoritarian or social morality.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_2018_WP_Crl_No_76_of_2016_22220007.pdf	b757be4192a5ce03af8ab6a82cbf3197f91dbf51d5434cdd2a69c1e345e5b664	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:20:37.252785+05:30	2026-10-25 11:20:37.252788+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.346708+05:30	\N	[]	Section 377 IPC, so far as it penalizes consensual sexual activity between adults, violates Articles 14, 15, 19 and 21 of the Constitution. Constitutional morality requires protection of individual autonomy and sexual orientation.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2018) 10 SCC 1]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2018) 10 SCC 1", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Constitution Bench Landmark)	Supreme Court of India\nWrit Petition (Criminal) No. 76 of 2016\nNavtej Singh Johar and Others v. Union of India and Others\nBench: Hon'ble Dipak Misra, C.J., R.F. Nariman, A.M. Khanwilkar, D.Y. Chandrachud, Indu Malhotra, JJ.\nDate: 2018-09-06\nCitation: (2018) 10 SCC 1\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nSection 377 IPC, so far as it penalizes consensual sexual activity between adults, violates Articles 14, 15, 19 and 21 of the Constitution. Constitutional morality requires protection of individual autonomy and sexual orientation.\n\nSUMMARY:\nFive-judge Constitution Bench struck down Section 377 IPC insofar as it criminalized consensual sexual acts between adults in private, affirming constitutional morality over majoritarian or social morality.\n\nSTATUTES: Section 377 IPC, Article 14, Article 15, Article 19(1)(a), Article 21\nDISPOSITION: Section 377 IPC Decriminalized for Consensual Adults	2	f	SCIN010000762016	Writ Petition	2018-09-06	Constitution Bench (5 Judges)	Navtej Singh Johar & Others	Union of India through Ministry of Law and Justice	Senior Advocate Mukul Rohatgi, Shyam Divan, Arvind Datar	Additional Solicitor General Tushar Mehta	Constitutional Law & Human Rights	Homosexuality between consenting adults in private is no longer a crime. The Supreme Court declared that LGBTQ+ citizens are entitled to full equality, dignity, privacy, and protection under the Constitution.	0	(2018) 10 SCC 1	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Navtej Singh Johar and Others v. Union of India and Others", "case_id": "22222222-0007-4000-8000-000000000007", "case_no": "Writ Petition (Criminal) No. 76 of 2016", "parties": "Navtej Singh Johar & Others v. Union of India through Ministry of Law and Justice", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Navtej Singh Johar & Others", "respondent": "Union of India through Ministry of Law and Justice", "legal_domain": "Constitutional Law & Human Rights", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Writ Petition / Criminal Appeal", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "IPC", "section": "377", "canonical": "Section 377 IPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "14", "canonical": "Section 14 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "15", "canonical": "Section 15 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "19", "canonical": "Section 19 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "21", "canonical": "Section 21 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["constitutional_fundamental"], "scenario_keywords": ["article 14", "article 21"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
22222222-0008-4000-8000-000000000008	Writ Petition (Criminal) No. 194 of 2017	Joseph Shine v. Union of India	Supreme Court of India	2019	Hon'ble Dipak Misra, C.J., R.F. Nariman, A.M. Khanwilkar, D.Y. Chandrachud, Indu Malhotra, JJ.	Joseph Shine v. Union of India	["Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973", "Indian Penal Code, 1860"]	["Section 497 IPC", "Section 198(2) CrPC", "Article 14", "Article 15", "Article 21"]	["adultery", "Section 497 IPC struck down", "gender equality", "proprietary ownership of woman", "sexual autonomy"]	Constitution Bench unanimously struck down Section 497 IPC (adultery) as archaic, patriarchal, and violative of Articles 14, 15 and 21. Adultery remains a civil ground for divorce but ceases to be a criminal offence.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_2019_WP_Crl_No_194_of_2017_22220008.pdf	86495156d5653374db0cbc648e8e230be5a7878970b868a5d317f7c9fd87cb49	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:20:37.274336+05:30	2026-10-25 11:20:37.27434+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.347396+05:30	\N	[]	Section 497 IPC deprives woman of agency, autonomy, and dignity by viewing her through the lens of patriarchal proprietary control. Criminalizing adultery violates constitutional guarantees of equality and personal liberty.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2019) 3 SCC 39]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2019) 3 SCC 39", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Constitution Bench Landmark)	Supreme Court of India\nWrit Petition (Criminal) No. 194 of 2017\nJoseph Shine v. Union of India\nBench: Hon'ble Dipak Misra, C.J., R.F. Nariman, A.M. Khanwilkar, D.Y. Chandrachud, Indu Malhotra, JJ.\nDate: 2018-09-27\nCitation: (2019) 3 SCC 39\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nSection 497 IPC deprives woman of agency, autonomy, and dignity by viewing her through the lens of patriarchal proprietary control. Criminalizing adultery violates constitutional guarantees of equality and personal liberty.\n\nSUMMARY:\nConstitution Bench unanimously struck down Section 497 IPC (adultery) as archaic, patriarchal, and violative of Articles 14, 15 and 21. Adultery remains a civil ground for divorce but ceases to be a criminal offence.\n\nSTATUTES: Section 497 IPC, Section 198(2) CrPC, Article 14, Article 15, Article 21\nDISPOSITION: Section 497 IPC & Section 198(2) CrPC Declared Unconstitutional	2	f	SCIN010001942017	Writ Petition	2018-09-27	Constitution Bench (5 Judges)	Joseph Shine	Union of India	Senior Advocate Kaleeswaram Raj	Additional Solicitor General Pinky Anand	Criminal Law & Gender Justice	Section 497 IPC treated women as property of their husbands and was declared unconstitutional. Adultery is no longer a crime in India, though it can still be a valid ground for civil divorce.	0	(2019) 3 SCC 39	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Joseph Shine v. Union of India", "case_id": "22222222-0008-4000-8000-000000000008", "case_no": "Writ Petition (Criminal) No. 194 of 2017", "parties": "Joseph Shine v. Union of India", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Joseph Shine", "respondent": "Union of India", "legal_domain": "Criminal Law & Gender Justice", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Writ Petition / Criminal Appeal", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "IPC", "section": "497", "canonical": "Section 497 IPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CRPC", "section": "198", "canonical": "Section 198 CRPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "14", "canonical": "Section 14 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "15", "canonical": "Section 15 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "21", "canonical": "Section 21 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["constitutional_fundamental"], "scenario_keywords": ["article 14", "article 21"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
22222222-0009-4000-8000-000000000009	Writ Petition (Civil) No. 118 of 2016	Shayara Bano & Others v. Union of India & Ministry of Law and Justice	Supreme Court of India	2017	Hon'ble J.S. Khehar, C.J., Kurian Joseph, R.F. Nariman, U.U. Lalit, S. Abdul Nazeer, JJ.	Shayara Bano & Others v. Union of India & Ministry of Law and Justice	["Shariat Act"]	["Section 2 Shariat Act"]	["Triple Talaq", "Talaq-e-Biddat", "arbitrary divorce", "Muslim women rights", "Article 14 manifest arbitrariness"]	Five-judge bench by 3:2 majority declared the practice of instant Triple Talaq (Talaq-e-Biddat) void, illegal, and unconstitutional, applying the test of manifest arbitrariness under Article 14.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_2017_WP_Civil_No_118_of_2016_22220009.pdf	9297d8b68ec5fc22ecd710eb71ad338db27539105deaaffac10b118efae6983e	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:20:37.29292+05:30	2026-10-25 11:20:37.292923+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.348113+05:30	\N	[]	Talaq-e-Biddat is manifestly arbitrary because it breaks marital ties capriciously and whimsically without any attempt at reconciliation. Manifest arbitrariness is a direct ground to invalidate legislation and personal practices under Article 14.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2017) 9 SCC 1]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2017) 9 SCC 1", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Constitution Bench Landmark)	Supreme Court of India\nWrit Petition (Civil) No. 118 of 2016\nShayara Bano and Others v. Union of India and Others\nBench: Hon'ble J.S. Khehar, C.J., Kurian Joseph, R.F. Nariman, U.U. Lalit, S. Abdul Nazeer, JJ.\nDate: 2017-08-22\nCitation: (2017) 9 SCC 1\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nTalaq-e-Biddat is manifestly arbitrary because it breaks marital ties capriciously and whimsically without any attempt at reconciliation. Manifest arbitrariness is a direct ground to invalidate legislation and personal practices under Article 14.\n\nSUMMARY:\nFive-judge bench by 3:2 majority declared the practice of instant Triple Talaq (Talaq-e-Biddat) void, illegal, and unconstitutional, applying the test of manifest arbitrariness under Article 14.\n\nSTATUTES: Section 2 Shariat Act, Article 14, Article 15, Article 21, Article 25\nDISPOSITION: Talaq-e-Biddat Declared Void and Illegal	2	f	SCIN010001182016	Writ Petition	2017-08-22	Constitution Bench (5 Judges)	Shayara Bano & Others	Union of India & Ministry of Law and Justice	Senior Advocate Balaji Srinivasan, Indira Jaising	Attorney General K.K. Venugopal, Kapil Sibal	Family Law & Constitutional Morality	Instant triple talaq (saying talaq three times in one go) is unconstitutional and illegal. It provides no scope for reconciliation and arbitrarily violates Muslim women's dignity and equality.	0	(2017) 9 SCC 1	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Shayara Bano and Others v. Union of India and Others", "case_id": "22222222-0009-4000-8000-000000000009", "case_no": "Writ Petition (Civil) No. 118 of 2016", "parties": "Shayara Bano & Others v. Union of India & Ministry of Law and Justice", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Shayara Bano & Others", "respondent": "Union of India & Ministry of Law and Justice", "legal_domain": "Family Law & Constitutional Morality", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Writ Petition / Criminal Appeal", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "14", "canonical": "Section 14 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "15", "canonical": "Section 15 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "21", "canonical": "Section 21 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "25", "canonical": "Section 25 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["constitutional_fundamental"], "scenario_keywords": ["article 14", "article 21"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
22222222-0010-4000-8000-000000000010	Criminal Appeal No. 103 of 1981	Mohd. Ahmed Khan v. Shah Bano Begum & Others	Supreme Court of India	1985	Hon'ble Y.V. Chandrachud, C.J., D.A. Desai, O. Chinnappa Reddy, E.S. Venkataramiah, R. Misra, JJ.	Mohd. Ahmed Khan v. Shah Bano Begum & Others	["Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973"]	["Section 125 CrPC", "Section 127(3)(b) CrPC"]	["maintenance to divorced wife", "Section 125 CrPC", "secular remedy", "vagrancy prevention", "iddat period"]	Five-judge bench affirmed that Section 125 CrPC is a secular provision applying to all citizens regardless of religion, entitling a divorced Muslim wife unable to maintain herself to maintenance beyond the iddat period.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_1985_Crl_App_No_103_of_1981_22220010.pdf	5156497e17fb3a775e11177d2e3c2602c677a7c30276e5a8f07c0f3404ad17e1	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:20:37.313663+05:30	2026-10-25 11:20:37.313667+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.349531+05:30	\N	[]	Section 125 CrPC is truly secular in character and applies to all persons irrespective of the religion professed by the parties. Liability to provide maintenance is not extinguished upon completion of iddat if the divorced wife cannot maintain herself.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [1985) 2 SCC 556]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "1985) 2 SCC 556", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Constitution Bench Landmark)	Supreme Court of India\nCriminal Appeal No. 103 of 1981\nMohd. Ahmed Khan v. Shah Bano Begum and Others\nBench: Hon'ble Y.V. Chandrachud, C.J., D.A. Desai, O. Chinnappa Reddy, E.S. Venkataramiah, R. Misra, JJ.\nDate: 1985-04-23\nCitation: (1985) 2 SCC 556\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nSection 125 CrPC is truly secular in character and applies to all persons irrespective of the religion professed by the parties. Liability to provide maintenance is not extinguished upon completion of iddat if the divorced wife cannot maintain herself.\n\nSUMMARY:\nFive-judge bench affirmed that Section 125 CrPC is a secular provision applying to all citizens regardless of religion, entitling a divorced Muslim wife unable to maintain herself to maintenance beyond the iddat period.\n\nSTATUTES: Section 125 CrPC, Section 127(3)(b) CrPC\nDISPOSITION: Maintenance Order in Favor of Divorced Wife Upheld	2	f	SCIN010001031981	Criminal Appeal	1985-04-23	Constitution Bench (5 Judges)	Mohd. Ahmed Khan	Shah Bano Begum & Others	Advocate Z.U. Ansari	Senior Advocate Daniel Latifi	Family & Maintenance Jurisprudence	Section 125 CrPC applies to everyone across all religions. A divorced wife who cannot maintain herself is entitled to monthly maintenance to prevent vagrancy and destitution.	0	(1985) 2 SCC 556	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Mohd. Ahmed Khan v. Shah Bano Begum and Others", "case_id": "22222222-0010-4000-8000-000000000010", "case_no": "Criminal Appeal No. 103 of 1981", "parties": "Mohd. Ahmed Khan v. Shah Bano Begum & Others", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Mohd. Ahmed Khan", "respondent": "Shah Bano Begum & Others", "legal_domain": "Family & Maintenance Jurisprudence", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Criminal Appeal Against Conviction", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CRPC", "section": "125", "canonical": "Section 125 CRPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CRPC", "section": "127", "canonical": "Section 127 CRPC", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "scenario_keywords": [], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Dismissed", "disposition": "Dismissed"}}
22222222-0011-4000-8000-000000000011	Writ Petition (Civil) No. 12739 of 1985	M.C. Mehta & Another v. Union of India & Shriram Foods & Fertilizer Industries	Supreme Court of India	1987	Hon'ble P.N. Bhagwati, C.J., R. Misra, G.L. Oza, M.M. Dutt, K.N. Singh, JJ.	M.C. Mehta & Another v. Union of India & Shriram Foods & Fertilizer Industries	["Constitution of India", "Factories Act, 1948"]	["Article 21", "Article 32", "Article 12"]	["Absolute Liability", "Oleum gas leak", "hazardous enterprise", "Rylands v. Fletcher discarded", "compensatory remedy"]	Five-judge Constitution Bench formulated the Principle of Absolute Liability: enterprises engaged in hazardous or inherently dangerous activities owe an absolute and non-delegable duty to the community, free from exceptions in Rylands v. Fletcher.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_1987_WP_Civil_No_12739_of_1985_22220011.pdf	00a1ce1aa83c8b60e8e2cdfdc314194df5f84743da93a14b70099ea95a124c65	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:20:37.334779+05:30	2026-10-25 11:20:37.334784+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.350983+05:30	\N	[]	Where an enterprise is engaged in a hazardous or inherently dangerous activity and harm results to anyone on account of an accident in the operation of such activity, the enterprise is strictly and absolutely liable to compensate all those who are affected.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [1987) 1 SCC 395]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "1987) 1 SCC 395", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Foundational Environmental Landmark)	Supreme Court of India\nWrit Petition (Civil) No. 12739 of 1985\nM.C. Mehta and Another v. Union of India and Others\nBench: Hon'ble P.N. Bhagwati, C.J., R. Misra, G.L. Oza, M.M. Dutt, K.N. Singh, JJ.\nDate: 1986-12-20\nCitation: (1987) 1 SCC 395\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nWhere an enterprise is engaged in a hazardous or inherently dangerous activity and harm results to anyone on account of an accident in the operation of such activity, the enterprise is strictly and absolutely liable to compensate all those who are affected.\n\nSUMMARY:\nFive-judge Constitution Bench formulated the Principle of Absolute Liability: enterprises engaged in hazardous or inherently dangerous activities owe an absolute and non-delegable duty to the community, free from exceptions in Rylands v. Fletcher.\n\nSTATUTES: Article 21, Article 32, Article 12\nDISPOSITION: Absolute Liability Doctrine Formulated and Enforced	2	f	SCIN010127391985	Writ Petition	1986-12-20	Constitution Bench (5 Judges)	M.C. Mehta & Another	Union of India & Shriram Foods & Fertilizer Industries	Petitioner-in-person M.C. Mehta	Senior Advocate F.S. Nariman, K.K. Venugopal	Environmental Law & Torts	If a factory deals with dangerous or hazardous materials and leaks occur, the enterprise must pay full compensation for harm caused. No defenses (such as act of god or third-party interference) are allowed.	0	(1987) 1 SCC 395	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "M.C. Mehta and Another v. Union of India and Others", "case_id": "22222222-0011-4000-8000-000000000011", "case_no": "Writ Petition (Civil) No. 12739 of 1985", "parties": "M.C. Mehta & Another v. Union of India & Shriram Foods & Fertilizer Industries", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "M.C. Mehta & Another", "respondent": "Union of India & Shriram Foods & Fertilizer Industries", "legal_domain": "Environmental Law & Torts", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Writ Petition / Criminal Appeal", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "21", "canonical": "Section 21 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "32", "canonical": "Section 32 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "12", "canonical": "Section 12 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["constitutional_fundamental"], "scenario_keywords": ["article 32", "article 21"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Directions Issued", "disposition": "Directions Issued"}}
22222222-0012-4000-8000-000000000012	Criminal Appeal No. 1267 of 2004	Selvi & Others v. State of Karnataka	Supreme Court of India	2010	Hon'ble K.G. Balakrishnan, C.J., R.V. Raveendran, J.M. Panchal, JJ.	Selvi & Others v. State of Karnataka	["Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973", "Evidence Act"]	["Section 27 Evidence Act"]	["narco-analysis", "polygraph test", "BEASP / brain mapping", "self-incrimination", "involuntary test unconstitutional"]	Three-judge bench held that involuntary administration of narco-analysis, lie-detector polygraph tests, and brain-mapping BEASP violates Article 20(3) (right against self-incrimination) and Article 21 personal privacy.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_2010_Crl_App_No_1267_of_2004_22220012.pdf	bd67bd051b967bb46ffa08700541e32838a8b01591c18a8df002571c7adb99ed	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:20:37.353189+05:30	2026-10-25 11:20:37.353193+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.352925+05:30	\N	[]	Involuntary administration of narco-analysis, polygraph test, or Brain Electrical Activation Profile (BEASP) violates the right against self-incrimination under Article 20(3) and procedural due process under Article 21.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2010) 7 SCC 263]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2010) 7 SCC 263", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding 3-Judge Precedent)	Supreme Court of India\nCriminal Appeal No. 1267 of 2004\nSelvi and Others v. State of Karnataka\nBench: Hon'ble K.G. Balakrishnan, C.J., R.V. Raveendran, J.M. Panchal, JJ.\nDate: 2010-05-05\nCitation: (2010) 7 SCC 263\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nInvoluntary administration of narco-analysis, polygraph test, or Brain Electrical Activation Profile (BEASP) violates the right against self-incrimination under Article 20(3) and procedural due process under Article 21.\n\nSUMMARY:\nThree-judge bench held that involuntary administration of narco-analysis, lie-detector polygraph tests, and brain-mapping BEASP violates Article 20(3) (right against self-incrimination) and Article 21 personal privacy.\n\nSTATUTES: Article 20(3), Article 21, Section 161(2) CrPC, Section 27 Evidence Act\nDISPOSITION: Involuntary Neuro-Scientific Investigative Tests Barred	2	f	SCIN010012672004	Criminal Appeal	2010-05-05	Three-Judge Bench	Selvi & Others	State of Karnataka	Senior Advocate Dushyant Dave	Senior Advocate Sanjay Hegde	Criminal Procedure & Constitutional Rights	Police cannot force anyone to undergo narco-analysis or lie-detector tests without their voluntary consent. Forcing chemical or brain tests infringes the constitutional protection against self-incrimination.	0	(2010) 7 SCC 263	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Selvi and Others v. State of Karnataka", "case_id": "22222222-0012-4000-8000-000000000012", "case_no": "Criminal Appeal No. 1267 of 2004", "parties": "Selvi & Others v. State of Karnataka", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Selvi & Others", "respondent": "State of Karnataka", "legal_domain": "Criminal Procedure & Constitutional Rights", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Criminal Appeal Against Conviction", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "20", "canonical": "Section 20 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "21", "canonical": "Section 21 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CRPC", "section": "161", "canonical": "Section 161 CRPC", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["constitutional_fundamental"], "scenario_keywords": ["article 21"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
22222222-0013-4000-8000-000000000013	Writ Petition (Civil) No. 215 of 2005	Common Cause (A Regd. Society) v. Union of India & Another	Supreme Court of India	2018	Hon'ble Dipak Misra, C.J., A.K. Sikri, A.M. Khanwilkar, D.Y. Chandrachud, Ashok Bhushan, JJ.	Common Cause (A Regd. Society) v. Union of India & Another	["Constitution of India"]	["Article 21", "Article 14", "Article 19"]	["passive euthanasia", "living will", "advance medical directive", "right to die with dignity", "terminal illness"]	Constitution Bench recognized the legal validity of Advance Medical Directives ('Living Will') and declared that the fundamental right to life under Article 21 includes the right to die with dignity through passive euthanasia.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_2018_WP_Civil_No_215_of_2005_22220013.pdf	50ab2b3191d56b7d2439c92b8b6961f4efdc07b4467f3d9c71b5ff97b7465833	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:20:37.377139+05:30	2026-10-25 11:20:37.377143+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.353674+05:30	\N	[]	An adult human being having mental capacity has the right to refuse medical treatment of any kind, including withdrawal of life support systems. The right to live with dignity under Article 21 encompasses the right to die with dignity.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2018) 5 SCC 1]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2018) 5 SCC 1", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Constitution Bench Landmark)	Supreme Court of India\nWrit Petition (Civil) No. 215 of 2005\nCommon Cause (A Regd. Society) v. Union of India and Another\nBench: Hon'ble Dipak Misra, C.J., A.K. Sikri, A.M. Khanwilkar, D.Y. Chandrachud, Ashok Bhushan, JJ.\nDate: 2018-03-09\nCitation: (2018) 5 SCC 1\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nAn adult human being having mental capacity has the right to refuse medical treatment of any kind, including withdrawal of life support systems. The right to live with dignity under Article 21 encompasses the right to die with dignity.\n\nSUMMARY:\nConstitution Bench recognized the legal validity of Advance Medical Directives ('Living Will') and declared that the fundamental right to life under Article 21 includes the right to die with dignity through passive euthanasia.\n\nSTATUTES: Article 21, Article 14, Article 19\nDISPOSITION: Passive Euthanasia & Advance Directives Guidelines Formulated	2	f	SCIN010002152005	Writ Petition	2018-03-09	Constitution Bench (5 Judges)	Common Cause (A Regd. Society)	Union of India & Another	Senior Advocate Prashant Bhushan	Additional Solicitor General P.S. Narasimha	Right to Die with Dignity & Medical Law	Every competent adult has the legal right to execute an advance medical directive (living will) specifying that they should not be kept alive on artificial life-support when suffering from terminal illness with no hope of recovery.	0	(2018) 5 SCC 1	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Common Cause (A Regd. Society) v. Union of India and Another", "case_id": "22222222-0013-4000-8000-000000000013", "case_no": "Writ Petition (Civil) No. 215 of 2005", "parties": "Common Cause (A Regd. Society) v. Union of India & Another", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Common Cause (A Regd. Society)", "respondent": "Union of India & Another", "legal_domain": "Right to Die with Dignity & Medical Law", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Writ Petition / Criminal Appeal", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "21", "canonical": "Section 21 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "14", "canonical": "Section 14 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "19", "canonical": "Section 19 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["constitutional_fundamental"], "scenario_keywords": ["article 14", "article 21"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
22222222-0014-4000-8000-000000000014	Criminal Appeal No. 267 of 1995	Gian Kaur v. State of Punjab	Supreme Court of India	1996	Hon'ble J.S. Verma, G.N. Ray, N.P. Singh, Faizan Uddin, G.T. Nanavati, JJ.	Gian Kaur v. State of Punjab	["Indian Penal Code, 1860"]	["Section 306 IPC", "Section 309 IPC", "Article 21", "Article 14"]	["abetment of suicide", "attempt to commit suicide", "Section 309 IPC constitutional", "P. Rathinam overruled", "sanctity of life"]	Five-judge Constitution Bench overruled P. Rathinam (1994) and upheld the constitutional validity of Section 306 (abetment of suicide) and Section 309 IPC, ruling that Article 21 protects life and does not include the right to commit suicide.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_1996_Crl_App_No_267_of_1995_22220014.pdf	07634625eaf05a07824733e7168bf57cb2e8589d27df98af7afc3cc01ef2e9b5	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:20:37.412254+05:30	2026-10-25 11:20:37.412258+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.354623+05:30	\N	[]	Article 21 cannot be construed to include within its ambit the 'right to die'. Right to life is a natural right while suicide is an unnatural termination of life. P. Rathinam v. Union of India is overruled.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [1996) 2 SCC 648]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "1996) 2 SCC 648", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Constitution Bench Landmark)	Supreme Court of India\nCriminal Appeal No. 267 of 1995\nGian Kaur v. State of Punjab\nBench: Hon'ble J.S. Verma, G.N. Ray, N.P. Singh, Faizan Uddin, G.T. Nanavati, JJ.\nDate: 1996-03-21\nCitation: (1996) 2 SCC 648\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nArticle 21 cannot be construed to include within its ambit the 'right to die'. Right to life is a natural right while suicide is an unnatural termination of life. P. Rathinam v. Union of India is overruled.\n\nSUMMARY:\nFive-judge Constitution Bench overruled P. Rathinam (1994) and upheld the constitutional validity of Section 306 (abetment of suicide) and Section 309 IPC, ruling that Article 21 protects life and does not include the right to commit suicide.\n\nSTATUTES: Section 306 IPC, Section 309 IPC, Article 21, Article 14\nDISPOSITION: Constitutional Validity of Sections 306 and 309 IPC Upheld	2	f	SCIN010002671995	Criminal Appeal	1996-03-21	Constitution Bench (5 Judges)	Gian Kaur	State of Punjab	Senior Advocate R.K. Jain	Senior Advocate R.S. Suri	Criminal Law & Constitutional Validity	Article 21 protects life up to its natural end. The court held that there is no fundamental 'right to die' by committing suicide, and assisting someone in suicide is a punishable criminal offence.	0	(1996) 2 SCC 648	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Gian Kaur v. State of Punjab", "case_id": "22222222-0014-4000-8000-000000000014", "case_no": "Criminal Appeal No. 267 of 1995", "parties": "Gian Kaur v. State of Punjab", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Gian Kaur", "respondent": "State of Punjab", "legal_domain": "Criminal Law & Constitutional Validity", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Criminal Appeal Against Conviction", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "IPC", "section": "306", "canonical": "Section 306 IPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "IPC", "section": "309", "canonical": "Section 309 IPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "21", "canonical": "Section 21 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "14", "canonical": "Section 14 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["constitutional_fundamental"], "scenario_keywords": ["article 14", "article 21"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Dismissed", "disposition": "Dismissed"}}
22222222-0015-4000-8000-000000000015	Writ Petition (Crl.) No. 445 of 1986	P. Rathinam v. Union of India & Another	Supreme Court of India	1994	Hon'ble B.L. Hansaria & R.M. Sahai, JJ.	P. Rathinam v. Union of India & Another	["Indian Penal Code", "Indian Penal Code, 1860"]	["Section 309 Indian Penal Code"]	["Section 309 IPC", "decriminalizing suicide", "overruled by Gian Kaur", "cruel penal sanction"]	Division bench held Section 309 IPC (attempted suicide) to be cruel, irrational, and unconstitutional under Article 21. Explicitly overruled by 5-Judge Constitution Bench in Gian Kaur (1996).	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_1994_WP_Crl_No_445_of_1986_22220015.pdf	bc82957d9fb7e5b115df9c9c5bb4aadd120e6215440164b7e63d2821f12b6268	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:20:37.432742+05:30	2026-10-25 11:20:37.432746+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.355428+05:30	\N	[]	Section 309 of the Indian Penal Code deserves to be effaced from the statute book to humanize our penal laws. [OVERRULED by Constitution Bench in Gian Kaur v. State of Punjab (1996)].	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [1994) 3 SCC 394]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "1994) 3 SCC 394", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Overruled by Supreme Court (Gian Kaur)	Supreme Court of India\nWrit Petition (Crl.) No. 445 of 1986\nP. Rathinam v. Union of India and Another\nBench: Hon'ble B.L. Hansaria & R.M. Sahai, JJ.\nDate: 1994-04-26\nCitation: (1994) 3 SCC 394\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nSection 309 of the Indian Penal Code deserves to be effaced from the statute book to humanize our penal laws. [OVERRULED by Constitution Bench in Gian Kaur v. State of Punjab (1996)].\n\nSUMMARY:\nDivision bench held Section 309 IPC (attempted suicide) to be cruel, irrational, and unconstitutional under Article 21. Explicitly overruled by 5-Judge Constitution Bench in Gian Kaur (1996).\n\nSTATUTES: Section 309 IPC, Article 21, Article 14\nDISPOSITION: Section 309 IPC Struck Down (Overruled in 1996)	2	f	SCIN010004451986	Writ Petition	1994-04-26	Division Bench (2 Judges)	P. Rathinam	Union of India & Another	Senior Advocate F.S. Nariman	Solicitor General Dipankar Gupta	Right to Life & Suicide Law	This 1994 decision briefly decriminalized suicide attempts, but it was overruled two years later by a 5-judge Constitution Bench in Gian Kaur.	0	(1994) 3 SCC 394	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "P. Rathinam v. Union of India and Another", "case_id": "22222222-0015-4000-8000-000000000015", "case_no": "Writ Petition (Crl.) No. 445 of 1986", "parties": "P. Rathinam v. Union of India & Another", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "P. Rathinam", "respondent": "Union of India & Another", "legal_domain": "Right to Life & Suicide Law", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Writ Petition / Criminal Appeal", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "IPC", "section": "309", "canonical": "Section 309 IPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "21", "canonical": "Section 21 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "14", "canonical": "Section 14 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["constitutional_fundamental"], "scenario_keywords": ["article 14", "article 21"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
