 {"test": "Section 29(1) NDPS Act", "mandatory": true}, {"test": "Section 25 NDPS Act", "mandatory": true}, {"test": "Section 483 BNSS", "mandatory": true}]	Allowed	["ble Supreme Court in P.K.Shaji vs. State of Kerala [(2005)13 SCC 283]"]	https://mhc.tn.gov.in/judis	verified	Good Law	CRL OP No. 23668 of 2026\n\nDATED: 09-10-2026\nCORAM\nTHE HON'BLE MR.JUSTICE N.RAMESH\nCRL OP No. 23668 of 2026\nCNR: {HCMA011901022026}\nManoj\n..Petitioner(s)\nVs\n1. The State Rep by\nThe Inspector of Police\nT6, Avadi Police Station,\nChennai. Crime No.186 of 2026.\n2. The Narcotics Intelligence Bureau\nChennai.\n(R2 is impleaded as per the order of this court\ndated 08.09.2026 in Crl.OP.No.23668 of 2026)\n..Respondent(s)\nPRAYER: This criminal original petitoin filed under Section 482 BNSS Act to\nrelease on bail in any condition pending investigation in Crime No.186 of 2026\non the file of the respondent police\nFor Petitioner(s): Mr. S.Shankar\nfor Mr. D.Jagan\nFor Respondent(s): Mr.M.M.I.Khaleel,\nGovt.Advocate (Crl.Side)\nORDER\nThis is a petition for regular bail under Section 483 of the BNSS, in a\ncase under the NDPSAct. The petitioner is the seventh accused in Crime No.186\nof 2026 on the file of the T-6 Avadi Police Station, since transferred to the\nNarcotics Intelligence Bureau, CID, Chennai, and re-numbered as Chennai\n__________\nPage1 of 10\n\nCRL OP No. 23668 of 2026\nNIB-CID Crime No.35 of 2026. The case is registered for offences under\nSections 8(c) read with 20(b)(ii)(C), 29(1) and 25 of the Act.\n2. The prosecution case is this. On 15.05.2026 at about 20.45 hours, on\nprior information, the Sub-Inspector of Police, T-6 Avadi Police Station,\nintercepted A1 Dhayalan, A2 Udhayaprasad and A4 Gopalakrishnan, who came\non two motorcycles near Vetri Nagar, Avadi. A bag carried by A1 held eleven\nbundles of ganja weighing about 62 kg. On what A1 then told the police, a\nfurther seven packets weighing about 38 kg were seized at about 23.55 hours\nfrom the first floor of the house of A1's sister, where A3 Gopi was found. The\ntotal seizure is 100 kg. The first information report was registered at 01.30\nhours on 16.05.2026. The petitioner is not named in it. The counter affidavit\nstates that eighteen samples were examined by the Forensic Sciences\nDepartment and that cannabinoids were detected in all of them.\n3. The case against the petitioner is set out in the counter affidavit and in\nthe additional counter affidavit filed by the Inspector of Police, NIB-CID. A1\nwas in police custody from 15.06.2026 to 17.06.2026. On 16.06.2026 he is said\nto have stated that he went to the Odisha border with A2 and A3 and procured\nabout 108 kg of ganja from one Vijay; that about 100 kg "formed the main\ntransaction"; and that the remaining quantity was distributed in Chennai among\nthe petitioner, Abdul Fasith, Madhavan and Dhanush. The petitioner was\narrested thereafter, and 350 grams of ganja is said to have been recovered from\n__________\nPage2 of 10\n\nCRL OP No. 23668 of 2026\nhim. The additional counter adds three matters. First, that bank records show\ntransfers to the petitioner from the account of A1's mother, Bhavani, of\nRs.24,000/- on 19.04.2026 and Rs.2,000/- on 11.05.2026. Second, that the call\ndetail records and tower locations of the numbers used by A1 and the petitioner\nshow "continuous communication, active connectivity, and real-time\ncoordination" between them up to four days before the date of the crime. Third,\nthat the petitioner is an accused in Crime No.404 of 2025 of the T-6 Avadi\nPolice Station under Sections 8(c) read with 20(b)(ii)(A) and 29(1) of the Act.\n4. The seizure in this crime is of ganja of 100 kg seized from A1 to A4 is\ncommercial quantity. The 350 grams said to have been recovered from the\npetitioner is below the small quantity.\n5. The petitioner was arrested on16.06.2026. The investigation is\npending. The petitioner’s earlier petition before this Court, Crl.O.P. No.20061\nof 2026, was dismissed on 27.07.2026, on the footing that it was premature to\nconsider bail while the investigation was pending. While the present petition\nwas pending, it came to light on 08.09.2026 that the investigation had been\ntransferred to the NIB-CID without intimation to the petitioner. The NIB-CID\nwas impleaded and has filed both counter affidavits.\n6. The learned counsel for the petitioner submitted that the petitioner was\nnot at the place of occurrence, that nothing was recovered from him, and that he\nwas arrested a month after the occurrence only on the confession of A1. He is a\n__________\nPage3 of 10\n\nCRL OP No. 23668 of 2026\nfinal year law student of Vels Law College, Chennai, and is said to have no\nantecedents. The learned counsel submitted that the petitioner has been\nimplicated by the T-6 Avadi Police out of revenge. About a year ago, the\npetitioner's mother, K. Rani, was threatened over the phone that her son would\nbe booked in a false case, and paid Rs.60,000/- to two policemen of that station.\nThe family complained to the Deputy Commissioner of Police with a recording\nof the conversation, and the two policemen were transferred after an enquiry.\nThe petition itself states that the police implicated him on the allegation that\n"few months back there was a bank transaction" between him and A1. The\npetitioner undertakes to abide by any condition.\n7. The learned counsel for the Government of Tamil Nadu (Criminal\nSide), on the two counter affidavits, opposed the petition. The counter says, at\nparagraph 7 of the additional counter, that the arrest "was not based solely on\nA1's statement; A1's statement provided an investigative lead, which was\nfollowed by further investigation, A7's identification, arrest, and recovery". The\nallegations about the petitioner's mother and the payment are said to be\n"unrelated to the present case". The investigation into the source, the network\nand the financial trail is said to be continuing.\n8. I have considered the rival submissions and perused the material placed\nbefore me.\n__________\nPage4 of 10\n\nCRL OP No. 23668 of 2026\n9. The earlier petition before this Court was not decided on the merits. It\nwas dismissed as premature. The State has now placed its material against the\npetitioner in two counter affidavits, and I take up the petition on that material.\n10. The first question is whether Section 37 of the Act stands in the\npetitioner's way at all. What was recovered from him is 350 grams, a small\nquantity. The commercial quantity was recovered from A1 to A4, a month\nbefore the petitioner was arrested. That quantity can be put to his account only\nif there is material of concert, and the material must be more than inference or\nconjecture, as held in Nadeem Ahamed v. State of West Bengal, 2025 INSC\n993. In deciding what that material is, the statement of A1 recorded in police\ncustody has to be left out. A confession to a police officer cannot be used\nagainst an accused under this Act, as held in Tofan Singh v. State of Tamil\nNadu, (2021) 4 SCC 1, and an arrest founded only on such a statement cannot\nsustain a refusal of bail, as held in State by (NCB) Bengaluru v. Pallulabid\nAhmad Arimutta, 2022 INSC 26.\n11. If A1's statement is struck out, three things remain. The first is the\nbank trail: two transfers from the account of A1's mother to the petitioner, the\nlater of them four days before the seizure. The petitioner's own affidavit accepts\nthat there was a bank transaction between him and A1 as they are neighbours.\nThe second is the call detail records and tower locations, which the counter says\nshow continuous contact between A1 and the petitioner up to four days before\n__________\nPage5 of 10\n\nCRL OP No. 23668 of 2026\nthe crime. It is stated on behalf of the petitioner that both are neighbours and\nobviously tower location will be close. The third is the recovery of 350 grams\nfrom the petitioner. None of these depends on what an accused said to the\npolice.\n12. The material has its weaknesses. The bank statements and the call\ndetail records are not before me; only the counter's description of them is. The\ncounter does not say where or when the 350 grams was recovered, and the\nmahazar is not produced. The recovery was not put forward when the earlier\npetitions were heard: the Special Court recorded the submission that nothing\nwas recovered from the petitioner, and the respondent did not say otherwise in\nCrl.O.P. No.20061 of 2026. The money moved from A1's family to the\npetitioner, and not from him to A1, and the counter does not explain what it was\npaid for. Investigation is pending.\n13. The petitioner is an accused in Crime No.404 of 2025 of the same\npolice station, under the same Act, for an offence of the smaller quantity of\nganja. The counter does not give the stage of that case. The petitioner's\ncomplaint that two policemen of that station extracted money from his mother is\ndenied in the counter only as being unrelated, and the counter does not say what\ncame of the enquiry.\n14. In view above stated circumstances and that the petitioner is a young\nlaw student in his final year is a real circumstance, and I have kept it in view\n__________\nPage6 of 10\n\nCRL OP No. 23668 of 2026\nand I hope that he will mend his ways. The discussion and observations made in\nthis petition are only for the purpose of deciding the bail petition and shall in\nany way influence the trial.\n15.In the result, the Criminal Original Petition is allowed and the\npetitioner is ordered to be released on bail on his executing a bond for a sum of\nRs.25,000/- (Rupees Twenty Five Thousand only) with two sureties, each for\na like sum to the satisfaction of the Judicial Magistrate No.II, Poonamallee, and\non further conditions that:\n[a] the sureties shall affix his photographs and Left Thumb\nImpression in the Application for Surety ship [Judicial Form\nNo.46 annexed to 'The Criminal Rules of Practice, 2019']. The\nlearned Magistrate shall obtain a copy of any one of the identity\nproofs to ensure his identity;\n(b) the petitioner shall report before the respondent police\ndaily at 10:30 A.M. until further orders;\n[c] the petitioner shall not abscond either during\ninvestigation or trial;\n[d] the petitioner shall not tamper with the evidence or\nwitness either during investigation or trial;\n[e] On breach of any of the aforementioned conditions, the\nlearned Magistrate/Trial Court is entitled to pass appropriate\norders against the petitioner in accordance with law as if the\naforementioned conditions have been imposed and the\npetitioner released on bail by the learned Magistrate/Trial\n__________\nPage7 of 10\n\nCRL OP No. 23668 of 2026\nCourt himself as laid down by the Hon'ble Supreme Court in\nP.K.Shaji vs. State of Kerala [(2005)13 SCC 283];\n[f] If the accused thereafter absconds, a fresh FIR can be\nregistered under Section 269 of B.N.S.\n09-10-2026\nIndex: Yes/No\nSpeaking/Non-speaking order\nNeutral Citation: Yes/No\nsms\nNote:\n1. Registry is directed to forthwith upload\nthis order in the Official Website of this\nCourt.\n2. All concerned to act on this order being\nuploaded in Official Website of this Court\nwithout insisting on certified hard copies. To\nbe noted, this order when uploaded in the\nofficial website of this Court will be\nwatermarked and will also have a QR code.\nTo\n1.Judicial Magistrate No.II, Poonamalle.\n2.The Superintendent of Police,\nCentral Puzhal Prison, Chennai.\n3.The Inspector of Police,\nT-6, Avadi Police Station,\nChennai.\n4.The narcotics Intelligence Bureau,\nChennai.\n5.The Public Prosecutor\nHigh Court, Madras.\n__________\nPage8 of 10\n\nCRL OP No. 23668 of 2026\nN.RAMESH, J.\nsms\nCRL OP No. 23668 of 2026\n09-10-2026\n__________\nPage9 of 10\n\nCRL OP No. 23668 of 2026\n__________\nPage10 of 10	10	t	HCMA011901022026	Criminal Original Petition (Bail)	2026-10-09	Single Bench	Manoj	State Rep. by Inspector of Police, Avadi & Anr.	Mr. S.Shankar	Mr.M.M.I.Khaleel, Govt.Advocate (Crl.Side)	Civil Law	In this criminal original petition under Civil Law, the Court ruled on the dispute between 14 Crl-Op-23668-2026 and the Respondent.	0	2026 LexGuard (Crim) AFCBE8	{"court": "Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)", "title": "Manoj v. State Rep. by Inspector of Police, Avadi & Anr.", "case_id": "afcbe898-eab7-42bf-a717-d0cec696182f", "case_no": "CRL OP No. 23668 of 2026", "parties": "Manoj v. State Rep. by Inspector of Police, Avadi & Anr.", "category": "NDPS", "petitioner": "Manoj", "respondent": "State Rep. by Inspector of Police, Avadi & Anr.", "legal_domain": "Civil Law", "category_facts": {"category": "NDPS", "custody_period": null, "contraband_type": "Ganja", "seized_quantity": "62 kg", "statutory_issue": "Bail / Pre-trial Release under NDPS Act", "quantity_category": "Commercial Quantity", "section_37_embargo": true, "search_compliance_sec_50": "Search procedure evaluated"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "BNSS", "section": "483", "canonical": "Section 483 BNSS", "equivalents": ["Section 439 CrPC"]}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["pre_trial_custody", "narcotics_contraband"], "scenario_keywords": ["regular bail", "ndps"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
22222222-0017-4000-8000-000000000017	Criminal Appeal No. 221 of 1960	Commander K.M. Nanavati v. State of Maharashtra	Supreme Court of India	1962	Hon'ble K. Subba Rao, Raghubar Dayal, J.R. Mudholkar, JJ.	Commander K.M. Nanavati v. State of Maharashtra	["Indian Penal Code, 1860", "Part I IPC"]	["Section 304 Part I IPC"]	["grave and sudden provocation", "cooling-off period", "jury verdict perversity", "murder vs culpable homicide"]	Foundational precedent on the test of 'grave and sudden provocation' under Exception 1 to Section 300 IPC. Sufficient cooling-off period negates the plea of sudden provocation.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_1962_Crl_App_No_221_of_1960_22220017.pdf	2fd42301a0947ea6a23742e6a4f0b719aedf23e63d95267cebcbc6030b8cbbfb	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:20:37.474607+05:30	2026-10-25 11:20:37.47461+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.697532+05:30	\N	[]	The test of grave and sudden provocation is whether a reasonable person placed in the situation of the accused would be so provoked as to lose his self-control. The fatal blow must be traced clearly to the influence of passion arising from that provocation, with no intervening cooling-off period.	[]	Disposed	[]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding 3-Judge Precedent)	Supreme Court of India\nCriminal Appeal No. 221 of 1960\nK.M. Nanavati v. State of Maharashtra\nBench: Hon'ble K. Subba Rao, Raghubar Dayal, J.R. Mudholkar, JJ.\nDate: 1961-11-24\nCitation: 1962 Supp (1) SCR 567\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nThe test of grave and sudden provocation is whether a reasonable person placed in the situation of the accused would be so provoked as to lose his self-control. The fatal blow must be traced clearly to the influence of passion arising from that provocation, with no intervening cooling-off period.\n\nSUMMARY:\nFoundational precedent on the test of 'grave and sudden provocation' under Exception 1 to Section 300 IPC. Sufficient cooling-off period negates the plea of sudden provocation.\n\nSTATUTES: Section 300 Exception 1 IPC, Section 302 IPC, Section 304 Part I IPC\nDISPOSITION: Conviction under Section 302 IPC Confirmed	2	f	SCIN010002211960	Criminal Appeal	1961-11-24	Three-Judge Bench	Commander K.M. Nanavati	State of Maharashtra	Senior Advocate Ram Jethmalani	Advocate General H.M. Seervai	Criminal Law & Grave Provocation	For an accused to claim sudden provocation, the act must happen immediately in the heat of passion. If time passes and the accused has time to calm down and plan, it is murder, not culpable homicide.	0	1962 Supp (1) SCR 567	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "K.M. Nanavati v. State of Maharashtra", "case_id": "22222222-0017-4000-8000-000000000017", "case_no": "Criminal Appeal No. 221 of 1960", "parties": "Commander K.M. Nanavati v. State of Maharashtra", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Commander K.M. Nanavati", "respondent": "State of Maharashtra", "legal_domain": "Criminal Law & Grave Provocation", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Criminal Appeal Against Conviction", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "IPC", "section": "300", "canonical": "Section 300 IPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "IPC", "section": "302", "canonical": "Section 302 IPC", "equivalents": ["Section 103 BNS"]}, {"act": "IPC", "section": "304", "canonical": "Section 304 IPC", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "scenario_keywords": [], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Dismissed", "disposition": "Dismissed"}}
22222222-0018-4000-8000-000000000018	OMP (ENF) (COMM) No. 17 of 2021	Amazon.com NV Investment Holdings LLC v. Future Retail Ltd., Future Coupons Pvt. Ltd. & Others	High Court of Delhi	2021	Hon'ble J.R. Midha, J.	Amazon.com NV Investment Holdings LLC v. Future Retail Ltd., Future Coupons Pvt. Ltd. & Others	["Arbitration Act", "the Act"]	["Section 17(2) Arbitration Act", "Section 17(2) the Act"]	["emergency arbitrator award", "SIAC rules", "interim order enforcement", "arbitration agreement sanctity"]	Landmark ruling upholding the enforceable legal status of an Emergency Arbitrator's interim order under Section 17(2) of the Arbitration Act, enforcing Singapore International Arbitration Centre (SIAC) directions against Future Group.	Disposed	storage/cases/DHC_2021_OMP_ENF_COMM_No_17_of_2021_22220018.pdf	44928d00679ed1f63dab51ca76209148e4d39f87a8ced0ca9e7bf67a99397bb2	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:20:37.492782+05:30	2026-10-25 11:20:37.492785+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.698361+05:30	\N	[]	An Emergency Arbitrator is an 'Arbitral Tribunal' within the meaning of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996, and orders passed by such emergency arbitral tribunal are directly enforceable under Section 17(2) of the Act.	[]	Disposed	[]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding Commercial Precedent)	High Court of Delhi\nOMP (ENF) (COMM) No. 17 of 2021\nAmazon.com NV Investment Holdings LLC v. Future Retail Ltd. and Others\nBench: Hon'ble J.R. Midha, J.\nDate: 2021-03-18\nCitation: 2021:DHC:1020\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nAn Emergency Arbitrator is an 'Arbitral Tribunal' within the meaning of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996, and orders passed by such emergency arbitral tribunal are directly enforceable under Section 17(2) of the Act.\n\nSUMMARY:\nLandmark ruling upholding the enforceable legal status of an Emergency Arbitrator's interim order under Section 17(2) of the Arbitration Act, enforcing Singapore International Arbitration Centre (SIAC) directions against Future Group.\n\nSTATUTES: Section 17(1), Section 17(2), Section 9, Section 36\nDISPOSITION: Emergency Arbitrator Interim Injunction Enforced	2	f	HCMA010000172021	OMP (ENF) (COMM)	2021-03-18	Single Judge	Amazon.com NV Investment Holdings LLC	Future Retail Ltd., Future Coupons Pvt. Ltd. & Others	Senior Advocate Gopal Subramanium, Rajiv Nayar	Senior Advocate Harish Salve, Darius Khambata	Commercial Arbitration & Enforcement	Emergency arbitrator awards issued under recognized institutional rules are fully valid and enforceable in Indian courts like court-ordered injunctions under Section 17.	0	2021:DHC:1020	{"court": "High Court of Delhi", "title": "Amazon.com NV Investment Holdings LLC v. Future Retail Ltd. and Others", "case_id": "22222222-0018-4000-8000-000000000018", "case_no": "OMP (ENF) (COMM) No. 17 of 2021", "parties": "Amazon.com NV Investment Holdings LLC v. Future Retail Ltd., Future Coupons Pvt. Ltd. & Others", "category": "COMMERCIAL_CIVIL", "petitioner": "Amazon.com NV Investment Holdings LLC", "respondent": "Future Retail Ltd., Future Coupons Pvt. Ltd. & Others", "legal_domain": "Commercial Arbitration & Enforcement", "category_facts": {"category": "COMMERCIAL_CIVIL", "dispute_type": "Arbitration & Conciliation Challenge", "statutory_basis": "Commercial Courts Act / Civil Procedure Code", "interim_injunction_granted": false}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["financial_commercial"], "scenario_keywords": ["section 9"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
22222222-0019-4000-8000-000000000019	Bail Application No. 2841 of 2023	Manish Sisodia v. Central Bureau of Investigation	High Court of Delhi	2023	Hon'ble Dinesh Kumar Sharma, J.	Manish Sisodia v. Central Bureau of Investigation	["B IPC", "Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973", "Indian Penal Code, 1860", "PC Act"]	["Section 120 B IPC", "Section 7 PC Act"]	["liquor excise policy", "Section 439 CrPC", "triple test", "witness tampering risk", "custody duration"]	Detailed consideration of regular bail parameters in complex economic offences involving public office holders. While bail was initially refused, principles of prolonged incarceration without trial were crystallized.	Disposed	storage/cases/DHC_2023_Bail_App_No_2841_of_2023_22220019.pdf	818fbd006770454bbb46704f677ab801dc2ff60bf7772e7e4fe205dfc99aed6c	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:20:37.524483+05:30	2026-10-25 11:20:37.524485+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.699136+05:30	\N	[]	Seriousness of economic allegations against high public functionaries must be balanced against individual liberties. However, where trial delay is not attributable to the accused and thousands of documents are yet to be marked, indefinite incarceration cannot serve as punitive custody.	[]	Disposed	[]	\N	unverified	Distinguished on Facts (Supreme Court granted bail in 2024)	High Court of Delhi\nBail Application No. 2841 of 2023\nManish Sisodia v. Central Bureau of Investigation\nBench: Hon'ble Dinesh Kumar Sharma, J.\nDate: 2023-07-03\nCitation: 2023:DHC:3842\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nSeriousness of economic allegations against high public functionaries must be balanced against individual liberties. However, where trial delay is not attributable to the accused and thousands of documents are yet to be marked, indefinite incarceration cannot serve as punitive custody.\n\nSUMMARY:\nDetailed consideration of regular bail parameters in complex economic offences involving public office holders. While bail was initially refused, principles of prolonged incarceration without trial were crystallized.\n\nSTATUTES: Section 439 CrPC, Section 7 PC Act, Section 120B IPC\nDISPOSITION: Bail Denied with Liberty to Move Fresh Application	2	f	HCMA010028412023	Bail Application	2023-07-03	Single Judge	Manish Sisodia	Central Bureau of Investigation	Senior Advocate Abhishek Manu Singhvi, Dayan Krishnan	Additional Solicitor General S.V. Raju	Criminal Law & Prevention of Corruption	Even in high-profile corruption or policy matters, protracted detention without commencement of trial infringes personal liberty under Article 21, establishing grounds for regular bail.	0	2023:DHC:3842	{"court": "High Court of Delhi", "title": "Manish Sisodia v. Central Bureau of Investigation", "case_id": "22222222-0019-4000-8000-000000000019", "case_no": "Bail Application No. 2841 of 2023", "parties": "Manish Sisodia v. Central Bureau of Investigation", "category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "petitioner": "Manish Sisodia", "respondent": "Central Bureau of Investigation", "legal_domain": "Criminal Law & Prevention of Corruption", "category_facts": {"category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "surety_count": null, "custody_period": null, "application_type": "Regular Bail", "bail_bond_amount": null, "trial_court_fine": null, "reason_for_relief": null, "reporting_condition": null, "trial_court_sentence": null, "trial_court_fine_paid": false}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CRPC", "section": "439", "canonical": "Section 439 CRPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "IPC", "section": "120B", "canonical": "Section 120B IPC", "equivalents": ["Section 61 BNS"]}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["pre_trial_custody"], "scenario_keywords": ["regular bail"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Dismissed", "disposition": "Dismissed"}}
22222222-0020-4000-8000-000000000020	HCP No. 1021 of 2023	Megala (w/o V. Senthil Balaji) v. Directorate of Enforcement & Others	Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)	2023	Hon'ble C.V. Karthikeyan, J. (Third Referee Judge)	Megala (w/o V. Senthil Balaji) v. Directorate of Enforcement & Others	["Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973", "Constitution of India", "the Code"]	["Section 167 the Code"]	["ED custody", "Section 167 CrPC", "Habeas Corpus after judicial remand", "hospitalization period exclusion"]	Deciding referee ruling holding that ED is entitled to seek custodial interrogation of an arrested person under Section 167 CrPC, and a Habeas Corpus petition is not maintainable once a competent judicial remand order is passed.	Disposed	storage/cases/MHC_2023_HCP_No_1021_of_2023_22220020.pdf	1a05ad70f7794b3383ae6bae89f92982d64391e160d26078cce72cc122d459be	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:20:37.544366+05:30	2026-10-25 11:20:37.544369+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.699726+05:30	\N	[]	An order of remand passed by a competent court of jurisdiction cannot be assailed as illegal detention through a writ of habeas corpus. The Enforcement Directorate has the power to take an accused into custody under Section 167 of the Code of Criminal Procedure.	[]	Disposed	[]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Affirmed by Supreme Court in 2023)	Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)\nHCP No. 1021 of 2023\nMegala v. State represented by Deputy Director, Directorate of Enforcement and Others\nBench: Hon'ble C.V. Karthikeyan, J. (Third Referee Judge)\nDate: 2023-07-14\nCitation: 2023:MHC:3125\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nAn order of remand passed by a competent court of jurisdiction cannot be assailed as illegal detention through a writ of habeas corpus. The Enforcement Directorate has the power to take an accused into custody under Section 167 of the Code of Criminal Procedure.\n\nSUMMARY:\nDeciding referee ruling holding that ED is entitled to seek custodial interrogation of an arrested person under Section 167 CrPC, and a Habeas Corpus petition is not maintainable once a competent judicial remand order is passed.\n\nSTATUTES: Section 19 PMLA, Section 167 CrPC, Article 226\nDISPOSITION: Habeas Corpus Petition Held Not Maintainable	2	f	TNCH010010212023	HCP	2023-07-14	Full Bench / Referee Single Bench	Megala (w/o V. Senthil Balaji)	Directorate of Enforcement & Others	Senior Advocate N.R. Elango	Solicitor General Tushar Mehta	Habeas Corpus & PMLA Custodial Remand	The Enforcement Directorate can take custodial remand under Section 167 CrPC. A Habeas Corpus petition cannot challenge an arrest once a magistrate has formally ordered remand to judicial custody.	0	2023:MHC:3125	{"court": "Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)", "title": "Megala v. State represented by Deputy Director, Directorate of Enforcement and Others", "case_id": "22222222-0020-4000-8000-000000000020", "case_no": "HCP No. 1021 of 2023", "parties": "Megala (w/o V. Senthil Balaji) v. Directorate of Enforcement & Others", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Megala (w/o V. Senthil Balaji)", "respondent": "Directorate of Enforcement & Others", "legal_domain": "Habeas Corpus & PMLA Custodial Remand", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Habeas Corpus (Preventive Detention Challenge)", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CRPC", "section": "167", "canonical": "Section 167 CRPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "226", "canonical": "Section 226 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["pre_trial_custody", "constitutional_fundamental"], "scenario_keywords": ["custodial interrogation", "remand", "writ of habeas corpus", "article 226"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Dismissed", "disposition": "Dismissed"}}
