======================\nCORAM:HONOURABLE MR. JUSTICE HASMUKH D. SUTHAR\nDate : 22/10/2026\nORDER\n[1.0] RULE. Learned APP waives service of rule for the respondent-State.\n[2.0] The present application is filed under Section 483 of the Bharatiya\nNagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 (for short “BNSS”) for regular bail in connection\nwith FIR being C.R. No.11993004260793 of 2026 registered with Bhachau\nPolice Station, Kachchh East-Gandhidham for the offences punishable under\nSections 109(1), 115(2), 117(2), 351(3), 3(5), 189(2), 189(4), 190, 191(1), 191(2),\n191(3), 324, 125(a) and 125(b) of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 (for short\n“BNS”) and section 135 of the Gujarat Police Act.\n[3.0] Learned advocate appearing on behalf of the applicants submits that\napplicants are innocent and have been falsely implicated in the offence and\nnow nothing is required to be recovered or discovered. He therefore submits\nthat, considering the nature of the offence, the applicants may be enlarged on\nregular bail by imposing suitable conditions.\n[4.0] Learned APP appearing on behalf of the respondent-State has opposed\nthe present application and requested to dismiss the present application for\nregular bail looking to the nature and gravity of the offence.\nPage 1 of 4\n\nR/CR.MA/23092/2026 ORDER DATED: 22/10/2026\n(GJHC240746942026)\n[4.1] Learned advocate for the original complainant has tendered affidavit of\nthe original complainant objecting to the present application, which is taken on\nrecord. He has also vehemently opposed the present application adopting the\nsubmissions made by the learned APP and requested to dismiss the present\napplication.\n[5.0] While granting bail, the Court has to consider the involvement of the\naccused in the alleged offence, the jurisdiction to grant bail has to be exercised\non the basis of the well settled principles having regard to the facts and\ncircumstances of each case and the following factors are to be taken into\nconsideration while considering an application for bail: (i) the nature of\naccusation and the severity of the punishment and the nature of the materials\nrelied upon by the prosecution; (ii) reasonable apprehension of tampering with\nthe witnesses and threat to the complainant or the witnesses; (iii) reasonable\npossibility of securing the presence of the accused at the time of trial or the\nlikelihood of his abscondence; (iv) character behaviour and standing of the\naccused and the circumstances which are peculiar to the accused; (v) larger\ninterest of the public or the State and similar other considerations are required\nto be considered.\n[6.0] I have heard the learned advocates appearing on behalf of the respective\nparties and perused the investigation papers. Following aspects have been\nconsidered:\n(1) In present case, cross-complaints have been filed and it is alleged that\nthe present applicants pelted stone due to which the injury was caused\nto the victim however, now the injured victim is out of danger and\ndischarged from the hospital and substantial part of investigation is\nover;\n(2) Applicants are behind the bars since 24.08.2026;\n(3) There is nothing to be recovered or discovered from the applicants;\n(4) The applicant No.1 is having one past antecedent however, is governed\nby presumption of innocence unless proven guilty and applicant Nos.2\nPage 2 of 4\n\nR/CR.MA/23092/2026 ORDER DATED: 22/10/2026\n(GJHC240746942026)\nand 3 	4	t	GJHC010230922026	Judicial Precedent	2026-10-22	Single Bench	ORS.	State of Gujarat	\N	\N	Constitutional Law	In this judgment under Commercial & Arbitration, the Court ruled on the dispute between Dalsukh @ Dalabhai Palabhai Barot & Ors. and State of Gujarat.	0	2026 LexGuard (Judg) 23F1BF	{"court": "High Court of Gujarat at Ahmedabad", "title": "Dalsukh @ Dalabhai Palabhai Barot & Ors. v. State of Gujarat", "case_id": "23f1bf9e-4d63-402b-ad79-c78e24aef3ce", "case_no": "R/Criminal Misc. Application No. 23092 of 2026", "parties": "Dalsukh 