rt": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Indra Sawhney and Others v. Union of India and Others", "status": "Good Law (9-Judge Constitution Bench Landmark)", "case_id": "22222222-0003-4000-8000-000000000003", "citation": "1992 Supp (3) SCC 217", "treatment": "Referred / Applied"}]	https://judgments.ecourts.gov.in	verified	Good Law	Civil Writ Jurisdiction Case No.20547 of 2018\n======================================================\nVishwambhar Singh Son of Late Ram Bahadur Singh, Resident of VillageSanduar, Post Office Dayalpur, Police Station- Janta Bazar, District Saran at\nChapra.\n... ... Petitioner/s\nVersus\n1. The State of Bihar through the Chief Secretary, Government of Bihar, Patna.\n2. The Principal Secretary, Human Resources Development Department,\nGovernment of Bihar, New Secretariat Building, Bailey Road, Patna-\n800001.\n3. The Principal Secretary, Rural Development Department, Government of\nBihar, Old Secretariat Building, Patna- 800015.\n4. Bihar Public Service Commission, through its Secretary, Bailey Road, Patna.\n5. The Chairman, Bihar Public Service Commission, Bailey Road, Patna.\n6. The Joint Secretary cum Examination Controller, Bihar Public Service\nCommission, Bailey Road, Patna.\n7. Archana Kumari, Daughter of and wife of name not known to the petitioner\nRural Development Officer, through the Principal Secretary, Rural\nDevelopment Department, Government of Bihar, Old Secretariat Building,\nPatna- 800015.\n8. Principal Secretary, General Administration Department, Bihar Government,\nPatna, Bihar.\n... ... Respondent/s\n======================================================\nAppearance :\nFor the Petitioner/s : Mr. Vishwambhar Singh (In-Person)\nFor the Respondent/s : Mr. G.P. 20\nFor the BPSC : Mr. Lalit Kishore, Sr. Advocate\nMr. Kanishka Shankar, Advocate\nFor the Respondent no.7: Mr. Bishwa Bibhuti Kumar Singh, Advocate\n======================================================\nCORAM: HONOURABLE MR. JUSTICE RITESH KUMAR\nCAV JUDGMENT\nDate : 23-06-2026\nHeard the parties.\n2. The present writ petition has been filed for the\nfollowing reliefs:-\n“(i) For issuance of a writ in the nature of\nwrit of certiorari for quashing the part of the\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n2/38\nresult published under the signature of Joint\nSecretary -cum-Examination Controller,\nBihar Public Service Commission, Patna by\nwhich the private respondent Archana\nKumari, whose name stands at Serial No. 22\nunder Item No. 9, Rural Development\nOfficer have been declared selected\ncandidates under the disabled O.H. being\nRoll No. 342867, merit list serial no. 1178\nagainst the advertisement published by\nBihar Public Service Commission of 56th,\n57th, 58th and 59th of combined competitive\nexamination as contained in Annexure-2.\n(ii) Also for declaring decision of selection\nof Archana Kumari who has got lesser mark\nthan the petitioner under disabled quota to\nItem no. 9 Rural Development Officer is\nbased on hostile discrimination and\nviolation of Articles 14, 16 and 21 of the\nConstitution of India.\n(iii) Also for commanding the respondents to\npublish the result of the petitioner as a\nsuccessful candidate under most backward\nclass and maintain the seniority of the\npetitioner on the basis of the marks obtained\nby the petitioner being total marks 820\nwhereas the private respondent has got 819\nmarks only\n(iv) Also for declaring the decision of\nselection of private respondent having lesser\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n3/38\nmarks than the petitioner is illegal and bad\nin law.\n(v) Also for giving the petitioner all\nconsequential benefits of service and\nmonitory benefit of service, seniority from\nthe date the juniors to the petitioner have\nbeen appointed against Advertisement No.\n58th Combined Competitive Examination\npublished in daily newspaper as contained in\nAnnexure-.\n(vi) Also for commanding the Bihar Public\nService Commission to recommend 4 more\nvacancies in general category. Only result of\n18 persons has been placed in place of 22 on\nthe ground total vacancies is 736.\n(vii) Also for necessary relief/reliefs as well\nas necessary direction be given for the ends\nof justice.\nBrief Facts Of Case\n3. The brief facts giving rise to the present writ\npetition are that an advertisement was issued for 56th to 59th\nCombined Preliminary Competitive Examination for 22\nservices, having total 746 vacancies, wherein it was indicated\nthat quota for disabled candidate will also be given as per the\nGovernment Resolution contained in memo no. 62 dated\n05.01.2007 and relaxation of age of 10 years will be given in the\ndisabled category. The petitioner having the requisite\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n4/38\nqualification submitted his application in the prescribed\nperforma within the cut-off date. Admit-card was issued to the\npetitioner for appearing in the examination, which was held on\n15.03.2015 and the petitioner appeared at Motihari Centre.\nResults were published, wherein the petitioner was declared\nsuccessful and subsequently, he appeared in the Mains\nExamination, which was conducted from 8th July, 2016 to 25th\nJuly 2016 and was also declared successful in the Mains\nExamination. Thereafter, he was called to appear for interview\non 08.05.2018, in which he also appeared on the said date. The\nfinal result was published by the Bihar Public Service\nCommission (hereinafter referred to as BPSC), Patna, on\n18.08.2018, wherein altogether 736 candidates were declared\nsuccessful, to be appointed in 21 services, however, the name of\nthe petitioner does not find place in the list of successful\ncandidates. The petitioner came to know that he has secured 820\nmarks. The petitioner belongs to disabled quota of Ortho. The\npetitioner obtained/downloaded the marks of some other\ncandidates namely Vikram Kumar, merit list serial no. 1042 and\nUmesh Kumar Das, merit list serial no. 1031. From perusal of\nthe marks obtained by both the above-mentioned candidates, the\npetitioner came to know that Vikram Kumar had obtained 819\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n5/38\nmarks in the category of Scheduled Castes candidates and\nUmesh Kumar Das had secured 820 marks in the same category.\nFurther, one another candidate namely Archana Kumari,\nbackward-class lady (BCL) category was placed at serial no.\n1178 in the merit list. It is the case of the petitioner that since\nVikram Kumar and Umesh Kumar Das who have secured 819\nand 820 marks respectively and the petitioner who has also\nsecured 820 marks, must be above in the merit list, than\nArchana Kumari, (respondent no.07) herein, who has been\nfinally selected to the post of Rural Development Officer. It is\nthe case of the petitioner that since the petitioner could not\nobtain the marks of Archana Kumari, but from the rank i.e.\n1178, it appears that the marks of Archana Kumari, who has\nbeen selected in the disabled category, must be lesser than the\npetitioner, only for the reason that Vikram Kumar who was\nplaced at serial no. 1042 in the merit list has been placed in\ngeneral rank. It is further case of the petitioner that as per the\nadvertisement 3% quota was to be given to the disabled\ncategory candidates in horizontal reservation, as per the\nresolution of the State Government dated 05.01.2007. The\ncircular dated 05.01.2007, prescribed for 3% quota for disabled\ncandidate, in tune with the Disabilities Security Act, 1995 and\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n6/38\nwere to be adjusted against the respective quota, meaning\nthereby that at the time of selection, there will be no\nreservation, rather after selection they will be adjusted against\ntheir respective quota and once the petitioner has secured 820\nmarks and the person who had obtained 820 marks has been\nplaced at merit list serial no. 1031 and Archana Kumari\n(respondent no. 07) who has been placed at merit list 1178, must\nhave got lesser marks than the petitioner, even then she has been\nfinally declared to be successful under the disabled O.H.\ncategory candidate, which is in violation of provisions\ncontained in Articles 14, 16 and 21 of the Constitution of India.\nIt is the case of the petitioner that since the petitioner has got\nhigher marks than Archana Kumari and both belongs to the\ndisabled quota, even then, Archana Kumari has been selected,\ntherefore, the action of the respondent authorities in selecting\nArchana Kumari in place of the petitioner is not permissible.\nFurther, as per the advertisement, total 736 vacancies were\navailable, therefore, the total seats for disabled category\ncandidates comes to 22, however, in the final result which has\nbeen published by the BPSC, only 18 candidates in the disabled\ncategory have been selected, which suggest that 3% horizontal\nreservation has not been given to the disabled category\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n7/38\ncandidates and 4 vacancies are still left for the said category, to\nbe filled up.\n4. A supplementary affidavit has been filed on behalf\nof the petitioner wherein it has been stated that he got\ninformation under Right to Information Act, 2005 that Archana\nKumari has secured 811 marks under disabled quota (Ortho).\nSince the petitioner has secured 820 marks and Archana Kumari\nhas secured 811 marks, she should have been placed below the\npetitioner in the merit list, but the name of the petitioner does\nnot find figure in the merit list and respondent no. 07, despite\nsecuring 811 marks has been declared to be successful.\n5. A counter affidavit has been filed on behalf of the\nBPSC, wherein it has been stated that 736 vacancies were\nadvertised and thereafter, the final merit list was prepared on the\nbasis of the marks of written examination, as well as marks\nobtained in interview and the selection has been done as per the\nmerit list. The recommendation of successful candidates were\nsent to the concerned departments in the month of October,\n2018 and the appointment process is already complete. The\npetitioner having reservation category general (01) and disabled\n(O.H.) secured 820 marks, merit serial no. 1032, could not be\nfinally declared successful, since the vacancy of the Rural\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n8/38\nDevelopment Officer, against which the private respondent no.\n07 has been selected, was earmarked for the female disabled\ncandidate of B.C.L. (06) category. This fact would be evident\nfrom the requisition letter bearing letter no. 189040 dated\n20.06.2014 of the Rural Development Department. The\nauthorities of the BPSC have completed the process of\nselection/recommendation on the basis of the resolution of the\nState Government dated 05.01.2007. Further, 18 vacancies were\ncalculated for disabled category candidates, out of 736 reported\nvacancies, since the vacancies of Police Service (Bihar Police\nService, Dy. S.P. Vigilance and Deputy Commandant) were not\nincluded in calculation of posts, in view of the conditions\nprescribed in the recruitment rule, which envisaged that “ A\ncandidate must be fit physically and mentally and free from any\nphysical defect likely to interfere with the efficient performance\nof duties of a member of police service”. Six vacancies were\nadmissible for each disabled category candidates i.e. visually\nimpaired, deaf and dumb and orthopedically handicapped\nrespectively, out of the 18 vacancies for disabled category\ncandidates. The petitioner belongs to O.H. disabled category\nhaving 820 marks, merit position 1032, but he was not finally\nselected, since 02 vacancies out of 22 vacancies of the Rural\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n9/38\nDevelopment Officer was earmarked for BCL (06) and out of\nthese 02 vacancies, 01 last remaining vacancy was earmarked\nfor female disabled candidate of BCL category. Since the\nvacancies earmarked for the disabled candidates were not\navailable in the category, therefore, the petitioner, although\nplaced at merit position 1032, along with one another candidate\nnamely Kumar Nirbhay (merit position 997) has not been\nrecommended for selection. Further two persons namely Vikram\nKumar and Umesh Kumar Das belongs to SC category and they\nhave been selected against the vacancies of SC category\ncandidates for the respective services/posts. The petitioner who\nis a candidate of general category, disabled cannot claim\nselection against the vacancies of the reserved category,\nalthough he has secured 820 marks, which is lesser than the cutoff marks of general category i.e. 848 marks. It has further been\nmentioned that the petitioner has taken a calculated risk by\nappearing in the examination, having not been selected, cannot\nchallenge the same in view of the judicial pronouncement of the\nHon’ble Supreme Court of India as well as this Hon’ble Court.\n6. A rejoinder to the counter affidavit filed on behalf\nof the BPSC, has been filed by the petitioner, wherein it has\nbeen stated that out of the 746 posts, which were advertised, 341\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n10/38\nposts was for general category candidates, 117 for SC category\ncandidates, 12 for ST category candidates, 128 for EBC\ncategory candidates, 85 for BC category candidates and 23 for\nBCL category candidates. On the date of advertisement, 3%\nreservation was existing for handicapped persons, as per the\nEqual Opportunity Right Protection and Completion Act, 1945,\ntherefore, the handicapped persons were entitled to be given\ntheir reserved quota against their category i.e. General, SC, ST,\nEBC, BC, and BCL. The 3% reservation has to be divided for\nall the three categores i.e. 1% for O.H. category, 1% for blind\ncandidates and 1% for deaf and dumb. The vacancy advertised\nof any department cannot ignore 3% reservation for\nhandicapped candidate and as such, the petitioner who is\northopedically handicapped, is entitled to be appointed against\n22 vacancies, wherein 8 posts should have been meant for\northopedically handicapped, 7 posts for deaf & dumb and 7\nposts for blind candidates. The petitioner is not challenging the\nprocedure of selection, relating to advertisement, rather he is\nclaiming his appointment against the disabled quota, as per the\nreservation policy, existing on the date of advertisement. He\nfurther submits that only 6 candidates have been selected and\ngiven the benefit of O.H. category, instead of 7 candidates and\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n11/38\nas per the reservation policy dated 05.01.2007, 3% reservation\nwas required to be given horizontally for the disabled category\ncandidates, which has not been followed by the department.\n7. A Supplementary affidavit has been filed on behalf\nof the petitioner wherein it has been stated that he came to know\nthat although the petitioner has secured 820 marks, but the\nrespondent no. 7 who has got 9 marks less than the petitioner,\nhas been finally selected, only on the ground that the vacancy of\nRural Development Officer, against which the respondent no. 07\nhas been selected, was earmarked for the female disabled\ncandidate of BCL category, although as per the advertisement,\nno such vacancy was earmarked for the female disabled\ncandidate. It has further been stated that as per the resolution of\nthe State Government dated 01.02.2007, which states that\nselection of the candidates of person with disability will be\nbased on general category, which clearly states that there should\nbe no reservation within reservation, for the persons with\ndisability category. It has further been stated that as per the\njudgment of the Hon’ble Supreme Court of India reported in\n(2017) 3 SCC 504 (Union of India and Ors. Vs. M.\nSelvakumar and another), wherein it has been observed that\nphysically handicapped category is a category in itself and the\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n12/38\npersons who are physically handicapped have to be treated alike\nin extending the relaxation and concession, regardless of the fact\nthat whether they belong to a general category or OBC category.\nHe further submits that similarly as per the decision of the\nHon’ble Supreme Court of India reported in (2007) 8 SCC 621\n(Mahesh Gupta and Ors. Vs. Yashwant Kumar Ahirwar\nand Ors.) it has been held that a disabled is a disabled and\ntherefore, the question of making further reservation on the\nbasis of caste, creed, and religion, would ordinarily not arise. It\nhas further been observed that the disabled are themselves a\nspecial class and to that extent their further classification may\nnot be justified. As per the resolution of the State Government,\nit is clear that, if any service deemed to be not appropriate for\nP.W.D. candidates, then the same should be compensated from\nanother service, equivalent to that service (in which reservation\nis not permitted to PWD candidates) in which reservation is\npermitted. Therefore, the respondents were duty-bound to select\n22 persons, as per the provisions contained in Persons with\nDisabilities Act.\n8. A counter affidavit has been filed on behalf of the\nprivate respondent no. 07, wherein it has been stated that the\nrespondent no. 07 being eligible, submitted her application,\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n13/38\npursuant to the advertisement issued by the BPSC for 56th to\n59th common Combined Competitive Examination and after\ngoing through the selection process, was selected as a female\ndisabled candidate of BCL (06) category, pursuant to the\nrequisition for vacancy to the post of Rural Development\nOfficer, Rural Development Department, Government of Bihar,\nPatna. The appointment of the respondent no. 07 has been made\nas a BCL category candidate, which was earmarked in view of\nletter dated 20.06.2014 of the Rural Development Department,\ntherefore, the appointment of the respondent no. 07 has been\nmade as per the vacancy, requisition for advertisement as well as\nthe eligibility of the candidate as a BCL category candidate,\ntherefore, there is no infirmity in selection of the respondent no.\n07. After final selection and recommendation, she has been\nappointed as a trainee Rural Development Officer in Bhojpur\nDistrict, vide memo no. 402929 dated 24.12.2018 and\nsubsequently, has been transferred and posted at different place.\n9. A counter affidavit has been filed on behalf of the\nRural Development Department, wherein it has been stated that\nvide letter no. 3694 dated 05.04.2011, the department sent\nrequisition for filling 534 posts of Rural Development Officer in\nthe pay-scale of Rs. 9300-34800/- with grade pay of Rs. 4200/-\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n14/38\nafter obtaining/clearance of roaster point against each category.\nAgain vide letter no. 135127 dated 11.01.2013, the department\nsent requisition for appointment of 534 posts of Rural\nDevelopment Officer to the Bihar Public Service Commission,\nafter obtaining clearance of roaster point as per the guidelines of\nthe General Administration Department as well as the under the\nprovisions of the Rural Development Service Cadre Rules,\n2010. The BPSC conducted examination for 53rd to 55th\nCombined Competitive Examination and results were also\npublished wherein recommendation was made for 533\ncandidates for appointment against the post of Rural\nDevelopment Officer. Altogether 267 candidates were\nrecommended in general category, 86 in SC category, 5 in ST\ncategory, 96 in MBC category, 64 in BC category and 16 posts\nin BC (woman category). It was mentioned by the BPSC that\ndue to non-availability of the candidate under disabled quota\n(O.H.) of BC (W) category, one posts remained vacant. Finally,\ndue to non-joining and other reasons 21 posts of Rural\nDevelopment Officer remained vacant, which did not include\none post of BC (W) of disabled (O.H) quota. Subsequently, vide\nletter dated 29.04.2014, again requisition for filing 21 posts of\nRural Development Officer was sent to the BPSC, for filling the\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n15/38\nsame through 56th to 59th Combined Competitive Examination.\nSubsequently, vide letter dated 03.06.2014, the BPSC requested\nthe department to send the requisition in prescribed format.\nAccordingly, vide letter dated 20.06.2014, the department sent\nrequisition for 22 posts, including one post of BC (W) category\nof disabled O.H. category candidate. After final publication of\nthe result, the BPSC recommended candidate for all the 22\nposts, including one post for BC (W) category candidate.\nPursuant thereto, all the 22 candidates, including respondent\nno.-07, who has been recommended under the BCL disability\nO.H. category candidate, have been appointed on the post of\nRural Development Officer.\n10. A counter affidavit has been filed on behalf of the\nGeneral Administration Department, pursuant to the order dated\n24.07.2025, passed in this writ petition, whereby on the request\nmade by the petitioner, who has appeared in person, to implead\nthe Principal Secretary, General Administration Department,\nGovernment of Bihar as respondent no.08. In the counter\naffidavit, it has been stated that requisition was sent for 534\nposts of Rural Development Officer for 53rd to 55th Bihar\nCompetitive Examination, to be conducted by the BPSC. In the\nsaid advertisement the 03% reservation for virtually impaired,\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n16/38\ndeaf & dumb and orthopedically handicapped was not made\navailable to the BPSC. After rectification, the same was again\nsent and 533 successful candidates were recommended by the\nBPSC, however, due to non-availability of suitable candidate,\none post for BC (W) category under disabled category was not\nfilled up. It has further been stated that vide letter no. 14428\ndated 05.8.2025 the General Administration Department found\nit to be anomaly, in terms of resolution contained in memo no.\n62 dated 05.01.2007. It has further been stated that as per the\nprovision for 3% reservation for disabled candidate, only 15\ndisabled candidates were recommended to the Rural\nDevelopment Department and due to non-availability of one\ncandidate in the disabled category O.H. seat remained vacant.\nSubsequently, requisition was sent for 22 posts in the Rural\nDevelopment Department for the 56th to 59th Competitive\nExamination. Further, so far the petitioner is concerned, he has\nobtained 820 marks, while respondent no. 07 has obtained 811\nmarks and in this context the BPSC has clarified to the\ndepartment that in between the petitioner and the Respondent\nno. 07 in the joint merit list of the candidates of the (O.H.)\ncategory the following candidates are there:-\nSL. Name Roll No. Merit Total Reservation\nNo. No. Marks Category\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n17/38\n1 Om 277260 1033 820 01 (O.H.)\nPrakash\nPandey\n2 Pankaj Kumar 204482 1126 814 01 (O.H.)\nJha\n11. The BPSC exclusively reported that apart from\nthe above, in the merit list the petitioner and one Kumar\nNirbhay Roll no. 271165, category O.H. had obtained 822,\nmarks has also not been selected. It has further been stated that\nthe department has the responsibility to make rules and\nregulations for providing the benefit of reservation to the\npersons who are in need, but it has to be implemented by the\nconcerned department/institution dealing with the subject. It has\nfurther been mentioned that from the report of the BPSC, it is\nclear that Bihar Public Service Commission made a flawed\nreport vide letter no. 152 dated 05.09.2013/11.09.2013 with its\nrecommendation, which was contrary to the provisions of the\nresolution contained in memo no. 62 dated 05.01.2007, which\ncaused the present discrepancies. It has further been mentioned\nthat as per the clarification submitted by the BPSC, in WBC\ncategory, there was one vacancy, which was reserved for\ndisabled woman and against that post, the respondent no. 07 has\nbeen appointed. The petitioner despite having higher marks,\ncould not be selected. It has further been mentioned that the\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n18/38\nBPSC has further clarified, that vide letter no. 4451568 dated\n08.08.2025 of the Rural Development Department, the BPSC\nhas been informed that requisition for total 399 posts of Rural\nDevelopment Officer have been sent for 70th joint Competitive\nExamination, but due to different reasons, at present 6 posts of\nbasic grade of Rural Development Officers are lying vacant. As\nper the reservation category, 02 posts are for unreserved\ncategory candidates, 01 for SC, 01 for EBC and 2 for WBC\ncandidate.\n12. A reply to the counter affidavit filed on behalf of\nthe Respondent no. 08 has been filed by the petitioner in person,\nwherein it has been mentioned that the counter affidavit dated\n27.08.2025 is not in consonance with the order dated\n01.05.2025 passed in this case, wherein it has categorically been\nstated that in view of the judgment dated 03.12.2013 passed in\nC.W.J.C. No. 19127 of 2013, in the category of handicapped\nthere cannot be any other division whether a candidate belongs\nto Extremely Backward-Class Category or General Category\nand therefore, the reservation given to the respondent no. 07\nunder BCL category is against the established law and is in\nviolation of the order passed by the this Hon’ble Court. It has\nfurther been stated that in view of the judgment of this Hon’ble\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n19/38\nCourt as well as the Hon’ble Supreme Court of India, any post\nunder the category of orthopedically handicapped cannot be\nreserved or kept backlog on the basis of the caste and creed. It\nhas further been stated that 4 candidates were not selected in the\ncategory of physically handicapped, in complete violation of the\nprovisions of the State Government issued vide resolution dated\n05.01.2007, which clearly states that the number of seats cannot\nbe reduced under handicapped category, against the quota of 3%\nreservation and if any post is not fit/suitable to the\nhandicapped ,then equivalent other post has to be given. The\nstand of the BPSC that 1 post of Rural Development Officer for\northopedically handicapped was backlog for BCL candidate, is\nagainst the established provision and in violation of the orders\npassed by this Hon’ble Court as well as the Hon’ble Supreme\nCourt. Since no candidate was selected in orthopedically\nhandicapped category on the basis of caste and creed for the 53rd\nto 55th Examination, therefore it was not justified to keep one\npost of Rural Development Officer towards backlog vacancy for\nBCL under Orthopedically handicapped category. Further in the\nnotification/advertisement for 56th to 59th BPSC combined\nexamination, it was not mentioned that any post has been kept\nreserved for BCL candidate, in the physically handicapped\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n20/38\ncategory. Further so far the contention of the BPSC that 01\ncandidate, who has secured more marks than the petitioner is\nconcerned, there might be possibility that he does not fulfill the\neligibility criteria and further he did not come before this\nHon’ble Court or raised any grievance, that despite him\nobtaining 822 marks, he has not been selected. It has been\nstated that the petitioner has secured 820 marks and the last\nselected candidate in the orthopedically handicapped category\nhad obtained 811 marks, therefore, the petitioner should have\nbeen appointed. So far the question of the candidate having\nsecured 822 marks is concerned, he has not come forward to\nraise his grievance, however, in no circumstance a candidate\nsecuring higher marks has not been selected and a candidate\nwith lower marks has been selected.\nSubmission of the Petitioner in Person\n13. The petitioner who is appearing in person submits\nthat despite the fact that he got 820 marks but even then he has\nnot been selected, whereas the respondent no.07, who has got\n811, marks has been finally selected, which is in complete\nviolation of the provisions contained in Articles 14, 16 and 21 of\nthe Constitution of India. He submits that the action of the\nrespondent authorities is in complete violation of the provisions\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n21/38\ncontained in the Constitution of India as well as the persons\nwith Disabilities Act. He further submits that even the 3% quota,\nas per the provisions contained in persons with Disabilities Act,\nhas not been given in as much as that out of 736 vacancies, 22\nvacancies should have been reserved for persons with\nDisabilities Act, however, in the present case, only 18\ncandidates have been selected in the disabled quota. He submits\nthat the action of the respondent authorities is contrary to the\nresolution of the State Government dated 05.01.2007.\n14. The petitioner in person submits that since he has\nsecured more marks than the last selected candidate in his\ncategory, he was fit to be selected, since, there cannot be\nreservation within reservation and this has been declared by the\nHon’ble Supreme Court of India as well as this Hon’ble Court\nand which is also clear from the gazette notification of the State\nGovernment dated 05.01.2007. He further submits that 4 lesser\ncandidates have been selected in the disabled category, since\nonly 18 candidates have been recommended, instead of 22\ncandidates and as per the statement made by the Bihar Public\nService Commission in their counter affidavit, it is clear that no\nreservation has been provided in the Bihar Police Service or\nVigilance Department. However, in view of the circular of the\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n22/38\nState Government dated 05.01.2007, if no reservation has been\nprovided in the Bihar Police Service, then in other services, the\nsame should have been provided to compensate the persons with\ndisabilities.\n15. The petitioner in person further submits that\nalthough 01 candidate namely Kumar Nirbhay has secured\nhigher marks than the petitioner, although he has not been\nselected, and has not come forward to ventilate his grievance,\neven then he can be selected, in place the respondent no. 07,\nwho has admittedly got lesser marks than the petitioner and said\nKumar Nirbhay. Further, the Rural Development Department\nhas informed the BPSC vide letter their dated 08.08.2025 that 6\nposts of Rural Development Officer are lying vacant and\ntherefore, the petitioner can be appointed on the said post.\nSubmission on behalf of the BPSC\n16. Per contra, the learned Senior Counsel for the\nBihar Public Service Commission submits that the BPSC being\na recruiting agency, acts upon the requisition forwarded by the\nrespective departments. As per the requisition dated 20.06.2014\nof the Rural Development Department for 22 posts of Rural\nDevelopment Officer, 01 post was earmarked for disabled\ncandidate belonging to the BCL category and since the\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n23/38\npetitioner is a male candidate, belonging to the general category\northopedically disabled, he cannot lay his claim on the vacancy\nspecifically earmarked for female candidate of the BCL\ncategory by the requisitioning department. He further submits\nthat the Commissions stand is neither arbitrary nor targeted\nagainst the petitioner. One another candidate namely Shri\nKumar Nirbhay merit list serial no. 997, who secured 822 marks\nwas also not selected for the same reasons i.e. due to nonavailability of vacancy in the category. The petitioner was not\neven within the zone of consideration on his own merit, since\nthe cut-off marks for general category was 848 and the\npetitioner secured 820 marks, therefore, he failed to qualify on\nmerits. So far the contention of the petitioner that 22 vacancies\nought to have been reserved for the disabled category candidates\ninstead of 18, is concerned, the same is incorrect and flawed. As\nper the advertisement, which was for 736 posts across 22\nservices, 135 posts were for police service, which are exempted\nfrom disabled category candidates, therefore, 3% statutory\nreservation for the disabled candidate was calculated on the\nremaining 601 posts and 3% of the said 601 posts comes to 18\nposts. Therefore, 18 posts were correctly and lawfully\nearmarked for the disabled category candidate as per the rules.\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n24/38\nSince, the petitioner did not meet the general category cut-off\nand the sole post in the Rural Development Department for\ndisabled candidate was reserved for BCL female, therefore, one\nanother candidate in the O.H, category, having secured higher\nmarks than the petitioner, has not been selected and the 3%\ndisability quota has been wrongly calculated by the petitioner.\nTherefore, the appointment of the respondent no. 07 is in terms\nof the provisions of the advertisement and in terms of the\nexisting rules.\nSubmission on Behalf of Private Respondent No. 07\n17. The learned counsel for the respondent no. 07\nsubmits that the respondent no. 07 being eligible, submitted her\napplication and after going through all the processes, she was\nrecommended for appointment as Rural Development Officer in\nthe Rural Development Department under the BCL (06)\ncategory which was earmarked, while sending requisition by the\nRural Development Department in 2014. Therefore, the\nselection of the respondent no. 07 has been made as per the\nvacancy, requisition for advertisement as well as eligibility of\nthe candidate as a female disabled category candidate of the\nBCL. That out of total 22 posts of Rural Development Officer\nunder the Rural Development Department, for which requisition\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n25/38\nwas sent, 12 posts were to be filled up from unreserved category\ncandidates, 01 from SC, 4 from EBC, 3 from BC and 2 from\nBCL, out of which 01 was reserved for BCL (O.H.) candidate.\nAfter appointment, the respondent no. 07 has already joined and\nis working. Further, the petitioner is a general category\ncandidate in the disabled quota male and as such he cannot be\nequated with the post earmarked for BCL (disabled category\ncandidate). The selection has been made as per the terms and\nconditions of the advertisement, as well as by giving horizontal\nand vertical reservation and after following the roaster. Further,\n01 candidate having higher marks than the petitioner has also\nnot been selected, since no post was available under the (O.H.)\ncategory.\nCase Law Relied By The Petitioner\n18. The petitioner in person refers to and relies on a\njudgment of the Hon’ble Supreme Court of India reported in\n(2017) 3 SCC 504 (Union of India and others Vs. M.\nSelvakumar and another), wherein in paragraph nos. 22 and\n23, the Hon’ble Supreme Court has held as follows:-\n22. Article 16 of the Constitution provides\nfor equality of opportunity in matters of\npublic employment. The State in terms of\nArticle 16 of the Constitution provides two\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n26/38\ntypes of reservations i.e. a vertical or social\nreservation as provided for in Article 16\nclause (4) and horizontal reservation which\nis referable to Article 16 clause (1). Special\nreservation in favour of physically\nhandicapped, women, etc. under Article\n16(1) or 15(3) of the Constitution are the\ninstances of horizontal reservation.\n23. A nine-Judge Bench in Indra Sawhney\nv.Union of India [Indra Sawhney v. Union of\nIndia, 1992 Supp (3) SCC 217 : 1992 SCC\n(L&S) Supp 1] had elaborately considered\nboth the concepts of reservation. In para 812\nof the said judgment, Justice B.P. Jeevan\nReddy, has referred to both the types of\nreservations. It was held that horizontal\nreservations cut across the vertical\nreservation. The following was stated : (SCC\npp. 735-36)\n“812. … There are two types of\nreservations, which may, for the sake of\nconvenience, be referred to as “vertical\nreservations” and “horizontal\nreservations”. The reservations in favour\nof Scheduled Castes, Scheduled Tribes\nand Other Backward Classes [under\nArticle 16(4)] may be called vertical\nreservations whereas reservations in\nfavour of physically handicapped [under\nclause (1) of Article 16] can be referred\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n27/38\nto as horizontal reservations. Horizontal\nreservations cut across the vertical\nreservations—what is called interlocking\nreservations. To be more precise, suppose\n3% of the vacancies are reserved in\nfavour of physically handicapped\npersons; this would be a reservation\nrelatable to clause (1) of Article 16. The\npersons selected against this quota will\nbe placed in the appropriate category; if\nhe belongs to SC category he will be\nplaced in that quota by making necessary\nadjustments; similarly, if he belongs to\nopen competition (OC) category, he will\nbe placed in that category by making\nnecessary adjustments. Even after\nproviding for these horizontal\nreservations, the percentage of\nreservations in favour of backward class\nof citizens remains—and should remain\n—the same. This is how these\nreservations are worked out in several\nStates and there is no reason not to\ncontinue that procedure.”\n19. The petitioner in person further refers to and\nrelies on a judgment of the Hon’ble Suprme Court of India\nreported in (2007) 8 SCC 621 (Mahesh Gupta and Ors. Vs.\nYashwant Kumar Ahirwar and Ors.), wherein in paragraph\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n28/38\nnos, 10 to 14, the Hon’ble Suprme Court has held as follows:\n“10. The State in terms of Article 16 of the\nConstitution of India may make two types of\nreservations—vertical and horizontal.\nArticle 16(4) provides for vertical\nreservation; whereas Clause (1) of Article 16\nprovides for horizontal reservation.\n11. The State adopted a policy decision for\nfilling up the reserved posts for handicapped\npersons. A special drive was to be launched\ntherefor. The circular letter was issued only\nfor the said purpose. A bare perusal of the\nsaid Circular Letter dated 29-3-1993 would\nclearly show that the State had made 3%\nreservation for blinds and 2% for other\nphysically handicapped persons. Such a\nreservation falling within Clause (1) of\nArticle 16 of the Constitution has nothing to\ndo with the object and purport sought to be\nachieved by reason of Clause (4) thereof.\n12. Disability has drawn the attention of the\nworldwide community. India is a signatory\nto various international treaties and\nconventions. The State, therefore, took a\npolicy decision to have horizontal\nreservation with a view to fulfil its\nconstitutional object as also its commitment\nto the international community. A disabled is\na disabled. The question of making any\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n29/38\nfurther reservation on the basis of caste,\ncreed or religion ordinarily may not arise.\nThey constitute a special class. The\nadvertisement, however, failed to mention in\nregard to the reservation for handicapped\npersons at the outset, but, as noticed\nhereinbefore, the vacant posts were required\nto be filled up for two categories of\ncandidates; one for Scheduled Caste and\nScheduled Tribe candidates and other for\nhandicapped candidates. Handicapped\ncandidates have not been further classified\nas belonging to Scheduled Castes, Scheduled\nTribes and general category candidates.\n13. It is a travesty of justice that despite the\nState clarified its own position in its order\ndated 1-1-2004 and stated that the posts\nwere vacant under the handicapped quota\nbut it completely turned turtle and took a\ndiagonally opposite stand when a contempt\npetition was filed. In its reply in the said\nproceedings, reference was made to the\naforementioned order dated 1-1-2004 but\nwithin a short time viz. on 4-2-2004 it opined\non a presumption that as the word\n“handicapped” was not mentioned in the\nheading of advertisement they were meant\nonly for Scheduled Caste and Scheduled\nTribe candidates. Rule of executive\nconstruction was given a complete go-by.\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n30/38\nReasonableness and fairness which is the\nhallmark of Article 14 of the Constitution of\nIndia was completely lost sight of. The\nofficers of the State behaved strangely. It\nprevaricated its stand only because a\ncontempt proceeding was initiated. If the\nState was eager to accommodate the writ\npetitioner-respondent, it could have done so.\nIt did not take any measure in that behalf. It\nchose to terminate the services of some of\nthe employees who had already been\nappointed. Such a course could not have\nbeen taken either in law or in equity. The\nState is expected to have a constitutional\nvision. It must give effect to the\nconstitutional mandate. Any act done by it\nshould be considered to have been effected\nin the light of the provisions contained in\nPart IV of the Constitution of India. The\nState in terms of the provisions contained in\nPart IV should have given effect to the\nprinciples embodied in Article 39 of the\nConstitution of India. Whereas a reasonable\nreservation within the meaning of Article 16\nof the Constitution of India should not\nordinarily exist (sic exceed), 50%, as has\nbeen held by this Court in Indra Sawhney v.\nUnion of India [1992 Supp (3) SCC 217 :\n1992 SCC (L&S) Supp 1 : (1992) 22 ATC\n385 : AIR 1993 SC 477] , reservation for\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n31/38\nwomen or handicapped persons would not\ncome within the purview thereof.\n14.Furthermore, when the decision was\ntaken, the Persons with Disabilities (Equal\nOpportunities, Protection of Rights and Full\nParticipation) Act, 1995 (for short “the 1995\nAct”) had come into force. In terms of the\n1995 Act, the States were obligated to make\nreservations for handicapped persons. The\nState completely lost sight of its commitment\nboth under its own policy decision as also\nthe statutory provision.”\n20. The petitioner in person further refers to and relies\non an unreported judgment of a learned Co-ordinate Bench of\nthis Court dated 03.12.2013 passed in C.W.J.C. No. 19127 of\n2013 (Rakesh Kumar Vs. State of Bihar and Ors.), wherein it\nhas been held as follows:-\n“Besides hearing learned counsel for the\nparties, I have also perused the materials\navailable on record. Fact remains that the\npetitioner had claimed his candidature as\nhandicap candidate. In the category of\nhandicap, there can not be any other\ndivision, whether a candidate belongs to\nExtremely Backward Category or General\nCategory as also there can not be any\ndistinction amongst the candidates of\northopedic handicap category. Orthopedic\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n32/38\nhandicap is one class and there can not be\nany division in the same.”\nConsideration and Analysis\n21. Having heard the Petitioner in person, the\nlearned counsel appearing on behalf of the state, the Learned\nSenior Counsel appearing on behalf of the Bihar Public Service\nCommission and after going through the documents, this court\nfinds that an advertisement was issued for 56th to 59th Combined\nPreliminary Competitive Examinations for 22 services, having\ntotal 746 vacancies, wherein in view of the circular of the State\nGovernment contained in memo no. 62 dated 05.01.2007\nrelaxation of 10 years was given to the persons with disabilities\nand they were required to obtain at least 32% marks in the\nPreliminary test, to be eligible for further participation in the\nselection. The petitioner being eligible, submitted his\napplication before the cut-off date. After going through the\nprocess prescribed i.e. Preliminary test and the Mains\nexamination, the Petitioner was called for Interview on\n08.05.2018. The petitioner appeared in the said Interview and\nthe final result was published on 18.08.2018, wherein altogether\n736 candidates were declared successful, to be appointed in total\n21 services. The name of the petitioner was not there in the list\nof successful candidates, although he secured 820 marks. The\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n33/38\npetitioner belongs to the Disability quota of Orthopaedics\n(O.H.). The petitioner started making enquiry and came to know\nthat one Archana Kumari (Respondent no.7), who had obtained\nthe rank 1178 i.e. below the rank of the petitioner, has been\nselected as Rural Development Officer in the category of\nBCL/disabled/OH, in the Rural Development Department,\nGovernment of Bihar. The petitioner filed an application under\nthe Right to Information Act and thereafter he came to know,\nthat the Respondent no.7 has got only 811 marks, even then she\nhas been selected. Thereafter, the petitioner filed the present writ\npetition, wherein he arrayed Archana Kumari as party\nRespondent no.7. He prayed for quashing part of the result\npublished under the signature of the Joint Secretary-cumExamination Controller, B.P.S.C., Patna, by which the\nRespondent no.7, whose name stands at serial no.22 under item\nno.9, Rural Development Officer, in the final result, has been\ndeclared as a selected candidate under the disabled O.H.\ncategory, despite being at serial no.1178 in the final merit list,\nagainst the advertisement for 56th two 59th Combined\nCompetitive Examination. Some other prayers were also there.\nA counter affidavit has been filed on behalf of the Bihar Public\nService Commission, wherein a stand has been taken that since\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n34/38\n1 seat under the category of Backward Class Lady (BCL) in the\nDisabled Category remained vacant, in absence of suitable\ndisabled candidate in the Rural Development Department, in\nterms of the advertisement for 53rd to 55th Combined\nCompetitive Examination, therefore the same was carried\nforward and was kept reserved for the BCL under the disabled\ncategory of O.H. Therefore, the Respondent no.7, despite having\nlesser marks, than the petitioner, has been selected under the\nsaid category. Vide order dated 24.07.2025 passed in this case,\nthe Principal Secretary, General Administration Department,\nGovernment of Bihar, Patna was impleaded as party Respondent\nno.8 and was directed to file his counter affidavit. Subsequent\nthereto a counter affidavit was filed on behalf of Respondent\nno.8, wherein it has been categorically stated that the\nDepartment makes certain rules and regulations to provide the\nbenefits of reservation to persons who are basically in need,\nhowever, so far, the question of implementation is concerned,\nthe implementation has to be carried out by the concerned\ninstitution dealing with the subject. It has further been stated\nthat after thorough examination, it is clear that the Bihar Public\nService Commission made a flawed report vide its letter no.152\ndated 05.09.2013/11.09.2013 with the recommendation. It was\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n35/38\ncontrary to the provisions of Resolution contained in memo\nno.62 dated 05.01.2007, which caused the discrepancy, for\nwhich the Commission is accountable.\n22. Further from the advertisement issued by the\nBihar Public Service Commission it would transpire that\nalthough the vacancies for each category i.e. General, S.C., S.T.,\nE.B.C., B.C. and B.C.L. were Compartmentalised, however the\nvacancies for Disabled Category Candidates were not\ncompartmentalised. In the opinion of this court, the\nadvertisement itself was issued in teeth of the Judgement of the\nHon’ble Supreme Court of India in the case of Saurabh Yadav &\nOrs. VS The State of U.P. & Ors as reported in 2021 (4) SCC\n542. Further the requisition which was sent by the Rural\nDevelopment Department, to the Special Secretary-cumExamination Controller, Bihar Public Service Commission vide\nletter no.189040 dated 20.06.2014 contained the details of 22\nPosts. Out of the said 22 Posts 12 Posts were unreserved, 01\nPost was reserved for S.C. candidate, 04 Post was reserved for\nS.T. category candidate, 03 Post was reserved for B.C. category\ncandidate, 01 Post was reserved for B.C.L. category candidate\nand 01 Post was reserved for disabled category candidate\nbelonging to the B.C.L. category. However, the Bihar Public\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n36/38\nService Commission advertised 02 Post for B.C.L. category\ncandidate, which itself was contrary to the requisition sent by\nthe Rural Development Department, since no post was shown in\nthe advertisement for disabled category candidate. Further in\nterms of paragraph no.54 of the Judgement of the Hon’ble\nSupreme Court of India in the case of Saurabh Yadav (Supra)\nwherein, it has been categorically held that as follows:-\n“54 Horizontal reservation on the other\nhand, by their nature, are not inviolate pools\nor carved in stone. They are premised on\ntheir overlaps and are ‘interlocking’\nreservations. As a sequel, they are to be\ncalculated concurrently and along with the\ninviolate ‘vertical’ (or “social”) reservation\nquotas, by application of the various steps\nlaid out with clarity in Paragraph 11 of\nJustice Lalit’s Judgement. They cannot be\ncarried forward. The first rule that applies\nfor filling horizontal reservation quota is\none of adjustment, i.e. examining whether\non merit any of the horizontal categories are\nadjusted in the merit list in the open\ncategory and then, in the quota for such\nhorizontal category within the particular\nspecified/social reservation”.\n23. From paragraph no.54 of the Judgement of the\nHon’ble Supreme Court of India in the case of Saurabh Yadav\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n37/38\n(Supra) it is clear that the horizontal reservation cannot be\ncarried forward. In the present case the 01 vacancy, which\nremained unfilled and vacant in the 53rd to 55th Combined\nCompetitive Examination, in the B.C.L. disabled category, was\ncarried forward, which is in teeth of the Judgement of the\nHon’ble Supreme Court of India in the case of Saurabh Yadav\n(Supra) and which in the opinion of this court is not permissible.\nThe correct course would have been that the vacancy which\nremained unfilled in the BCL O.H. category would have been\nincluded in the advertisement for 56th to 59th combined\ncompetitive examination in the O.H. category and the candidate\nbelonging to the said category would have been considered and\nselected, as per his/her merit position in the merit list in the\nO.H. category. In the present case no such exercise has been\ncarried out and the Post was kept reserved for BCL category\nO.H. candidate and finally the respondent no.8, although, lower\nin the merit list, was given the benefit of both the BCL and the\nO.H. category, has been selected, which is contrary to the\nJudgement of the Hon’ble Supreme Court of India in the case of\nSaurabh Yadav (Supra).\n24. From the consideration made above, it is\ndirected that one vacancy meant for Disabled O.H. category\n\nPatna High Court CWJC No.20547 of 2018 dt.23-06-2026\n38/38\ncandidate is thus required to be reworked out and assessed by\nthe B.P.S.C., after considering all the eligible candidates\nbelonging to the said category and benefit of the same has to be\nextended accordingly upon its final outcome/adjudication\nmaking selection and recommendation in favour of such person.\nTill then the Respondent no.7 would not be disturbed and her\nselection and appointment shall be governed by such final\noutcome upon re-assessment as directed above. The entire\nexercise, as directed above, must be completed within a period\nof 4 months from the date of receipt/production of a copy of the\norder.\n25. With the aforementioned observations and\ndirections the present writ petition is disposed of.\n26. Pending applications, if any, shall also stands\ndisposed of.\n(Ritesh Kumar, J)\nkrishnakant/-\nAFR/NAFR NAFR\nCAV DATE 07.04.2026\nUploading Date 23.06.2026\nTransmission Date NA	38	t	HCMA010205472026	Judicial Precedent	\N	Single Bench	Vishwambhar Singh	State of Bihar & Ors.	\N	\N	Constitutional Law	In this judgment under Civil Law, the Court ruled on the dispute between 27 Cwjc-20547-2018-Patna and the Respondent.	0	2026 LexGuard (Judg) 2ED467	{"court": "Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)", "title": "Vishwambhar Singh v. State of Bihar & Ors.", "case_id": "2ed467ca-5dd2-42a1-a806-66bed4daa710", "case_no": "Civil Writ Jurisdiction Case No. 20547 of 2018", "parties": "Vishwambhar Singh v. State of Bihar & Ors.", "category": "QUASHING", "petitioner": "Vishwambhar Singh", "respondent": "State of Bihar & Ors.", "legal_domain": "Constitutional Law", "category_facts": {"category": "QUASHING", "civil_cloak": false, "quash_ground": "Absence of prima facie ingredients", "compromise_affidavit": false, "bhajan_lal_principles": true, "proceedings_challenged": "First Information Report (FIR)"}, "activity_events": ["judgment pronounced"], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [], "activity_clusters": ["trial_outcome"], "scenario_clusters": ["constitutional_fundamental"], "scenario_keywords": ["article 226"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
33333333-0001-4000-8000-000000000001	Writ Petition (Civil) No. 880 of 2017	Association for Democratic Reforms (ADR) & Another v. Union of India & Ministry of Finance	Supreme Court of India	2024	Hon'ble D.Y. Chandrachud, C.J., Sanjiv Khanna, B.R. Gavai, J.B. Pardiwala, Manoj Misra, JJ.	Association for Democratic Reforms (ADR) & Another v. Union of India & Ministry of Finance	["Companies Act"]	["Section 182 Companies Act"]	["Electoral Bonds Scheme struck down", "voter right to know", "political funding transparency", "corporate donations"]	Unanimous five-judge Constitution Bench struck down the Electoral Bond Scheme as unconstitutional, holding that anonymous corporate donations violate the voters' fundamental right to information under Article 19(1)(a).	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_2024_WP_Civil_No_880_of_2017_33330001.pdf	6da2215b677382b1ac641aab3faf30af02b2f75b62c065486f4d5da9aaa4b074	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:22:39.067112+05:30	2026-10-25 11:22:39.067119+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.707804+05:30	\N	[]	Information about funding to political parties is essential for a voter to exercise their freedom to vote in an effective manner. The Electoral Bond Scheme infringes the right to information under Article 19(1)(a) and does not satisfy the proportionality standard.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2024) 5 SCC 1]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2024) 5 SCC 1", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (5-Judge Constitution Bench Landmark)	Supreme Court of India\nWrit Petition (Civil) No. 880 of 2017\nAssociation for Democratic Reforms and Another v. Union of India and Others\nBench: Hon'ble D.Y. Chandrachud, C.J., Sanjiv Khanna, B.R. Gavai, J.B. Pardiwala, Manoj Misra, JJ.\nDate: 2024-02-15\nCitation: (2024) 5 SCC 1\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nInformation about funding to political parties is essential for a voter to exercise their freedom to vote in an effective manner. The Electoral Bond Scheme infringes the right to information under Article 19(1)(a) and does not satisfy the proportionality standard.\n\nSUMMARY:\nUnanimous five-judge Constitution Bench struck down the Electoral Bond Scheme as unconstitutional, holding that anonymous corporate donations violate the voters' fundamental right to information under Article 19(1)(a).\n\nSTATUTES: Article 19(1)(a), Section 29C RPA, Section 182 Companies Act\nDISPOSITION: Electoral Bonds Scheme Declared Unconstitutional and Struck Down	2	f	SCIN010008802017	Writ Petition	2024-02-15	Constitution Bench (5 Judges)	Association for Democratic Reforms (ADR) & Another	Union of India & Ministry of Finance	Senior Advocate Kapil Sibal, Prashant Bhushan, Shadan Farasat	Attorney General R. Venkataramani, Solicitor General Tushar Mehta	Electoral Law & Right to Information	Electoral bonds allowing secret corporate donations to political parties were declared unconstitutional. Voters have a fundamental right to know who funds political parties.	0	(2024) 5 SCC 1	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Association for Democratic Reforms and Another v. Union of India and Others", "case_id": "33333333-0001-4000-8000-000000000001", "case_no": "Writ Petition (Civil) No. 880 of 2017", "parties": "Association for Democratic Reforms (ADR) & Another v. Union of India & Ministry of Finance", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Association for Democratic Reforms (ADR) & Another", "respondent": "Union of India & Ministry of Finance", "legal_domain": "Electoral Law & Right to Information", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Writ Petition / Criminal Appeal", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "19", "canonical": "Section 19 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "scenario_keywords": [], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
33333333-0002-4000-8000-000000000002	Writ Petition (Civil) No. 1099 of 2019	In Re Article 370 Petitions v. Union of India & State of Jammu and Kashmir	Supreme Court of India	2024	Hon'ble D.Y. Chandrachud, C.J., Sanjay Kishan Kaul, Sanjiv Khanna, B.R. Gavai, Surya Kant, JJ.	In Re Article 370 Petitions v. Union of India & State of Jammu and Kashmir	["Constitution of India"]	["Article 370", "Article 367", "Article 356", "Article 3"]	["abrogation of Article 370", "temporary provision", "sovereignty merger", "restoration of statehood"]	Five-judge Constitution Bench unanimously upheld the abrogation of Article 370, holding that Article 370 was a temporary transitional provision and Jammu and Kashmir did not retain any internal sovereignty after accession to India.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_2024_WP_Civil_No_1099_of_2019_33330002.pdf	90e126e787fcbf390b45055b12b0e310001f84e1ac06967c97a60c528be90172	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:22:39.128008+05:30	2026-10-25 11:22:39.128015+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.708299+05:30	\N	[]	Article 370 was a feature of asymmetric federalism and not sovereignty. The power of the President under Article 370(3) to issue a notification declaring Article 370 inoperative did not cease to exist upon dissolution of the Constituent Assembly.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2024) 6 SCC 1]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2024) 6 SCC 1", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (5-Judge Constitution Bench Landmark)	Supreme Court of India\nWrit Petition (Civil) No. 1099 of 2019\nIn Re: Article 370 of the Constitution of India\nBench: Hon'ble D.Y. Chandrachud, C.J., Sanjay Kishan Kaul, Sanjiv Khanna, B.R. Gavai, Surya Kant, JJ.\nDate: 2023-12-11\nCitation: (2024) 6 SCC 1\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nArticle 370 was a feature of asymmetric federalism and not sovereignty. The power of the President under Article 370(3) to issue a notification declaring Article 370 inoperative did not cease to exist upon dissolution of the Constituent Assembly.\n\nSUMMARY:\nFive-judge Constitution Bench unanimously upheld the abrogation of Article 370, holding that Article 370 was a temporary transitional provision and Jammu and Kashmir did not retain any internal sovereignty after accession to India.\n\nSTATUTES: Article 370, Article 367, Article 356, Article 3\nDISPOSITION: Constitutional Orders CO 272 and CO 273 Upheld	2	f	SCIN010010992019	Writ Petition	2023-12-11	Constitution Bench (5 Judges)	In Re Article 370 Petitions	Union of India & State of Jammu and Kashmir	Senior Advocate Kapil Sibal, Gopal Subramanium, Zafar Shah	Attorney General R. Venkataramani, Solicitor General Tushar Mehta	Constitutional Law & Asymmetric Federalism	The Supreme Court upheld the revocation of special status for Jammu and Kashmir under Article 370, confirming that J&K became an integral part of India with no residual sovereignty.	0	(2024) 6 SCC 1	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "In Re: Article 370 of the Constitution of India", "case_id": "33333333-0002-4000-8000-000000000002", "case_no": "Writ Petition (Civil) No. 1099 of 2019", "parties": "In Re Article 370 Petitions v. Union of India & State of Jammu and Kashmir", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "In Re Article 370 Petitions", "respondent": "Union of India & State of Jammu and Kashmir", "legal_domain": "Constitutional Law & Asymmetric Federalism", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Writ Petition / Criminal Appeal", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "370", "canonical": "Section 370 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "367", "canonical": "Section 367 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "356", "canonical": "Section 356 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "3", "canonical": "Section 3 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "scenario_keywords": [], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Dismissed", "disposition": "Dismissed"}}
33333333-0003-4000-8000-000000000003	Writ Petition (Civil) No. 400 of 2012	National Legal Services Authority (NALSA) v. Union of India & Others	Supreme Court of India	2014	Hon'ble K.S. Radhakrishnan & A.K. Sikri, JJ.	National Legal Services Authority (NALSA) v. Union of India & Others	["Constitution of India"]	["Article 14", "Article 15", "Article 19(1)(a)", "Article 21"]	["third gender", "gender self-determination", "transgender rights", "affirmative action"]	Landmark ruling recognizing transgender persons as the 'third gender' and affirming their constitutional right to self-determine their gender identity without surgical or medical intervention.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_2014_WP_Civil_No_400_of_2012_33330003.pdf	ad714ba3465cff1a6cc2ab5882079b88ff5609186d8e71d57d63990fadf56ed9	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:22:39.180291+05:30	2026-10-25 11:22:39.180296+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.708938+05:30	\N	[]	Gender identity is one of the most-fundamental aspects of life which refers to a person's intrinsic sense of being male, female, or transgender. Self-determination of gender is an integral part of personal autonomy and freedom under Articles 19(1)(a) and 21.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2014) 5 SCC 438]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2014) 5 SCC 438", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding Human Rights Landmark)	Supreme Court of India\nWrit Petition (Civil) No. 400 of 2012\nNational Legal Services Authority (NALSA) v. Union of India and Others\nBench: Hon'ble K.S. Radhakrishnan & A.K. Sikri, JJ.\nDate: 2014-04-15\nCitation: (2014) 5 SCC 438\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nGender identity is one of the most-fundamental aspects of life which refers to a person's intrinsic sense of being male, female, or transgender. Self-determination of gender is an integral part of personal autonomy and freedom under Articles 19(1)(a) and 21.\n\nSUMMARY:\nLandmark ruling recognizing transgender persons as the 'third gender' and affirming their constitutional right to self-determine their gender identity without surgical or medical intervention.\n\nSTATUTES: Article 14, Article 15, Article 19(1)(a), Article 21\nDISPOSITION: Legal Recognition of Third Gender Conferred	2	f	SCIN010004002012	Writ Petition	2014-04-15	Division Bench (2 Judges)	National Legal Services Authority (NALSA)	Union of India & Others	Senior Advocate Colin Gonsalves	Additional Solicitor General Siddharth Luthra	Constitutional Law & Gender Identity	Transgender persons have the full constitutional right to self-identify their gender as male, female, or third gender. They are entitled to fundamental rights and affirmative action benefits.	0	(2014) 5 SCC 438	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "National Legal Services Authority (NALSA) v. Union of India and Others", "case_id": "33333333-0003-4000-8000-000000000003", "case_no": "Writ Petition (Civil) No. 400 of 2012", "parties": "National Legal Services Authority (NALSA) v. Union of India & Others", "category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "petitioner": "National Legal Services Authority (NALSA)", "respondent": "Union of India & Others", "legal_domain": "Constitutional Law & Gender Identity", "category_facts": {"category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "surety_count": null, "custody_period": null, "application_type": "Anticipatory Bail", "bail_bond_amount": null, "trial_court_fine": null, "reason_for_relief": null, "reporting_condition": null, "trial_court_sentence": null, "trial_court_fine_paid": false}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "14", "canonical": "Section 14 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "15", "canonical": "Section 15 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "19", "canonical": "Section 19 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "21", "canonical": "Section 21 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["constitutional_fundamental"], "scenario_keywords": ["article 14", "article 21"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
33333333-0004-4000-8000-000000000004	Writ Petition (Civil) No. 1031 of 2019	Anuradha Bhasin (Executive Editor, Kashmir Times) v. Union of India & UT of Jammu and Kashmir	Supreme Court of India	2020	Hon'ble N.V. Ramana, R. Subhash Reddy, B.R. Gavai, JJ.	Anuradha Bhasin (Executive Editor, Kashmir Times) v. Union of India & UT of Jammu and Kashmir	["Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973"]	["Article 19(1)(a)", "Article 19(1)(g)", "Section 144 CrPC", "Suspension Rules, 2017"]	["internet freedom", "indefinite shutdown unconstitutional", "proportionality test", "Section 144 CrPC safeguards"]	Three-judge bench declared that freedom of speech and expression and freedom to practice trade or profession through the medium of the internet is a fundamental right under Article 19(1)(a) and 19(1)(g). Indefinite internet suspension orders are illegal.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_2020_WP_Civil_No_1031_of_2019_33330004.pdf	2228a25acdaee576347c8ecddf81e73ae0556d634130608beee5177a52c4d130	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:22:39.237783+05:30	2026-10-25 11:22:39.237787+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.709432+05:30	\N	[]	Freedom of speech and expression through the internet is an integral part of Article 19(1)(a). Suspension of internet services cannot be indefinite; it must adhere to the principle of proportionality and must be published and subjected to periodic review.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2020) 3 SCC 637]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2020) 3 SCC 637", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding 3-Judge Precedent)	Supreme Court of India\nWrit Petition (Civil) No. 1031 of 2019\nAnuradha Bhasin v. Union of India and Others\nBench: Hon'ble N.V. Ramana, R. Subhash Reddy, B.R. Gavai, JJ.\nDate: 2020-01-10\nCitation: (2020) 3 SCC 637\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nFreedom of speech and expression through the internet is an integral part of Article 19(1)(a). Suspension of internet services cannot be indefinite; it must adhere to the principle of proportionality and must be published and subjected to periodic review.\n\nSUMMARY:\nThree-judge bench declared that freedom of speech and expression and freedom to practice trade or profession through the medium of the internet is a fundamental right under Article 19(1)(a) and 19(1)(g). Indefinite internet suspension orders are illegal.\n\nSTATUTES: Article 19(1)(a), Article 19(1)(g), Section 144 CrPC, Suspension Rules, 2017\nDISPOSITION: Periodic Review of Internet Suspension Orders Mandated	2	f	SCIN010010312019	Writ Petition	2020-01-10	Three-Judge Bench	Anuradha Bhasin (Executive Editor, Kashmir Times)	Union of India & UT of Jammu and Kashmir	Senior Advocate Kapil Sibal, Sanjay Hegde	Attorney General K.K. Venugopal, Solicitor General Tushar Mehta	Freedom of Speech & Telecommunications Law	Access to the internet is protected under the fundamental rights of free speech and carrying on business. The government cannot impose indefinite internet shutdowns and must review all suspension orders periodically.	0	(2020) 3 SCC 637	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Anuradha Bhasin v. Union of India and Others", "case_id": "33333333-0004-4000-8000-000000000004", "case_no": "Writ Petition (Civil) No. 1031 of 2019", "parties": "Anuradha Bhasin (Executive Editor, Kashmir Times) v. Union of India & UT of Jammu and Kashmir", "category": "SERVICE_WRIT", "petitioner": "Anuradha Bhasin (Executive Editor, Kashmir Times)", "respondent": "Union of India & UT of Jammu and Kashmir", "legal_domain": "Freedom of Speech & Telecommunications Law", "category_facts": {"category": "SERVICE_WRIT", "effective_dates": null, "monetary_claims": "Notional vs Actual consequential arrears", "delay_laches_raised": false, "service_relief_sought": "Pensionary / Retiral Benefits and Arrears"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "19", "canonical": "Section 19 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CRPC", "section": "144", "canonical": "Section 144 CRPC", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["service_employment"], "scenario_keywords": ["suspension order"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed in Part", "disposition": "Allowed in Part"}}
33333333-0005-4000-8000-000000000005	Arbitration Petition No. 38 of 2020	Cox and Kings Ltd. v. SAP India Pvt. Ltd. & Another	Supreme Court of India	2024	Hon'ble D.Y. Chandrachud, C.J., Hrishikesh Roy, P.S. Narasimha, J.B. Pardiwala, Manoj Misra, JJ.	Cox and Kings Ltd. v. SAP India Pvt. Ltd. & Another	["Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996", "Companies Act, 2013"]	["Section 7", "Section 8", "Section 9", "Section 11(6)"]	["Group of Companies doctrine", "non-signatory binding", "mutual intention", "commercial arbitration"]	Five-judge Constitution Bench upheld the Group of Companies Doctrine, holding that a non-signatory affiliate company can be bound by an arbitration agreement if the common intention of all parties was to bind both signatories and non-signatories.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_2024_Arb_Pet_No_38_of_2020_33330005.pdf	1474c50c896c0989e01967ef5518ed08bebdef3d2b6b10f525af2b2df0334afe	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:22:39.271484+05:30	2026-10-25 11:22:39.271487+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.709912+05:30	\N	[]	The Group of Companies doctrine is an integral part of Indian arbitration jurisprudence. The court or arbitral tribunal must examine cumulative factors: mutual intention, direct relationship to the signatory, commonality of subject matter, and composite transaction.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2024) 4 SCC 1]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2024) 4 SCC 1", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (5-Judge Constitution Bench Landmark)	Supreme Court of India\nArbitration Petition No. 38 of 2020\nCox and Kings Ltd. v. SAP India Pvt. Ltd. and Another\nBench: Hon'ble D.Y. Chandrachud, C.J., Hrishikesh Roy, P.S. Narasimha, J.B. Pardiwala, Manoj Misra, JJ.\nDate: 2023-12-06\nCitation: (2024) 4 SCC 1\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nThe Group of Companies doctrine is an integral part of Indian arbitration jurisprudence. The court or arbitral tribunal must examine cumulative factors: mutual intention, direct relationship to the signatory, commonality of subject matter, and composite transaction.\n\nSUMMARY:\nFive-judge Constitution Bench upheld the Group of Companies Doctrine, holding that a non-signatory affiliate company can be bound by an arbitration agreement if the common intention of all parties was to bind both signatories and non-signatories.\n\nSTATUTES: Section 7, Section 8, Section 9, Section 11(6)\nDISPOSITION: Group of Companies Doctrine Affirmed and Refined	2	f	SCIN010000382020	Arbitration Petition	2023-12-06	Constitution Bench (5 Judges)	Cox and Kings Ltd.	SAP India Pvt. Ltd. & Another	Senior Advocate Sanjeev Sen	Senior Advocate Neeraj Kishan Kaul	Commercial Arbitration Jurisprudence	Even if an affiliated parent or subsidiary did not sign the contract directly, they can still be included in the arbitration if their involvement and actions show they intended to be bound.	0	(2024) 4 SCC 1	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Cox and Kings Ltd. v. SAP India Pvt. Ltd. and Another", "case_id": "33333333-0005-4000-8000-000000000005", "case_no": "Arbitration Petition No. 38 of 2020", "parties": "Cox and Kings Ltd. v. SAP India Pvt. Ltd. & Another", "category": "COMMERCIAL_CIVIL", "petitioner": "Cox and Kings Ltd.", "respondent": "SAP India Pvt. Ltd. & Another", "legal_domain": "Commercial Arbitration Jurisprudence", "category_facts": {"category": "COMMERCIAL_CIVIL", "dispute_type": "Arbitration & Conciliation Challenge", "statutory_basis": "Commercial Courts Act / Civil Procedure Code", "interim_injunction_granted": false}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["financial_commercial"], "scenario_keywords": ["section 9"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Referred to Tribunal", "disposition": "Referred to Tribunal"}}
33333333-0006-4000-8000-000000000006	Criminal Appeal No. 152 of 2013	Tofan Singh v. State of Tamil Nadu	Supreme Court of India	2021	Hon'ble R.F. Nariman, Navin Sinha, Indira Banerjee, JJ.	Tofan Singh v. State of Tamil Nadu	["Evidence Act", "NDPS Act", "Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances Act, 1985"]	["Section 25 Evidence Act", "Section 26 Evidence Act", "Section 53 NDPS Act"]	["confessional statement inadmissible", "Section 67 NDPS statement", "police officer status of NCB", "Section 25 Evidence Act bar"]	Three-judge bench held that officers invested with powers under Section 53 of the NDPS Act are 'police officers' within Section 25 of the Evidence Act, rendering any confessional statement recorded under Section 67 inadmissible as substantive evidence.	Quashed	storage/cases/SC_2021_Crl_App_No_152_of_2013_33330006.pdf	fc50a8b7dde1b7ec671e4a4ac6cd4582b7ce30d28e02e03b22e9761e15c282ac	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:22:39.302587+05:30	2026-10-25 11:22:39.302591+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.711136+05:30	\N	[]	Officers invested with powers under Section 53 of the NDPS Act are police officers within the meaning of Section 25 of the Evidence Act. A confessional statement made to them under Section 67 cannot be used as substantive evidence against the accused.	[]	Allowed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2021) 4 SCC 1]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2021) 4 SCC 1", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding 3-Judge Precedent)	Supreme Court of India\nCriminal Appeal No. 152 of 2013\nTofan Singh v. State of Tamil Nadu\nBench: Hon'ble R.F. Nariman, Navin Sinha, Indira Banerjee, JJ.\nDate: 2020-10-29\nCitation: (2021) 4 SCC 1\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nOfficers invested with powers under Section 53 of the NDPS Act are police officers within the meaning of Section 25 of the Evidence Act. A confessional statement made to them under Section 67 cannot be used as substantive evidence against the accused.\n\nSUMMARY:\nThree-judge bench held that officers invested with powers under Section 53 of the NDPS Act are 'police officers' within Section 25 of the Evidence Act, rendering any confessional statement recorded under Section 67 inadmissible as substantive evidence.\n\nSTATUTES: Section 67 NDPS, Section 53 NDPS, Section 25 Evidence Act, Section 26 Evidence Act\nDISPOSITION: Conviction Based Solely on Section 67 Statements Quashed	2	f	SCIN010001522013	Criminal Appeal	2020-10-29	Three-Judge Bench	Tofan Singh	State of Tamil Nadu	Senior Advocate Sushil Kumar Jain	Additional Solicitor General Aman Lekhi	Narcotics Law & Law of Evidence	Confessions made to NCB or narcotics officers under Section 67 NDPS cannot be used as proof against an accused. They are treated like police statements and are barred by Section 25 of the Evidence Act.	0	(2021) 4 SCC 1	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Tofan Singh v. State of Tamil Nadu", "case_id": "33333333-0006-4000-8000-000000000006", "case_no": "Criminal Appeal No. 152 of 2013", "parties": "Tofan Singh v. State of Tamil Nadu", "category": "NDPS", "petitioner": "Tofan Singh", "respondent": "State of Tamil Nadu", "legal_domain": "Narcotics Law & Law of Evidence", "category_facts": {"category": "NDPS", "custody_period": null, "contraband_type": "Contraband / Narcotics", "seized_quantity": null, "statutory_issue": "Bail / Pre-trial Release under NDPS Act", "quantity_category": "Intermediate Quantity", "section_37_embargo": false, "search_compliance_sec_50": "Search procedure evaluated"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["narcotics_contraband"], "scenario_keywords": ["ndps"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
33333333-0007-4000-8000-000000000007	Criminal Appeal No. 370 of 1996	State of Punjab v. Baldev Singh & Others	Supreme Court of India	1999	Hon'ble A.S. Anand, C.J., S.B. Majmudar, Sujata V. Manohar, K. Venkataswami, V.N. Khare, JJ.	State of Punjab v. Baldev Singh & Others	["Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances Act, 1985"]	["Section 50 NDPS", "Section 42 NDPS"]	["Section 50 mandatory search", "gazetted officer or magistrate", "informing accused of legal right", "trial vitiation"]	Five-judge Constitution Bench held that informing the accused of their right to be searched before a Gazetted Officer or Magistrate under Section 50 NDPS is mandatory, and failure to comply vitiates the recovery and trial.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_1999_Crl_App_No_370_of_1996_33330007.pdf	ef1daf2ff714baa1ce0361c5ede30db9fa5a3a54b2daf23887142b2de77bd5f9	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:22:39.343249+05:30	2026-10-25 11:22:39.343256+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.712028+05:30	\N	[]	It is an imperative requirement of Section 50 that the suspect must be made aware of his legal right to be searched before a Gazetted Officer or a Magistrate. Non-compliance with this mandatory obligation renders the recovery suspect and vitiates conviction.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [1999) 6 SCC 172]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "1999) 6 SCC 172", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Constitution Bench Landmark)	Supreme Court of India\nCriminal Appeal No. 370 of 1996\nState of Punjab v. Baldev Singh\nBench: Hon'ble A.S. Anand, C.J., S.B. Majmudar, Sujata V. Manohar, K. Venkataswami, V.N. Khare, JJ.\nDate: 1999-07-21\nCitation: (1999) 6 SCC 172\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nIt is an imperative requirement of Section 50 that the suspect must be made aware of his legal right to be searched before a Gazetted Officer or a Magistrate. Non-compliance with this mandatory obligation renders the recovery suspect and vitiates conviction.\n\nSUMMARY:\nFive-judge Constitution Bench held that informing the accused of their right to be searched before a Gazetted Officer or Magistrate under Section 50 NDPS is mandatory, and failure to comply vitiates the recovery and trial.\n\nSTATUTES: Section 50 NDPS, Section 42 NDPS\nDISPOSITION: Acquittal for Non-Compliance with Section 50 Affirmed	2	f	SCIN010003701996	Criminal Appeal	1999-07-21	Constitution Bench (5 Judges)	State of Punjab	Baldev Singh & Others	Senior Advocate R.S. Sodhi	Senior Advocate U.R. Lalit	Narcotics Law & Search Safeguards	Before police search someone for drugs, they are legally required to inform the person of their right to be searched in front of a Magistrate or Gazetted Officer. Failing to do so invalidates the search.	0	(1999) 6 SCC 172	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "State of Punjab v. Baldev Singh", "case_id": "33333333-0007-4000-8000-000000000007", "case_no": "Criminal Appeal No. 370 of 1996", "parties": "State of Punjab v. Baldev Singh & Others", "category": "NDPS", "petitioner": "State of Punjab", "respondent": "Baldev Singh & Others", "legal_domain": "Narcotics Law & Search Safeguards", "category_facts": {"category": "NDPS", "custody_period": null, "contraband_type": "Contraband / Narcotics", "seized_quantity": null, "statutory_issue": "Bail / Pre-trial Release under NDPS Act", "quantity_category": "Intermediate Quantity", "section_37_embargo": false, "search_compliance_sec_50": "Section 50 mandatory search safeguards compliance examined"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["narcotics_contraband"], "scenario_keywords": ["ndps", "section 50 ndps"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Dismissed", "disposition": "Dismissed"}}
33333333-0008-4000-8000-000000000008	Criminal Appeal No. 175 of 2010	Hardeep Singh & Others v. State of Punjab & Others	Supreme Court of India	2014	Hon'ble B.S. Chauhan, F.M.I. Kalifulla, Chandramauli Kr. Prasad, T.S. Thakur, A.R. Dave, JJ.	Hardeep Singh & Others v. State of Punjab & Others	["Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973"]	["Section 319 CrPC", "Section 190 CrPC", "Section 209 CrPC"]	["Section 319 CrPC", "summoning additional accused", "test of strong probability", "examination-in-chief sufficient"]	Five-judge Constitution Bench settled the law on Section 319 CrPC, holding that courts have extraordinary power to summon any person not named in the chargesheet if evidence recorded in court shows strong probability of their complicity.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_2014_Crl_App_No_175_of_2010_33330008.pdf	ad5a5e0eb41cf085ad4e706f29a2802893315e1e85bef6663abb3535c4a97f53	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:22:39.380971+05:30	2026-10-25 11:22:39.380976+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.712031+05:30	\N	[]	Power under Section 319 CrPC is discretionary and extraordinary. The degree of satisfaction required is more than a prima facie case as at the stage of framing charges, but short of satisfaction beyond reasonable doubt.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2014) 3 SCC 92]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2014) 3 SCC 92", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Constitution Bench Landmark)	Supreme Court of India\nCriminal Appeal No. 175 of 2010\nHardeep Singh and Others v. State of Punjab and Others\nBench: Hon'ble B.S. Chauhan, F.M.I. Kalifulla, Chandramauli Kr. Prasad, T.S. Thakur, A.R. Dave, JJ.\nDate: 2014-01-10\nCitation: (2014) 3 SCC 92\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nPower under Section 319 CrPC is discretionary and extraordinary. The degree of satisfaction required is more than a prima facie case as at the stage of framing charges, but short of satisfaction beyond reasonable doubt.\n\nSUMMARY:\nFive-judge Constitution Bench settled the law on Section 319 CrPC, holding that courts have extraordinary power to summon any person not named in the chargesheet if evidence recorded in court shows strong probability of their complicity.\n\nSTATUTES: Section 319 CrPC, Section 190 CrPC, Section 209 CrPC\nDISPOSITION: Guidelines on Summoning Additional Accused under Section 319 CrPC Settled	2	f	SCIN010001752010	Criminal Appeal	2014-01-10	Constitution Bench (5 Judges)	Hardeep Singh & Others	State of Punjab & Others	Senior Advocate K.T.S. Tulsi	Senior Advocate P.S. Patwalia	Criminal Procedure & Trial Powers	During a criminal trial, if evidence in court points towards someone who was omitted by the police, the judge has full power to summon that person as an accused.	0	(2014) 3 SCC 92	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Hardeep Singh and Others v. State of Punjab and Others", "case_id": "33333333-0008-4000-8000-000000000008", "case_no": "Criminal Appeal No. 175 of 2010", "parties": "Hardeep Singh & Others v. State of Punjab & Others", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Hardeep Singh & Others", "respondent": "State of Punjab & Others", "legal_domain": "Criminal Procedure & Trial Powers", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Criminal Appeal Against Conviction", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CRPC", "section": "319", "canonical": "Section 319 CRPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CRPC", "section": "190", "canonical": "Section 190 CRPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CRPC", "section": "209", "canonical": "Section 209 CRPC", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "scenario_keywords": [], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Directions Issued", "disposition": "Directions Issued"}}
33333333-0009-4000-8000-000000000009	Special Leave Petition (Crl.) No. 2745 of 2002	Sunderbhai Ambalal Desai v. State of Gujarat	Supreme Court of India	2002	Hon'ble M.B. Shah & D.M. Dharmadhikari, JJ.	Sunderbhai Ambalal Desai v. State of Gujarat	["Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973"]	["Section 451 CrPC", "Section 457 CrPC"]	["release of seized vehicles", "muddamal custody", "interim custody of vehicles", "photographic inventory"]	Landmark directions mandating prompt release of seized vehicles, cash, and valuable property under Section 451 CrPC on bond rather than allowing them to decay in police station yards.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_2002_SLP_Crl_No_2745_of_2002_33330009.pdf	3dd7d0635d1b3b251c59e47e5392a71d9dc385144c2d612a8eb24d8ecf2bd286	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:22:39.413053+05:30	2026-10-25 11:22:39.413058+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.712791+05:30	\N	[]	Powers under Section 451 CrPC should be exercised expeditiously and judiciously. It is of no use to keep seized vehicles at police stations for a long period. Magistrates should pass appropriate orders for interim release taking adequate security and photographic records.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2002) 10 SCC 283]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2002) 10 SCC 283", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding Procedural Landmark)	Supreme Court of India\nSpecial Leave Petition (Crl.) No. 2745 of 2002\nSunderbhai Ambalal Desai v. State of Gujarat\nBench: Hon'ble M.B. Shah & D.M. Dharmadhikari, JJ.\nDate: 2002-10-01\nCitation: (2002) 10 SCC 283\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nPowers under Section 451 CrPC should be exercised expeditiously and judiciously. It is of no use to keep seized vehicles at police stations for a long period. Magistrates should pass appropriate orders for interim release taking adequate security and photographic records.\n\nSUMMARY:\nLandmark directions mandating prompt release of seized vehicles, cash, and valuable property under Section 451 CrPC on bond rather than allowing them to decay in police station yards.\n\nSTATUTES: Section 451 CrPC, Section 457 CrPC\nDISPOSITION: Comprehensive Disposal of Seized Property Guidelines Laid Down	2	f	SCIN010027452002	Special Leave Petition (Crl.)	2002-10-01	Division Bench (2 Judges)	Sunderbhai Ambalal Desai	State of Gujarat	Advocate Sushil Kumar Jain	Senior Advocate D.A. Dave	Criminal Procedure & Property Custody	Police cannot keep seized cars, motorcycles, or valuables rusting in police stations for years. Magistrates must release them on interim custody to the rightful owner within weeks.	0	(2002) 10 SCC 283	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Sunderbhai Ambalal Desai v. State of Gujarat", "case_id": "33333333-0009-4000-8000-000000000009", "case_no": "Special Leave Petition (Crl.) No. 2745 of 2002", "parties": "Sunderbhai Ambalal Desai v. State of Gujarat", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Sunderbhai Ambalal Desai", "respondent": "State of Gujarat", "legal_domain": "Criminal Procedure & Property Custody", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Writ Petition / Criminal Appeal", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CRPC", "section": "451", "canonical": "Section 451 CRPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CRPC", "section": "457", "canonical": "Section 457 CRPC", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "scenario_keywords": [], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Directions Issued", "disposition": "Directions Issued"}}
33333333-0010-4000-8000-000000000010	Writ Petition No. 7284 of 2021	S. Sushma & Another (Same-sex couple) v. Commissioner of Police & State of Tamil Nadu	Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)	2021	Hon'ble N. Anand Venkatesh, J.	S. Sushma & Another (Same-sex couple) v. Commissioner of Police & State of Tamil Nadu	["Constitution of India"]	["Article 21", "Article 14", "Article 226"]	["LGBTQIA+ police protection", "conversion therapy banned", "sensitization guidelines", "educational curricula reform"]	Pathbreaking judgment by Justice N. Anand Venkatesh issuing all-India guidelines to protect LGBTQIA+ persons from police harassment, banning conversion therapy, and mandating police and judicial sensitization.	Disposed	storage/cases/MHC_2021_WP_No_7284_of_2021_33330010.pdf	211f60a118b9abdd90b7203577e2b063787f810addfffd36e5784dd7cb36615b	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:22:39.439191+05:30	2026-10-25 11:22:39.439195+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.713163+05:30	\N	[]	The LGBTQIA+ community cannot be left in a vulnerable state where their fundamental rights under Article 21 are constantly threatened. Coercive action by police on missing complaints filed by parents of adult LGBTQIA+ individuals is illegal.	[]	Disposed	[]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding Human Rights Precedent)	Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)\nWrit Petition No. 7284 of 2021\nS. Sushma and Another v. Commissioner of Police, Greater Chennai and Others\nBench: Hon'ble N. Anand Venkatesh, J.\nDate: 2021-06-07\nCitation: 2021:MHC:2481\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nThe LGBTQIA+ community cannot be left in a vulnerable state where their fundamental rights under Article 21 are constantly threatened. Coercive action by police on missing complaints filed by parents of adult LGBTQIA+ individuals is illegal.\n\nSUMMARY:\nPathbreaking judgment by Justice N. Anand Venkatesh issuing all-India guidelines to protect LGBTQIA+ persons from police harassment, banning conversion therapy, and mandating police and judicial sensitization.\n\nSTATUTES: Article 21, Article 14, Article 226\nDISPOSITION: Comprehensive LGBTQIA+ Protection Guidelines Issued	2	f	TNCH010072842021	Writ Petition	2021-06-07	Single Judge	S. Sushma & Another (Same-sex couple)	Commissioner of Police & State of Tamil Nadu	Advocate S. Manuraj	Public Prosecutor Hasan Mohammed Jinnah	Human Rights & LGBTQIA+ Protection	Police cannot harass consensual adult same-sex couples or register runaway complaints against them. Medical professionals are banned from conducting conversion therapy.	0	2021:MHC:2481	{"court": "Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)", "title": "S. Sushma and Another v. Commissioner of Police, Greater Chennai and Others", "case_id": "33333333-0010-4000-8000-000000000010", "case_no": "Writ Petition No. 7284 of 2021", "parties": "S. Sushma & Another (Same-sex couple) v. Commissioner of Police & State of Tamil Nadu", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "S. Sushma & Another (Same-sex couple)", "respondent": "Commissioner of Police & State of Tamil Nadu", "legal_domain": "Human Rights & LGBTQIA+ Protection", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Writ Petition / Criminal Appeal", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "21", "canonical": "Section 21 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "14", "canonical": "Section 14 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "226", "canonical": "Section 226 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["constitutional_fundamental"], "scenario_keywords": ["article 226", "fundamental rights", "article 14", "article 21"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed with Ongoing Mandamus", "disposition": "Allowed with Ongoing Mandamus"}}
33333333-0011-4000-8000-000000000011	Civil Appeal No. 5802 of 2022	X (Unmarried Woman) v. Principal Secretary, Health & Family Welfare Department, GNCTD	Supreme Court of India	2023	Hon'ble D.Y. Chandrachud, A.S. Bopanna, J.B. Pardiwala, JJ.	X (Unmarried Woman) v. Principal Secretary, Health & Family Welfare Department, GNCTD	["MTP Act"]	["Section 3(2)(b) MTP Act"]	["unmarried women abortion rights", "reproductive autonomy", "bodily integrity", "Rule 3B purposive interpretation"]	Three-judge bench held that excluding unmarried or single women from terminating pregnancies between 20 and 24 weeks under Rule 3B is unconstitutional. Marital status cannot dictate reproductive rights.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_2023_CA_No_5802_of_2022_33330011.pdf	130df5acf25be7ff440c07c0b518140160353f3557ca3e4da453f1cb9eb97954	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:22:39.462215+05:30	2026-10-25 11:22:39.462219+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.713542+05:30	\N	[]	The artificial distinction between married and unmarried women under Rule 3B is discriminatory and violative of Article 14. Reproductive autonomy is a facet of personal liberty and bodily integrity under Article 21.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2023) 9 SCC 433]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2023) 9 SCC 433", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding 3-Judge Precedent)	Supreme Court of India\nCivil Appeal No. 5802 of 2022\nX v. Principal Secretary, Health and Family Welfare Department, Govt. of NCT of Delhi\nBench: Hon'ble D.Y. Chandrachud, A.S. Bopanna, J.B. Pardiwala, JJ.\nDate: 2022-09-29\nCitation: (2023) 9 SCC 433\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nThe artificial distinction between married and unmarried women under Rule 3B is discriminatory and violative of Article 14. Reproductive autonomy is a facet of personal liberty and bodily integrity under Article 21.\n\nSUMMARY:\nThree-judge bench held that excluding unmarried or single women from terminating pregnancies between 20 and 24 weeks under Rule 3B is unconstitutional. Marital status cannot dictate reproductive rights.\n\nSTATUTES: Section 3(2)(b) MTP Act, Rule 3B MTP Rules, Article 21, Article 14\nDISPOSITION: Rule 3B MTP Rules Interpreted Inclusively	2	f	SCIN010058022022	Civil Appeal	2022-09-29	Three-Judge Bench	X (Unmarried Woman)	Principal Secretary, Health & Family Welfare Department, GNCTD	Senior Advocate Dr. Colin Gonsalves	Additional Solicitor General Aishwarya Bhati	Reproductive Rights & Medical Law	Unmarried and single women have the exact same rights as married women to access safe and legal abortion up to 24 weeks of pregnancy under the MTP Act.	0	(2023) 9 SCC 433	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "X v. Principal Secretary, Health and Family Welfare Department, Govt. of NCT of Delhi", "case_id": "33333333-0011-4000-8000-000000000011", "case_no": "Civil Appeal No. 5802 of 2022", "parties": "X (Unmarried Woman) v. Principal Secretary, Health & Family Welfare Department, GNCTD", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "X (Unmarried Woman)", "respondent": "Principal Secretary, Health & Family Welfare Department, GNCTD", "legal_domain": "Reproductive Rights & Medical Law", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Writ Petition / Criminal Appeal", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "21", "canonical": "Section 21 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "14", "canonical": "Section 14 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["constitutional_fundamental"], "scenario_keywords": ["article 14", "article 21"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
33333333-0012-4000-8000-000000000012	Criminal Appeal No. 963 of 2010	Damodar S. Prabhu v. Sayed Babalal H.	Supreme Court of India	2010	Hon'ble K.G. Balakrishnan, C.J., P. Sathasivam, J.M. Panchal, JJ.	Damodar S. Prabhu v. Sayed Babalal H.	["Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973", "NI Act", "Negotiable Instruments Act", "Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881"]	["Section 138 NI Act", "Section 138 Negotiable Instruments Act", "Section 147 NI Act"]	["cheque bounce compounding", "graded cost schedule", "Section 147 NI Act", "preventing court clogging"]	Three-judge bench laid down graded cost guidelines for compounding offences under Section 138 NI Act: 10% costs if compounded at Sessions/High Court, 15% at Supreme Court, to encourage early settlement at trial stage.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_2010_Crl_App_No_963_of_2010_33330012.pdf	ca9dae2ece127118a697825aaef77eb54b9d220de80362cf8c43cc034df81791	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:22:39.484542+05:30	2026-10-25 11:22:39.484546+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.713915+05:30	\N	[]	Offences under Section 138 of the Negotiable Instruments Act are primarily of a civil nature clad in criminal disguise. Section 147 is an enabling provision providing for compounding without requiring leave of court, subject to graded deterrent costs.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2010) 5 SCC 663]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2010) 5 SCC 663", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding Commercial Precedent)	Supreme Court of India\nCriminal Appeal No. 963 of 2010\nDamodar S. Prabhu v. Sayed Babalal H.\nBench: Hon'ble K.G. Balakrishnan, C.J., P. Sathasivam, J.M. Panchal, JJ.\nDate: 2010-05-03\nCitation: (2010) 5 SCC 663\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nOffences under Section 138 of the Negotiable Instruments Act are primarily of a civil nature clad in criminal disguise. Section 147 is an enabling provision providing for compounding without requiring leave of court, subject to graded deterrent costs.\n\nSUMMARY:\nThree-judge bench laid down graded cost guidelines for compounding offences under Section 138 NI Act: 10% costs if compounded at Sessions/High Court, 15% at Supreme Court, to encourage early settlement at trial stage.\n\nSTATUTES: Section 138 NI Act, Section 147 NI Act, Section 320 CrPC\nDISPOSITION: Guidelines on Graded Costs for Compounding Prescribed	2	f	SCIN010009632010	Criminal Appeal	2010-05-03	Three-Judge Bench	Damodar S. Prabhu	Sayed Babalal H.	Advocate A. Venayagam Balan	Advocate S.K. Kulkarni	Negotiable Instruments & Commercial Offences	Cheque bounce cases are essentially commercial disputes that can be settled at any stage. However, settling later before appellate courts attracts graded financial costs to compensate for wasting court time.	0	(2010) 5 SCC 663	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Damodar S. Prabhu v. Sayed Babalal H.", "case_id": "33333333-0012-4000-8000-000000000012", "case_no": "Criminal Appeal No. 963 of 2010", "parties": "Damodar S. Prabhu v. Sayed Babalal H.", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Damodar S. Prabhu", "respondent": "Sayed Babalal H.", "legal_domain": "Negotiable Instruments & Commercial Offences", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Criminal Appeal Against Conviction", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CRPC", "section": "320", "canonical": "Section 320 CRPC", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["financial_commercial"], "scenario_keywords": ["cheque bounce", "section 138", "negotiable instruments"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed in Part", "disposition": "Allowed in Part"}}
33333333-0013-4000-8000-000000000013	Criminal Appeal No. 416 of 2018	Dr. Subhash Kashinath Mahajan v. State of Maharashtra & Another	Supreme Court of India	2018	Hon'ble A.K. Goel & U.U. Lalit, JJ.	Dr. Subhash Kashinath Mahajan v. State of Maharashtra & Another	["Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973"]	["Section 18 SC/ST Act", "Section 438 CrPC", "Article 21"]	["anticipatory bail under SC/ST Act", "preliminary inquiry", "arrest permission", "overruled by Union of India"]	Division bench introduced requirements of preliminary inquiry and senior police permission before arrest under SC/ST Act. Overruled by 3-Judge Bench in Union of India v. State of Maharashtra (2020) and nullified by Parliament's 2018 Amendment inserting Section 18A.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_2018_Crl_App_No_416_of_2018_33330013.pdf	1307d70700e262977b9f9175078466958f440da20e23017bd8ab679e4f397e2f	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:22:39.50983+05:30	2026-10-25 11:22:39.509834+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.714245+05:30	\N	[]	Directions mandating preliminary inquiry and prior permission before arrest under the SC/ST Act placed an impermissible judicial clog on the statute. [OVERRULED by 3-Judge Bench in Union of India v. State of Maharashtra (2020)].	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2018) 6 SCC 454]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2018) 6 SCC 454", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Overruled by Supreme Court (Union of India v. State of Maharashtra)	Supreme Court of India\nCriminal Appeal No. 416 of 2018\nDr. Subhash Kashinath Mahajan v. State of Maharashtra and Another\nBench: Hon'ble A.K. Goel & U.U. Lalit, JJ.\nDate: 2018-03-20\nCitation: (2018) 6 SCC 454\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nDirections mandating preliminary inquiry and prior permission before arrest under the SC/ST Act placed an impermissible judicial clog on the statute. [OVERRULED by 3-Judge Bench in Union of India v. State of Maharashtra (2020)].\n\nSUMMARY:\nDivision bench introduced requirements of preliminary inquiry and senior police permission before arrest under SC/ST Act. Overruled by 3-Judge Bench in Union of India v. State of Maharashtra (2020) and nullified by Parliament's 2018 Amendment inserting Section 18A.\n\nSTATUTES: Section 18 SC/ST Act, Section 438 CrPC, Article 21\nDISPOSITION: Guidelines Imposing Prior Permission Barred (Overruled in 2020)	2	f	SCIN010004162018	Criminal Appeal	2018-03-20	Division Bench (2 Judges)	Dr. Subhash Kashinath Mahajan	State of Maharashtra & Another	Senior Advocate Shekhar Naphade	Additional Solicitor General Maninder Singh	Criminal Law & Atrocities Prevention	This 2018 decision had restricted immediate arrest under the SC/ST Act, but it was subsequently overruled by a larger bench and Parliament passed an amendment reaffirming that no preliminary inquiry is required.	0	(2018) 6 SCC 454	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Dr. Subhash Kashinath Mahajan v. State of Maharashtra and Another", "case_id": "33333333-0013-4000-8000-000000000013", "case_no": "Criminal Appeal No. 416 of 2018", "parties": "Dr. Subhash Kashinath Mahajan v. State of Maharashtra & Another", "category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "petitioner": "Dr. Subhash Kashinath Mahajan", "respondent": "State of Maharashtra & Another", "legal_domain": "Criminal Law & Atrocities Prevention", "category_facts": {"category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "surety_count": null, "custody_period": null, "application_type": "Anticipatory Bail", "bail_bond_amount": null, "trial_court_fine": null, "reason_for_relief": null, "reporting_condition": null, "trial_court_sentence": null, "trial_court_fine_paid": false}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CRPC", "section": "438", "canonical": "Section 438 CRPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "21", "canonical": "Section 21 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["pre_trial_custody", "constitutional_fundamental", "caste_status"], "scenario_keywords": ["anticipatory bail", "article 21", "scheduled tribe", "scheduled caste"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed (Later Overruled)", "disposition": "Allowed (Later Overruled)"}}
05236cfb-68cb-487b-a900-be01cde71512	CRL OP No. 30956 of 2026	Navenkumar Kamalnadhan v. The State rep by the Inspector of Police W-26, All Women Police Station	Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)	2026	Hon'ble Mr. Justice N.Ramesh	Navenkumar Kamalnadhan v. The State rep by the Inspector of Police W-26, All Women Police Station	["Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023", "Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023", "of BNS", "of BNSS"]	["Section 483 of BNSS", "Section 69 of BNS"]	["civil & constitutional law", "mhc", "disposed"]	Judgment in NO. 30956 of 2026 delivered by Hon'ble Bench at Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai). The proceedings concern Civil & Constitutional Law with respect to procedural compliance.	Bail Granted	storage/cases/MHC_2026_NO_30956_of_2026_05236cfb.pdf	8c7fa6f175b5ac5629f7bf2d95efbc302cd32fbd5f3486f06eb46e5d3ab84a41	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 14:51:43.266188+05:30	2026-10-25 14:51:43.266196+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.290164+05:30	2. The case of the prosecution is that the petitioner on false promise to marry the defacto complainant had physical relationship with her and thereafter refused to marry her. Now the defacto complainant is 8 months pregnant. Hence, the case. 3.	[]	Regular bail granted in CRL OP No. 30956 of 2026 under applicable statutory provisions, considering the duration of incarceration and absence of flight risk, subject to executing bond and standard reporting conditions.	[]	Allowed	["ble Supreme Court in P.K.Shaji vs. State of Kerala [(2005)13 SCC 283]"]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding Precedent)	CRL OP No. 30956 of 2026\nIN THE HIGH COURT OF JUDICATURE AT MADRAS\nDATED: 23-10-2026\nCORAM\nTHE HON'BLE MR.JUSTICE N.RAMESH\nCRL OP No. 30956 of 2026\nCNR: {HCMA012562302026}\nNavenkumar Kamalnadhan\nS/o Kamalnadhan,\n367, Pillaiar Kovil,\nGangavaram,\nTiruvannamalai,\nTamilnadu.\n..Petitioner(s)\nVs\nThe State rep by the Inspector of Police\nW-26, All Women Police Station,\nAshok Nagar,\nChennai.\ncrime no.24 of 2026.\n..Respondent(s)\nPrayer: This petition is filed under section 483 of BNSS to enlarge\nthe petitioner on Bail in Crime no.24 of 2026 on the file of the Inspector of\nPolice, W-26, All Women Police Station, Ashok Nagar, chennai and pass any\nsuch other order or orders.\nFor Petitioner(s): Mr. P. Paul Prathap\nFor Respondent(s): Mr. M.M.I.KHALEEL GOVERNMENT\nADVOCATE (CRL.SIDE)\nORDER\nThe Petitioner who was arrested and remanded to judicial custody on\n17.09.2026 for the alleged offence under Section 69 of BNS in Crime No.24 of\n2026 on the file of the respondent police, seeks bail.\n__________\nPage1 of 6\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\nCRL OP No. 30956 of 2026\n2. The case of the prosecution is that the petitioner on false promise to\nmarry the defacto complainant had physical relationship with her and thereafter\nrefused to marry her. Now the defacto complainant is 8 months pregnant.\nHence, the case.\n3. The learned counsel for the petitioner submitted that the petitioner is a\ninnocent and he has been falsely implicated in this case. He further submitted\nthat the petitioner is in judicial custody since 17.09.2026. He also submits that\nif the DNA test report of the child turns that the petitioner is the father of the\nchild, he is ready to marry the victim girl and he is ready to file an affidavit to\nthat effect before the Court below. Hence, he prays for grant of bail to the\nPetitioners.\n4. Per contra, the learned Government Advocate (Criminal Side)\nappearing for the Respondent Police reiterated the prosecution case and\nsubmitted that the petitioner, had physical relationship with the victim and\nthereafter refused to marry her. Now the victim girl is 8 months pregnant.\nHence, he opposed the grant of bail to the petitioner.\n5. I have given my anxious consideration to either side submissions and\nperused the materials available on record.\n__________\nPage2 of 6\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\nCRL OP No. 30956 of 2026\n6. Considering the nature and gravity of the allegations and the petitioner\nis ready to file an affidavit before the Court below that if the DNA test report of\nthe child turns that the petitioner is the father of the child, he is ready to marry\nthe victim girl and the period of incarceration undergone by the petitioner, this\nCourt is inclined to grant bail to the petitioners, subject to certain conditions.\n7. Accordingly, the petitioners are ordered to be released on bail on their\nexecuting a bond for a sum of Rs.25,000/- each (Rupees Twenty Five\nThousand only) with two sureties each, for a like sum to the satisfaction of the\nlearned XXIII Magistrate Court, Saidapet and on further conditions that:\n[a] the sureties shall affix their photographs and Left\nThumb Impression in the Application for Surety ship\n[Judicial Form No.46 annexed to 'The Criminal Rules of\nPractice, 2019']. The learned Magistrate shall obtain a copy\nof any one of the identity proofs to ensure their identity;\n[b]the petitioner shall file an affidavit before the\njurisdictional Magistrate undertaking that if the DNA\ntest report of the child turns that she is the father of the\nchild, he is ready to marry the victim girl\n[c] the petitioners shall report before the respondent\npolice daily at 10.30 a.m., until further orders.\n[d] the petitioners shall not abscond either during\n__________\nPage3 of 6\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\nCRL OP No. 30956 of 2026\ninvestigation or trial;\n[e] the petitioners shall not tamper with the evidence or\nwitness either during investigation or trial;\n[f] On breach of any of the aforementioned conditions, the\nlearned Magistrate/Trial Court is entitled to pass appropriate\norders against the petitioners in acc with law as if the\naforementioned conditions have been imposed and the\npetitioners released on bail by the learned Magistrate/Trial\nCourt himself as laid down by the Hon'ble Supreme Court in\nP.K.Shaji vs. State of Kerala [(2005)13 SCC 283];\n[g] If the accused thereafter absconds, a fresh FIR can be\nregistered under Section 269 of B.N.S.\n23-10-2026\nsmn\nNote:\n1. Registry is directed to forthwith upload\nthis order in the Official Website of this\nCourt.\n2. All concerned to act on this order being\nuploaded in Official Website of this Court\nwithout insisting on certified hard copies. To\nbe noted, this order when uploaded in the\nofficial website of this Court will be\nwatermarked and will also have a QR code.\n__________\nPage4 of 6\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\nCRL OP No. 30956 of 2026\nTo.\n1. The XXIII Magistrate Court, Saidapet\n2.The Inspector of Police\nW-26, All Women Police Station,Ashok Nagar,Chennai.\n3. The Superintendent Central Prison, Puzhal II, Chennai\n4. The Public Prosecutor, High Court, Madras\n__________\nPage5 of 6\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\nCRL OP No. 30956 of 2026\nN.RAMESH, J.\nSMN\nCRL OP No. 30956 of 2026\n23-10-2026\n__________\nPage6 of 6\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis	6	t	HCMA012562302026	Criminal Original Petition (Bail)	2026-10-23	Single Bench	Navenkumar Kamalnadhan	The State rep by the Inspector of Police W-26, All Women Police Station	Mr. P. Paul Prathap	Mr. M.M.I.KHALEEL GOVERNMENT ADVOCATE (CRL.SIDE)	Civil & Constitutional Law	In this matter, the Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai) adjudicated issues between Tamilnadu and The State rep by the Inspector of Police W-26, All Women Police Station. The Court rendered a decision disposing of the application under statutory provisions.	0	2026 LexGuard (MHC) 05236CFB	{"court": "Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)", "title": "Tamilnadu v. The State rep by the Inspector of Police W-26, All Women Police Station", "case_id": "05236cfb-68cb-487b-a900-be01cde71512", "case_no": "NO. 30956 of 2026", "parties": "Tamilnadu v. The State rep by the Inspector of Police W-26, All Women Police Station", "category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "petitioner": "Tamilnadu", "respondent": "The State rep by the Inspector of Police W-26, All Women Police Station", "legal_domain": "Civil & Constitutional Law", "category_facts": {"category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "surety_count": 2, "custody_period": null, "application_type": "Regular Bail", "bail_bond_amount": 25000.0, "trial_court_fine": null, "reason_for_relief": null, "reporting_condition": "report before the respondent", "trial_court_sentence": null, "trial_court_fine_paid": false}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "scenario_keywords": [], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Disposed", "disposition": "Disposed"}}
33333333-0014-4000-8000-000000000014	Review Petition (Crl.) No. 228 of 2018	Union of India v. State of Maharashtra & Others	Supreme Court of India	2020	Hon'ble Arun Mishra, M.R. Shah, B.R. Gavai, JJ.	Union of India v. State of Maharashtra & Others	["Scheduled Castes and the Scheduled Tribes (Prevention of Atrocities) Act, 1989"]	["Section 18", "Section 18A", "Article 15", "Article 21"]	["overruling Subhash Mahajan", "no prior sanction required", "caste discrimination reality", "affirmative penal protection"]	Three-judge bench recalled and overruled Subhash Mahajan (2018), confirming that Section 18 of the SC/ST Act bars anticipatory bail and that adding judicial filters like preliminary inquiry weakens protective legislation.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_2020_Review_Pet_No_228_of_2018_33330014.pdf	dc3fb92d69340b35e82f66447432ab98260b7b2e5160348ffc73361a41d6c2f8	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:22:39.537675+05:30	2026-10-25 11:22:39.537684+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.71458+05:30	\N	[]	The directions issued in Subhash Mahajan ordering preliminary inquiry and prior permission before arrest placed an unreasonable burden on underprivileged victims. Section 18 bar is constitutional.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2020) 4 SCC 761]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2020) 4 SCC 761", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding 3-Judge Precedent)	Supreme Court of India\nReview Petition (Crl.) No. 228 of 2018\nUnion of India v. State of Maharashtra and Others\nBench: Hon'ble Arun Mishra, M.R. Shah, B.R. Gavai, JJ.\nDate: 2019-10-01\nCitation: (2020) 4 SCC 761\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nThe directions issued in Subhash Mahajan ordering preliminary inquiry and prior permission before arrest placed an unreasonable burden on underprivileged victims. Section 18 bar is constitutional.\n\nSUMMARY:\nThree-judge bench recalled and overruled Subhash Mahajan (2018), confirming that Section 18 of the SC/ST Act bars anticipatory bail and that adding judicial filters like preliminary inquiry weakens protective legislation.\n\nSTATUTES: Section 18, Section 18A, Article 15, Article 21\nDISPOSITION: Subhash Mahajan Directions Recalled and Overruled	2	f	SCIN010002282018	Review Petition (Crl.)	2019-10-01	Three-Judge Bench	Union of India	State of Maharashtra & Others	Attorney General K.K. Venugopal	Senior Advocate Shekhar Naphade	Criminal Law & Caste Equality	The Supreme Court recalled its earlier decision, confirming that the bar on anticipatory bail under the SC/ST Act is valid and police do not need prior senior approval to act against caste atrocities.	0	(2020) 4 SCC 761	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Union of India v. State of Maharashtra and Others", "case_id": "33333333-0014-4000-8000-000000000014", "case_no": "Review Petition (Crl.) No. 228 of 2018", "parties": "Union of India v. State of Maharashtra & Others", "category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "petitioner": "Union of India", "respondent": "State of Maharashtra & Others", "legal_domain": "Criminal Law & Caste Equality", "category_facts": {"category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "surety_count": null, "custody_period": null, "application_type": "Anticipatory Bail", "bail_bond_amount": null, "trial_court_fine": null, "reason_for_relief": null, "reporting_condition": null, "trial_court_sentence": null, "trial_court_fine_paid": false}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "15", "canonical": "Section 15 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "21", "canonical": "Section 21 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["pre_trial_custody", "constitutional_fundamental", "caste_status"], "scenario_keywords": ["anticipatory bail", "article 21", "scheduled tribe", "scheduled caste"], "procedural_postur