11111111-0003-4000-8000-000000000003	Criminal Appeal No. 1277 of 2014	Arnesh Kumar v. State of Bihar & Another	Supreme Court of India	2014	Hon'ble Chandramauli Kr. Prasad & Pinaki Chandra Ghose, JJ.	Arnesh Kumar v. State of Bihar & Another	["A CrPC", "A IPC", "Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973", "Dowry Prohibition Act", "Indian Penal Code, 1860"]	["Section 4 Dowry Prohibition Act", "Section 41 A CrPC", "Section 498 A IPC"]	["arrest checklist", "Section 498A IPC", "harassment", "magistrate remand scrutiny", "preventing routine arrest"]	Mandatory safeguards restraining routine and mechanical arrests under Section 498-A IPC and offences punishable with imprisonment up to 7 years without satisfying Section 41 CrPC checklist.	Allowed	storage/cases/SC_2014_Crl_A_No_1277_of_2014_11110003.pdf	10e23efbe4db323804f4330c1a4475e5a35613f0c62259042d446ad7be158d5d	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	\N	\N	2026-10-25 00:13:23.914826+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.303575+05:30	\N	[]	Police officers shall not automatically arrest the accused when a case under Section 498-A IPC or offences punishable with imprisonment for a term which may be less than seven years is registered. Police must satisfy themselves about the necessity of arrest based on the parameters in Section 41 CrPC, furnish a checklist, and magistrates must record satisfaction before authorizing detention.	[]	Allowed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2014) 8 SCC 273]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2014) 8 SCC 273", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding Precedent)	IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA\nCRIMINAL LAW & ARREST SAFEGUARDS JURISDICTION\nCriminal Appeal No. 1277 of 2014\nCNR: SCIN010012772014\nCITATION: (2014) 8 SCC 273\n\nBEFORE:\nHon'ble Chandramauli Kr. Prasad & Pinaki Chandra Ghose, JJ.\n\nArnesh Kumar v. State of Bihar & Another\n\nPETITIONER: Arnesh Kumar\nRESPONDENT: State of Bihar & Another\nCOUNSEL FOR PETITIONER: Advocate Kumar Shivam\nCOUNSEL FOR RESPONDENT: Advocate Gopal Singh\n\nJUDGMENT DATE: 02-07-2014\n\nSUMMARY & HEADNOTE:\nMandatory safeguards restraining routine and mechanical arrests under Section 498-A IPC and offences punishable with imprisonment up to 7 years without satisfying Section 41 CrPC checklist.\n\nPLAIN-LANGUAGE EXPLANATION:\nPolice cannot arrest individuals automatically upon filing of a matrimonial dispute or complaint for offences carrying less than 7 years. They must serve a Section 41A notice to appear first.\n\nSTATUTES AND PROVISIONS CONSIDERED:\nCode of Criminal Procedure, 1973, Indian Penal Code, 1860\nSection 41 CrPC, Section 41A CrPC, Section 498A IPC, Section 4 Dowry Prohibition Act\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nPolice officers shall not automatically arrest the accused when a case under Section 498-A IPC or offences punishable with imprisonment for a term which may be less than seven years is registered. Police must satisfy themselves about the necessity of arrest based on the parameters in Section 41 CrPC, furnish a checklist, and magistrates must record satisfaction before authorizing detention.\n\nFINAL ORDER & DISPOSITION:\nArrest Guidelines Enacted Nationwide. The petition is Allowed.\nPrecedential Status: Good Law (Binding Precedent).	1	f	SCIN010012772014	Criminal Appeal	2014-07-02	Division Bench (2 Judges)	Arnesh Kumar	State of Bihar & Another	Advocate Kumar Shivam COUNSEL FOR RESPONDENT: Advocate Gopal Singh	Advocate Gopal Singh	Criminal Law & Arrest Safeguards	Police cannot arrest individuals automatically upon filing of a matrimonial dispute or complaint for offences carrying less than 7 years. They must serve a Section 41A notice to appear first.	3	(2014) 8 SCC 273	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Arnesh Kumar v. State of Bihar & Another", "case_id": "11111111-0003-4000-8000-000000000003", "case_no": "Criminal Appeal No. 1277 of 2014", "parties": "Arnesh Kumar v. State of Bihar & Another", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Arnesh Kumar", "respondent": "State of Bihar & Another", "legal_domain": "Criminal Law & Arrest Safeguards", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Criminal Appeal Against Conviction", "judicial_disposition": "Allowed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CRPC", "section": "41", "canonical": "Section 41 CRPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CRPC", "section": "41A", "canonical": "Section 41A CRPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "IPC", "section": "498A", "canonical": "Section 498A IPC", "equivalents": ["Section 85 BNS"]}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["pre_trial_custody"], "scenario_keywords": ["remand"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
