 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05]	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	\N	\N	2026-10-24 20:34:01.047514+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.720324+05:30	2. The case of the prosecution is that the defacto complainant is a resident of Bangalore and she is holding State level post in Bharatiya Janata Party, that the petitioner/first accused was one of the General Secretary of the Bharatiya Janata Party in Tamil Nadu, that the petitioner became acquainted with the defacto complainant through the second 2/18 https://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis CRL OP(MD). No.22636 of 2026 accused in July 2024, that due to the family dispute between the defacto complainant and her husband, divorce proceeding is pending in a Court at Bangalore, that the petitioner assuring to marry her, settled the defacto complainant and her two children at Srirangam and started to live with them, that the petitioner persuaded the defacto complainant to sell her house situated in Bangalore and due to the pressure exerted, she executed a sale deed on 14.01.2025 in favour of one Hariharan, but the entire sale consideration amounting to approximately Rs.4Crores was swindled by the petitioner, that the petitioner started to abuse the complainant and compelled her to have sexual relationship with other politicians in New Delhi and Chennai, that since the defacto complainant quest	[]	Principles governing criminal jurisdiction and interim protections.	[]	Dismissed	["in Martanda Singh Vs. State of Chhattisgarh and another reported in 2026 SCC Online SC 2058 and"]	\N	unverified	Good Law	CRL OP(MD). No.22636 of 2026\nBEFORE THE MADURAI BENCH OF MADRAS HIGH COURT\n( Criminal Jurisdiction )\nRESERVED ON:09.10.2026\nPRONOUNCED ON: 16.10.2026\nPRESENT\nThe Hon`ble Mr.Justice K.MURALI SHANKAR\nCRL OP(MD). No.22636 of 2026\nM.Muruganantham @ Karuppu ... Petitioner/\nAccused No.1\nVs\nThe State of Tamilnadu,\nrepresented by\nThe Inspector of Police,\nAll Women Police Station,\nSri Rangam,\nTrichy City.\n(Crime No.32 of 2026) ...Respondent/Complainant\nFor Petitioner : Mr.R.Gandhi\nSenior Counsel\nfor Mr.R.Venkatesan\nFor Respondent : Mr.John Sathyan\nState Public Prosecutor\nassisted by\nMr.T.Lenin Kumar\nAdditional Public Prosecutor\nFor Intervenor : Mr.J.Jeyakumaran\nAdvocate\n1/18\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). No.22636 of 2026\nPETITION FOR ANTICIPATORY BAIL Under Section 482\nof B.N.S.S.\nPRAYER :-\nFor Anticipatory Bail in Crime No. 32 of 2026 on\nthe file of the Respondent police.\nORDER : The Court made the following order :-\nThe petitioner/accused No.1, who apprehends\narrest at the hands of the respondent police for the\noffences punishable under Sections 69, 77, 318,\n351(3) of BNS and 66E and 67 of I.T., Act Crime No.32\nof 2026 on the file of the respondent police, seeks\nanticipatory bail.\n2. The case of the prosecution is that the\ndefacto complainant is a resident of Bangalore and\nshe is holding State level post in Bharatiya Janata\nParty, that the petitioner/first accused was one of\nthe General Secretary of the Bharatiya Janata Party\nin Tamil Nadu, that the petitioner became acquainted\nwith the defacto complainant through the second\n2/18\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). No.22636 of 2026\naccused in July 2024, that due to the family dispute\nbetween the defacto complainant and her husband,\ndivorce proceeding is pending in a Court at\nBangalore, that the petitioner assuring to marry her,\nsettled the defacto complainant and her two children\nat Srirangam and started to live with them, that the\npetitioner persuaded the defacto complainant to sell\nher house situated in Bangalore and due to the\npressure exerted, she executed a sale deed on\n14.01.2025 in favour of one Hariharan, but the entire\nsale consideration amounting to approximately\nRs.4Crores was swindled by the petitioner, that the\npetitioner started to abuse the complainant and\ncompelled her to have sexual relationship with other\npoliticians in New Delhi and Chennai, that since the\ndefacto complainant questioned about the sale\nconsideration and transfer of the said property to\nanother person, he started to harass the defacto\ncomplainant, that the defacto complainant was\nconstrained to lodge a complaint before the party\nhead in New Delhi and after enquiry, the petitioner\nwas removed from the post of the General Secretary,\n3/18\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). No.22636 of 2026\nthat the petitioner in order to take revenge, in\ncollusion with the other accused, have uploaded the\nnude photos and videos of the defacto complainant in\nthe Porn websites, that the defacto complainant,\nafter coming to know about the same and while\nchecking it, she came to know that the petitioner had\ntaken secret videos of the defacto complainant at the\ntime of bathng and in other situation, that the\naccused including the petitioner have created AI\ngenerated nude videos and they have also morphed the\nface of the defaco complainant along with the other\npolitical leaders in Tamil Nadu and uploaded the\nsame, that the petitioner subjected the defacto\ncomplainant to threats and intimidations continuously\nand that therefore, she was forced to lodge the above\ncomplaint.\n3. The case of the petitioner/first accused is\nthat he is innocent and he has not committed any\noffence as alleged in the F.I.R., that the petitioner\nhas played a vital role in the BJP party and he has\nbeen doing so many welfare measures to the people\n4/18\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). No.22636 of 2026\nthrough his party, that the defacto complainant was\nintroduced by the second accused in 2024 stating that\nher husband had committed cruelties and she has been\nabandoned by her husband along with her children and\nher divorce petition is pending, that the defacto\ncomplainant wanted to join the political party for\nthe strong support and safety for herself and her\nchildren, that the defacto complainant demanded money\nas a hand loan from the petitioner for the education\npurposes of her children and the petitioner had also\ngiven huge amount through bank transfers to the\ndefacto complainant's bank account and her relatives,\nthat the petitioner took her to the political\nmeetings held anywhere by spending expenses, that in\nthe meanwhile, the petitioner came to know that the\ndefacto complainant has been cheating him for\nextorting money and she had developed relationship\nwith some other persons and subsequently there arose\ndifference of opinion between them, that the\ncomplainant started to make threats, that if the\ndefacto complainant is not made out as MLA or MP, she\nwill infringe the reputation of the petitioner among\n5/18\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). No.22636 of 2026\nthe public, that the defacto complainant suppresing\nthe material facts, lodged a complaint with false and\nuntenable allegations and that therefore, he may be\ngranted anticipatory bail.\n4. The respondent police filed a counter\naffidavit raising serious objections for grant of\nanticipatory bail.\n5. The learned Senior Counsel appearing for the\npetitioner would submit that the entire prosecution\ncase is false, that the relationship between the\npetitioner and the defacto complainant was consensual\nand there was never any false promise of marriage,\nthat the defacto complainant is admittedly a married\nwoman and her divorce proceedings are pending between\nher and her husband before the Bangalore Court and\nthat the allegation of sexual intercourse on a false\npromise of marriage is legally unsustainable.\n6. The learned Senior Counsel would further\ncontend that the petitioner has produced his bank\n6/18\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). No.22636 of 2026\nstatements to show that he had transferred the\namounts approximately to the tune of Rs.59,00,000/-\nto the bank account of the defacto complainant and\nher relatives, that the above transactions would go\nto show the financial dealings between the parties\nand falsify the allegation of the defacto complainant\nthat the petitioner had dishonestly misappropriated\nthe sale consideration belonging to the defacto\ncomplainant, that the petitioner has not created,\nuploaded or circulated any nude photographs, videos\nor morphed images of the complainant and that there\nis no material connecting the petitioner with the\nalleged circulation of the photographs and videos.\n7. The learned Senior Counsel, in support of his\nsubmission, placed reliance upon the judgments of the\nHon'ble Supreme Court in Rajnish Singh @ Soni Vs.\nState of U.P., and another reported in 2025 LiveLaw\n(SC) 279; in Martanda Singh Vs. State of Chhattisgarh\nand another reported in 2026 SCC Online SC 2058 and\nin Pramod Kumar Navratna Vs. State of Chhattisgarh\n7/18\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). No.22636 of 2026\nand others reported in 2026 LiveLaw (SC) 118 and\ncontended that the consensual relationship between\nthe two adult persons cannot be converted into a\ncriminal offence, merely because the relationship has\nfailed or promise of marriage has not been fulfilled.\n8. The learned Counsel for the\nintervenor/defacto complainant would submit that the\npetitioner, taking advantage of the confidence\nreposed in him, exploited her physically and\nfinancially, that the divorce proceedings pending\nbefore the Bangalore Court were themselves initiated\nwith the assistance of the petitioner, that the\npetitioner alone had induced the complainant to\ndispose of her immovable property at Bangalore and he\nhad taken away the entire sale consideration, that\nthe bank statement produced by the petitioner do not\nshow that the entire sale consideration was paid to\nthe defacto complainant, that the subsequent\ncirculation of nude photographs, videos, morphed\nimages has caused serious distress and humiliation to\n8/18\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). No.22636 of 2026\nthe defacto complainant and that the persons\nresponsible for creating, uploading and sending such\nmaterials have to be identified.\n9. The learned State Public Prosecutor would\nreiterate the submissions of the learned Counsel for\nthe defacto complainant and would further contend\nthat the allegations against the petitioner are grave\nand serious, that the present case involves not only\na dispute arising out a personal relationship, but\nalso the allegations of financial exploitation,\ncriminal intimidation and creation and circulation of\nobjectionable photographs and videos of the defacto\ncomplainant, that the electronic devices, digital\nrecords and financial transactions and the role\nplayed by the persons involved in creating and\nuploading the objectionable photographs and videos\nhave to be investigated and that granting of\nanticipatory bail at this stage would hamper the\nongoing investigation.\n9/18\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). No.22636 of 2026\n10. No doubt, the petitioner has taken a\nspecific stand that the relationship between him and\nthe defacto complainant was consensual and that he\nhad never made any promise of marriage, but the said\ndefence cannot, by itself, be considered as an answer\nto the said allegations levelled against him.\nWhether any promise of marriage was made, whether the\nsaid promise was false from its inception and whether\nthe defacto complainant consented to the physical\nrelationship on the basis of such promise, are\nmatters required to be investigated in the light of\nthe materials collected during investigation.\n11. In Rajnish Singh @ Soni's case referred\nsupra, the Hon'ble Supreme Court considered the\ncircumstances relating to a prolonged consensual\nrelationship for a period of 16 years, her conduct\nin representing herself as the wife of the accused\nand the absence of sufficient material showing\ndeception from the inception and quashed the criminal\nproceedings. In Martanda Singh's case referred above\nalso, the Hon'ble Supreme Court dealt with the\n10/18\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). No.22636 of 2026\nallegatins arising out of the consensual relationship\nextending over some years. In Pramod Kumar\nNavratna's case, the Hon'ble Supreme Court\nspecifically considered the effect of subsistence of\nthe complainant's marriage and the pendency of the\ndivorce proceedings, while considering the allegation\nof the sexual intercourse on a false promise of\nmarriage.\n12. There can be no dispute with regard to the\nlegal dictum laid down in the above decisions. The\nHon'ble Supreme Court has reiterated the distinction\nbetween a false promise of marriage made without any\nintention of fulfilling it from the very inception,\nand a genuine promise of marriage which could not be\nsubsequently fulfilled. In the case on hand, no\ndoubt, as already pointed out, the marriage between\nthe defacto complainant and her husband is\nsubsisting, as the divorce proceedings are pending\nbefore the competent Court at Bangalore. As rightly\ncontended by the learned Counsel for the defacto\ncomplainant, the present petition is one seeking\n11/18\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). No.22636 of 2026\nanticipatory bail and not for quashing of the\ncriminal proceedings.\n13. The learned Senior Counsel for the\npetitioner would further contend that, except the\noffence under Section 69 BNS, the remaining offences\nalleged against the petitioner are punishable with\nimprisonment not exceeding 7 years and that in view\nof the principles laid down by the Hon'ble Supreme\nCourt governing arrest in such cases, the petitioner\nis entitled to anticipatory bail.\n14. No doubt, in respect of offences punishable\nwith imprisonment not exceeding 7 years, the power of\narrest is regulated by Section 35 of BNSS. The\nHon'ble Supreme Court in Satender Kumar Antil Vs.\nCentral Bureau of Investigation and another, has\nreiterated the legal position that issuance of notice\nis the rule and the arrest is an exception in such\ncases. But at the same time, the said principle does\nnot confer any automatic or indefeasible right to\nanticipatory bail merely on the basis of the quantum\n12/18\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). No.22636 of 2026\nof punishment prescribed for the alleged offences.\nThe petitioner has disputed the applicability of\nSection 69 BNS by placing upon the decisions referred\nsupra. But at the same time, the question whether\nthe ingredients of Section 69 BNS are made out,\ncannot be decided in the present proceedings.\n15. As rightly contended by the Special Public\nProsecutor, the other allegations concerning the\ncriminal intimidation and the creation and the\ncirculation of nude photographs and videos and\nmorphed images require independent investigation. No\ndoubt, the mere fact that the punishment prescribed\nfor the other alleged offences does not exceed 7\nyears, would not by itself entitle the petitioner to\nget anticipatory bail. The Investigating Agency is\nduty bound to comply with the statutory safeguards\ngoverning arrest and cannot effect an arrest merely\nbecause the allegation are serious and that the\ninvestigation is pending.\n13/18\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). No.22636 of 2026\n16. As already pointed out, the petitioner has\nproduced bank statements showing that the had\ntransferred the amounts to the tune of Rs.59,00,000/-\nto the defacto complainant, but at the same time, the\ndefacto complainant has specifically alleged that the\npetitioner has swindled the entire sale consideration\nto the tune of Rs.4Crores. Considering the above,\nthe purpose of the transfers relied upon by the\npetitioner, the manner in which the sale\nconsideration was received and utilised and the\nnature of financial dealings between the parties are\nin dispute, requiring the examination of relevant\nrecords.\n17. As rightly contended by the learned Counsel\nfor the defacto complainant, the allegations\nregarding the creation and circulation of nude\nphotographs and videos and morphed images, if proved,\nwould involve a serious invasion of the privacy,\ndignity and reputation of the defacto complainant and\nas such, the Investigating Officer is required to\nascertain the origin of the objectionable materials,\n14/18\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). No.22636 of 2026\nelectronic devices and the accounts used for its\ncreation and circulation, the persons responsible for\nuploading and forwarding it and the role if any\nplayed by the petitioner and the other accused. No\ndoubt, as rightly pointed out by the learned Senior\nCounsel for the petitioner, the mere circulation of\nobjectionable material by itself would not show the\ninvolvement of the petitioner, but his alleged\nparticipation has to be decided on the basis of the\nmaterials collected during the investigation.\n18. It is pertinent to note that this Court\nwhile reserving the above petion for orders, directed\nthe respondent police to take immediate and necessary\nsteps to ensure the removal of nude photographs,\nvideos and morphed images of the defacto complainant\nfrom the websites and the said direction was issued,\ntaking note of the submission of the learned Counsel\nfor the defacto complainant that the same are still\navailable in certain websites and online platforms.\n15/18\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). No.22636 of 2026\n19. As rightly contended by the learned Special\nPublic Prosecutor, the investigation involves several\naspects, including the identification of the persons\nresponsible for creating, uploading and circulating\nthe objectionable photographs and videos, devices\nused for such purpose, the communications between the\naccused and the financial transactons alleged in the\ncomplaint. The political positon allegedly held by\nthe petitioner, the relationship between the parties\nand the financial transactions claimed by the\npetitioner are not by themselves sufficient enough to\ngrant the petitioner, the relief claimed.\n20. Considering the above facts and\ncircumstances, the nature and gravity of the\nallegations, the requirement of tracing the sources\nof objectionable electronic materials, the\napprehension of the defacto complainant with regard\nto the interference with the ongoing investigation\nand the criminal intimidation by the petitioner and\nmore importantly, the investigation is at crucial\nstage, this Court is of the considered view that the\n16/18\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). No.22636 of 2026\npetitioner has not made out a case for the grant of\nanticipatory bail at this stage.\n21. In the result, the Criminal Original\nPetition is dismissed. The respondent police shall\nproceed with the investigation in accordance with law\nand also take all necessary steps to comply with the\ndirections already issued by this Court regarding the\nremoval of objectionable photographs and videos from\nthe website and online platforms concerned.\n(K M S J)\n16.10.2026\nSSL\nTO\n1. The The Inspector of Police,\nAll Women Police Station,\nSri Rangam,\nTrichy City.\n2. The Additional Public Prosecutor,\nMadurai Bench of Madras High Court,\nMadurai.\n17/18\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). No.22636 of 2026\nK.MURALI SHANKAR,J\nSSL\nPRE-DELIVERY ORDER MADE IN\nCRL OP(MD). No.22636 of 2026\n16.10.2026\n18/18\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis	18	t	HCMD010226362026	Criminal Original Petition (Bail)	2026-10-16	Single Bench	M.Muruganantham @ Karuppu	State of Tamil Nadu Rep. by Inspector of Police, Sri Rangam	Mr.R.Gandhi	Mr.John Sathyan State Public Prosecutor	Civil Law	In this criminal original petition under Criminal Law, the Court ruled on the dispute between . No.22636 of 2026 M.Muruganantham @ Karuppu and ..	0	2026 LexGuard (Crim) 39B36F	{"court": "Madras High Court (Madurai Bench)", "title": "M.Muruganantham @ Karuppu v. State of Tamil Nadu Rep. by Inspector of Police, Sri Rangam", "case_id": "39b36feb-65c6-491c-aa64-84cd0954758c", "case_no": "CRL OP(MD). No.22636 of 2026", "parties": "M.Muruganantham @ Karuppu v. State of Tamil Nadu Rep. by Inspector of Police, Sri Rangam", "category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "petitioner": "M.Muruganantham @ Karuppu", "respondent": "State of Tamil Nadu Rep. by Inspector of Police, Sri Rangam", "legal_domain": "Civil Law", "category_facts": {"category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "surety_count": null, "custody_period": null, "application_type": "Anticipatory Bail", "bail_bond_amount": null, "trial_court_fine": null, "reason_for_relief": null, "reporting_condition": null, "trial_court_sentence": null, "trial_court_fine_paid": false}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["pre_trial_custody"], "scenario_keywords": ["anticipatory bail"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Disposed of with directions.", "disposition": "Disposed of with dire