44444444-0008-4000-8000-000000000008	Criminal Appeal No. 943 of 2005	Vijaysinh Chandubha Jadeja v. State of Gujarat	Supreme Court of India	2011	Hon'ble D.K. Jain, P. Sathasivam, C.K. Prasad, R.M. Lodha, H.L. Dattu, JJ.	Vijaysinh Chandubha Jadeja v. State of Gujarat	["NDPS Act", "Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances Act, 1985"]	["Section 50 NDPS Act"]	["mandatory search before Gazetted Officer", "substantial compliance insufficient", "strict compliance required"]	Five-judge Constitution Bench held that substantial compliance with Section 50 NDPS is insufficient; there must be strict compliance with the requirement of searching the suspect in the presence of a Gazetted Officer or Magistrate.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_2011_Crl_App_No_943_of_2005_44440008.pdf	6dcb2c77001d9ec1b99779d17aab4c0dd854a0b38d9d5471f44228e73772d090	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:24:40.681123+05:30	2026-10-25 11:24:40.681127+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:08.539091+05:30	\N	[]	The concept of 'substantial compliance' with Section 50 of the NDPS Act is neither permissible nor acceptable. The suspect has an indefeasible right to be searched before a Gazetted Officer or Magistrate.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2011) 1 SCC 609]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2011) 1 SCC 609", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Constitution Bench Landmark)	Supreme Court of India\nCriminal Appeal No. 943 of 2005\nVijaysinh Chandubha Jadeja v. State of Gujarat\nBench: Hon'ble D.K. Jain, P. Sathasivam, C.K. Prasad, R.M. Lodha, H.L. Dattu, JJ.\nDate: 2010-10-29\nCitation: (2011) 1 SCC 609\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nThe concept of 'substantial compliance' with Section 50 of the NDPS Act is neither permissible nor acceptable. The suspect has an indefeasible right to be searched before a Gazetted Officer or Magistrate.\n\nSUMMARY:\nFive-judge Constitution Bench held that substantial compliance with Section 50 NDPS is insufficient; there must be strict compliance with the requirement of searching the suspect in the presence of a Gazetted Officer or Magistrate.\n\nSTATUTES: Section 50 NDPS, Section 42 NDPS\nDISPOSITION: Strict Compliance with Section 50 NDPS Mandated	2	f	SCIN010009432005	Criminal Appeal	2010-10-29	Constitution Bench (5 Judges)	Vijaysinh Chandubha Jadeja	State of Gujarat	Senior Advocate K.T.S. Tulsi	Senior Advocate Hemantika Wahi	Narcotics Law & Search Protocol	Police must strictly follow Section 50 NDPS. 'Substantial' or casual compliance is not enough. If police fail to take the suspect before a Gazetted Officer or Magistrate upon request, the entire drug seizure is illegal.	0	(2011) 1 SCC 609	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Vijaysinh Chandubha Jadeja v. State of Gujarat", "case_id": "44444444-0008-4000-8000-000000000008", "case_no": "Criminal Appeal No. 943 of 2005", "parties": "Vijaysinh Chandubha Jadeja v. State of Gujarat", "category": "NDPS", "petitioner": "Vijaysinh Chandubha Jadeja", "respondent": "State of Gujarat", "legal_domain": "Narcotics Law & Search Protocol", "category_facts": {"category": "NDPS", "custody_period": null, "contraband_type": "Contraband / Narcotics", "seized_quantity": null, "statutory_issue": "Bail / Pre-trial Release under NDPS Act", "quantity_category": "Intermediate Quantity", "section_37_embargo": false, "search_compliance_sec_50": "Section 50 mandatory search safeguards compliance examined"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["narcotics_contraband"], "scenario_keywords": ["ndps", "section 50 ndps"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
44444444-0009-4000-8000-000000000009	Criminal Appeal No. 11 of 2011	Kailas & Others v. State of Maharashtra	Supreme Court of India	2011	Hon'ble Markandey Katju & Gyan Sudha Misra, JJ.	Kailas & Others v. State of Maharashtra	["Indian Penal Code, 1860"]	["Section 3(1)(xi) SC/ST Act", "Section 354 IPC", "Section 323 IPC"]	["Adivasi rights", "historical injustice", "original inhabitants of India", "atrocities on tribal women"]	Bench led by Justice Markandey Katju denounced centuries of atrocities against Scheduled Tribes (Adivasis), affirming their status as the original inhabitants of India and mandating severe punishment for offences against tribal women.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_2011_Crl_App_No_11_of_2011_44440009.pdf	7f055aec274d0c448f6f4ee3fdf655e4d0f3bbf487232f0b5e25eba65223d36d	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:24:40.703085+05:30	2026-10-25 11:24:40.703089+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:08.540042+05:30	\N	[]	Adivasis are the descendants of the original inhabitants of India who were displaced and marginalized. Offenses against tribal women strike at the root of constitutional dignity and must be dealt with utmost severity.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2011) 1 SCC 793]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2011) 1 SCC 793", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding Human Rights Precedent)	Supreme Court of India\nCriminal Appeal No. 11 of 2011\nKailas and Others v. State of Maharashtra\nBench: Hon'ble Markandey Katju & Gyan Sudha Misra, JJ.\nDate: 2011-01-05\nCitation: (2011) 1 SCC 793\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nAdivasis are the descendants of the original inhabitants of India who were displaced and marginalized. Offenses against tribal women strike at the root of constitutional dignity and must be dealt with utmost severity.\n\nSUMMARY:\nBench led by Justice Markandey Katju denounced centuries of atrocities against Scheduled Tribes (Adivasis), affirming their status as the original inhabitants of India and mandating severe punishment for offences against tribal women.\n\nSTATUTES: Section 3(1)(xi) SC/ST Act, Section 354 IPC, Section 323 IPC\nDISPOSITION: Conviction under SC/ST Act Confirmed with Strictest Strictures	2	f	SCIN010000112011	Criminal Appeal	2011-01-05	Division Bench (2 Judges)	Kailas & Others	State of Maharashtra	Advocate Sanjay Jain	Advocate Sushil Karanjkar	Criminal Law & Indigenous Human Rights	The Supreme Court strongly condemned violence against tribal women, noting that Adivasis are the original inhabitants of India and courts must strictly punish those committing atrocities against them.	0	(2011) 1 SCC 793	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Kailas and Others v. State of Maharashtra", "case_id": "44444444-0009-4000-8000-000000000009", "case_no": "Criminal Appeal No. 11 of 2011", "parties": "Kailas & Others v. State of Maharashtra", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Kailas & Others", "respondent": "State of Maharashtra", "legal_domain": "Criminal Law & Indigenous Human Rights", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Criminal Appeal Against Conviction", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "IPC", "section": "354", "canonical": "Section 354 IPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "IPC", "section": "323", "canonical": "Section 323 IPC", "equivalents": ["Section 115 BNS"]}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["caste_status"], "scenario_keywords": ["scheduled tribe", "scheduled caste"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Dismissed", "disposition": "Dismissed"}}
44444444-0010-4000-8000-000000000010	Criminal Appeal No. 907 of 2002	K.G. Premshanker v. Inspector of Police & Another	Supreme Court of India	2002	Hon'ble M.B. Shah, B.N. Agrawal, Arijit Pasayat, JJ.	K.G. Premshanker v. Inspector of Police & Another	["Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973"]	["Sections 40 to 43 Evidence Act", "Section 482 CrPC"]	["civil court decree not binding on criminal court", "independent evaluation", "standard of proof difference"]	Three-judge bench held that a judgment or decree of a Civil Court is not conclusively binding on a Criminal Court, and criminal proceedings cannot be quashed automatically merely because a civil suit on similar facts succeeded or failed.	Quashed	storage/cases/SC_2002_Crl_App_No_907_of_2002_44440010.pdf	57b8b58f615bb0a573550b310c7d25a8b2ef3bf9164607f90b0c2b42fdf6ad0f	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:24:40.724125+05:30	2026-10-25 11:24:40.724129+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:08.540045+05:30	\N	[]	Previous judgment of a civil court is not binding in all cases on criminal proceedings. The criminal court has to decide guilt or innocence independently based on the evidence adduced before it under the standard of proof beyond reasonable doubt.	[]	Allowed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2002) 8 SCC 87]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2002) 8 SCC 87", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding 3-Judge Precedent)	Supreme Court of India\nCriminal Appeal No. 907 of 2002\nK.G. Premshanker v. Inspector of Police and Another\nBench: Hon'ble M.B. Shah, B.N. Agrawal, Arijit Pasayat, JJ.\nDate: 2002-09-12\nCitation: (2002) 8 SCC 87\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nPrevious judgment of a civil court is not binding in all cases on criminal proceedings. The criminal court has to decide guilt or innocence independently based on the evidence adduced before it under the standard of proof beyond reasonable doubt.\n\nSUMMARY:\nThree-judge bench held that a judgment or decree of a Civil Court is not conclusively binding on a Criminal Court, and criminal proceedings cannot be quashed automatically merely because a civil suit on similar facts succeeded or failed.\n\nSTATUTES: Sections 40 to 43 Evidence Act, Section 482 CrPC\nDISPOSITION: Quashing Refused; Independent Criminal Trial Upheld	2	f	SCIN010009072002	Criminal Appeal	2002-09-12	Three-Judge Bench	K.G. Premshanker	Inspector of Police & Another	Senior Advocate K.V. Viswanathan	Senior Advocate Altaf Ahmad	Criminal Procedure & Evidence Relevancy	Winning or losing a civil lawsuit does not automatically stop a criminal case on the same facts. Criminal courts decide guilt independently using the standard of proof beyond reasonable doubt.	0	(2002) 8 SCC 87	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "K.G. Premshanker v. Inspector of Police and Another", "case_id": "44444444-0010-4000-8000-000000000010", "case_no": "Criminal Appeal No. 907 of 2002", "parties": "K.G. Premshanker v. Inspector of Police & Another", "category": "QUASHING", "petitioner": "K.G. Premshanker", "respondent": "Inspector of Police & Another", "legal_domain": "Criminal Procedure & Evidence Relevancy", "category_facts": {"category": "QUASHING", "civil_cloak": false, "quash_ground": "Absence of prima facie ingredients", "compromise_affidavit": false, "bhajan_lal_principles": true, "proceedings_challenged": "FIR / Investigation"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CRPC", "section": "482", "canonical": "Section 482 CRPC", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "scenario_keywords": [], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Dismissed", "disposition": "Dismissed"}}
44444444-0011-4000-8000-000000000011	Criminal Appeal No. 841 of 2004	Rathinam v. State of Tamil Nadu	Supreme Court of India	2011	Hon'ble P. Sathasivam & B.S. Chauhan, JJ.	Rathinam v. State of Tamil Nadu	["A IPC", "B IPC", "Evidence Act", "Indian Penal Code, 1860"]	["Section 113B Evidence Act", "Section 304 B IPC", "Section 498 A IPC"]	["dowry death", "soon before death cruelty", "statutory presumption", "Section 113B Evidence Act"]	Detailed analysis of the statutory presumption under Section 113B Evidence Act in Section 304B IPC dowry death prosecutions, clarifying the temporal proximity requirement of 'soon before death'.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_2011_Crl_App_No_841_of_2004_44440011.pdf	9e54bcada2e1653d9718e5533ab68bbf1508df3e06fc77e91fb94b8d130c88c4	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:24:40.745644+05:30	2026-10-25 11:24:40.74565+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:08.54074+05:30	\N	[]	Where prosecution establishes that the death occurred within seven years of marriage under abnormal circumstances and cruelty for dowry was meted out 'soon before death', the statutory presumption under Section 113B Evidence Act operates mandatorily against the accused.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2011) 11 SCC 140]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2011) 11 SCC 140", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding Criminal Law Precedent)	Supreme Court of India\nCriminal Appeal No. 841 of 2004\nRathinam v. State of Tamil Nadu\nBench: Hon'ble P. Sathasivam & B.S. Chauhan, JJ.\nDate: 2011-04-18\nCitation: (2011) 11 SCC 140\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nWhere prosecution establishes that the death occurred within seven years of marriage under abnormal circumstances and cruelty for dowry was meted out 'soon before death', the statutory presumption under Section 113B Evidence Act operates mandatorily against the accused.\n\nSUMMARY:\nDetailed analysis of the statutory presumption under Section 113B Evidence Act in Section 304B IPC dowry death prosecutions, clarifying the temporal proximity requirement of 'soon before death'.\n\nSTATUTES: Section 304B IPC, Section 498A IPC, Section 113B Evidence Act\nDISPOSITION: Conviction under Section 304B and Section 498A IPC Upheld	2	f	SCIN010008412004	Criminal Appeal	2011-04-18	Division Bench (2 Judges)	Rathinam	State of Tamil Nadu	Senior Advocate R. Shanmugasundaram	Senior Advocate S. Thananjayan	Criminal Law & Dowry Death Presumption	If a married woman dies an unnatural death within 7 years of marriage and cruelty for dowry occurred soon before her death, the court legally presumes the husband and in-laws caused the dowry death.	0	(2011) 11 SCC 140	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Rathinam v. State of Tamil Nadu", "case_id": "44444444-0011-4000-8000-000000000011", "case_no": "Criminal Appeal No. 841 of 2004", "parties": "Rathinam v. State of Tamil Nadu", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Rathinam", "respondent": "State of Tamil Nadu", "legal_domain": "Criminal Law & Dowry Death Presumption", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Criminal Appeal Against Conviction", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "IPC", "section": "304B", "canonical": "Section 304B IPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "IPC", "section": "498A", "canonical": "Section 498A IPC", "equivalents": ["Section 85 BNS"]}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "scenario_keywords": [], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Dismissed", "disposition": "Dismissed"}}
44444444-0012-4000-8000-000000000012	Criminal Appeal No. 712 of 2013	Sarabjit Singh & Another v. State of Punjab & Another	Supreme Court of India	2013	Hon'ble T.S. Thakur & Gyan Sudha Misra, JJ.	Sarabjit Singh & Another v. State of Punjab & Another	["NI Act", "Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881"]	["Section 138 NI Act", "Section 147 NI Act"]	["compounding after conviction", "recovery of dues", "quashing conviction upon full payment"]	Affirmed that offences under Section 138 NI Act can be compounded even after conviction and appellate confirmation, if the complainant is fully compensated and satisfied.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_2013_Crl_App_No_712_of_2013_44440012.pdf	e63e71526e6cbddfcee91db4b5ab620c46eed1aa25c1bc9ce76e3dc6bb1df748	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:24:40.779077+05:30	2026-10-25 11:24:40.779081+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:08.541099+05:30	\N	[]	The primary object of Section 138 NI Act is compensatory rather than punitive. Once the cheque amount is paid in full with reasonable costs, compounding under Section 147 is permissible at any stage, even after final conviction.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2013) 6 SCC 800]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2013) 6 SCC 800", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding Commercial Precedent)	Supreme Court of India\nCriminal Appeal No. 712 of 2013\nSarabjit Singh and Another v. State of Punjab and Another\nBench: Hon'ble T.S. Thakur & Gyan Sudha Misra, JJ.\nDate: 2013-05-03\nCitation: (2013) 6 SCC 800\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nThe primary object of Section 138 NI Act is compensatory rather than punitive. Once the cheque amount is paid in full with reasonable costs, compounding under Section 147 is permissible at any stage, even after final conviction.\n\nSUMMARY:\nAffirmed that offences under Section 138 NI Act can be compounded even after conviction and appellate confirmation, if the complainant is fully compensated and satisfied.\n\nSTATUTES: Section 138 NI Act, Section 147 NI Act\nDISPOSITION: Conviction Set Aside Pursuant to Full Compounding	2	f	SCIN010007122013	Criminal Appeal	2013-05-03	Division Bench (2 Judges)	Sarabjit Singh & Another	State of Punjab & Another	Senior Advocate Jasbir Singh Malik	Senior Advocate Kuldip Singh	Commercial Law & Cheque Dishonor	Even if someone is convicted in a cheque bounce case, if they pay the entire cheque amount with interest and the complainant agrees, the conviction can be set aside through compounding.	0	(2013) 6 SCC 800	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Sarabjit Singh and Another v. State of Punjab and Another", "case_id": "44444444-0012-4000-8000-000000000012", "case_no": "Criminal Appeal No. 712 of 2013", "parties": "Sarabjit Singh & Another v. State of Punjab & Another", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Sarabjit Singh & Another", "respondent": "State of Punjab & Another", "legal_domain": "Commercial Law & Cheque Dishonor", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Criminal Appeal Against Conviction", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["financial_commercial"], "scenario_keywords": ["section 138", "negotiable instruments"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
44444444-0013-4000-8000-000000000013	CS (OS) No. 1407 of 2010	Tata Sons Ltd. v. Greenpeace International & Another	High Court of Delhi	2011	Hon'ble S. Ravindra Bhat, J.	Tata Sons Ltd. v. Greenpeace International & Another	["Trade Marks Act"]	["Section 29 Trade Marks Act"]	["trademark parody", "free speech vs trademark", "interim injunction refused", "environmental protest game"]	Landmark intellectual property ruling holding that parody and critical commentary using a trademark in an environmental campaign is protected under free speech Article 19(1)(a) and does not constitute trademark infringement.	Disposed	storage/cases/DHC_2011_CS_OS_No_1407_of_2010_44440013.pdf	6f08246f9f643e46bf770c32ae4117fb1ae960da64d0343b65dc9ea90396292d	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:24:40.79869+05:30	2026-10-25 11:24:40.798693+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:08.541595+05:30	\N	[]	An interim injunction cannot be granted to suppress peaceful parody or social commentary. The use of a mark in a non-commercial game advocating environmental causes is protected speech under Article 19(1)(a) and is not trademark infringement.	[]	Disposed	[]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding IP Landmark)	High Court of Delhi\nCS (OS) No. 1407 of 2010\nTata Sons Ltd. v. Greenpeace International and Another\nBench: Hon'ble S. Ravindra Bhat, J.\nDate: 2011-01-28\nCitation: 2011:DHC:452\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nAn interim injunction cannot be granted to suppress peaceful parody or social commentary. The use of a mark in a non-commercial game advocating environmental causes is protected speech under Article 19(1)(a) and is not trademark infringement.\n\nSUMMARY:\nLandmark intellectual property ruling holding that parody and critical commentary using a trademark in an environmental campaign is protected under free speech Article 19(1)(a) and does not constitute trademark infringement.\n\nSTATUTES: Section 29 Trade Marks Act, Article 19(1)(a)\nDISPOSITION: Interim Injunction against Environmental Parody Refused	2	f	HCMA010014072011	CS (OS)	2011-01-28	Single Judge	Tata Sons Ltd.	Greenpeace International & Another	Senior Advocate Rajiv Nayar	Senior Advocate Sanjay Parikh	Intellectual Property & Freedom of Speech	Using a company logo in a game or cartoon to criticize its environmental impact is protected satire and free speech, not trademark infringement. Courts will not ban peaceful parody.	0	2011:DHC:452	{"court": "High Court of Delhi", "title": "Tata Sons Ltd. v. Greenpeace International and Another", "case_id": "44444444-0013-4000-8000-000000000013", "case_no": "CS (OS) No. 1407 of 2010", "parties": "Tata Sons Ltd. v. Greenpeace International & Another", "category": "COMMERCIAL_CIVIL", "petitioner": "Tata Sons Ltd.", "respondent": "Greenpeace International & Another", "legal_domain": "Intellectual Property & Freedom of Speech", "category_facts": {"category": "COMMERCIAL_CIVIL", "dispute_type": "Trademark Infringement & Passing Off (Section 2(1)(c) Commercial Courts Act)", "statutory_basis": "Commercial Courts Act / Civil Procedure Code", "interim_injunction_granted": true}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "19", "canonical": "Section 19 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "scenario_keywords": [], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Dismissed", "disposition": "Dismissed"}}
44444444-0014-4000-8000-000000000014	Writ Petition (Civil) No. 956 of 2020	Firoz Iqbal Khan v. Union of India & Sudarshan News TV	High Court of Judicature at Bombay	2020	Hon'ble Nitin Jamdar & Milind Jadhav, JJ.	Firoz Iqbal Khan v. Union of India & Sudarshan News TV	["Cable TV Act"]	["Section 19 Cable TV Act"]	["hate speech telecast", "Programme Code violation", "vilification of minority community", "restraint order"]	Division bench restrained the telecast of episodes targeting minority civil services aspirants, affirming that hate speech vilifying an entire community cannot masquerade as freedom of press.	Disposed	storage/cases/BHC_2020_WP_No_956_of_2020_44440014.pdf	8b49ec7ff6975e64dbdca350ff71db6bb9b9ccf728cfe5ecc88fa2f3956e648c	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:24:40.818418+05:30	2026-10-25 11:24:40.818422+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:08.54246+05:30	\N	[]	Freedom of speech under Article 19(1)(a) does not include a license to incite hatred or denigrate any community. When broadcasts show prima facie violation of the Programme Code, courts have the power to interdict them.	[]	Disposed	[]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding Media Law Precedent)	High Court of Judicature at Bombay\nWrit Petition (Civil) No. 956 of 2020\nFiroz Iqbal Khan v. Union of India and Others\nBench: Hon'ble Nitin Jamdar & Milind Jadhav, JJ.\nDate: 2020-09-21\nCitation: 2020:BHC:412\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nFreedom of speech under Article 19(1)(a) does not include a license to incite hatred or denigrate any community. When broadcasts show prima facie violation of the Programme Code, courts have the power to interdict them.\n\nSUMMARY:\nDivision bench restrained the telecast of episodes targeting minority civil services aspirants, affirming that hate speech vilifying an entire community cannot masquerade as freedom of press.\n\nSTATUTES: Section 19 Cable TV Act, Article 19(1)(a), Article 19(2), Article 21\nDISPOSITION: Telecast Restrained Pending Programme Code Inquest	2	f	BHCB010009562020	Writ Petition	2020-09-21	Division Bench (2 Judges)	Firoz Iqbal Khan	Union of India & Sudarshan News TV	Senior Advocate Shadan Farasat	Senior Advocate J. Sai Deepak	Media Law & Hate Speech Regulation	TV channels cannot broadcast hate speech or conspiracy theories targeting religious communities under the guise of free speech. Broadcasts must comply with the Programme Code.	0	2020:BHC:412	{"court": "High Court of Judicature at Bombay", "title": "Firoz Iqbal Khan v. Union of India and Others", "case_id": "44444444-0014-4000-8000-000000000014", "case_no": "Writ Petition (Civil) No. 956 of 2020", "parties": "Firoz Iqbal Khan v. Union of India & Sudarshan News TV", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Firoz Iqbal Khan", "respondent": "Union of India & Sudarshan News TV", "legal_domain": "Media Law & Hate Speech Regulation", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Writ Petition / Criminal Appeal", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "19", "canonical": "Section 19 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "21", "canonical": "Section 21 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["constitutional_fundamental"], "scenario_keywords": ["article 21"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
44444444-0015-4000-8000-000000000015	Criminal Petition No. 4921 of 2023	Naveen Kumar & Elderly Parents v. State of Karnataka & Estranged Wife	High Court of Karnataka	2023	Hon'ble M. Nagaprasanna, J.	Naveen Kumar & Elderly Parents v. State of Karnataka & Estranged Wife	["A IPC", "Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973", "Indian Penal Code, 1860"]	["Section 498 A IPC"]	["Section 498A abuse", "distant in-laws quashing", "omnibus allegations", "preventing harassment of elderly parents"]	Justice M. Nagaprasanna quashed Section 498A IPC proceedings against distant relatives and elderly parents-in-law living separately, holding that omnibus allegations without specific dates or overt acts constitute abuse of process.	Quashed	storage/cases/KHC_2023_Crl_Pet_No_4921_of_2023_44440015.pdf	e94245b50b42b9237de1f42648cb2aa67e3a1fce70d377bab8566f92e3ce0156	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:24:40.837612+05:30	2026-10-25 11:24:40.837616+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:08.54403+05:30	\N	[]	Impleading all relatives of the husband on general and omnibus allegations without specific overt acts is an abuse of judicial process. The inherent power under Section 482 CrPC must be exercised to prevent vexatious harassment.	[]	Allowed	[]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding Section 482 Precedent)	High Court of Karnataka\nCriminal Petition No. 4921 of 2023\nNaveen Kumar and Others v. State of Karnataka and Another\nBench: Hon'ble M. Nagaprasanna, J.\nDate: 2023-08-14\nCitation: 2023:KHC:4921\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nImpleading all relatives of the husband on general and omnibus allegations without specific overt acts is an abuse of judicial process. The inherent power under Section 482 CrPC must be exercised to prevent vexatious harassment.\n\nSUMMARY:\nJustice M. Nagaprasanna quashed Section 498A IPC proceedings against distant relatives and elderly parents-in-law living separately, holding that omnibus allegations without specific dates or overt acts constitute abuse of process.\n\nSTATUTES: Section 482 CrPC, Section 498A IPC, Section 506 IPC\nDISPOSITION: Criminal Proceedings against In-Laws Quashed	2	f	HCMA010049212023	Criminal Petition	2023-08-14	Single Judge	Naveen Kumar & Elderly Parents	State of Karnataka & Estranged Wife	Advocate Harshavardhana	High Court Government Pleader Mahesh Shetty	Criminal Law & Quashing u/s 482 CrPC	General, vague allegations against elderly in-laws or relatives who live in different cities cannot be used to drag them into criminal courts. High Courts will quash such harassment under Section 482 CrPC.	0	2023:KHC:4921	{"court": "High Court of Karnataka", "title": "Naveen Kumar and Others v. State of Karnataka and Another", "case_id": "44444444-0015-4000-8000-000000000015", "case_no": "Criminal Petition No. 4921 of 2023", "parties": "Naveen Kumar & Elderly Parents v. State of Karnataka & Estranged Wife", "category": "QUASHING", "petitioner": "Naveen Kumar & Elderly Parents", "respondent": "State of Karnataka & Estranged Wife", "legal_domain": "Criminal Law & Quashing u/s 482 CrPC", "category_facts": {"category": "QUASHING", "civil_cloak": false, "quash_ground": "Absence of prima facie ingredients", "compromise_affidavit": false, "bhajan_lal_principles": true, "proceedings_challenged": "FIR / Investigation"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CRPC", "section": "482", "canonical": "Section 482 CRPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "IPC", "section": "498A", "canonical": "Section 498A IPC", "equivalents": ["Section 85 BNS"]}, {"act": "IPC", "section": "506", "canonical": "Section 506 IPC", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["quashing_proceeding"], "scenario_keywords": ["abuse of judicial process"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
44444444-0016-4000-8000-000000000016	Criminal Misc. Anticipatory Bail Application No. 12984 of 2024	Manoj Kumar v. State of U.P. & Informant	High Court of Judicature at Allahabad	2024	Hon'ble Krishan Pahal, J.	Manoj Kumar v. State of U.P. & Informant	["A CrPC", "Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973", "Indian Penal Code, 1860"]	["Section 41 A CrPC"]	["anticipatory bail", "Section 41A CrPC non-compliance", "Satender Kumar Antil applied", "commercial cheating dispute"]	Justice Krishan Pahal granted anticipatory bail applying Satender Kumar Antil and Arnesh Kumar, holding that where offences carry punishment under 7 years and police fail to serve Section 41A CrPC notice, custodial arrest is unjustified.	Disposed	storage/cases/AHC_2024_Antic_Bail_No_12984_of_2024_44440016.pdf	5920e6706e18eeec18ef41b7aec239d63b6618213f5bd1e08434568efe9b93bd	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:24:40.858194+05:30	2026-10-25 11:24:40.858199+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:08.544034+05:30	\N	[]	Investigating officers are bound by the mandatory dictates of Section 41A CrPC as reiterated in Arnesh Kumar and Satender Kumar Antil. Arrest without justifiable recording of reasons for offences punishable up to 7 years entitles the applicant to pre-arrest bail.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Satender Kumar Antil v. Central Bureau of Investigation", "status": "Good Law (Binding Precedent)", "case_id": "11111111-0002-4000-8000-000000000002", "citation": "(2022) 10 SCC 51", "ratio_key": "Categorization of offences and non-custodial bail guidelines", "treatment": "Followed / Relied Upon"}, {"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Arnesh Kumar v. State of Bihar", "status": "Good Law (Binding Precedent)", "case_id": "11111111-0003-4000-8000-000000000003", "citation": "(2014) 8 SCC 273", "ratio_key": "Section 41A CrPC notice and safeguards against automatic arrest", "treatment": "Followed / Relied Upon"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding HC Bail Precedent)	High Court of Judicature at Allahabad\nCriminal Misc. Anticipatory Bail Application No. 12984 of 2024\nManoj Kumar v. State of U.P. and Another\nBench: Hon'ble Krishan Pahal, J.\nDate: 2024-04-22\nCitation: 2024:AHC:12984\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nInvestigating officers are bound by the mandatory dictates of Section 41A CrPC as reiterated in Arnesh Kumar and Satender Kumar Antil. Arrest without justifiable recording of reasons for offences punishable up to 7 years entitles the applicant to pre-arrest bail.\n\nSUMMARY:\nJustice Krishan Pahal granted anticipatory bail applying Satender Kumar Antil and Arnesh Kumar, holding that where offences carry punishment under 7 years and police fail to serve Section 41A CrPC notice, custodial arrest is unjustified.\n\nSTATUTES: Section 438 CrPC, Section 41A CrPC, Section 420 IPC, Section 406 IPC\nDISPOSITION: Anticipatory Bail Granted with Cooperation Conditions	2	f	HCMA010129842024	Criminal Misc. Anticipatory Bail Application	2024-04-22	Single Judge	Manoj Kumar	State of U.P. & Informant	Advocate Sanjay Kumar Yadav	Additional Government Advocate Anurag Verma	Anticipatory Bail & Police Notice	For offences punishable with up to 7 years in jail, police must first issue a Section 41A notice to appear. If police threaten arrest without notice, anticipatory bail will be granted.	0	2024:AHC:12984	{"court": "High Court of Judicature at Allahabad", "title": "Manoj Kumar v. State of U.P. and Another", "case_id": "44444444-0016-4000-8000-000000000016", "case_no": "Criminal Misc. Anticipatory Bail Application No. 12984 of 2024", "parties": "Manoj Kumar v. State of U.P. & Informant", "category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "petitioner": "Manoj Kumar", "respondent": "State of U.P. & Informant", "legal_domain": "Anticipatory Bail & Police Notice", "category_facts": {"category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "surety_count": null, "custody_period": null, "application_type": "Anticipatory Bail", "bail_bond_amount": null, "trial_court_fine": null, "reason_for_relief": null, "reporting_condition": null, "trial_court_sentence": null, "trial_court_fine_paid": false}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CRPC", "section": "438", "canonical": "Section 438 CRPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CRPC", "section": "41A", "canonical": "Section 41A CRPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "IPC", "section": "420", "canonical": "Section 420 IPC", "equivalents": ["Section 318 BNS"]}, {"act": "IPC", "section": "406", "canonical": "Section 406 IPC", "equivalents": ["Section 316 BNS"]}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["pre_trial_custody"], "scenario_keywords": ["anticipatory bail"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
44444444-0017-4000-8000-000000000017	Criminal Misc. Application No. 4512 of 2023	Teesta Atul Setalvad v. State of Gujarat	High Court of Gujarat at Ahmedabad	2023	Hon'ble Nirzar S. Desai, J.	Teesta Atul Setalvad v. State of Gujarat	["Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973", "Indian Penal Code, 1860"]	["Section 482 CrPC", "Section 194 IPC", "Section 211 IPC", "Section 468 IPC"]	["fabricating false evidence", "SIT clean chit", "regular bail rejection", "Supreme Court subsequent intervention"]	Detailed consideration of prima facie offences of fabricating false evidence to secure capital conviction. High Court refused regular bail, though Supreme Court subsequently granted bail observing applicant was on interim bail without violating conditions.	Disposed	storage/cases/GHC_2023_Crl_Misc_App_No_4512_of_2023_44440017.pdf	e98699a1b0b62ccf8df0f9b9107af498354be8b1ea9657b7235ee819f3fbd7dd	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:24:40.891127+05:30	2026-10-25 11:24:40.891132+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:08.545727+05:30	\N	[]	Where an accused is alleged to have fabricated evidence to implicate innocent persons in capital offences, seriousness of offence outweighs personal liberty at the prima facie stage. [Modified by Supreme Court on appeal in 2023 granting regular bail].	[]	Disposed	[]	\N	unverified	Distinguished on Facts (Supreme Court granted bail)	High Court of Gujarat at Ahmedabad\nCriminal Misc. Application No. 4512 of 2023\nTeesta Atul Setalvad v. State of Gujarat\nBench: Hon'ble Nirzar S. Desai, J.\nDate: 2023-07-01\nCitation: 2023:GHC:4512\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nWhere an accused is alleged to have fabricated evidence to implicate innocent persons in capital offences, seriousness of offence outweighs personal liberty at the prima facie stage. [Modified by Supreme Court on appeal in 2023 granting regular bail].\n\nSUMMARY:\nDetailed consideration of prima facie offences of fabricating false evidence to secure capital conviction. High Court refused regular bail, though Supreme Court subsequently granted bail observing applicant was on interim bail without violating conditions.\n\nSTATUTES: Section 482 CrPC, Section 194 IPC, Section 211 IPC, Section 468 IPC\nDISPOSITION: Bail Rejected with Direction to Surrender (Overturned by SC)	2	f	GJHC010045122023	Criminal Misc. Application	2023-07-01	Single Judge	Teesta Atul Setalvad	State of Gujarat	Senior Advocate Mihir Joshi	Public Prosecutor Mitesh Amin	Criminal Law & Quashing u/s 482	Examines whether an NGO activist fabricated affidavits in riots cases. While the High Court rejected bail, the Supreme Court later protected the petitioner's liberty.	0	2023:GHC:4512	{"court": "High Court of Gujarat at Ahmedabad", "title": "Teesta Atul Setalvad v. State of Gujarat", "case_id": "44444444-0017-4000-8000-000000000017", "case_no": "Criminal Misc. Application No. 4512 of 2023", "parties": "Teesta Atul Setalvad v. State of Gujarat", "category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "petitioner": "Teesta Atul Setalvad", "respondent": "State of Gujarat", "legal_domain": "Criminal Law & Quashing u/s 482", "category_facts": {"category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "surety_count": null, "custody_period": null, "application_type": "Regular Bail", "bail_bond_amount": null, "trial_court_fine": null, "reason_for_relief": null, "reporting_condition": null, "trial_court_sentence": null, "trial_court_fine_paid": false}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CRPC", "section": "482", "canonical": "Section 482 CRPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "IPC", "section": "194", "canonical": "Section 194 IPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "IPC", "section": "211", "canonical": "Section 211 IPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "IPC", "section": "468", "canonical": "Section 468 IPC", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["pre_trial_custody"], "scenario_keywords": ["regular bail"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Dismissed (Modified by S