.. State of Maharashtra\nand others )\nWITH\nWRIT PETITION NO. 750 OF 2022\n( Pandhari Bhaurao Sarnayak ..vs.. State of Maharashtra and others )\nWITH\nWRIT PETITION NO. 725 OF 2022\n( Ku. Anjali Rameshwar Raut and others ..vs.. State of Maharashtra and others )\nWITH\nWRIT PETITION NO. 722 OF 2022\n( Prabphul Ashok Jadhav ..vs.. State of Maharashtra and others )\nWITH\nWRIT PETITION NO. 727 OF 2022\n( Ku. Sunayana Tukaram Rathod ..vs.. State of Maharashtra and others )\nWITH\nWRIT PETITION NO. 723 OF 2022\n( Kailas Pandurang Tekade and another ..vs.. State of Maharashtra and others )\nWITH\nWRIT PETITION NO. 5401 OF 2022\n( Vaibhav Pandurang Raut and others ..vs.. State of Maharashtra and others )\nWITH\nWRIT PETITION NO. 5520 OF 2018\n( Chandrakant Kisan Waghmare ..vs.. State of Maharashtra and others )\nWITH\nWRIT PETITION NO. 5521 OF 2018\n( Sudhir Ramchandra Bharsakhade ..vs.. State of Maharashtra and others )\nWITH\nWRIT PETITION NO. 5820 OF 2018\n( Manoj s/o Arunrao Khadse and others ..vs.. Deputy Director of Education, Amravati and others )\n\n2\nwp 6275.18\nWITH\nWRIT PETITION NO. 5843 OF 2018\n( Suraj s/o Sharadrao Thakare ..vs.. State of Maharashtra and others )\n- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -\nOffice Notes, Office Memoranda of Coram, Court's or Judge's orders\nappearances, Court's orders of directions\nand Registrar's orders\n- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -\nMr. Anand Parchure, Counsel for the petitioners in WP Nos. 6275/2018, 6274/2018,\n6188/2018, 6180/2018, WP 5674/2018\nMr. V.B. Gavali, Counsel for the petitioner in WP No.5830/2018,\nMr. B.G. Kulkarni, Counsel for the petitioner in W.P. No. 3033/2023, WP 750/2022,WP\n725/2022, WP 722/2022, WP 727/2022, WP 723/2022, 5401/2022,\nMr. A.R. Deshpande, Counsel for the petitioner in WP 5520/2018, 5521/2018,\nMr. N.S. Khubalkar, Counsel for the petitioner in WP 5820/2018,\nMr. A.D. Mohgaonkar, Counsel for the petitioner in WP 5843/2018,\nMr. A.V. Palshikar, Counsel for the respondents/State,\nMr. A.J. Gilda, Counsel for respondent Nos. 6 and 7 in WP 722/2022, WP 725/2022, WP\n723/2022\nMr. H.R. Gadhia, Counsel for respondent Nos. 6 and 7 in WP 5520/2018, 5521/2018,\nCORAM : ANIL L. PANSARE & NIVEDITA P. MEHTA, JJ.\nDATED : 22 nd OCTOBER, 2026\nHeard.\n2. The Counsels for the petitioners and the learned A.G.P. are ad\nidem that the issue involved is covered by order dated 2/7/2026 passed\nby the Division Bench of this Court in Writ Petition No. 3183/2018 with\nconnected petitions.\n3. In view thereof, and for the reasons set out in the said order, we\nallow the petitions.\n4. The orders passed by the Deputy Director of Education cancelling\napproval granted to the petitioners are quashed and set aside. The Deputy\nDirector of Education, before taking any decision, shall adhere to the\nGovernment Resolution dated 23/8/2017 and the judgment dated\n5/9/2022 passed by this Court in Writ Petition No. 1315/2022\n\n3\nwp 6275.18\n(Dilipkumar Patle & Anr. Vs. Deputy Director of Education, Nagpur\nDivision, Nagpur & Ors.).\n(Nivedita P. Mehta, J.) (Anil L. Pansare, J.)\nadgokar	3	t	BHCB010062752026	Judicial Precedent	2026-10-23	Single Bench	Petitioner	Respondent / State	\N	Nos. 6 and 7 in WP 722/2022, WP 725/2022, WP	Civil & Constitutional Law	In this matter, the High Court of Judicature at Bombay (Nagpur Bench) adjudicated issues between Petitioner and Respondent / State. The Court rendered a decision disposing of the application under statutory provisions.	0	2026 LexGuard (BHC) 1F172791	{"court": "High Court of Judicature at Bombay (Nagpur Bench)", "title": "Petitioner v. Respondent / State", "case_id": "1f172791-c4a1-460b-8b5a-2cae4600d49b", "case_no": "NO. 6275 OF 2018", "parties": "Petitioner v. Respondent / State", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Petitioner", "respondent": "Respondent / State", "legal_domain": "Civil & Constitutional Law", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Writ Petition / Criminal Appeal", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "scenario_keywords": [], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Disposed", "disposition": "Disposed"}}
22222222-0006-4000-8000-000000000006	Writ Petition No. 13 of 1950	A.K. Gopalan v. State of Madras	Supreme Court of India	1950	Hon'ble Harilal Kania, C.J., Saiyid Fazl Ali, M. Patanjali Sastri, Mehr Chand Mahajan, B.K. Mukherjea, Sudhi Ranjan Das, JJ.	A.K. Gopalan v. State of Madras	["Constitution of India", "Preventive Detention Act, 1950"]	["Article 21", "Article 19", "Article 22", "Article 14"]	["procedure established by law", "due process", "separate silos", "preventive detention", "personal liberty"]	Early ruling holding that 'procedure established by law' under Article 21 merely required enacted legislative procedure without examining substantive reasonableness, treating Articles 19 and 21 as mutually exclusive silos. Overruled by Maneka Gandhi (1978).	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_1950_WP_No_13_of_1950_22220006.pdf	e2b8c12c00cdf5c55c2577606ebbaad1e18f6c3d97928da108b21aa26087ed4a	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:20:37.23059+05:30	2026-10-25 11:20:37.230593+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.346326+05:30	\N	[]	'Procedure established by law' in Article 21 means procedure prescribed by the statute, not principles of natural justice. Articles 19 and 21 are mutually exclusive. [OVERRULED by Maneka Gandhi v. Union of India (1978)].	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Maneka Gandhi v. Union of India", "status": "Good Law (Foundational 7-Judge Landmark)", "case_id": "11111111-0008-4000-8000-000000000008", "citation": "(1978) 1 SCC 248", "ratio_key": "Substantive due process and golden triangle under Article 21", "treatment": "Followed / Relied Upon"}]	\N	unverified	Overruled by Supreme Court (Maneka Gandhi)	Supreme Court of India\nWrit Petition No. 13 of 1950\nA.K. Gopalan v. State of Madras\nBench: Hon'ble Harilal Kania, C.J., Saiyid Fazl Ali, M. Patanjali Sastri, Mehr Chand Mahajan, B.K. Mukherjea, Sudhi Ranjan Das, JJ.\nDate: 1950-05-19\nCitation: AIR 1950 SC 27\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\n'Procedure established by law' in Article 21 means procedure prescribed by the statute, not principles of natural justice. Articles 19 and 21 are mutually exclusive. [OVERRULED by Maneka Gandhi v. Union of India (1978)].\n\nSUMMARY:\nEarly ruling holding that 'procedure established by law' under Article 21 merely required enacted legislative procedure without examining substantive reasonableness, treating Articles 19 and 21 as mutually exclusive silos. Overruled by Maneka Gandhi (1978).\n\nSTATUTES: Article 21, Article 19, Article 22, Article 14\nDISPOSITION: Preventive Detention Upheld (Overruled in 1978)	2	f	SCIN010000131950	Writ Petition	1950-05-19	Constitution Bench (6 Judges)	A.K. Gopalan	State of Madras	Senior Advocate M.K. Nambyar	Advocate General K. Rajah Ayyar	Personal Liberty & Due Process	The court originally held that as long as Parliament passes a law, courts cannot check if the procedure is fair or just. This narrow view was overruled in Maneka Gandhi, which established that procedure must be just, fair, and reasonable.	0	AIR 1950 SC 27	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "A.K. Gopalan v. State of Madras", "case_id": "22222222-0006-4000-8000-000000000006", "case_no": "Writ Petition No. 13 of 1950", "parties": "A.K. Gopalan v. State of Madras", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "A.K. Gopalan", "respondent": "State of Madras", "legal_domain": "Personal Liberty & Due Process", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Writ Petition / Criminal Appeal", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "21", "canonical": "Section 21 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "19", "canonical": "Section 19 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "22", "canonical": "Section 22 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "14", "canonical": "Section 14 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["constitutional_fundamental"], "scenario_keywords": ["article 14", "article 21"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Dismissed", "disposition": "Dismissed"}}
22222222-0016-4000-8000-000000000016	Writ Petition (Civil) No. 422 of 1994	R. Rajagopal @ R.R. Gopal & Nakkheeran Publications v. State of Tamil Nadu & Others	Supreme Court of India	1994	Hon'ble B.P. Jeevan Reddy & S.C. Sen, JJ.	R. Rajagopal @ R.R. Gopal & Nakkheeran Publications v. State of Tamil Nadu & Others	["Indian Penal Code, 1860"]	["Article 19(1)(a)", "Article 19(2)", "Article 21", "Section 499 IPC"]	["prior restraint", "Auto Shankar autobiography", "freedom of press", "public records", "privacy of public officials"]	Landmark ruling laying down that public officials cannot obtain prior injunctions to suppress press publications relating to their official acts. Right to privacy does not extend to information contained in public records.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_1994_WP_Civil_No_422_of_1994_22220016.pdf	f1d5b48407f57ddd9f9e4b6e7c52fa1f1622c0c695c97feec1f1a89dca0d6252	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 11:20:37.454268+05:30	2026-10-25 11:20:37.454272+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.696684+05:30	\N	[]	Citizens have a right to safeguard the privacy of their home, family, and person. However, once a matter becomes a matter of public record including court proceedings, the right to privacy no longer subsists and the press can publish without prior censorship.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [1994) 6 SCC 632]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "1994) 6 SCC 632", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding Freedom of Press Landmark)	Supreme Court of India\nWrit Petition (Civil) No. 422 of 1994\nR. Rajagopal alias R.R. Gopal and Another v. State of Tamil Nadu and Others\nBench: Hon'ble B.P. Jeevan Reddy & S.C. Sen, JJ.\nDate: 1994-10-07\nCitation: (1994) 6 SCC 632\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nCitizens have a right to safeguard the privacy of their home, family, and person. However, once a matter becomes a matter of public record including court proceedings, the right to privacy no longer subsists and the press can publish without prior censorship.\n\nSUMMARY:\nLandmark ruling laying down that public officials cannot obtain prior injunctions to suppress press publications relating to their official acts. Right to privacy does not extend to information contained in public records.\n\nSTATUTES: Article 19(1)(a), Article 19(2), Article 21, Section 499 IPC\nDISPOSITION: Prior Restraint on Press Publication Refused	2	f	SCIN010004221994	Writ Petition	1994-10-07	Division Bench (2 Judges)	R. Rajagopal @ R.R. Gopal & Nakkheeran Publications	State of Tamil Nadu & Others	Senior Advocate Soli J. Sorabjee	Senior Advocate K.K. Venugopal	Media Law, Freedom of Speech & Privacy	The press cannot be gagged with prior injunctions by government officials. Anything based on public court records can be reported freely without fear of defamation lawsuits.	0	(1994) 6 SCC 632	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "R. Rajagopal alias R.R. Gopal and Another v. State of Tamil Nadu and Others", "case_id": "22222222-0016-4000-8000-000000000016", "case_no": "Writ Petition (Civil) No. 422 of 1994", "parties": "R. Rajagopal @ R.R. Gopal & Nakkheeran Publications v. State of Tamil Nadu & Others", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "R. Rajagopal @ R.R. Gopal & Nakkheeran Publications", "respondent": "State of Tamil Nadu & Others", "legal_domain": "Media Law, Freedom of Speech & Privacy", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Writ Petition / Criminal Appeal", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "19", "canonical": "Section 19 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "21", "canonical": "Section 21 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "IPC", "section": "499", "canonical": "Section 499 IPC", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["constitutional_fundamental"], "scenario_keywords": ["article 21"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed in Part", "disposition": "Allowed in Part"}}
