he could do anything. 4.	[]	Principles governing criminal jurisdiction and interim protections.	[]	Disposed	[]	\N	unverified	Good Law	CRL OP(MD). Nos.20105 and 21721 of 2026\nBEFORE THE MADURAI BENCH OF MADRAS HIGH COURT\n( Criminal Jurisdiction )\nRESERVED ON:30.09.2026\nPRONOUNCED ON: 09.10.2026\nPRESENT\nThe Hon`ble Mr.Justice K.MURALI SHANKAR\nCRL OP(MD). Nos.20105 and 21721 of 2026\nCrl.O.P.(MD)No.20105 of 2026:\nSivasankari @ Sankari ... Petitioner/\nAccused No.6\nVs\n1.The State of Tamilnadu,\nrepresented by\nThe Inspector of Police,\nOthakadai Police Station,\nMadurai.\n(Crime No.395 of 2026) ...Respondent/Complainant\n2.Kamarani ... 2nd Respondent\nFor Petitioner : Mr.S.Prabhu\nFor Respondent : Mr.T.Lenin Kumar\nAdditional Public Prosecutor\n: Mr.R.Anand\nfor Defacto complainant\n1/15\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). Nos.20105 and 21721 of 2026\nCrl.O.P.(MD)No.21721 of 2026:\n1.S.Palanivel\n2.P.Kangarajan ... Petitioners/\nAccused No.2 and 4\nVs\nState represented by\nThe Inspector of Police,\nOthakadai Police Station,\nMadurai.\nIn Cr.No.395 of 2026. ... Respondent/Complainant\nFor Petitioners : Mr.E.Sathish Rajkumar\nFor Respondent : Mr.T.Lenin Kumar\nAdditional Public Prosecutor\n: Mr.R.Anand\nfor Defacto compalinant\nPETITION FOR ANTICIPATORY BAIL Under Section 482\nof B.N.S.S.\nPRAYER :-\nFor Anticipatory Bail in Crime No.395 of 2026 on\nthe file of the Respondent police.\nCOMMON ORDER : The Court made the following order :-\nThe petitioner/accused in Crl.O.P.(MD)No.20105 of\n2026, who apprehends arrest at the hands of the\n2/15\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). Nos.20105 and 21721 of 2026\nrespondent police for the offences punishable under\nSections 329(4), 351(2), 305(a), 316(2) and 318(4) of\nBNS in Crime No.395 of 2026 on the file of the\nrespondent police, seeks anticipatory bail.\n2. The petitioners/accused in Crl.O.P.(MD)No.\n21721 of 2026, who apprehend arrest at the hands of\nthe respondent police for the offences punishable\nunder Sections 329(4), 351(2), 305(a), 316(2) and\n318(4) of BNS in Crime No.395 of 2026 on the file of\nthe respondent police, seek anticipatory bail.\n3. The case of the prosecution is that the\ndefacto complainant borrowed a sum of Rs.60,00,000/-\nfrom the sixth accused and handed over five cheque\nleaves and sale deed as security, that after\nrepayment of loan amount, the sixth accused did not\nreturn the security documents, that on the\ninstigation of the sixth accused, the other accused\npersons went to the defacto complainant's house,\npicked their cell phones, deleted the phone chat\nrecords, forcibly took the cheques as well as the car\n3/15\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). Nos.20105 and 21721 of 2026\nkeys and caused intimidation. It is the further case\nof the prosecution that the second accused while\nthreatening the defacto complainant and her husband,\nhe displayed on his mobile phone a photograph showing\nhim along with a sitting Judge of this Court and\nrepresented that on account of his proximity to the\nsaid Judge, he could do anything.\n4. The case of the petitioners/accused 2, 4 and\n6 is that they are innocents and that they have been\nfalsely implicated in the above case.\n5. The case of the sixth accused is that she is\na Doctor by profession and is residing at Chennai,\nthat the defacto complainant and her husband were\njointly running online trading business and on\nbelieving their words, the petitioner and others\ninvested huge amounts, that several others have\nalready lodged complaints against the defacto\ncomplainant and her husband at Coimbatore, that the\ndefacto complainant in order to escape from the\ncomplaints, lodged the above false complain, that the\n4/15\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). Nos.20105 and 21721 of 2026\nsixth accused appeared before the District Crime\nBranch, Madurai on 04.072026 and submitted her\ndetailed explanation, that no such incident had\noccurred as alleged by the defacto complainant and\nthat the sixth accused is in no way connected with\nthe alleged occurrence.\n6. The case of the accused 2 and 4 is that the\nsecond accused is a friend of the fourth accused,\nthat the second accused visited the residence of the\ndefacto complainant in an attempt to mediate, pacify,\nand facilitate a resolution of the ongoing dispute\nbetween the parties, that the second accused has\nnever had any monetary dealings, contractual\nrelations or financial transactions with the defacto\ncomplainant, that the other allegation of extortion\nagainst the accused 2 and 4 is baseless and\nfabricated, that the defacto complainant voluntarily\nhanded over four cheques directly to the fourth\naccused as security towards a pre-existing liability\nas the complainant had previously received a sum of\nRs.43,28,800/- from the fourth accused, that the\n5/15\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). Nos.20105 and 21721 of 2026\ncomplainant's allegation that the second accused\ndisplayed a photograph of a sitting Judge of this\nCourt on his mobile phone and intimidated her by\nclaiming close proximity to the said Judge is\ncompletely fabricated, malicious and devoid of truth,\nthat the second accused voluntarily surrendered his\nApple iPhone to the respondent police, which was\nsubsequently transmitted to the Cyber Crime Wing for\ndetailed forensic analysis and upon comprehensive\nforensic examination and retrieval of all data and\nmedia files from the said device, no such photograph,\nimage or related material as alleged by the\ncomplainant was found, that the fourth accused has\nalready lodged a specific complaint against the\ndefacto complainant on 08.07.2026 before the\nVelakoundampatti Police Station, Namakkal District\nalleging the acts of forgery and fabrication and on\nthat basis, CSR NO.113 of 2026 is pending and that\nthe present proceedings are a counter blast\ninstituted with malafide intention to suppress the\nearlier genuine grievance of the fourth accused.\n6/15\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). Nos.20105 and 21721 of 2026\n7. It is the further case of the accused 2 and 4\nis that the first accused has already preferred a\ncomplaint against the defacto complainant and her\nhusband before the District Crime Branch, Madurai and\nthe case came to be registered on 19.05.2026, in\nCr.No.11 of 2026 for the offences under Sections\n316(2) and 318(4) of BNS and that the defacto\ncomplainant and her husband persuaded the first\naccused to invest substantial amount into a forex\ntrading venture with the dishonest intention from the\ninception, siphoned and swindled the invested money\nby means of fabrication and forgery.\n8. It is pertinent to note that the main\ncomplaint is that there were financial transaction\nbetween the defacto complainant and the other accused\nand that on 05.06.2026, the accused persons went to\nthe residence of the defacto complainant, intimidated\nher and her husband and forcedly obtained signed\ncheques and other articles. But according the second\naccused, he had no independent financial transaction\nwith the defacto complainant and had accompanied the\n7/15\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). Nos.20105 and 21721 of 2026\nfourth accused only with a view to mediate and\namicably settle the dispute between the fourth\naccused and the defacto complainant.\n9. As already pointed out, a specific and\nserious allegation was made in the complaint stating\nthat the second accused while threatening the defacto\ncomplainant and her husband, displayed on his mobile\nphone a photograph showing him along with a sitting\nJudge of this Court and represented that on account\nof his proximity to the said Judge, he could get\nanything done. Taking note of the said allegation,\nthis Court, by earlier, order suo motu impleaded the\ndefacto complainant as second respondent and directed\nfor issuance of notice to her.\n10. It is not in dispute that during the course\nof enquiry, the second accused has produced his Apple\niphone before the Investigating Officer and the same\nwas sent for forensic examination.\n8/15\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). Nos.20105 and 21721 of 2026\n11. The learned Additional Public Prosecutor\nwould submit that the entire contents / data\navailable in the said mobile phone were retrieved and\nexamined and no photograph of the nature alleged by\nthe defacto complainant was found therein. The\nlearned Additional Public Prosecutor would further\nsubmit that as of now no other material has been\ncollected to substantiate the allegation that the\nsecond accused had displayed such photograph to the\ndefacto complainant and her husband.\n12. When the matter was taken up for final\nhearing on 30.09.2026, the defacto complainant was\npresent before this Court and the learned Counsel for\nthe second accused handed over the cheques, which had\nallegedly been handed over by the defacto complainant\nin connection with the financial dealings between the\nparties, in open Court.\n13. The learned Additional Public Prosecutor\nwould further submit that considering the report of\nthe forensic examination and the materials collected\n9/15\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). Nos.20105 and 21721 of 2026\nso far, the possibility of the allegation involving\nthe name of the sitting Judge of this Court having\nbeen deliberately introduced by the defacto\ncomplainant cannot be ruled out.\n14. Since the investigation is still in\nprogress, this Court is not inclined to record any\nfinding regarding the truth or otherwise of the said\nallegation at this stage. But at the same time, this\nCourt cannot lose sight of the seriousness of the\nmatter. The name and office of a Judge of a\nConstitutional Court cannot be casually or\ndeliberately included in a criminal complaint so as\nto give credibility or gravity to the accusation or\nto denigrate the person against whom such accusations\nare made. In case, if the investigation reveals that\nthe allegation involving the name of the Judge was\ndeliberately and falsely introduced by the defacto\ncomplainant, she shall necessarily have to face all\nconsequences that may follow in accordance with law,\nincluding any criminal or other proceedings warranted\n10/15\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). Nos.20105 and 21721 of 2026\nby the facts. The respondent police shall\ninvestigate this aspect and proceed further.\n15. Regarding the sixth accused, she is stated\nto be a skin Doctor, practising and residing at\nChennai. As already pointed out, it is her specific\ncase that she had invested money with the defacto\ncomplainant and her husband in connection with their\nonline trading activities and that when the amount\nwas not returned, the complaint came to be lodged\nagainst them.\n16. According to the sixth accused, she had\nalready appeared before the District Crime Branch,\nMadurai and furnished her explanation. It is also\npertinent to note that there existed financial\ntransaction between the parties, more importantly\nbetween the defacto complainant and the accused 4 and\n6 and filing of complaints and counter complaints\nbetween the parties. No doubt, the rival allegations\nof cheating and forcebly obtaining the cheques are\nmatters required to be investigated. But at the same\n11/15\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). Nos.20105 and 21721 of 2026\ntime, considering the materials gathered so far, more\nparticularly the result of the forensic examination\nof the mobile phone of the second accused and also\nthe fact that the relevant transactions are capable\nof being investigated on the basis of the documentary\nand electronic devices, this Court is of the view\nthat the custodial interrogation of the accused 2,4\nand 6 is not warranted.\n17. Considering the above facts and\ncircumstances, the nature of the allegations levelled\nand also the undertaking of the petitioners to co-\noperate with the investigation and that the\npetitioners are not having any pending previous cases\nfor serious offences or other similar offences, this\nCourt is inclined to grant anticipatory bail to the\npetitioners with certain conditions.\n18. Accordingly, the petitioners are ordered to\nbe released on bail in the event of arrest or on\ntheir appearance, within a period of fifteen days\nfrom the date of receipt of a copy of this order,\n12/15\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). Nos.20105 and 21721 of 2026\nbefore the learned Judicial Magistrate Court, Melur,\nMadurai District, on condition that the petitioners\nshall execute a bond for a sum of Rs.25,000/-(Rupees\nTwenty Five Thousand only) each with two sureties\neach for a like sum to the satisfaction of the\nlearned Magistrate concerned and on further\nconditions that:\n[a]the petitioners and the sureties shall affix\ntheir photographs and left thumb impression in the\nsurety bond and the Magistrate may obtain a copy of\ntheir Aadhaar card or bank pass book to ensure their\nidentity;\n[b]the petitioners/accused 2 and 4 shall report\nbefore the respondent police daily at 10.30 a.m., for\na period of one month and thereafter, as and when\nrequired for interrogation and the petitioner/6th\naccused shall appear before the respondent police as\nand when required for interrogation;\n[c]the petitioners shall not tamper with the\nevidence or witness either during investigation or\ntrial;\n13/15\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). Nos.20105 and 21721 of 2026\n[d]the petitioners shall not abscond either\nduring investigation or trial;\n[e]On breach of any of the aforesaid conditions,\nthe learned Magistrate/Trial Court is entitled to\ntake appropriate action against the petitioners in\naccordance with law as if the conditions have been\nimposed and the petitioners released on bail by the\nlearned Magistrate/Trial Court himself as laid down\nby the Hon'ble Supreme Court in P.K.Shaji vs. State\nof Kerala [(2005)AIR SCW 5560];\n[f]If the accused thereafter absconds, a fresh\nFIR can be registered under Section 269 BNS, 2023.\n(K M S J)\n09.10.2026\nSSL\nTO\n1. The Judicial Magistrate Court, Melur,\nMadurai District.\n2. The Inspector of Police,\nOthakadai Police Station,\nMadurai.\n3. The Additional Public Prosecutor,\nMadurai Bench of Madras High Court,\nMadurai.\n14/15\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). Nos.20105 and 21721 of 2026\nK.MURALI SHANKAR,J\nSSL\nPRE-DELIVERY ORDER MADE IN\nCRL OP(MD). Nos.20105 and 21721 of 2026\n09.10.2026\n15/15\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis	15	t	HCMD010201052026	Criminal Original Petition (Bail)	2026-10-09	Single Bench	Sivasankari @ Sankari	.	Mr.S.Prabhu	Mr.T.Lenin Kumar Additional Public Prosecutor	Civil Law	In this criminal original petition under Criminal Law, the Court ruled on the dispute between Sivasankari @ Sankari and ..	0	2026 LexGuard (Crim) 0B8C4D	{"court": "Madras High Court (Madurai Bench)", "title": "Sivasankari @ Sankari v. .", "case_id": "0b8c4db5-a594-4cbf-8352-d1eea1cbc564", "case_no": "CRL OP(MD). Nos.20105 and 21721 of 2026", "parties": "Sivasankari @ Sankari v. .", "category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "petitioner": "Sivasankari @ Sankari", "respondent": ".", "legal_domain": "Civil Law", "category_facts": {"category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "surety_count": 2, "custody_period": null, "application_type": "Anticipatory Bail", "bail_bond_amount": 25000.0, "trial_court_fine": null, "reason_for_relief": null, "reporting_condition": "report\\nbefore the respondent police daily at 10", "trial_court_sentence": null, "trial_court_fine_paid": false}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "scenario_keywords": [], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Disposed of with directions.", "disposition": "Disposed of with directions."}}
