of India\n... Respondent(s) / State\n\nSTATUTORY PROVISIONS & ACTS REFERRED:\nRepresentation of the People Act 1951, Constitution of India\n\nSECTIONS & CLAUSES UNDER CONSIDERATION:\nRPA Section 33A, Article 19(1)(a), Article 324\n\nKEY LEGAL DOMAINS & TOPICS:\nElectoral Reforms, Voter Right to Information, Asset Disclosure, Article 19(1)(a), Free and Fair Elections\n\n================================================================================\n                                   JUDGMENT\n================================================================================\n\n1. FACTUAL MATRIX:\nPetition seeking direction to ECI to mandate disclosure of virtual digital assets (cryptocurrency, NFTs) and offshore trust holdings by contesting candidates.\n\n2. ISSUES FRAMED FOR ADJUDICATION:\n1. Does voter right to information under Article 19(1)(a) encompass disclosure of digital and offshore assets of candidates?\n\n3. STATUTORY FRAMEWORK & PRECEDENTS:\nVoters' right to know candidate credentials flows from freedom of speech and expression under Article 19(1)(a) as held in Union of India v. ADR (2002).\n\n4. RATIO DECIDENDI:\nRight to informed choice is an integral facet of Article 19(1)(a); candidates must disclose all digital assets, cryptocurrencies, and offshore holdings in Form 26 affidavits.\n\n5. FINAL DISPOSITION & ORDER:\nWrit Petition Allowed; Mandatory disclosure of candidate digital assets and offshore interests directed.\n\nDecreed and ordered accordingly.\n\nHon'ble Constitution Bench (5 Judges)\nSupreme Court of India\n[LEXGUARD CRYPTOGRAPHIC VERIFIED REPOSITORY RECORD]\n	3	t	SCIN010000892026	Writ Petition	\N	Single Bench	Association for Democratic Reforms	Union of India	\N	\N	Civil Law	In this writ petition under Constitutional Law, the Court ruled on the dispute between Association for Democratic Reforms and Union of India.	0	2026 LexGuard (Writ) 0BB530	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Association for Democratic Reforms v. Union of India