05 001\n2. The Director\nDirectorate of Higher and Technical Education\nLawspet, Puducherry - 605 008\n__________\nPage5 of 20\n\nWP No. 36623 of 2026\n3. The Director,\nDirectorate of Sports and Youth Affairs\nKamarajar Centenary Complex,\nNellithope,\nAnna Nagar,\nPuducherry- 605 005\n4. The Convenor (CENTAC)\nKamarajar Manimandapam,\nNear Sivaji Statute,\nKaruvadikuppam,\nPuducherry 605 008\n5. The Co-ordinator (Admission),\n(CENTAC),\nKamarajar Manimandapam,\nNear Sivaji Statute,\nKaruvadikuppam,\nPuducherry - 605 008\n..Respondent(s)\nW.P.No.36623 of 2026\nPrayer: Petition filed under Article 226 of the Constitution of India\npraying for the issuance of a writ of certiorari to call for records pertaining to\nthe Provisional Allotment list Ref.No.CENTAC/UG-NEET/2026/13 dated\n03.09.2026 issued by the fourth respondent and quash the same insofar as the\npetitioners have been omitted there from and consequently direct the fourth\nrespondent to include the names of the petitioners in the provisional Allotment\nList dated 03.09.2026 in accordance with their entitlement under the applicable\nMSP quota and the marks/merit secured by them.\n__________\nPage6 of 20\n\nWP No. 36623 of 2026\nWP No. 28148 of 2026\nPrayer: Petition filed under Article 226 of the Constitution of India praying for\nthe issuance of a writ of certiorarified mandamus to call for the records of the\n3rd respondent in G.O.16 dated 14.07.2026 and to quash the same as\nillegal,arbitrary and in so far as it seeks to exclude the sport/game of Pencak\nSilat from the list of games considered for admission to educational institution\nunder sports quota and consequently direct the respondents to include the\ngame/sport of Pencak Silat in the list of games under sports quota for\ncounselling by CENTAC for admission to educational institutions this year.\nWP No. 29331 of 2026\nPrayer: Petition filed under Article 226 of the Constitution of India praying for\nthe issuance of a writ of certiorarified mandamus calling for the concerned\nrecords relating to the G.O.Ms.No.16 dated 14.07.2026 issued by the 1st\nrespondent and followed by the corrigendum\nRef.No.CENTAC/UG-NN/2026/08 dated 15.7.26 issued by the 1st respondent\nin sofar as it orders for deletion of Atya patya from the list of approved games\nand consequently direct the respondents to consider the petitioners under MSP\nquota by the respondents 4 and 5 for the purpose of medical admission on the\nbasis of the certificates possessed by them.\n__________\nPage7 of 20\n\nWP No. 36623 of 2026\nWP No. 33709 of 2026\nPrayer: Petition filed under Article 226 of the Constitution of India\npraying for the issuance of a writ of mandamus to forbear the respondents from\nfilling up MSP Quota in respect of Non NEET and NEET for the year 2026-\n2027 on the basis of the G.O.Ms.No.16 dated 14.07.2026.\nFor Petitioner(s): Mr.L.Chandrakumar\nfor Mr.M.Gnanasekar\nin W.P.Nos.36623, 29331 and 33709 of 2026\nMr.Vikram Veerasamy\nfor TVJ Associates\nin W.P.No.28148 of 2026\nFor Respondent(s): Mr.AR.L.Sundaresan\nAdditional Solicitor General of India\nassisted by Ms.V.Usha\nAdditional Government Pleader\n(Puducherry)\nin W.P.Nos.36623, 29331 and 33709 of 2026\nMr.AR.L.Sundaresan\nAdditional Solicitor General of India\nassisted by Mr.Ramaswamy Meyyappan\nGovernment Advocate\n(Puducherry)\nin W.P.No.28148 of 2026\n- - - - -\n__________\nPage8 of 20\n\nWP No. 36623 of 2026\nC O M M O N O R D E R\nWrit Petition No.36623 of 2026 has been filed to quash the order dated\n3/9/2026 passed by the fourth respondent in Provisional Allotment list\nRef.No.CENTAC/UG-NEET/2026/13 and consequently, direct the fourth\nrespondent to include the names of the petitioners in the provisional Allotment\nList dated 03.09.2026 in accordance with their entitlement under the applicable\nMSP quota and the marks/merit secured by them.\n2. Writ Petition No.28148 of 2026 has been filed to quash the\nG.O.Ms.No.16 dated 14/7/2026 issued by the third respondent to exclude the\nsport/game of Pencak Silat from the list of games considered for admission to\neducational institution under sports quota and consequently direct the\nrespondents to include the game/sport of Pencak Silat in the list of games under\nsports quota for counselling by CENTAC for admission to educational\ninstitutions this year.\n3. Writ Petition No.29331 of 2026 has been filed to quash\nG.O.Ms.No.16 dated 14/7/2026 issued by the first respondent and followed by\nthe corrigendum Ref.No.CENTAC/UG-NN/2026/08 dated 15.7.26 issued by the\n1st respondent in sofar as it orders for deletion of Atya Patya from the list of\napproved games and consequently, direct the respondents to consider the\n__________\nPage9 of 20\n\nWP No. 36623 of 2026\npetitioners under MSP quota by the respondents 4 and 5 for the purpose of\nmedical admission on the basis of the certificates possessed by them.\n4. Writ Petition No.33709 of 2026 has been filed to forbear the\nrespondents from filling up MSP Quota in respect of Non NEET and NEET for\nthe year 2026-2027 on the basis of the G.O.Ms.No.16 dated 14.07.2026.\n5. Briefly, the main grievance of the petitioners in all these writ petitions\nconcerns their applications for admission to different courses in colleges within\nthe Union Territory of Puducherry. The Centralised Admission Committee\n(CENTAC) administers these admissions. All the petitioners claim seats under\nthe Meritorious Sports Person (MSP) quota, under which a specified percentage\nof seats is reserved for admission to NEET and non-NEET courses.\n6. The Government of Puducherry/CENTAC issued notices on 17/4/2026\nand 14/5/2026 for admission to NEET and non-NEET courses. The last date for\nfiling applications was the end of May, 2026.\n7. On 14/7/2026, after the deadline of applications, the Government of\nPuducherry issued G.O.Ms.No.16, Chief Secretariat (Higher & Technical\nEducation), whereby the number of sports disciplines previously recognised for\n__________\nPage10 of 20\n\nWP No. 36623 of 2026\neligibility under the MSP quota was reduced from 65 to 35 by deleting 30\ndisciplines.\n8. The petitioners are sportspersons who participated in sports disciplines\nincluded among the 30 disciplines deleted from consideration under the MSP\nquota.\n9. The principal submission of the learned counsel appearing for the\npetitioners is that, on the date of their applications, all 65 sports disciplines were\nrecognised as eligible for the sports quota. However, without notice to those\naffected, the Government decided to delete 30 sports disciplines and retain only\n35, which caused prejudice to the petitioners.\n10. It is contended that the notification adversely affects students who\napplied under the MSP quota. According to the petitioners, any such change can\nbe implemented only from the next academic year and cannot be applied to the\ncurrent admission process, as its application to that process would violate\nArticle 14 of the Constitution of India.\n11. The learned counsel appearing for the petitioners relied upon the\njudgment of the Hon’ble Supreme Court in Col. A.S. Sangwan v. Union of\nIndia and Others, 1980 Supp SCC 559, and submitted that Article 14 of the\n__________\nPage11 of 20\n\nWP No. 36623 of 2026\nConstitution of India requires any change in policy to be made fairly, without\ngiving the impression that the authorities are acting arbitrarily or on the basis of\nulterior considerations.\n12. On the other hand, the learned Additional Solicitor General appearing\non behalf of CENTAC submitted that Annexure 15 to the NEET based courses\nbrochure for 2026–2027, specifically identifies 35 sports disciplines as eligible\nfor the sports quota. Since the petitioners participated in sports disciplines not\nincluded in Annexure 15, their claim for consideration under the MSP quota\ncannot be entertained. It was further submitted that the Government had taken a\npolicy decision and that interference under Article 226 of the Constitution of\nIndia was unwarranted in the absence of arbitrariness or an ulterior motive.\n13. The learned Additional Solicitor General also relied upon the\njudgment of the Hon’ble Supreme Court in State of Tamil Nadu and Another v.\nP. Krishnamurthy and Others, (2006) 4 SCC 517, in support of the submission\nthat the State Government is empowered to make or amend rules within the\nscope of its authority and that legislation carries a presumption of\nconstitutionality or validity.\n14. The learned counsel appearing for the petitioners sought to\ndemonstrate why the deleted sports disciplines ought to have been retained,\n__________\nPage12 of 20\n\nWP No. 36623 of 2026\npointing out that they are recognised in other selection processes, including\nrecruitment by the State Government. That contention does not, by itself,\nwarrant substituting this Court’s assessment for the policy decision. It is not the\npetitioners’ case that the G.O. was issued with an ulterior motive to benefit\nparticular named candidates or to deny consideration to particular named\ncandidates. The decision was taken by a panel after considering various factors.\n15. The absence of an allegation of mala fides does not, by itself, exclude\njudicial review. A policy decision must nevertheless satisfy Article 14. The\nCourt’s scrutiny concerns the legality and fairness of the decision and its\nimplementation; it does not extend to selecting the sports disciplines that the\nCourt considers preferable. The power to revise the policy and the question\nwhether the revision can govern an admission process already commenced\nrequire separate consideration.\n16. In fact, Annexure 15 to the NEET based courses brochure specifies\nonly 35 sports disciplines as eligible for the sports quota. Nothing has been\nplaced on record to show that the brochure produced by the respondents was\nissued after G.O.Ms.No.16. The petitioners have not produced any brochure for\nadmission to NEET-based courses recognising 65 sports disciplines as eligible\nfor the sports quota.\n__________\nPage13 of 20\n\nWP No. 36623 of 2026\n17. In these circumstances, the material placed before this Court does not\nestablish that the eligibility conditions published for the NEET admission\nprocess were altered after applications were invited. The claim concerning\nNEET admissions must therefore be distinguished from the claim concerning\nnon-NEET admissions, for which Annexure 14 to the brochure identifies 65\nsports disciplines.\n18. The judgment relied on by the counsel appearing for the petitioners\nin Col.A.S.Sangwan’s case, differs on facts since the Hon’ble Supreme Court\nwas dealing with a situation where specific Officers were denied opportunity of\nposting. In the said circumstances, the Hon’ble Supreme Court had made such\nobservations. The said observations in the present facts of the case cannot be\napplied.\n19. The petitioners in W.P.Nos.36623, 33709 and 29331 of 2026\napproached this Court after the seats had been filled during counselling. By\norder dated 10/9/2026, this Court passed the following direction:\n“5. Since there is only one seat under the MSP quota and\nthe same has already been filled up, this Court directs to reserve\none additional seat under MSP quota in Government College or\notherwise, in private College under Government quota, if any seats\n__________\nPage14 of 20\n\nWP No. 36623 of 2026\navailable under General category, as the petitioners mark would\nbe higher than the mark obtained by the original allottee, in the\nevent if the subject sport is included in the list for MSP quota as\nprevious academic years.”\n20. The candidates already selected under the MSP quota have not been\nimpleaded as parties to these writ petitions. Any order affecting their selection\nor admission would operate to their prejudice without affording them an\nopportunity of being heard. Consequently, no adverse order can be passed\nagainst those candidates in these proceedings, and their selections and\nadmissions shall remain undisturbed. 20. In the peculiar circumstances of this\ncase, although the seat originally available under the MSP quota had already\nbeen filled, this Court considered it appropriate, by the aforesaid interim order,\nto direct the reservation of one additional seat. The learned Additional Solicitor\nGeneral submitted that, pursuant to the orders of this Court, one additional seat\nhas been kept vacant and will be filled in accordance with the directions of this\nCourt.\n21. Admissions already made cannot be unsettled. The petitioners’\neligibility for consideration against the seat reserved pursuant to the interim\norder can be examined by CENTAC. This Court finds no reason to quash\n__________\nPage15 of 20\n\nWP No. 36623 of 2026\nG.O.Ms.No.16, Chief Secretariat (Higher & Technical Education), Government\nof Puducherry, dated 14/7/2026.\n22. The fact that the petitioners approached this Court after the original\nallotments had been made limits the relief that can now be granted, particularly\nwhen the selected candidates are not parties to these proceedings. Nevertheless,\nthe additional seat kept vacant pursuant to the interim order permits\nconsideration of the petitioners’ claims without displacing any admitted\ncandidate. The limited relief is therefore confined to that vacant seat in the\npeculiar circumstances of this case; it does not reopen completed counselling or\nconfer a general right to seek admission after the prescribed schedule.\n23. Insofar as NEET courses are concerned, the brochure already\nspecified that only 35 sports disciplines would be eligible, and that position is\nupheld. Insofar as non-NEET courses are concerned, for the current academic\nyear and the ongoing admission process, all 65 sports disciplines mentioned in\nAnnexure 14 to the brochure shall be considered.\n__________\nPage16 of 20\n\nWP No. 36623 of 2026\n24. The limited direction concerning non-NEET admissions preserves the\neligibility conditions published for the ongoing process. It does not invalidate\nthe Government’s revised classification of sports disciplines for future\nadmission processes. A candidate’s entitlement to consideration under the\npublished conditions is distinct from an entitlement to admission, which\nremains subject to comparative merit and the applicable admission\nrequirements.\n25. The distinction rests on the eligibility conditions published for the\nrespective admission processes. For non-NEET admissions, exclusion of\ndisciplines included in Annexure 14 during the ongoing process would\nwithdraw the basis on which the affected candidates sought consideration.\nFairness under Article 14 requires that the limited consideration directed in this\ncase proceed on those published conditions. This does not prevent the\nGovernment from prescribing the revised list for future admission processes\nafter duly notifying it.\n26. The additional seat kept vacant pursuant to the interim order shall be\nfilled by CENTAC in accordance with the foregoing directions. Accordingly, the\npetitioners eligible under those directions may compete for that additional seat,\n__________\nPage17 of 20\n\nWP No. 36623 of 2026\nand their marks based on the sports disciplines in which they participated shall\nbe considered.\n27. Consideration against the reserved seat shall be undertaken in\naccordance with the applicable eligibility requirements and comparative merit.\nThis direction does not confer an automatic right to admission upon any\npetitioner and shall not disturb admissions already completed.\n28. The reference to one additional seat is to be read in the context of the\ninterim direction, which contemplated reservation against an available vacant\nseat. It shall not be construed as a direction to increase the sanctioned intake of\nany institution. CENTAC shall implement the direction within the applicable\nadmission schedule and subject to the governing statutory and regulatory\nrequirements.\n29. Accordingly, these writ petitions are disposed of in terms of the\nforegoing directions. The challenge seeking to quash G.O.Ms.No.16 is rejected,\nsubject to the limited direction concerning its application to the ongoing non-\n__________\nPage18 of 20\n\nWP No. 36623 of 2026\nNEET admission process. No costs. Consequently, the connected miscellaneous\npetitions are closed.\n(K.SURENDER,J)\n9/10/2026\nNeutral Citation: Yes/No\nmvs.\nTo\n1. The Secretary to Government (Higher and technical education)\nEducation Department, chief secretariat, gubert avenue,\nPuducherry - 605001.\n2. The Director\nDirectorate of Higher and Technical education, lawspet,\npuducherry - 605008.\n3. The Director\nDirectorate of sports and youth affairs, kamarajar centenary complex,\nnellithope, anna nagar, puducherry - 605005.\n4. The convenor (CENTAC)\nkamarajarmanimandapam, near sivaji statute, karuvadikuppam,\npuducherry - 605008.\n5. The Co-Ordinator (Admission)\n(CENTAC)\nKamarajar manimandapam, near sivaji statute, karuvadikuppam, puducherry\n- 605008.The Government of Puducherry\nBy its Chief Secretary,\nChief Secretariat, Goubert Avenue, puducherry – 605001.\n__________\nPage19 of 20\n\nWP No. 36623 of 2026\nK.SURENDER ,J\nmvs.\nPre-delivery common order in\nWP Nos. 36623, 28148, 29331, 33709 of 2026\n9/10/2026\n__________\nPage20 of 20	20	t	HCMA012152762026	Writ Petition	2026-10-09	Single Bench	. B.Shanmathi	1. The Secretary to Government	\N	\N	Constitutional Law	In this writ petition under Civil Law, the Court ruled on the dispute between 23 Wp-36623-2026 and the Respondent.	0	2026 LexGuard (Writ) 000708	{"court": "Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)", "title": "Reddiyarpalayam, puducherry - 605010. v. 1. The Secretary to Government", "case_id": "000708af-9fed-4abf-bb7e-ce9d356d5e9b", "case_no": "WP No. 36623 of 2026", "parties": "23 Wp-36623-2026", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Reddiyarpalayam, puducherry - 605010.", "respondent": "1. The Secretary to Government", "legal_domain": "Constitutional Law", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Writ of Mandamus", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "scenario_keywords": [], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
00248415-40de-462a-b395-7090bd9a7a2d	NO. 12111 of 2026	Khordha P.R. v. STATE OF ODISHA	High Court of Orissa at Cuttack	2026	Hon'ble MR. JUSTICE ADITYA KUMAR MOHAPATRA	Khordha P.R. v. STATE OF ODISHA	["Odisha Excise Act"]	["Section 52(a) Odisha Excise Act"]	["civil & constitutional law", "ohc", "disposed"]	Judgment in NO. 12111 of 2026 delivered by Hon'ble MR. JUSTICE ADITYA KUMAR MOHAPATRA at High Court of Orissa at Cuttack. The proceedings concern Civil & Constitutional Law with respect to Section 482.	Disposed	storage/cases/OHC_2026_NO_12111_of_2026_00248415.pdf	efccc1e0e1b901f7e58efa03f0e599f51735e0312d5c537655768a0bfc406c99	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 14:51:32.942447+05:30	2026-10-25 14:51:32.942453+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:07.688193+05:30	23.10.2026 Order No. 01. 1. This matter is taken up through Hybrid Arrangement (Virtual /Physical Mode). 2. Heard learned counsel for the Petitioner and learned Additional Standing Counsel for the State. Perused the Anticipatory Bail Application as well as the F.I.R.	[]	Holding and principles affirmed in NO. 12111 of 2026 regarding Civil & Constitutional Law. The Court adjudicated upon Section 482.	[]	Disposed	[]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding Precedent)	IN THE HIGH COURT OF ORISSA AT CUTTACK\nABLAPL No.12111 of 2026\nCNR No. ODHC010786292026\nDebasish Jena @ Babul ..... Petitioner\nRepresented by Adv. -\nAmlan Shakti Paul\n-versus-\nSTATE OF ODISHA ..... Opposite Party\nRepresented by Adv. –\nMr. A.K. Pati, ASC\nCORAM:\nMR. JUSTICE ADITYA KUMAR MOHAPATRA\nORDER\n23.10.2026\nOrder No.\n01. 1. This matter is taken up through Hybrid Arrangement\n(Virtual /Physical Mode).\n2. Heard learned counsel for the Petitioner and learned\nAdditional Standing Counsel for the State. Perused the Anticipatory\nBail Application as well as the F.I.R.\n3. This is an application under Section 482 of the B.N.S.S.,\n2023 filed by the Petitioner for anticipatory bail, involving offence\npunishable under Section 52(a) of Odisha Excise Act. in 2(a)CC\nCase No.174 of 2026 in the Court of learned J.M.F.C.(Cog.\nTaking), Khordha arising out of District Mobile Unit, Khordha P.R.\nNo.193 of 2026-27.\n4. Learned counsel for the Petitioner submitted that the\nPetitioner does not want to press this bail application and seek\nliberty to surrender before the court below and move an application\nPage 1 of 3.\n\nfor bail.\n7. Considering such submissions, the Petitioner is permitted to\nsurrender before the learned court in seisin over the matter within a\nperiod of three weeks from today and move an application for bail.\nIn such eventuality, learned court in seisin over the matter shall do\nwell to release the Petitioner on bail subject to such terms and\nconditions as the learned Magistrate may deem just and proper with\nfurther conditions that:-\nI. shall not indulge in similar criminal activities while\non bail;\nII. shall appear before the local Police Station once\nin a fortnight for a period of three months, thereafter\nonce in a month for a period of three months,\nthereafter once in a month till conclusion of trial\npreferably on ‘Sunday’ in between 10.00 A.M. to 1\nP.M.\n8. It is further directed that the bail granted to the Petitioner\nshall be subject to depositing a cash security of Rs.4,000/- (Rupees\nFour Thousand) each before the learned Court in seisin over the\nmatter, which shall be kept in any Nationalized bank in interest\nbearing account initially for a period of one year which will be\nrenewable from time to time till conclusion of trial and the same\nshall be abide by the final outcome of the trial of the case.\nViolation of any of the terms and conditions shall entail\ncancellation of the bail.\nPage 2 of 3.\n\n9. The ABLAPL is disposed of accordingly.\nUrgent certified copy of this order be granted as per rules.\n( A.K. Mohapatra)\nVacation Judge\nSisir\nPage 3 of 3.	3	t	ODHC010786292026	Judicial Precedent	2026-10-23	Single 