5, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05, 0.05]	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	\N	\N	2026-10-24 20:34:08.622054+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:08.529245+05:30	BEFORE THE MADURAI BENCH OF MADRAS HIGH COURT\n( Criminal Jurisdiction )\nMonday, the Ninth day of February Two Thousand Fifteen\nPRESENT\nThe Hon`ble Mr Justice P.N.PRAKASH\nCRL OP(MD) No.718 of 2015\nHAJA NAZUMUDEEN ... PETITIONER / ACCUSED No.1\nVs\nTHE STATE REP. BY THE INSPECTOR OF POLICE\nKEELAKARAI POLICE STATION,\nRAMANATHAPURAM DISTRICT.\nCR.NO. 39/2014. ... RESPONDENT / COMPLAINANT\nFor Petitioner : M/S.S.DEENADHAYALAN Advocate\nFor Respondent : Mr.A.P.Balasurbramani Govt. Advocate ( Crl. Side)\nPETITION FOR BAIL Under Sec. 439 Cr.P.C.\nORDER : The Court Made the following order :-\nThe petitioner, who was arrested and remanded to judicial custody on\n01.03.2014 for the alleged offences punishable under Sections 8(c) r/w 21\n(c) of NDPS Act in Crime No.39 of 2014 on the file of the respondent\npolice, seeks bail.\n2. Heard the learned counsel for the petitioner and the learned\nGovernment Advocate (Crl.Side).\n3. This is second bail petition and the first bail petition in\nCrl.O.P.(MD) No.19122 of 	[]	Principles governing criminal jurisdiction and interim protections.	[]	Allowed	[]	\N	unverified	Good Law	BEFORE THE MADURAI BENCH OF MADRAS HIGH COURT\n( Criminal Jurisdiction )\nMonday, the Ninth day of February Two Thousand Fifteen\nPRESENT\nThe Hon`ble Mr Justice P.N.PRAKASH\nCRL OP(MD) No.718 of 2015\nHAJA NAZUMUDEEN ... PETITIONER / ACCUSED No.1\nVs\nTHE STATE REP. BY THE INSPECTOR OF POLICE\nKEELAKARAI POLICE STATION,\nRAMANATHAPURAM DISTRICT.\nCR.NO. 39/2014. ... RESPONDENT / COMPLAINANT\nFor Petitioner : M/S.S.DEENADHAYALAN Advocate\nFor Respondent : Mr.A.P.Balasurbramani Govt. Advocate ( Crl. Side)\nPETITION FOR BAIL Under Sec. 439 Cr.P.C.\nORDER : The Court Made the following order :-\nThe petitioner, who was arrested and remanded to judicial custody on\n01.03.2014 for the alleged offences punishable under Sections 8(c) r/w 21\n(c) of NDPS Act in Crime No.39 of 2014 on the file of the respondent\npolice, seeks bail.\n2. Heard the learned counsel for the petitioner and the learned\nGovernment Advocate (Crl.Side).\n3. This is second bail petition and the first bail petition in\nCrl.O.P.(MD) No.19122 of 2014 was dismissed on 17.11.2014. The respondent\nhave filed a counter in which they have stated as follows:\n“The case of prosecution is that on 01.03.2014, the respondent police\nconducted a vehicle check-up, at that time, the petitioner is the rider of\nbike along with 2nd accused on a road in Yamaha Vehicle bearing\nregistration No.TN 65 L 0990, the respondent police waylaid the vehicle\nand checked up and found that 4 kilo 900 grams of prohibited product viz.,\n'Charas'.”\n4. Learned counsel for the petitioner would strenuously contend that\nthere is a clear violation of Section 50 of the NDPS Act inasmuch as this\npetitioner was not given options contemplated under Section 50 of the NDPS\nAct, which has been held to be mandatory by a Constitution Bench of the\nHon'ble Supreme Court in State of Punjab vs. Baldev Singh etc., reported\nin 1999 CRI.L.J.3672. He relied upon the Paragraph No.52 of the said\njudgment and submitted that even presumption under Section 54 of NDPS Act\nhttps://hcservices.ecourts.gov.in/hcservices/\ncannot be raised, when there is a violation of Section 50.\n5. I am unable to persuade myself to agree with the contention of the\nlearned counsel for the petitioner in the facts and circumstances of the\ncase, because the contraband was recovered from a bag in the vehicle of\nthe accused and the recovery was not pursuant to the search of the person\nof the accused. In Paragraph No.53 of the said judgment, the Hon'ble\nSupreme Court has clearly stated as follows:\n“53. We, therefore, hold that an illicit article seized from the\nperson of an accused, during search conducted in violation of the\nsafeguards provided in Section 50 of the Act, cannot by itself be used as\nadmissible evidence of proof of unlawful possession of the contraband on\nthe accused. Any other material / article recovered during that search\nmay, however, be relied upon by the prosecution in other / independent\nproceedings against an accused notwithstanding the recovery of that\nmaterial during an illegal search and its admissibility would depend upon\nthe relevancy of that material and the facts and circumstances of that\ncase.”\n6. The Hon'ble Supreme Court stated that if any recovery is effected\nfrom the person of the accused in violation of Section 50 NDPS Act, then\nthe search is vitiated. However, in this case, there is no violation of\nSection 50 of NDPS Act inasmuch as the contraband was not recovered from\nthe body search of the accused, but from the vehicle.\n7. In the light of Section 37 of NDPS Act, there are overwhelming\nmaterials against this petitioner. Under such circumstances, this Court is\nnot inclined to grant bail to this petitioner. Hence, the Criminal\nOriginal Petition is dismissed.\nsd/-\n09/02/2015\n/ TRUE COPY /\nSub-Assistant Registrar (C.S.)\nTO\n1 THE ADDITIONAL PUBLIC PROSECUTOR,\nMADURAI BENCH OF MADRAS HIGH COURT, MADURAI\n2 THE OFFICER INCHARGE, DISTRICT PRISON, RAMANATHAPURAM.\n3 THE INSPECTOR OF POLICE\nKEELAKARAI POLICE STATION, RAMANATHAPURAM DISTRICT.\nTS/11.02.2015/2P-4C\nORDER\nIN\nCRL OP(MD) No.718 of 2015\nDate :09/02/2015\nhttps://hcservices.ecourts.gov.in/hcservices/	2	t	HCMD010007182015	Criminal Original Petition (Bail)	\N	Single Bench	No.718 of 2015 HAJA NAZUMUDEEN	2014.	M/S.S.DEENADHAYALAN Advocate	Mr.A.P.Balasurbramani Govt. Advocate ( Crl. Side)	Criminal Law	In this criminal original petition under Criminal Law, the Court ruled on the dispute between No.718 of 2015 HAJA NAZUMUDEEN and 2014..	0	2015 LexGuard (Crim) 104D74	{"court": "Madras High Court (Madurai Bench)", "title": "No.718 of 2015 HAJA NAZUMUDEEN v. 2014.", "case_id": "104d74d6-06f0-4834-98b2-fb4e2741090b", "case_no": "CRL OP(MD) No.718 of 2015", "parties": "No.718 of 2015 HAJA NAZUMUDEEN v. 2014.", "category": "NDPS", "petitioner": "No.718 of 2015 HAJA NAZUMUDEEN", "respondent": "2014.", "legal_domain": "Criminal Law", "category_facts": {"category": "NDPS", "custody_period": null, "contraband_type": "Charas", "seized_quantity": "4 kilo", "statutory_issue": "Bail / Pre-trial Release under NDPS Act", "quantity_category": "Intermediate Quantity", "section_37_embargo": false, "search_compliance_sec_50": "Search from vehicle/bag (Section 50 personal search held inapplicable)"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["pre_trial_custody", "narcotics_contraband"], "scenario_keywords": ["remand", "ndps"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Disposed of with directions.", "disposition": "Disposed of with directions."}}
11111111-0001-4000-8000-000000000001	Writ Petition (Crl.) No. 68 of 2008	Lalita Kumari v. Government of Uttar Pradesh & Others	Supreme Court of India	2014	Hon'ble P. Sathasivam, C.J., B.S. Chauhan, Ranjana P. Desai, Ranjan Gogoi, S.A. Bobde, JJ.	Lalita Kumari v. Government of Uttar Pradesh & Others	["Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973", "Indian Penal Code, 1860", "the Code"]	["Section 154 the Code"]	["mandatory FIR", "cognizable offence", "preliminary inquiry", "Section 154 CrPC", "police duty"]	Five-Judge Constitution Bench ruling declaring that registration of FIR under Section 154 CrPC is mandatory upon receipt of information disclosing commission of a cognizable offence.	Disposed	storage/cases/SC_2014_WP_Crl_No_68_of_2008_11110001.pdf	c469253ab25d03bd058aef2ce20cd0765004e6579fe4cd555f899ec5a59b7280	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	\N	\N	2026-10-25 00:13:23.781516+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:08.530333+05:30	\N	[]	Registration of FIR is mandatory under Section 154 of the Code if the information discloses commission of a cognizable offence and no preliminary inquiry is permissible in such a situation. If the information does not disclose a cognizable offence but indicates the necessity for an inquiry, a preliminary inquiry may be conducted only to ascertain whether a cognizable offence is disclosed or not within a maximum period of seven days.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2014) 2 SCC 1]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2014) 2 SCC 1", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding Precedent)	IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA\nCRIMINAL PROCEDURE & POLICE POWERS JURISDICTION\nWrit Petition (Crl.) No. 68 of 2008\nCNR: SCIN010000682008\nCITATION: (2014) 2 SCC 1\n\nBEFORE:\nHon'ble P. Sathasivam, C.J., B.S. Chauhan, Ranjana P. Desai, Ranjan Gogoi, S.A. Bobde, JJ.\n\nLalita Kumari v. Government of Uttar Pradesh and Others\n\nPETITIONER: Lalita Kumari\nRESPONDENT: Government of Uttar Pradesh & Others\nCOUNSEL FOR PETITIONER: Senior Advocate Colin Gonsalves\nCOUNSEL FOR RESPONDENT: Solicitor General of India\n\nJUDGMENT DATE: 12-11-2013\n\nSUMMARY & HEADNOTE:\nFive-Judge Constitution Bench ruling declaring that registration of FIR under Section 154 CrPC is mandatory upon receipt of information disclosing commission of a cognizable offence.\n\nPLAIN-LANGUAGE EXPLANATION:\nPolice officers are legally obligated to register an FIR whenever someone reports a serious crime. Preliminary inquiry is only allowed in exceptional cases like matrimonial disputes, medical negligence, or corruption.\n\nSTATUTES AND PROVISIONS CONSIDERED:\nCode of Criminal Procedure, 1973, Indian Penal Code, 1860\nSection 154 CrPC, Section 156(3) CrPC, Section 157 CrPC\n\nRATIO DECIDENDI:\nRegistration of FIR is mandatory under Section 154 of the Code if the information discloses commission of a cognizable offence and no preliminary inquiry is permissible in such a situation. If the information does not disclose a cognizable offence but indicates the necessity for an inquiry, a preliminary inquiry may be conducted only to ascertain whether a cognizable offence is disclosed or not within a maximum period of seven days.\n\nFINAL ORDER & DISPOSITION:\nPetition Disposed with Binding Directions. The petition is Guidelines Issued.\nPrecedential Status: Good Law (Binding Precedent).	1	f	SCIN010000682008	Writ Petition	2013-11-12	Constitution Bench (5 Judges)	Lalita Kumari	Government of Uttar Pradesh & Others	Senior Advocate Colin Gonsalves	Solicitor General of India	Criminal Procedure & Police Powers	Police officers are legally obligated to register an FIR whenever someone reports a serious crime. Preliminary inquiry is only allowed in exceptional cases like matrimonial disputes, medical negligence, or corruption.	0	(2014) 2 SCC 1	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Lalita Kumari v. Government of Uttar Pradesh and Others", "case_id": "11111111-0001-4000-8000-000000000001", "case_no": "Writ Petition (Crl.) No. 68 of 2008", "parties": "Lalita Kumari v. Government of Uttar Pradesh & Others", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Lalita Kumari", "respondent": "Government of Uttar Pradesh & Others", "legal_domain": "Criminal Procedure & Police Powers", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Writ Petition / Criminal Appeal", "judicial_disposition": "Allowed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CRPC", "section": "154", "canonical": "Section 154 CRPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CRPC", "section": "156", "canonical": "Section 156 CRPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CRPC", "section": "157", "canonical": "Section 157 CRPC", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "scenario_keywords": [], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Guidelines Issued", "disposition": "Guidelines Issued"}}
5b11c391-f673-4679-a113-16a76c8c7d38	NO. 14016 OF 2026	Petitioner v. Respondent / State	High Court of Judicature at Bombay	2026	Hon'ble SANDEEP V. MARNE, J	Petitioner v. Respondent / State	[]	[]	["civil & constitutional law", "bhc", "disposed"]	Judgment in NO. 14016 OF 2026 delivered by Hon'ble SANDEEP V. MARNE, J at High Court of Judicature at Bombay. The proceedings concern Civil & Constitutional Law with respect to procedural compliance.	Disposed	storage/cases/BHC_2026_NO_14016_OF_2026_5b11c391.pdf	48f4f78982791479006c854b3213c3b3940407000d5eb8e891d0970fd4900517	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 14:52:04.158761+05:30	2026-10-25 14:52:04.158769+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:08.555188+05:30	\N	[]	Holding and principles affirmed in NO. 14016 OF 2026 regarding Civil & Constitutional Law. The Court adjudicated upon applicable judicial standards.	[]	Disposed	[]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding Precedent)	Neeta Sawant 27-WP-14016-2026.docx\nIN THE HIGH COURT OF JUDICATURE AT BOMBAY\nCIVIL APPELLATE JURISDICTION\nWRIT PETITION NO. 14016 OF 2026\nWITH\nINTERIM APPLICATION (ST.) NO. 30722 OF 2026\nSuper Creative Graphics Pvt. Ltd. ...Petitioner\n: Versus :\nThe State of Maharashtra & Ors. ...Respondents\nMr. Deepan Dixit for the Petitioner.\nMs. Neeta Solanki a/w. Mr. Rahul Prajapati i/b. Unison Legal, for\nRespondent No.4-society.\nDr. Dhruti Kapadia, AGP for State.\nCORAM: SANDEEP V. MARNE, J.\nDATED: 19 OCTOBER 2026.\nP.C.:\n1) By this petition, Petitioner challenges order dated 22 July\n2026 passed by the Joint Registrar, Co-operative Societies, Navi\nMumbai dismissing Revision Application No. 57 of 2025 and\nconfirming order dated 14 August 2025 passed by the Deputy Registrar\ndismissing Petitioner's application for grant of membership under the\nPAGE NO. 1 of 5\nMonday, 19 October 2026\n\nNeeta Sawant 27-WP-14016-2026.docx\nprovisions of Section 22(2) of the Maharashtra Co-operative Societies\nAct, 1960 (MCS Act).\n2) I have heard Mr. Dixit, the learned counsel appearing for\nthe Petitioner and Ms. Solanki, learned counsel appearing for\nRespondent No.4-Society and considered the submissions canvassed by\nthem.\n3) Petitioner claims to have purchased five flats situated in the\nbuilding of Respondent No.4-society through the auction process\nconducted by the Liquidator of M/s. Ashapura Garments Limited in\npursuance of various orders passed by National Company Law Tribunal,\nAhemdabad Bench. It is Petitioner's contention that the liquidator had\nissued public notices before putting the flats for auction and if\nRespondent No.4-society had any maintenance claims qua the five flats,\nit ought to have registered its claim before the liquidator. In my view, it\nis the duty of the auction purchaser to approach the society and verify\nwhether there are any past dues in respect of the flats put up for auction.\nUnder the provisions of Section 154B-(7) of the MCS Act, membership\nin a society cannot be granted unless all dues of the housing society are\npaid. Section 154B-7 of the MCS Act reads thus :\n154B-7. Restriction of transfer of share or interest of a Member\nSubject to the provisions of this Act, in case of a housing society, no\ntransfer of share or interest of a Member or the occupancy right, except\nthe transfer of his heir or a nominee, shall be effective unless, –\n(a) the dues of housing society are paid;\n(b) the transferee applies and acquires Membership of the co-\noperative housing society in due course of time:\nProvided that, the transfer of share or interest in respect of lease hold\nproperties shall be governed by the terms of the lease, which are not\nPAGE NO. 2 of 5\nMonday, 19 October 2026\n\nNeeta Sawant 27-WP-14016-2026.docx\ninconsistent with lease of land to the co-operative housing society or with\nlease by housing society to its Members.\nExplanation. — For the purpose of this section, occupancy right shall not\ninclude right of a tenant or a licensee on leave and license basis.\n4) In my view, it would be too onerous to expect co-operative\nhousing society to keep a tap on notices published in newspapers qua its\nmembers in liquidation for the purpose of recovery of maintenance\ndutes by registration of its claim with the Liquidator. On the other\nhand, when the auction purchaser decides to purchase a flat through\nauction process, which is a part of co-operative housing society, the flat\npurchaser need to perform due diligence and approach the society for\nverifying the maintenance dues. In the present case, Petitioner cannot\nbe granted exemption in respect of the maintenance dues of the five flats\npurchased by it. If Petitioner desires membership of Respondent No.4-\nsociety, it will have to clear the entire past maintenance dues.\n5) Mr. Dixit submits that Respondent No.3-society has\ninitiated proceedings against the past member for recovery of the\nmaintenance dues. He submits that the Petitioner, per-se does not have\nobjection to the society recovering maintenance dues, but so long as the\nsame are not recovered by auctioning the flats purchased by the\nPetitioner. I am unable to agree. As of now, the issue involved before\nthe Deputy Registrar and the Joint Registrar was about Petitioner's\nright to secure membership in the society. As per the provisions of\nSection 154B-7 of the MCS Act, membership cannot be granted to the\nPetitioner without clearing the past maintenance dues in respect of the\nflat. Therefore, filing of proceedings by Respondent No.4-society\ncannot be a reason for the Petitioner to claim membership of the society.\nMere filing of proceedings by Respondent No.4-society for recovery of\nPAGE NO. 3 of 5\nMonday, 19 October 2026\n\nNeeta Sawant 27-WP-14016-2026.docx\ndues from ex-member would not automatically entitle the Petitioner to\nclaim membership in respect of the flats purchased by it. Since\nPetitioner is not admitted as member of the society and since name of\nex-member may still continues in its ‘I’ and ‘J’ Registers, the society is\nleft with no alternative but to file recovery proceedings in the name of\nits member. However the society can enforce such Recovery Certificate\nagainst the flats which are now owned by the Petitioner. In the present\ncase apparently Recovery Certificates have been issued and the society\nwould be entitled to enforce the same against the flats in question. If\nPetitioner wants membership in respect of the purchased flats and\nwants to save them from being auctioned, it will have to clear the dues\nof the society.\n6) If Petitioner's contention of impermissibility to recover\nmaintenance dues from it is accepted, the society would be deprived of\nthe maintenance dues in respect of 5 flats. Maintenance dues are\ncollected by the society for the purpose of collective management of the\nbuilding. If any member fails to pay the maintenance dues, the burden\nfalls on the other members. In the present case, if maintenance dues in\nrespect of the five flats are not paid by the Petitioner, the society will not\nbe able to recover the same from ex-member who is already liquidated.\nThis would result in a situation where other residents of the building\nwill have to bear the dues payable in respect of the flats. On the other\nhand, if the Petitioner is made liable to pay maintenance dues as a pre-\ncondition for grant of membership to the society, it would result in a\nsituation where maintenance dues in respect of the building would be\nshared by all the residents. Petitioner has purchased the flats in\nquestion with its eyes wide open about past maintenance dues of Ex-\nPAGE NO. 4 of 5\nMonday, 19 October 2026\n\nNeeta Sawant 27-WP-14016-2026.docx\nmember. It cannot seek to escape the liability by taking shelter of\npurchase of flats in liquidation proceedings.\n7) At this stage, Mr. Dixit, submits that Petitioner may be\ngranted time of 4 weeks for the purpose of clearing past dues in respect\nof the flats purchased by it. He further submits that some relaxation be\ngranted in respect of the interest charged by the society since heavy\ninterest and penalty has been charged by the society. He submits that\nPetitioner is not responsible for non-payment of maintenance dues\nwithin the stipulated time. Considering these unique facts, in my view,\nends of justice would meet if the society levies simple interest @ 10%\np.a. on the maintenance dues in respect of the five flats purchased by the\nPetitioner-society. Petitioner shall pay maintenance dues alongwith\nsimple interest @ 10% p.a. to Respondent No.4-society within a period\nof 6 weeks. In order to enable the Petitioner to make payment of\nmaintenance dues with interest, no coercive steps shall be taken for\nenforcement of Recovery Certificates against the five flats purchased by\nthe Petitioner for a period of 6 weeks. Statement made about clearance\nof maintenance dues alongwith simple interest @ 10% p.a. is accepted as\nan undertaking given to the Court.\n8) In view of the above discussion, the Writ Petition is\ndisposed of. The Interim Application also stands disposed of.\n[SANDEEP V. MARNE , J.]\nDigitally\nsigned by\nNEETA\nNEETA SHAILESH\nSHAILESHSAWANT\nSAWANT Date:\n2026.10.22\n11:31:29\n+0530\nPAGE NO. 5 of 5\nMonday, 19 October 2026	5	t	BHCB010140162026	Judicial Precedent	2026-10-23	Single Bench	Petitioner	Respondent / State	\N	No.4-society.	Civil & Constitutional Law	In this matter, the High Court of Judicature at Bombay adjudicated issues between Petitioner and Respondent / State. The Court rendered a decision disposing of the application under statutory provisions.	0	2026 LexGuard (BHC) 5B11C391	{"court": "High Court of Judicature at Bombay", "title": "Petitioner v. Respondent / State", "case_id": "5b11c391-f673-4679-a113-16a76c8c7d38", "case_no": "NO. 14016 OF 2026", "parties": "Petitioner v. Respondent / State", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Petitioner", "respondent": "Respondent / State", "legal_domain": "Civil & Constitutional Law", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Writ Petition / Criminal Appeal", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "scenario_keywords": [], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Disposed", "disposition": "Disposed"}}
5e552ec6-ad7d-4896-af18-f6c9e218e79b	WP Crl.(MD) NO. 5804 of 2026	M.Arockia Pamila v. 1. The Superintendent of Police, Central Prison	Madras High Court (Madurai Bench)	2026	Hon'ble Mr. Justice G.R.Swaminathan	M.Arockia Pamila v. 1. The Superintendent of Police, Central Prison	["Constitution of India"]	["Article 226"]	["civil & constitutional law", "mhc", "allowed"]	Judgment in NO. 5804 of 2026 delivered by Hon'ble THE HON'BLE MR JUSTICE G.R.SWAMINATHAN at Madras High Court (Madurai Bench). The proceedings concern Civil & Constitutional Law with respect to Article 226.	Disposed	storage/cases/MHC_2026_NO_5804_of_2026_5e552ec6.pdf	36bfe2a6cd9b9517d55dd05e2b5bfdf94ee346c172e08bea8580ffc96d9685ff	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 14:51:52.19828+05:30	2026-10-25 14:51:52.198286+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:08.556361+05:30	Heard both sides. 2.The petitioner's husband(Iyyappan) is undergoing life sentence at Central Prison, Palayamkottai(PID No.159542). The petitioner alleged that her mother-in-law is suffering from serious illness and she wanted to meet her son / petitioner’s husband. Hence, she seeks six days emergency leave for her husband.	[]	Holding and principles affirmed in NO. 5804 of 2026 regarding Civil & Constitutional Law. The Court adjudicated upon Article 226.	[]	Disposed	[]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding Precedent)	WP Crl.(MD) No. 5804 of 2026\nBEFORE THE MADURAI BENCH OF MADRAS HIGH COURT\nDATED: 23-10-2026\nCORAM\nTHE HON'BLE MR JUSTICE G.R.SWAMINATHAN\nAND\nTHE HON'BLE DR.JUSTICE P.MURUGAN\nWP Crl.(MD) No. 5804 of 2026\nCNR:{HCMD011529392026}\nM.Arockia Pamila\nW/o.Iyyappan,\nNo.4/210, Valar Nagar Street,\nThambathukonam,\nNagercoil,\nKanyakumari District - 629 704.\n..Petitioner(s)\nVs\n1. The Superintendent of Police,\nCentral Prison,\nPalayamkottai.\n2. The Inspector of Police\nRajakkamangalam Police Station,\nKanyakumari District.\n..Respondent(s)\nPrayer: Writ petition filed under Article 226 of the Constitution of\nIndia, to issue a Writ of Certiorarified Mandamus, calling for the records\nrelating to the impugned order passed by the first respondent vide his\nproceedings in No.4393/ThaKu.2/2026 dated 07.10.2026 and quash the\nsame as illegal and void, consequential direction may be issued to the first\nrespondent to grant six days emergency leave without police escort to\nIyyappan, S/o.Selvaraj, PID No.159542, now confined at Central Prison,\nPalayamkottai forthwith considering the representation dated 06.09.2026.\n__________\nPage1 of 5\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\nWP Crl.(MD) No. 5804 of 2026\nFor Petitioner(s): Mr.C.Karthikeyan\nFor Respondent(s): Mr.C.Christopher,\nAdditional Public Prosecutor.\nORDER\nHeard both sides.\n2.The petitioner's husband(Iyyappan) is undergoing life sentence at\nCentral Prison, Palayamkottai(PID No.159542). The petitioner alleged that\nher mother-in-law is suffering from serious illness and she wanted to meet\nher son / petitioner’s husband. Hence, she seeks six days emergency leave\nfor her husband.\n3.Considering the special facts and circumstances pleaded in the writ\npetition, we direct the first respondent to grant three(3) days emergency\nleave for the convict from 28.10.2026 to 30.10.2026 without escort. The\nimpugned order is accordingly set aside and this writ petition stands\nallowed accordingly in the following terms:-\n(a)The convict prisoner, Iyyappan [PID No.159542], now\ndetained in the Central Prison, Palayamkottai is granted\nthree(3) days emergency leave without escort from\n28.10.2026 to 30.10.2026\n(b) The convict prisoner shall be released from the\nprison at 5.00 pm., on 27.10.2026 and he shall return to the\n__________\nPage2 of 5\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\nWP Crl.(MD) No. 5804 of 2026\nprison at 10.00 am., on 31.10.2026.\n(c) During the said leave period, he shall report before\nthe second respondent police daily at 7.00 p.m., from\n28.10.2026 onwards without fail.\n(d) During the leave period, the convict prisoner shall\nabide by all the conditions prescribed in the Jail Manual.\n4.With the above direction, the Writ Petition stands allowed. No\ncosts.\n(G.R.S.,J.) (P.M.N.,J.)\n23-10-2026\nIndex: Yes/No\nSpeaking/Non-speaking order\nNeutral Citation: Yes/No\nPMU\n__________\nPage3 of 5\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\nWP Crl.(MD) No. 5804 of 2026\nTo\n1. The Superintendent of Police,\nCentral Prison,\nPalayamkottai.\n2. The Inspector of Police\nRajakkamangalam Police Station,\nKanyakumari District.\n3. The Additional Public Prosecutor,\nMadurai Bench of Madras High Court,\nMadurai.\n__________\nPage4 of 5\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\nWP Crl.(MD) No. 5804 of 2026\nG.R.SWAMINATHAN, J.\nAND\nDR.P.MURUGAN, J.\nPMU\nWP Crl.(MD) No. 5804 of 2026\n23-10-2026\n__________\nPage5 of 5\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis	5	t	HCMD011529392026	Writ Petition	2026-10-23	Single Bench	M.Arockia Pamila	1. The Superintendent of Police, Central Prison	Mr.C.Karthikeyan	Mr.C.Christopher, Additional Public Prosecutor.	Civil & Constitutional Law	In this matter, the Madras High Court (Madurai Bench) adjudicated issues between Kanyakumari District - 629 704 and 1. The Superintendent of Police, Central Prison. The Court rendered a decision disposing of the application under statutory provisions.	0	2026 LexGuard (MHC) 5E552EC6	{"court": "Madras High Court (Madurai Bench)", "title": "Kanyakumari District - 629 704 v. 1. The Superintendent of Police, Central Prison", "case_id": "5e552ec6-ad7d-4896-af18-f6c9e218e79b", "case_no": "NO. 5804 of 2026", "parties": "Kanyakumari District - 629 704 v. 1. The Superintendent of Police, Central Prison", "category": "QUASHING", "petitioner": "Kanyakumari District - 629 704", "respondent": "1. The Superintendent of Police, Central Prison", "legal_domain": "Civil & Constitutional Law", "category_facts": {"category": "QUASHING", "civil_cloak": false, "quash_ground": "Absence of prima facie ingredients", "compromise_affidavit"