69a9c8e6-d546-4a04-ac88-0e880ce426ba	CRL OP No. 30688 of 2026	Nithesh Anand v. The State Rep By, The Inspector of Police Neyveli Township Police Station, Cuddalore.	Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)	2026	Hon'ble Mr. Justice N.Ramesh	Nithesh Anand v. The State Rep By, The Inspector of Police Neyveli Township Police Station, Cuddalore.	["Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023", "of BNS"]	["Section 103(1) of BNS", "Section 109 of BNS", "Section 118(1) of BNS", "Section 126(2) of BNS", "Section 127(2) of BNS", "Section 191(2) of BNS", "Section 191(3) of BNS", "Section 296(b) of BNS", "Section 351(3) of BNS"]	["civil & constitutional law", "mhc", "disposed"]	Judgment in NO. 30688 of 2026 delivered by Hon'ble Bench at Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai). The proceedings concern Civil & Constitutional Law with respect to procedural compliance.	Bail Granted	storage/cases/MHC_2026_NO_30688_of_2026_69a9c8e6.pdf	d19213fdd9a0c60012d3ba22eb6b046487b0e660cd42f4b4d86eaea9b30846b0	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 14:51:38.440331+05:30	2026-10-25 14:51:38.440335+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:08.563292+05:30	2. The case of the prosecution is that on 16.08.2026, due to previous enmity, the accused persons attempted to attack Anandharaj. When the deceased Naresh intervened, accused 1 and 2 attacked the deceased on his head with a knife resulting in his death. The petitioner also allegedly attacked Anandharaj. Hence, the case. 3.	[]	Regular bail granted in CRL OP No. 30688 of 2026 under applicable statutory provisions, considering the duration of incarceration and absence of flight risk, subject to executing bond and standard reporting conditions.	[]	Allowed	["ble Supreme Court in P.K.Shaji vs. State of Kerala [(2005)13 SCC 283]"]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding Precedent)	CRL OP No. 30688 of 2026\nIN THE HIGH COURT OF JUDICATURE AT MADRAS\nDATED: 23-10-2026\nCORAM\nTHE HON'BLE MR.JUSTICE N.RAMESH\nCRL OP No. 30688 of 2026\nCNR: {HCMA012561242026}\nNithesh Anand\nS/o Anandaraj,\nNo.B-24, Bokharo Street,\nBlock NO.26, Neyeveli,\nCuddalore.\n..Petitioner(s)\nVs\nThe State Rep By, The Inspector of Police\nNeyveli Township Police Station,\nCuddalore.\nCrime No.235 of 2026\n..Respondent(s)\nPRAYER : Criminal Original Petition filed under Section 483 of Bharatiya\nNagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023, to enlarge the petitioner herein on bail in\nCrime No.235 of 2026 on the file of the Inspector of Police, Neyveli Township\nPolice Station, Cuddalore.\nFor Petitioner(s): Mr.R.Karthikeyan\nFor Respondent(s): Mr.M.M.I. Khaleel\nGovernment Advocate (Crl.Side)\nORDER\nThe petitioner, who was arrested and remanded to judicial custody on\n20.08.2026 for the alleged offence under Sections 191(2), 191(3), 296(b),\n126(2), 127(2), 118(1), 351(3), 109 and 103(1) of BNS, 2023, in Crime No.235\nof 2026 on the file of the respondent police, seeks bail.\n__________\nPage1 of 6\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\nCRL OP No. 30688 of 2026\n2. The case of the prosecution is that on 16.08.2026, due to previous\nenmity, the accused persons attempted to attack Anandharaj. When the\ndeceased Naresh intervened, accused 1 and 2 attacked the deceased on his head\nwith a knife resulting in his death. The petitioner also allegedly attacked\nAnandharaj. Hence, the case.\n3. The learned counsel appearing for the petitioner submitted that the\npetitioner is innocent, aged 19 years and has been falsely implicated in the\npresent case and that he has not at all involved in the occurrence and he is a\nfriend of the brother of the deceased. He further submitted that the co-accused\nhave already been granted bail by this Court in Crl.OP.No.30082 of 2026 dated\n15.10.2026. Hence, he prays for grant of bail to the petitioner.\n4. Per contra, the learned Government Advocate (Criminal Side)\nappearing for the Respondent Police reiterated the prosecution case and\nsubmitted that the specific overt act against the petitioner is that he waylaid the\ndeceased and that he was also present in the said occurrence. He further\nsubmitted that the investigation is almost completed. However, he opposed the\ngrant of bail to the petitioner.\n5. I have given my anxious consideration to either side submissions and\nperused the materials available on record.\n__________\nPage2 of 6\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\nCRL OP No. 30688 of 2026\n6. On perusal of the FIR, it is seen that the petitioner name is not found\nplace in the FIR. However, it is submitted by the learned counsel for the\nrespondent that the petitioner was present in the said occurrence and waylaid the\ndeceased. Except that, there is no specific overt act against the petitioner.\nConsidering the nature of the allegations, the period of incarceration of the\npetitioner and the fact that the co-accused have already been released on bail\nand also considering the age of the petitioner, this Court is inclined to enlarge\nthe petitioner on bail subject to certain conditions.\n7. Accordingly, the petitioner is ordered to be released on bail on his\nexecuting a bond for a sum of Rs.25,000/- (Rupees Twenty Five Thousand\nonly) with two sureties each, for a like sum to the satisfaction of the learned\nDistrict Munsif cum Judicial Magistrate, Neyveli, Cuddalore District, and\non further conditions that:\n[a] the sureties shall affix their photographs and Left\nThumb Impression in the Application for Surety ship\n[Judicial Form No.46 annexed to 'The Criminal Rules of\nPractice, 2019']. The learned Magistrate shall obtain a copy\nof any one of the identity proofs to ensure their identity;\n[b] the petitioner shall report before the respondent\npolice twice daily at 10.30 a.m., and 5.30 p.m., until\nfurther orders;\n[c] the petitioner shall not abscond either during\ninvestigation or trial;\n__________\nPage3 of 6\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\nCRL OP No. 30688 of 2026\n[d] the petitioner shall not tamper with the evidence or\nwitness either during investigation or trial;\n[e] On breach of any of the aforementioned conditions,\nthe learned Magistrate/Trial Court is entitled to pass\nappropriate orders against the petitioner in accordance with\nlaw as if the aforementioned conditions have been imposed\nand the petitioner released on bail by the learned\nMagistrate/Trial Court himself as laid down by the Hon'ble\nSupreme Court in P.K.Shaji vs. State of Kerala [(2005)13\nSCC 283];\n[f] If the accused thereafter absconds, a fresh FIR can be\nregistered under Section 269 of B.N.S.\n23-10-2026\nDRL\nNote:\n1. Registry is directed to forthwith upload\nthis order in the Official Website of this\nCourt.\n2. All concerned to act on this order being\nuploaded in Official Website of this Court\nwithout insisting on certified hard copies. To\nbe noted, this order when uploaded in the\nofficial website of this Court will be\nwatermarked and will also have a QR code.\n__________\nPage4 of 6\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\nCRL OP No. 30688 of 2026\nTo\n1. The District Munsif cum Judicial\nMagistrate, Neyveli, Cuddalore District.\n2. The Inspector of Police\nNeyveli Township Police Station,\nCuddalore.\n3.The Superintendent, Central Prison,\nCuddalore.\n4.The Public Prosecutor,\nHigh Court, Madras.\n__________\nPage5 of 6\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\nCRL OP No. 30688 of 2026\nN.RAMESH, J.\nDRL\nCRL OP No. 30688 of 2026\n23-10-2026\n__________\nPage6 of 6\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis	6	t	HCMA012561242026	Criminal Original Petition (Bail)	2026-10-23	Single Bench	Nithesh Anand	The State Rep By, The Inspector of Police Neyveli Township Police Station, Cuddalore.	Mr.R.Karthikeyan	Mr.M.M.I. Khaleel Government Advocate (Crl.Side)	Civil & Constitutional Law	In this matter, the Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai) adjudicated issues between Cuddalore and The State Rep By, The Inspector of Police Neyveli Township Police Station. The Court rendered a decision disposing of the application under statutory pro