167d4194-21eb-4c86-8d21-85ff7fb3ea7e	NO. 390 OF 2025	Applicant v. The State of Maharashtra and anr	High Court of Judicature at Bombay	2026	Hon'ble M.M. SATHAYE, J	Applicant v. The State of Maharashtra and anr	["Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973", "Indian Penal Code", "Indian Penal Code, 1860", "the Code", "the IPC"]	["Section 227 the Code", "Section 306 Indian Penal Code", "Section 506 Indian Penal Code", "Section 506 the IPC"]	["civil & constitutional law", "bhc", "dismissed"]	Judgment in NO. 390 OF 2025 delivered by Hon'ble M.M. SATHAYE, J at High Court of Judicature at Bombay. The proceedings concern Civil & Constitutional Law with respect to procedural compliance.	Disposed	storage/cases/BHC_2026_NO_390_OF_2025_167d4194.pdf	481592b30ce151740b47406a91a4704bb078433f9a371e0f356aad670c4be806	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 14:51:48.912827+05:30	2026-10-25 14:51:48.912833+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:08.532473+05:30	\N	[]	Holding and principles affirmed in NO. 390 OF 2025 regarding Civil & Constitutional Law. The Court adjudicated upon applicable judicial standards.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2026) 6 SCC 251]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2026) 6 SCC 251", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}, {"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2026) 6 SCC 233]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2026) 6 SCC 233", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}, {"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [1979) 3 SCC 4]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "1979) 3 SCC 4", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}, {"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2014) 11 SCC 709]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2014) 11 SCC 709", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}, {"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2021) 11 SCC 191]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2021) 11 SCC 191", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}, {"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2023) 17 SCC 688]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2023) 17 SCC 688", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding Precedent)	2.cri.revn.390.2025 @1.doc\nIN THE HIGH COURT OF JUDICATURE AT BOMBAY\nCRIMINAL APPELLATE JURISDICTION\nCRIMINAL REVISION APPLICATION NO. 390 OF 2025\nBhaiyasasheb alias Sanjay\nRamchandra Gawande … Applicant\nVersus\nThe State of Maharashtra and anr. … Respondents\nSNEHA ***\nNITIN Mr. Subodh Desai, Senior Advocate a/w Mr. Kartik Garg for\nCHAVAN\nApplicant.\nDigitally signed\nby SNEHA\nNITIN CHAVAN\nDate: 2026.10.23 Mr. Balraj Kulkarni, APP for Respondent-State.\n19:20:03 +0530\nMr. Abhishek Karnik for Respondent No.2.\nPSI Sardar Vasekar, Panvel Taluka Police Station, present.\n***\nCORAM : M.M. SATHAYE, J.\nDATE : 22nd OCTOBER, 2026\nP.C. :\n1. Heard learned Counsel for the parties. Perused the record.\n2. The Applicant is the sole accused. C.R.No.52 of 2021 was\nregistered against the Applicant for the offences punishable under\nSections 306 and 506 of the Indian Penal Code (for short, ‘IPC’),\nwhich resulted in Sessions Case No.98 of 2023. Applicant filed an\napplication below Exhibit 4 therein, seeking discharge under Section\n227 of the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973 (‘CrPC’ for short). By\nthe impugned order dated 15.07.2025, the Additional Sessions\nJudge, Panvel-Raigad, rejected the said Application. This order is\nchallenged in the present Revision Application.\nVina Khadpe 1\n\n2.cri.revn.390.2025 @1.doc\n3. One Mr. Keshav Gajanan Gawande is deceased who committed\nsuicide. Respondent No.2 is the wife of the deceased. She has filed\nFIR.\n4. According to prosecution, the deceased was working as a\nmanager at Suvidha Bharat Gas Agency which is owned by the\nApplicant. In September, 2020, the Applicant made allegations\nagainst the deceased about shortage of 900 to 1000 gas cylinders,\nfalsification of accounts and misappropriation of Rs. 30 Lakh. The\nApplicant called the deceased and his father to his farm house and\nforcibly obtained statements. The father of the deceased was also\nmade to sign on the statements. Original documents were kept by\nthe Applicant. Properties were made to be transferred in the name of\nApplicant’s relatives. Finally, a document was prepared on\n04.01.2021, stating balance payable amount. Therefore, the deceased\nwas in depression. On 20.02.2021, at about 9.30 pm., the deceased\ncommitted suicide by consuming poison. He died in the hospital on\n22.02.2021.\n5. During investigation, the statements of the witnesses were\nrecorded, and the suicide note was seized. In the suicide note,\nApplicant’s name is mentioned as a person responsible for suicide. It\nis mentioned that the accused had abused and threatened the\ndeceased, and forcibly obtained the sale deeds in respect of the\nproperties. Deceased has also made video recording before suicide.\nIn short, according to the complainant and the prosecution, the\naccused had threatened the deceased and got documents executed\nfor repayment of the amount allegedly misappropriated in the\nVina Khadpe 2\n\n2.cri.revn.390.2025 @1.doc\nbusiness of Gas Agency. The deceased, who was working as a\nManager, came under pressure and was living in a state of tension,\nwhich has resulted into his suicide.\n6. Learned Senior Counsel for the Applicant submitted that the\nlast alleged document was executed on 04.01.2021, wherein it was\nmentioned that, if the remaining amount is not paid within the\nspecified time, then the Applicant would be free to initiate the legal\nproceedings. He submitted that thereafter the suicide took place on\n20.02.2021 i.e. more than one and a half months after the execution\nof last document. He submitted that efforts to recover\nmisappropriated amount cannot be considered as instigation or direct\nact abetting the suicide. He submitted that there was neither\ninstigation nor aiding nor mens rea on the part of the Applicant. He\nfurther submitted that considering the chronology of events, there is\nno proximate cause connecting the Applicant with the act of suicide.\nHe submitted that there are no circumstances to indicate that, due to\nacts of the Applicant, the deceased was left with no option, but to\ntake the extreme step. Therefore, the necessary ingredients of the\nalleged offence are not made out. He relied upon the Judgment in the\ncase of Prakash and others Vs. State of Maharashtra and another.\n(2026) 6 SCC 251, and Abhinav Mohan Delkar Vs. State of\nMaharashtra and others. (2026) 6 SCC 233, in support of his case.\n7. On the other hand, learned APP has supported the impugned\norder, contending, inter alia, that the accused is named in the suicide\nnote. He contended that deceased’s wife has stated, that the deceased\nwas living under tension and his relatives, including his father were\nVina Khadpe 3\n\n2.cri.revn.390.2025 @1.doc\ncalled at the farmhouse of the accused where documents were\nforcibly obtained by the accused. He submitted that conduct of the\nApplicant is sufficient to prosecute him for the offences of abetment\nof suicide and criminal intimidation and that the same is a matter of\ntrial for the Court to decide.\n8. Learned Counsel for Respondent No.2 / informant has also\nsupported the impugned order, contending inter alia, that the\naccused was solely responsible for the deceased taking extreme step.\nHe has also invited the Court’s attention to the statement of the\ndeceased’s wife and the suicide note.\n9. I have considered the rival submissions and perused the record.\n10. Admittedly, it appears from the material gathered by the\nprosecution that the allegation relates to misappropriated amount of\nRs.30,00,000/- from the Gas Agency owned by the Applicant /\naccused. The deceased, seems to have executed the statement on\n20.09.2020. Statement is recorded on the letter head of the Gas\nAgency which was signed by the deceased, accused and other\nwitnesses. In the said statement, the deceased has apparently\nadmitted to the act of misappropriation of the amount. Another\nstatement seems to have been executed by the father of the deceased\non 22.09.2020, once again taking responsibility for repayment of the\nmisappropriated amount. The father has stated that he will try to\nrepay in installments and if not possible he will transfer S. No. 162\nadmeasuring 2 acre land worth Rs. 18 Lakh to the Applicant. The\nthird document is of 06.11.2020 executed by father of deceased\nVina Khadpe 4\n\n2.cri.revn.390.2025 @1.doc\nrecording repayments made so far and also agreeing to sell further\nproperty. Fourth document is dated 04.01.2021, which seems to be an\nagreement executed between the accused and the deceased,\nrecording the payments made by the deceased including sale of a\nland Gat No. 162/ 161 – 1 Acre and the remaining amount for which\npost dated cheques were issued. It is further stated that if the\nremaining amount is not repaid, the accused would be free to initiate\nthe legal proceedings.\n11. During investigation, a copy of sale deed date 30.12.2020\ncame on record, whereby the father of the deceased sold 22R land\nout of Gut No.161 to a relative of the accused. Another sale deed\nalso come on record which was executed by father of deceased in\nfavour of the same relative of the accused for the sale of 21R of land\nout of Gut No.162. It has come on record as per the statement of the\ndeceased’s father recorded on the letter head of the Gas Agency, that\nthe price of the land was much higher than the amount for which it is\nshown to have been sold.\n12. From the aforesaid discussion, it is clear that alleging\nmisappropriation of the amount in the Gas Agency business, the\nApplicant has got several documents executed by the deceased and\nhis father, including a statement accepting the guilt and the\ndocuments for sale of properties in favour of Applicant’s relative for\nan amount lower than the market price. This shows that there were\nmonetary transactions between the parties, in connection with which,\nthe deceased and his father were made to execute the aforesaid\ndocuments. It is also clear from the statement of the deceased’s wife\nVina Khadpe 5\n\n2.cri.revn.390.2025 @1.doc\nthat due to the pending dispute between the parties regarding the\nalleged misappropriation and execution of several documents by the\ndeceased and his father, the deceased was in pressure and living in a\nstate of tension.\n13. It is not disputed that suicide note names the Applicant as a\nperson responsible for the suicide. It is also stated in the suicide note\nthat the accused threatened the deceased with dire consequences.\n14. Whether the conduct of the Applicant / accused amounts to\ninstigation or constitutes an act of abetment having a proximate\nconnection with the deceased’s suicide is a matter to be decided at\nthe time of trial. In that view of the matter, there is no merit in the\nsubmission of learned Counsel for the Applicant that there was no\nmens rea or intention and that there is absence of proximate cause.\n15. It is settled position of law that at the time of deciding\ndischarge application, the Court has to look into prima facie case and\nit is not necessary to enter into pros and cons of the matter or\nweighing and balancing of the evidence. The Court is expected to\nmerely sift the evidence in order to find out whether there is\nsufficient ground for proceeding against the accused. It is trite that at\nthe stage of considering discharge application, the Court has to\nproceed with assumption that the material brought on record by the\nprosecution is true and evaluate it with a view to find out whether\nfacts emerging taken at its face value discloses existence of the\noffence. Also at the stage of deciding discharge application, mini trial\nis not permitted and the High Court sitting in revisional jurisdiction\nVina Khadpe 6\n\n2.cri.revn.390.2025 @1.doc\ncannot sit as an Appellate Court and start appreciating the evidence\nand the High Court has to be cognizant of the fact that the Trial Court\nwas dealing with discharge application. Useful reference can be made\nto paragraph No. 7 of Union of India v/s. Prafulla Kumar Samal and\nAnr. (1979) 3 SCC 4, paragraph No. 29 of State of Tamil Nadu V/s.\nN. Suresh Rajan and Ors. (2014) 11 SCC 709, paragraph No. 15 of\nState of Rajasthan v/s. Ashok Kumar Kashyap (2021) 11 SCC 191\nand Paragraph Nos. 10 and 17 of State of Gujarat v/s. Dilipsinh\nKishorsinh Rao. (2023) 17 SCC 688.\n16. So far as the judgments relied upon by learned Counsel for the\nApplicant are concerned, the proposition of law laid down therein\ncannot be disputed. However, in given set of facts, whether the\nconduct of the Applicant amounts to direct instigation or constitutes a\nproximate reason for the suicide or whether it amounts to abetment,\ndepends entirely on the facts and circumstances of each case. In that\nview of the matter, the said judgments will not advance the case of\nthe Applicant.\n17. It is also important to note that the Applicant is also being\nprosecuted under Section 506 of the IPC in addition to the offence of\nabetment of suicide. The manner in which the documents were\nexecuted by the deceased and his father and the case that those\ndocuments were executed at the Applicant’s farmhouse begs\nconsideration of angle of criminal intimidation also, at the time of\ntrial.\n18. In the facts and circumstances of the case, and for the reasons\nVina Khadpe 7\n\n2.cri.revn.390.2025 @1.doc\nindicated above, no case for discharge is made out. Impugned order\ncan not be faulted. There is no reason to interfere with the impugned\norder.\n19. Criminal Revision Application is rejected.\n(M.M. SATHAYE, J.)\nVina Khadpe 8	8	t	BHCB010003902026	Judicial Precedent	2026-10-23	Single Bench	Applicant	The State of Maharashtra and anr	\N	State.	Civil & Constitutional Law	In this matter, the High Court of Judicature at Bombay adjudicated issues between Bhaiyasasheb alias Sanjay Ramchandra Gawande and The State of Maharashtra and anr. The Court rendered a decision disposing of the application under statutory provisions.	0	2026 LexGuard (BHC) 167D4194	{"court": "High Court of Judicature at Bombay", "title": "Bhaiyasasheb alias Sanjay Ramchandra Gawande v. The State of Maharashtra and anr", "case_id": "167d4194-21eb-4c86-8d21-85ff7fb3ea7e", "case_no": "NO. 390 OF 2025", "parties": "Bhaiyasasheb alias Sanjay Ramchandra Gawande v. The State of Maharashtra and anr", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Bhaiyasasheb alias Sanjay Ramchandra Gawande", "respondent": "The State of Maharashtra and anr", "legal_domain": "Civil & Constitutional Law", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Writ Petition / Criminal Appeal", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "scenario_keywords": [], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Dismissed", "disposition": "Dismissed"}}
