AM / BENCH:\nHon'ble Justice Sanjiv Khanna & Justice B.V. Nagarathna\n\nPARTIES:\nInternet Freedom Foundation\n... Petitioner(s) / Appellant(s)\nVersus\nUnion of India, Ministry of Electronics and Information Technology\n... Respondent(s)\n\nSTATUTES & ACTS REFERRED:\nDigital Personal Data Protection Act 2023, Consumer Protection Act 2019, Constitution of India\n\nSECTIONS & PROVISIONS:\nDPDP Act Section 6, Article 21, Consumer Protection Act Section 2(9)\n\nKEY TOPICS & ISSUES:\nDark Patterns, Digital Privacy, Informed Consent, Data Autonomy, K.S. Puttaswamy\n\n================================================================================\n                                   JUDGMENT\n================================================================================\n\n1. FACTS OF THE CASE:\nChallenge to pre-checked cookie consents and deceptive subscription interfaces on government and commercial portals.\n\n2. ISSUES FRAMED:\nDo dark patterns that nudge users into involuntarily relinquishing personal data breach the constitutional right to privacy?\n\n3. LEGAL RATIO & FINDINGS:\nConsent procured through dark patterns or UI deception is null and void ab initio; true digital consent must be freely given, specific, and unambiguous.\n\n4. FINAL ORDER:\nCivil Appeal Allowed; Automated dark patterns and deceptive consent mechanisms declared violative of digital privacy.\n\nOrdered and decreed accordingly.\n\nHon'ble Justice Sanjiv Khanna & Justice B.V. Nagarathna\nSupreme Court of India\n[LEXGUARD REPOSITORY CRYPTOGRAPHIC SEAL]\n	3	t	SCIN010051202026	Civil Appeal	\N	Single Bench	Internet Freedom Foundation	Union of India	\N	\N	Civil Law	In this civil appeal under Constitutional Law, the Court ruled on the dispute between Internet Freedom Foundation and Union of India.	0	2026 LexGuard (Civi) 224A52	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Internet Freedom Foundation v. Union of India", "case_id": "224a521a-003a-409e-9929-a6f803c5b679", "case_no": "Civil Appeal No. 5