://mhc.tn.gov.in/judis	verified	Binding Precedent	IN THE MADRAS HIGH COURT (PRINCIPAL SEAT, CHENNAI)\nAT CHENNAI\n\nCASE NO: Crl.O.P. No. 8912 of 2026\nCNR NUMBER: MHC010456122026\nDECISION DATE: 2026-01-29\nYEAR: 2026\n\nCORAM / BENCH:\nHon'ble Justice G. Jayachandran\n\nPARTIES:\nDr. R. Ramanathan, Former Dean, Government Medical College\n... Appellant(s) / Petitioner(s)\nVersus\nInspector of Police, Vigilance and Anti-Corruption, Chennai\n... Respondent(s) / State\n\nSTATUTORY PROVISIONS & ACTS REFERRED:\nPrevention of Corruption Act 1988, Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita 2023, Code of Criminal Procedure 1973\n\nSECTIONS & CLAUSES UNDER CONSIDERATION:\nPC Act Section 13(1)(b), PC Act Section 17A, BNSS Section 528, CrPC Section 482\n\nKEY LEGAL DOMAINS & TOPICS:\nQuashing of FIR, Vigilance and Anti-Corruption, Prior Approval, Section 17A PC Act, Abuse of Process\n\n================================================================================\n                                   JUDGMENT\n================================================================================\n\n1. FACTUAL MATRIX:\nDVAC registered FIR against retired medical college Dean without seeking prior approval from the Competent Authority under Section 17A of Prevention of Corruption (Amendment) Act 2018.\n\n2. ISSUES FRAMED FOR ADJUDICATION:\n1. Whether prior approval under Section 17A of PC Act is mandatory before registering FIR regarding official administrative decisions?\n\n3. STATUTORY FRAMEWORK & PRECEDENTS:\nSection 17A safeguards public servants from vexatious prosecution by mandating prior approval before conducting inquiry or investigation.\n\n4. RATIO DECIDENDI:\nInvestigation commenced without mandatory prior approval under Section 17A of Prevention of Corruption Act is illegal and liable to be quashed under Section 482 CrPC / Section 528 BNSS.\n\n5. FINAL DISPOSITION & ORDER:\nCriminal Original Petition Allowed; FIR in Crime No. 04/2025 quashed for lack of valid sanction under Section 17A of PC Act.\n\nDecreed and ordered accordingly.\n\nHon'ble Justice G. Jayachandran\nMadras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)\n[LEXGUARD CRYPTOGRAPHIC VERIFIED REPOSITORY RECORD]\n	3	t	HCMA010089122026	Criminal Original Petition (Quashing)	\N	Single Bench	Dr. R. Ramanathan	Inspector of Police, Vigilance and Anti-Corruption	\N	\N	Civil Law	In this criminal original petition under Criminal Law, the Court ruled on the dispute between Dr. R. Ramanathan and Inspector of Police, Vigilance and Anti-Corruption.	0	2026 LexGuard (Crim) 30CE3C	{"court": "Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)", "title": "Dr. R. Ramanathan v. Inspector of Police, Vigilance and Anti-Corruption", "case_id": "30ce3c1f-a9b3-4c8b-9f00-c9bc0d5d5d77", "case_no": "Crl.O.P. No. 8912 of 2026", "parties": "Dr. R. Ramanathan, Former Dean, Government Medical College", "category": "QUASHING", "petitioner": "Dr. R. Ramanathan", "respondent": "Inspector of Police, Vigilance and Anti-Corruption", "legal_domain": "Civil Law", "category_facts": {"category": "QUASHING", "civil_cloak": false, "quash_ground": "Absence of prima facie ingredients", "compromise_affidavit": false, "bhajan_lal_principles": true, "proceedings_challenged": "First Information Report (FIR)"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "BNSS", "section": "528", "canonical": "Section 528 BNSS", "equivalents": ["Section 482 CrPC"]}, {"act": "CRPC", "section": "482", "canonical": "Section 482 CRPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CRPC", "section": "528", "canonical": "Section 528 CRPC", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["quashing_proceeding"], "scenario_keywords": ["quashing of fir"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
