ent	IN THE MADRAS HIGH COURT (PRINCIPAL SEAT, CHENNAI)\nAT CHENNAI\n\nCASE NO: Crl.A. No. 412 of 2026\nCNR NUMBER: MHC010198452026\nDECISION DATE: 2026-02-14\nYEAR: 2026\n\nCORAM / BENCH:\nHon'ble Justice N. Anand Venkatesh\n\nPARTIES:\nS. Murugesan\n... Appellant(s) / Petitioner(s)\nVersus\nState represented by Inspector of Police, Crime Branch, Chennai\n... Respondent(s) / State\n\nSTATUTORY PROVISIONS & ACTS REFERRED:\nIndian Penal Code, Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita 2023, Indian Evidence Act, Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam 2023\n\nSECTIONS & CLAUSES UNDER CONSIDERATION:\nIPC Section 302, BNS Section 103, IEA Section 27, BSA Section 23, CrPC Section 313\n\nKEY LEGAL DOMAINS & TOPICS:\nCircumstantial Evidence, Last Seen Theory, Recovery Memo, Reasonable Doubt, Acquittal\n\n================================================================================\n                                   JUDGMENT\n================================================================================\n\n1. FACTUAL MATRIX:\nThe appellant was convicted for murder under Section 302 IPC primarily based on circumstantial evidence and recovery of weapon under Section 27 of Evidence Act. No independent eyewitness was produced by prosecution.\n\n2. ISSUES FRAMED FOR ADJUDICATION:\n1. Whether the circumstantial chain was complete without any broken link?\n2. Whether discovery under Section 27 IEA / Section 23 BSA meets evidentiary standards in absence of independent panch witnesses?\n\n3. STATUTORY FRAMEWORK & PRECEDENTS:\nReiterating Sharad Birdhichand Sarda v. State of Maharashtra, circumstantial evidence must unerringly point to guilt and be inconsistent with any hypothesis of innocence.\n\n4. RATIO DECIDENDI:\nMere discovery of weapon under Section 27 of Evidence Act without corroborating chain of circumstances cannot sustain conviction under Section 302 IPC.\n\n5. FINAL DISPOSITION & ORDER:\nCriminal Appeal Allowed; Conviction under Section 302 IPC set aside, Appellant acquitted.\n\nDecreed and ordered accordingly.\n\nHon'ble Justice N. Anand Venkatesh\nMadras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)\n[LEXGUARD CRYPTOGRAPHIC VERIFIED REPOSITORY RECORD]\n	3	t	HCMA010004122026	Criminal Appeal	\N	Single Bench	S. Murugesan	State represented by Inspector of Police	\N	\N	Criminal Law	In this criminal appeal under Criminal Law, the Court ruled on the dispute between S. Murugesan and State represented by Inspector of Police.	0	2026 LexGuard (Crim) 326F70	{"court": "Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)", "title": "S. Murugesan v. State represented by Inspector of Police", "case_id": "326f70b2-77fa-421e-9b5c-9d09b0533e7b", "case_no": "Crl.A. No. 412 of 2026", "parties": "S. Murugesan", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "S. Murugesan", "respondent": "State represented by Inspector of Police", "legal_domain": "Criminal Law", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Criminal Appeal Against Conviction", "judicial_disposition": "Allowed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "IPC", "section": "302", "canonical": "Section 302 IPC", "equivalents": ["Section 103 BNS"]}, {"act": "BNS", "section": "103", "canonical": "Section 103 BNS", "equivalents": ["Section 302 IPC"]}, {"act": "IEA", "section": "27", "canonical": "Section 27 IEA", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "BSA", "section": "23", "canonical": "Section 23 BSA", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "CRPC", "section": "313", "canonical": "Section 313 CRPC", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "BNS", "section": "302", "canonical": "Section 302 BNS", "equivalents": []}, {"act": "BSA", "section": "27", "canonical": "Section 27 BSA", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "scenario_keywords": [], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
