Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2022 SCC OnLine Del 791", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}, {"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2001 SCC OnLine Del 637]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2001 SCC OnLine Del 637", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}, {"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2023 SCC OnLine SC 1028]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2023 SCC OnLine SC 1028", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	https://mhc.tn.gov.in/judis	verified	Good Law	A. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\n\nRESERVED ON : 01.10.2026\nPRONOUNCED ON : 09.10.2026\nCORAM:\nTHE HONOURABLE DR. JUSTICE A.D. MARIA CLETE\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026\n& O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026 in\nC.S. (Comm.Div.) No. 223 of 2026\nA Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026:\nAnnapurna Swadisht Limited\nChatterjee International Building\nRepresented by its Authorized Signatory\nMr.Aninda Kumar Bhattavharya,\n13th Floor Units A01, A02 and 33A,\nJawaharlal Nehru Road, Kolkata 700071\n..Applicant(s) in both\nVs\nGEF Foods India Private Limited\n15/29, Thambu Chetty Street,\nGeorge Town,Chennai 600001\nand also at\n4th Floor, FREEDOM HOUSE,\n8-2-334/70 & 71, Road No. 5,\nBanjara Hills, Hyderabad - 500 034\nRep by its Authorised Signatory\nMr. A. Saravanan\n..Respondent(s) in both\nPRAYER IN A Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026:\nTo vacate the interim injunction granted Order dated 05.08.2026 in O.A.Nos.\n779 & 778 of 2026 in C.S.(Comm.Div.) No. 223 of 2026.\n\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\nFor Applicant(s): Mr.Sathish Parasaran, Senior Advocate for\nM/s.P.S.Deepika\nFor Respondent(s): Mr.Arun C. Mohan\nO.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026:\nGEF Foods India Private Limited\n15/29, Thambu Chetty Street, George\nTown,Chennai 600001 and also at 4th Floor,\nFREEDOM HOUSE, 8-2-334/70 71, Road No. 5,\nBanjara Hills, Hyderabad - 500 034 Rep by its\nAuthorised Signatory Mr. A. Saravanan\n..Applicant(s) in both\nVs\nAnnapurna Swadisht Limited\nChatterjee International Building\n13th Floor, Units A01, A02, and 33A,\nJawaharlal Nehru Road,\nKolkata-700071\n..Respondent(s) in both\nPRAYER IN O.A. No. 778 of 2026:\nTo grant an ad-interim injunction restraining the Respondent, its partners,\nsuccessors-in-business, servants, agents, distributors, dealers, stockists,\nwholesalers, retailers, shop keepers, representatives, assigns and all other\npersons claiming through them from infringing the registered trademark\nANNAPOORNA by the use of an identical mark ANNAPURNA or any other\nmark identical or deceptively similar thereto in respect of any goods or services\nor in any other manner whatsoever, pending disposal of the suit.\n\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\nPRAYER IN O.A. No. 779 of 2026:\nTo grant an ad interim injunction restraining the Respondent,themselves, its\nproprietor/directors/partners as the case may be successors in business, servants,\nagents, distributors, dealers, stockists, shop keepers, wholesalers, retailers,\nrepresentatives, assigns and all other persons claiming through or under them\nfrom in any manner passing off and enabling others to pass off the Respondent’s\nblended spices as and for the Applicant’s blended spices by use of the trade\nname/mark ANNAPURNA or any other mark deceptively similar to the\nApplicant’s trademark ANNAPOORNA in any manner whatsoever, pending\ndisposal of the suit.\nFor Applicant(s): Mr.Arun C. Mohan\nFor Respondent(s): Mr.Sathish Parasaran, Senior Advocate for\nM/s.P.S.Deepika\nC O M M O N O R D E R\nThe plaintiff, GEF Foods India Private Limited, has filed this suit against the\ndefendant, Annapurna Swadisht Limited, for infringement of its registered trade\nmarks and for passing off. The plaintiff's mark is ANNAPOORNA. The\ndefendant uses the name and mark ANNAPURNA, including as\n"ANNAPURNA SWADISHT" and "ANNAPURNA SNACKS". In O.A.\nNo.778 of 2026, the plaintiff seeks to restrain the defendant from using\nANNAPURNA in respect of any goods or services or in any other manner\nwhatsoever. In O.A. No.779 of 2026, it seeks to restrain the defendant from\npassing off its blended spices as the plaintiff's by using the name\n\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\nANNAPURNA.\n2. On 05.08.2026, this Court heard the plaintiff and granted an ad interim\ninjunction in both applications, because the plaintiff held registrations in Class\n30 and the defendant had adopted a mark which sounds similar and was likely\nto confuse buyers, and ordered notice. The defendant then filed A. No.3875 of\n2026 to vacate the order in O.A. No.779 of 2026, and A. No.3876 of 2026 to\nvacate the order in O.A. No.778 of 2026, under Order XXXIX Rule 4 of the\nCode of Civil Procedure, 1908 ("CPC"). As all four applications concern the\nsame marks and facts, they were heard together and are disposed of by this\ncommon order. The parties are referred to as they are ranked in the suit.\n3. The plaintiff's case is that, the business of the plaintiff's predecessor,\n"Sree Annapoorna Foods", goes back to about 1980, when Mr. Damodaraswamy\nNaidu and his brothers, who ran the Sree Annapoorna hotels and restaurants in\nCoimbatore, began making spice powders and allied food products under the\nmark "Sree Annapoorna". The firm was renamed "Sree Annapoorna Foods" on\n24.11.1984 and was reconstituted from time to time. Its products include\nturmeric, chilli and coriander powders, sambar and rasam powders, a range of\nmasalas, idli-dosai powder, dal mix, vadagam, appalams, gulab jamun mix and\n\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\npongal mixes etc.\n4. The predecessor obtained registrations Nos.988210, 988211 and 988212\nin Class 30, applied for on 06.02.2001, and No.1803022 in Class 29, applied for\non 06.04.2009, besides copyright registration No.A-114222/2015 for the artistic\nwork in its label. By an Agreement and an IP Assignment Deed, both dated\n31.01.2025, the predecessor transferred its business, and assigned the trade\nmarks, the trade name and the goodwill, to the plaintiff, which applied to the\nTrade Marks Registry on 24.03.2025 to have the registrations brought into its\nname. The plaintiff sets out sales and promotional figures from 2000-2001\nonwards; for 2023-2024 the sales shown are about Rs.41.41 crores. According\nto the plaintiff, by reason of continuous use, extensive sales and promotion for\nover four decades, the mark "ANNAPOORNA" has acquired substantial\ngoodwill, reputation and distinctiveness among consumers.\n5. The plaintiff states that in or about March 2026 it came to know that the\ndefendant was making and selling snacks, fryums and allied food products\nunder the names "ANNAPURNA SNACKS" and "ANNAPURNA\nSWADISHT", which, it says, sound the same as and look like its mark\nANNAPOORNA. It lists seven applications made by the defendant to register\n\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\nANNAPURNA-formative device marks in Class 30, four of which are shown as\n"Opposed". It states that it sent a cease-and-desist notice on 23.03.2026, which\nthe defendant neither answered nor complied with, and that the defendant's\nadoption is dishonest. It pleads that these are low-priced goods, bought by\nordinary people, including those who cannot read, on the strength of an\nimperfect memory of the mark. It claims that, as it sought urgent interim relief,\nit was not required to undergo pre-institution mediation under Section 12A of\nthe Commercial Courts Act, 2015. The suit was instituted in July 2026.\n6. In support of the applications for interim injunction, the plaintiff contends\nthat it has established a strong prima facie case on the basis of its prior use,\nstatutory registrations and the alleged deceptive similarity between the\ncompeting marks. It is further averred that the defendant's continued use of the\nimpugned mark would cause confusion among consumers, dilute the\ndistinctiveness of the plaintiff's trademark and result in irreparable injury to its\ngoodwill and reputation, which cannot be adequately compensated in monetary\nterms. According to the plaintiff, the balance of convenience lies in its favour, as\nit has invested substantial time and resources in establishing its trademark,\nwhereas the defendant would suffer no prejudice if restrained from using the\nimpugned mark. On these grounds, the plaintiff seeks interim injunctions\n\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\nrestraining the defendant from infringing its registered trademark\n"ANNAPOORNA" and from passing off its goods as those of the plaintiff,\npending disposal of the suit.\n7. The defendant's case in the applications to vacate : In the affidavit filed in\nsupport of A. Nos.3875 and 3876 of 2026, the defendant states that a partnership\nfirm, M/s. Annapurna Agro Industries, was formed at Kolkata on 27.11.2015,\nwith a factory in Burdwan District, West Bengal, and began making papads,\nfryums and allied products under the name ANNAPURNA. After some\nreconstitutions, the partners incorporated Annapurna Swadisht Private Limited\non 11.02.2022, and by a Deed of Assignment of the same date transferred the\nfirm's business, assets and goodwill to the company. The company was\nconverted into a public limited company on 08.07.2022, and its shares were\nlisted on the National Stock Exchange on 27.09.2022. It makes packaged\nsnacks, namkeens, pellets, corn tortilla chips, puffed corn, corn curls, cheese\npuffs, dried dumplings (bori) etc., and savouries, under sub-brands such as\n"Chatpata", "Rambo" and "Badam Pakora" etc., and does not make or sell raw\nspices, spice powders or blended masalas. Its turnover is stated as Rs.160.17\ncrores in 2022-2023, Rs.264.43 crores in 2023-2024, Rs.374.10 crores in 2024-\n2025 and about Rs.414.92 crores in 2025-2026.\n\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\n8. The defendant avers that the ad interim order restrains it in respect of\n"any goods", although the plaintiff, on its own showing and under Clause 2.6 of\nits Assignment Deed, deals only in blended spices, masala powders, condiments\nand ready-to-cook mixes, and no trader can claim a monopoly over every item\nin a broad class. Ready-to-eat snacks and cooking spices are different goods,\nsold on different shelves for different purposes, and no instance of actual\nconfusion has been shown. "Annapurna" is the name of the Hindu goddess of\nfood and is widely used in the food trade. The plaintiff trades mainly in South\nIndia, especially Tamil Nadu, while the defendant trades in the Eastern,\nNorthern and Western States. The plaintiff's predecessor had opposed the\ndefendant's applications from February 2023, and Annexure D to the plaintiff's\nown Assignment Deed lists those oppositions; the statement that the plaintiff\nlearnt of the defendant only in March 2026 is therefore untrue, and was made to\ncreate a false urgency, to obtain an order without notice and to avoid Section\n12A; and the defendant is taking steps to file an application under Order VII\nRule 11 CPC. The order, unless varied, would force the closure of factories each\nemploying about 800 to 900 workers, cause default on bank loans and cause\nloss to public shareholders, while the plaintiff would suffer no loss if snacks and\nnamkeens were excluded. Without prejudice, the defendant offers to undertake\nnot to make or sell raw spices or blended masala powders under the name\n\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\nANNAPURNA, and to show clearly on its snack packets its place of\nmanufacture and corporate identity. In the body of the affidavit the defendant\nasks for the order to be modified or partly discharged so as to exclude its goods;\nin the prayer it asks for the order to be vacated.\n9. Learned Senior Counsel for the defendant submitted that the defendant\nhas a turnover exceeding Rs.400 crores and has been carrying on business\ncontinuously from 2015, initially as Annapurna Agro Industries and thereafter\nthrough the company incorporated in 2022, which subsequently became a public\nlimited company and was listed on the National Stock Exchange.\n10. The principal contention is that the ex parte order was obtained by\nsuppression of material facts. Though paragraph 18 of the plaint states that the\nplaintiff came to know of the defendant’s mark only in or about March 2026, the\nplaintiff’s predecessor had opposed the defendant’s trade mark applications as\nearly as February 2023. Those oppositions are also recorded in Annexure D to\nthe Assignment Deed dated 31.01.2025, through which the plaintiff claims title.\nIt was therefore submitted that the plaintiff could not rely upon the\npredecessor’s goodwill and rights while disowning its predecessor’s knowledge.\nAttention was also drawn to the discrepancy regarding the plaintiff’s year of\n\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\nincorporation.\n11. Reliance was placed on Kent RO System Ltd. v. Gattubhai, (2022) 2\nHCC (Del) 701 : 2022 SCC OnLine Del 791, wherein an ex parte injunction\nwas vacated for suppression and concealment of material facts, and on B.L. &\nCo. v. Pfizer Products Inc., 2001 SCC OnLine Del 637 (DB), where delay was\nheld to be a sufficient ground for refusing ex parte restraint.\n12. It was further contended that the same facts bear upon the maintainability\nof the suit under Section 12A of the Commercial Courts Act, 2015. According to\nthe defendant, the plaintiff, through its predecessor, had knowledge from\nFebruary 2023 but instituted the suit only in July 2026 without undergoing preinstitution mediation. Relying on Yamini Manohar v. T.K.D. Keerthi, 2023\nSCC OnLine SC 1028, it was submitted that urgency cannot be used as a\ndisguise or camouflage to bypass the statutory requirement of pre-litigation\nmediation. It was also pointed out that even the plaintiff’s passing-off prayer is\nconfined to blended spices.\n13. Referring to Clause 2.6 of the Assignment Deed, learned Senior Counsel\nsubmitted that the rights assigned to the plaintiff were confined to the specified\n\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\n“Permitted Categories”. Reliance was placed on Section 37 of the Trade Marks\nAct, 1999 and on the principle that an assignee cannot acquire a better or wider\nright than that possessed by the assignor. The defendant’s packaged snacks,\nnamkeens, fryums, pellets and savouries, it was submitted, fall outside those\npermitted categories. Reliance was also placed on Vishnudas Trading v. Vazir\nSultan Tobacco Co. Ltd., (1997) 4 SCC 201, for the proposition that\nregistration in respect of an entire class does not necessarily entitle a proprietor\nto claim monopoly over every product within that class when actual use is\nconfined to particular goods.\n14. As regards acquiescence, reliance was placed on Power Control\nAppliances v. Sumeet Machines (P) Ltd., (1994) 2 SCC 448. It was submitted\nthat the plaintiff’s predecessor, despite having knowledge from February 2023,\npermitted the defendant to expand its business, obtain listing on the National\nStock Exchange, complete its public issue and build its brand before issuing a\ncease-and-desist notice only on 23.03.2026. Kent RO System Ltd. v. Gattubhai\n(2022) SCC Online Del 791 was again relied upon to contend that a plaintiff\nwho knowingly permits a defendant to build up its reputation cannot thereafter\nseek equitable injunctive relief.\n\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\n15. In the written submissions dated 01.10.2026, the defendant contended\nthat a claim under Section 29(4) of the Act requires the plaintiff to establish a\nreputation in India, use by the defendant without due cause, and unfair\nadvantage or detriment to the distinctive character or repute of the mark. It was\ncontended that N. Ranga Rao & Sons Pvt. Ltd. v. Sree Annapoorna Agro Foods,\nO.S.A.(CAD) No.3 of 2022, does not assist the plaintiff, because the Division\nBench interfered only after finding that the mark "CYCLE" had acquired a\nreputation of its own, independent of agarbattis; whereas here the plaintiff's\npleaded market is largely confined to the Southern States, and the defendant\ntrades mainly in the Eastern, Northern and Western regions. In the course of oral\narguments, learned Senior Counsel advanced the same theme in a broader way.\nIt was submitted that the reputation of the "Annapoorna" name belongs to the\npredecessor at Coimbatore, which, in learned Senior Counsel's words, "carved\nout a small portion" for the plaintiff; that the plaintiff, incorporated only in\n2024, obtained rights in 2025 for selected products different from the\ndefendant's; that the plaintiff received the predecessor's oppositions "but they\ndon't get the well known mark status"; and that it was not open to the plaintiff to\nclaim the reputation of the Annapoorna restaurants as its own.\n\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\n16. It was also submitted that “Annapurna” or “Annapoorna” is a common\ndevotional reference associated with food and nourishment and is widely used\nin the food trade. The defendant does not dispute the validity of the plaintiff’s\nregistration, but contends that the expression is not invented and that mere\nregistration of the marks in Class 30 cannot confer a monopoly over every food\nproduct within that class. It was further pointed out that, in O.A.No.779 of 2026,\nthe interim order proceeds on the footing that the defendant deals in blended\nspices, whereas the defendant’s case is that it does not manufacture or sell such\nproducts.\n17. The Learned Senior Counsel further submitted that there is no evidence\nof any actual confusion, mistaken purchase, consumer complaint or distributor\nconfusion despite the defendant’s long-standing presence in the market.\nReliance was placed on Wander Ltd. v. Antox India (P) Ltd., 1990 Supp SCC\n727, to contend that the fact that the defendant is already carrying on business is\nrelevant to the balance of convenience and that interim relief should preserve,\nrather than disturb, the existing position.\n18. Learned Senior Counsel further submitted that the defendant employs\nabout 800 to 900 workers in each unit, has substantial banking and financial\n\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\ncommitments and, being a listed company, would suffer serious prejudice if\ncompelled to immediately discontinue its existing packaging and branding. At\nthe same time, it was fairly accepted that, though the rival marks may differ\nvisually, they are phonetically similar. The defendant therefore offered to\nconfine its use to “Annapurna Swadisht”, reiterated its undertaking not to sell\nraw spices or blended masala under the mark ANNAPURNA and offered to\nprominently disclose its corporate identity on the snack packets. On these\ngrounds, it was prayed that the interim order be modified so as to permit the\ndefendant to manufacture and sell packaged snacks, namkeens, fryums, pellets\nand savouries under the marks “ANNAPURNA” and “ANNAPURNA\nSWADISHT”. In the written submission of the defendants, they submitted that\nthe defendant does not seek complete vacation of the order dated 05.08.2026,\nbut only its modification so as to permit the defendant to manufacture and sell\npackaged snacks, namkeens, fryums, pellets and savouries, which are stated to\nbe different from the plaintiff’s blended spices and masala powders.\n19. Learned counsel for the plaintiff submitted that its predecessor had\ncontinuously used the trademark "SREE ANNAPOORNA" since about 1980,\nnotwithstanding successive reconstitutions of the partnership firm. In support of\nits prior use and goodwill, the plaintiff has produced partnership deeds and\n\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\nbusiness records from 1984, newspaper advertisements, invoices from 1984 to\n2024, trademark registration certificates relating to applications made in 2001\nand 2009, the Assignment Deed dated 31.01.2025 and its own invoices for\n2025. The registrations cover various food products in Classes 29 and 30 and\nremain unchallenged. The defendant's adoption of "ANNAPURNA", even\naccording to its own case, commenced only in 2015, several decades after the\nplaintiff's predecessor had adopted and registered its marks. Therefore, the\ndefendant cannot claim honest and concurrent use merely on the strength of its\nsubsequent adoption and turnover.\n20. As regards the defendant's evidence, learned counsel submitted that the\nturnover figures are not supported by underlying sales records and that the\nadvertisements produced are merely promotional images without particulars of\npublication, such as the newspaper's name, date, edition or circulation. It was\nfurther pointed out that the invoices dated 01.04.2022, 08.04.2022 and\n01.06.2022 bear the name "Annapurna Swadisht Limited", although, according\nto the plaintiff, the defendant was converted into a public limited company\nunder that name only on 08.07.2022. This discrepancy, it was submitted, raises\nserious doubts regarding the genuineness of those invoices and calls for\nproduction and scrutiny of the original records. Learned counsel further\n\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\ncontended that the defendant, being an established commercial enterprise, ought\nto have verified the existing registrations before adopting the mark. Having\nadopted it despite the plaintiff's prior rights, the defendant cannot rely upon its\nsubsequent business expansion, substantial turnover or financial commitments\nto justify continued use.\n21. On the nature of the goods, learned counsel submitted that the defendant\nitself sought registration of "ANNAPURNA" in Class 30, in which the plaintiff\nholds prior registrations, and therefore cannot contend that the competing goods\nare wholly unrelated. It was argued that spices, snacks, fryums and other\npackaged food products are allied and cognate goods, marketed through similar\ntrade channels to the same class of consumers. Even assuming that the goods\nare dissimilar, Section 29(4) of the Trade Marks Act, 1999 affords protection to\na registered mark where the statutory requirements are satisfied. Reliance was\nplaced on N. Ranga Rao & Sons Pvt. Ltd. v. Sree Annapoorna Agro Foods,\nO.S.A.(CAD) No.3 of 2022, particularly paragraphs 21 to 23, and Midas\nHygiene Industries (P) Ltd. v. Sudhir Bhatia, (2004) 3 SCC 90, paragraph 7, to\ncontend that infringement of a registered trademark ordinarily warrants\ninjunctive protection.\n\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\n22. With regard to Clause 2.6 of the Assignment Deed dated 31.01.2025,\nlearned counsel submitted that the clause governs only the rights and obligations\nof the parties to that deed. The defendant, being a stranger to the agreement,\ncannot rely upon a contractual restriction concerning the plaintiff's product\nrange as permission to use a deceptively similar mark. According to the\nplaintiff, the extent of its actual use cannot be equated with the extent of\nstatutory protection available to its registered trademarks.\n23. On delay and acquiescence, learned counsel submitted that mere\nknowledge of the defendant's mark or delay in instituting proceedings does not\namount to consent or acquiescence. It was pointed out that the plaintiff and its\npredecessor had consistently asserted their rights by opposing the defendant's\ntrademark applications and issuing the cease-and-desist notice dated\n23.03.2026. Acquiescence requires positive conduct amounting to\nencouragement, assent or consent, which is absent in the present case. Reliance\nwas placed on Midas Hygiene Industries (P) Ltd. v. Sudhir Bhatia, (2004) 3\nSCC 90, Power Control Appliances v. Sumeet Machines (P) Ltd., (1994) 2\nSCC 448, Hindustan Pencils (P) Ltd. v. India Stationery Products Co.,\nI.A.No.2275 of 1988 in Suit No.941 of 1988, and V. Rajendran v. Kovai Heart\nFoundation, MANU/TN/0794/2026. It was submitted that mere delay cannot\n\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\ndefeat an injunction, particularly where the alleged infringement is continuing\nand the adoption is dishonest.\n24. As regards Section 12A of the Commercial Courts Act, 2015, learned\ncounsel submitted that the defendant was aware of the opposition proceedings\nand had failed to comply with the cease-and-desist notice, but continued using\nthe impugned mark. The defendant cannot, therefore, insist that the plaintiff\nshould have further delayed the proceedings by resorting to pre-institution\nmediation. Relying on Novenco Building and Industry A/S v. Xero Energy\nEngineering Solutions Pvt. Ltd., 2025 INSC 1256, particularly paragraphs 24\nand 25, learned counsel submitted that urgency must be assessed from the plaint\nand supporting materials and that, in cases of continuing infringement, the\npassage of time does not necessarily extinguish the need for urgent interim\nprotection.\n25. Finally, learned counsel submitted that the defendant's plea of hardship\narising from its business operations, employment commitments and financial\nliabilities cannot override the plaintiff's prior statutory rights. It was contended\nthat the plaintiff has established a strong prima facie case based on longstanding\nprior use, subsisting registrations and the assignment of the business and\n\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\ngoodwill in its favour. The defendant has not demonstrated any subsequent\nchange in circumstances warranting vacation or modification of the interim\ninjunction dated 05.08.2026. On the contrary, permitting the defendant to\ncontinue using the impugned mark during the pendency of the suit would enable\nit to further expand its business under the disputed mark and cause continuing\ninjury to the plaintiff's goodwill and reputation. Accordingly, learned counsel\nprayed that A.Nos.3875 and 3876 of 2026 be dismissed and that the interim\ninjunction already granted in O.A.Nos.778 and 779 of 2026 be continued and\nmade absolute pending disposal of the suit.\n26. In reply to the allegation that the invoices were fabricated, learned Senior\nCounsel for the defendant submitted that the charge was made recklessly and\nwithout any supporting affidavit. It was pointed out that the invoices, both\nbefore and after the change of name, bear the same Corporate Identity Number,\nGSTIN and PAN, showing continuity of the same company. The omission of the\nword “Private” in the earlier invoices was attributed to the accounting software\nreflecting the subsequently updated company name, and was said to be a clerical\nor software issue rather than fabrication or back-dating. The defendant also\noffered to produce the original invoices.\n\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\n27. It was further submitted that, even ignoring the defendant’s existence\nprior to 2022, its turnover was Rs.160.17 crores in 2022-2023 and increased to\nabout Rs.414.92 crores by 2025-2026. According to the defendant, a listed\ncompany with public shareholders and business of such magnitude could not\nhave operated secretly, and the allegation of fabrication or perjury was therefore\nbaseless.\nDiscussion:\n28. The defendant contends that the plaintiff's business, as described in\nClause 2.6 of the Assignment Deed, is confined to spices, masala powders,\ncondiments and ready-to-cook mixes, and that the registration of the trade\nmarks relied upon by it does not confer a monopoly over every product falling\nwithin the same class. While it is true that registration does not confer a\nmonopoly over an entire class of goods, this contention does not, by itself,\ndefeat the plaintiff's claim. Under Section 29(2) of the Trade Marks Act, 1999,\nprotection extends to similar goods where the similarity between the competing\nmarks and goods is likely to cause confusion among the public, including an\nassociation with the registered trademark. Clause 2.6 regulates what the\nplaintiff, as assignee, may do as between itself and its assignor; the defendant is\na stranger to it, and it confers no right on any third party. Section 37 of the Act,\n\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\nrelied on by the defendant, deals with the registered proprietor's power to assign\nthe mark. The principle that an assignee takes no better right than its assignor\nhad is sound, but it does not help the defendant at this stage: the predecessor\nheld the registrations in Classes 30 and 29, the plaintiff sues on those\nregistrations, and the defendant has not shown that the registrations themselves\nwere assigned only in part. Even the categories listed in Clause 2.6 include\npapadam, vadam and wafers, which are the defendant's own original goods.\nWhether the assignment limits the registered rights in any respect may be\nexamined at the trial. Vishnudas Trading concerns the rectification of a\nregistration that is wider than the goods actually traded in, which is worked out\nin rectification proceedings; the defendant has taken none.\n29. The distinction pointed out by the defendant that the plaintiff's products\nrequire cooking, whereas the defendant's snacks are ready for consumption, is\nrelevant but not conclusive. The question is whether, considering the nature of\nthe products, their trade channels, the class of purchasers and the overall\nsimilarity of the marks, an ordinary consumer with imperfect recollection is\nlikely to believe that the defendant's products originate from the plaintiff or an\nundertaking commercially connected with it. The close phonetic similarity\nbetween "ANNAPOORNA" and "ANNAPURNA", when used for cooking\n\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\ningredients and packaged snacks respectively, may create an impression that the\nlatter constitute another product line of the same manufacturer. Such confusion\nrelates to the commercial origin of the goods, even though consumers may be\nable to distinguish the products themselves. The presumption of confusion\nunder Section 29(3), on which the plaintiff relied, applies only where both the\nmark and the goods are identical, which is not the case here; the plaintiff must\ntherefore show a likelihood of confusion or association under Section 29(2)(b).\nTo the ear, the two marks are the same word, as learned Senior Counsel fairly\naccepted. The goods are low-priced foodstuffs bought from memory, often by\npersons who cannot read the English letters on the packet. The word\n"SWADISHT" simply means "tasty"; it describes the goods and does not\ndistinguish their source, so the offer to confine use to "Annapurna Swadisht"\ndoes not remove the confusion. The defendant's business began with papads and\nfryums, while the plaintiff's registrations, on its submission, cover appalams and\nwafers, and papads and appalams, fryums and vadams, are practically the same\ngoods. This holds for the defendant's other snacks as well, because\nANNAPURNA is used by the defendant as its house mark across its entire\nrange; if the same house mark, barred on papads and fryums, were allowed to\ncontinue on the other snacks of the same maker, sold in the same shops to the\nsame buyers, the association with a single source would carry on undiminished.\n\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\nThe absence of proof of actual confusion does not alter this, because Section\n29(2) requires only a likelihood of confusion or association, and an interim\ninjunction is granted precisely so that actual confusion does not occur.\n30. Therefore, the defendant cannot defeat the plaintiff's claim merely by\ndescribing its products as ready-to-eat snacks. However, the objection regarding\nthe use of the expression "any goods" in the interim injunction requires separate\nconsideration. The restraint must be confined to the goods in dispute and such\nsimilar or allied goods in respect of which the materials on record establish a\nlikelihood of confusion, and cannot be extended indiscriminately to unrelated\ngoods.\n31. In the present case, the relevant consideration is whether the use of\n"ANNAPURNA" for packaged ready-to-eat snacks, when compared with the\nplaintiff's mark "ANNAPOORNA" used for spices, masala powders,\ncondiments and ready-to-cook mixes, is likely to suggest a common commercial\norigin. The mere fact that one product requires cooking while the other is ready\nfor consumption does not, by itself, exclude the possibility of such an\nassociation. Since the plaintiff's case is made out under Section 29(2), it is\nunnecessary at this stage to decide whether its mark has the "reputation in India"\n\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\nthat Section 29(4) requires, on which the parties have relied on N. Ranga Rao\n& Sons Pvt. Ltd. v. Sree Annapoorna Agro Foods; that question is best decided\non evidence at the trial.\n32. The defendant further contends that "ANNAPURNA" is the name of the\nHindu Goddess of Food and is commonly used in the food trade, and therefore\nthe plaintiff cannot claim exclusive rights over the name. However, the religious\nsignificance of a name does not, by itself, render its use as a trademark\nincapable of protection. The plaintiff does not claim exclusive rights over the\nname of the Goddess for all purposes, but seeks protection of its registered\ntrademark as an identifier of the commercial origin of its goods.\n33. The relevant question is whether the plaintiff's mark has come to identify\nits spices, masala powders, condiments and ready-to-cook mixes, and whether\nthe defendant's use of a closely resembling name for ready-to-eat snacks is\nlikely to make purchasers believe that such products originate from the plaintiff\nor an undertaking commercially connected with it. This question cannot be\ndecided merely on the basis of the religious origin of the name.\n34. The contention that the name is common to the food trade must also be\n\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\nsupported by evidence of its relevant commercial use. If such use is established,\nit may have a bearing on the distinctiveness of the mark and the extent of\nprotection available. Nevertheless, the defendant cannot claim immunity from\nan otherwise confusing use merely because the name refers to a deity.\nTherefore, the objection based solely on the religious significance of\n"ANNAPURNA" is insufficient to defeat the plaintiff's claim.\n35. The defendant also contends that the plaintiff's business is predominantly\nconfined to South India, particularly Tamil Nadu, whereas the defendant carries\non business in the Eastern, Northern and Western States. However, this\ncircumstance does not, by itself, constitute a defence to an action for\ninfringement. The geographical extent of the plaintiff's actual sales cannot be\nequated with the territorial extent of its statutory rights. Unless the registration\nis subject to a territorial restriction, the plaintiff's registered rights are not\nconfined to the States in which it predominantly carries on business.\n36. Though geographical separation may be relevant in assessing the\nlikelihood of confusion, the parties' trade channels and the appropriate extent of\ninterim relief, it does not independently entitle the defendant to use a\nconfusingly similar mark for similar goods. The defendant must establish an\n\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\napplicable statutory defence, and the mere assertion that the parties principally\ncarry on business in different regions is insufficient. No such defence is\navailable on the defendant's own dates. The defence of prior use in Section 34 of\nthe Act protects only a person whose continuous use began before the registered\nproprietor's use or registration, whichever is earlier; the predecessor's use goes\nback to 1984 and its registrations to 2001, while the defendant, on its own case,\nbegan in 2015. For the same reason, this Court need not decide the serious\ncharge that the defendant's three earliest invoices were fabricated. The\ndefendant has offered an explanation and the original invoices, and whether that\nexplanation is true is a matter for the trial; a finding of fraud cannot be recorded\non a summary hearing, and it is unnecessary, since even if the defendant's use\nfrom 27.11.2015 is accepted in full, the plaintiff's predecessor is earlier by about\nthree decades.\n37. The defendant's contention regarding the plaintiff's prior knowledge of its\nmark also does not, by itself, establish acquiescence. The opposition\nproceedings initiated against the defendant's trademark applications constitute\nan assertion of rights rather than consent to its use. Mere delay, without any\nconduct indicating assent or encouragement, does not amount to acquiescence,\nas explained in Power Control Appliances and Midas Hygiene. Further,\n\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\nparagraph 19 of the plaint discloses that the defendant's trademark applications\nwere opposed. The defendant's public issue, listing and brand-building all took\nplace after its applications had been opposed, that is, with full notice of the\nchallenge to its mark. Kent RO(supra), relied on for estoppel, turned on facts\nwhere both parties were registered proprietors and had exchanged a draft coexistence agreement; there is no such conduct by the plaintiff or its predecessor\nhere. B.L. & Co (supra). considered whether, in view of delay, an ex parte\nrestraint ought to have been granted at all; the defendant has now been fully\nheard, and on the question whether the injunction should continue on the merits,\ndelay alone is not a bar.\n38. Nevertheless, the inaccurate statement concerning the plaintiff's\nknowledge is material while considering the urgency pleaded for obtaining the\nex parte interim injunction dated 05.08.2026. The first proviso to Order XXXIX\nRule 4 CPC applies where a party has knowingly made a false or misleading\nstatement in relation to a material particular in the injunction application or\nsupporting affidavit. Even assuming that the said requirement is satisfied, this\nCourt considers that complete vacation of the injunction is unnecessary in the\ninterests of justice. The opposition proceedings were disclosed in the plaint, the\ninaccuracy does not displace the plaintiff's prima facie registered rights, and\n\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\ninterim protection remains warranted to the extent supported by the findings on\nsimilarity and likelihood of confusion. Therefore, the unrestricted restraint\nconcerning "any goods" requires modification by confining it to the goods for\nwhich prima facie protection has been established. This distinguishes Kent RO,\nwhere documents detrimental to the plaintiff were deliberately withheld from\nthe Court; here the plaintiff itself filed the Assignment Deed with its Annexure\nD and its certificate of incorporation. This Court nonetheless disapproves of the\nstatement on knowledge, and of the reference in the plaint to incorporation in\n2008 when the certificate shows 23.12.2024; a party seeking an order without\nnotice to the other side must state the facts accurately and fairly. The plaintiff's\nplea that no change in circumstances has been shown does not arise, since that\nrequirement, in the second proviso to Order XXXIX Rule 4, applies only to an\norder passed after hearing the party.\n39. The objection raised under Section 12A of the Commercial Courts Act,\n2015, is left open for consideration. Any application filed under Order VII Rule\n11 CPC shall be decided on its own merits, including the question whether the\nsuit genuinely contemplated urgent interim relief at the time of its institution.\nNo finding on that issue is rendered in the present order.\n\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\n40. The hardship pleaded by the defendant, including its financial\ncommitments, manufacturing operations and employment obligations, deserves\nconsideration while assessing the balance of convenience and determining the\nappropriate relief. However, the injunction restrains the use of the impugned\nmark and does not prohibit the defendant from manufacturing or selling snacks\nand namkeens under a non-infringing mark. Therefore, the contention that the\ninjunction would necessarily result in closure of its factories must be supported\nby materials showing why compliance through a change of branding is\nimpracticable.\n41. Similarly, it cannot be presumed that the plaintiff would suffer no injury\nmerely because it does not presently manufacture snacks or namkeens. If the\ndefendant's use of the impugned mark is prima facie likely to suggest a common\ncommercial origin, the resulting injury would affect the plaintiff's goodwill and\nits control over the reputation associated with its trademark, apart from any\nimmediate loss of sales. The scale of the defendant's business cannot, by itself,\noutweigh such injury. As to the status quo under Wander Ltd (supra)., the\ndefendant's use has been under challenge in the Trade Marks Registry since\n2023, and its growth took place in the face of that challenge; protecting\nregistered rights against a prima facie infringing use does not upset the status\n\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\nquo.\n42. Accordingly, the hardship pleaded by the defendant does not justify\nexcluding snacks and namkeens altogether from the injunction when those are\nthe very goods in respect of which a likelihood of confusion has been prima\nfacie established. Any genuine operational difficulty may instead be addressed\nby granting a limited period for changing labels and packaging, subject to\nappropriate safeguards, without permitting indefinite continuation of the\nimpugned use.\n43. The defendant's offer to disclose its corporate identity and place of\nmanufacture on its packaging is relevant, but such disclosure may not\nnecessarily eliminate confusion arising from the prominent use of the impugned\nmark. Even where a purchaser understands that the goods are manufactured by a\ndifferent company, the purchaser may still believe that the company is\nassociated with, licensed by or otherwise commercially connected with the\nplaintiff. Whether the proposed disclosures are sufficient to remove such\nconfusion must be assessed from the overall presentation of the packaging. The\nundertaking, by itself, does not establish that the likelihood of confusion would\nbe eliminated.\n\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\n44. Though the defendant has sought modification, variation or partial\ndischarge of the injunction in the body of its affidavit, the final prayer seeks\ncomplete vacation of the order. Nevertheless, the substance of the defendant's\nrequest is clear, and this Court is empowered to consider appropriate\nmodification under Order XXXIX Rule 4 CPC. However, the undertaking\noffered by the defendant does not justify excluding snacks and namkeens from\nthe injunction when the use of the impugned mark on those goods has been\nfound prima facie likely to cause confusion. As regards O.A. No.779 of 2026,\nthe passing off claim is confined to blended spices, which the defendant says it\ndoes not sell and undertakes not to sell under ANNAPURNA; an injunction in\nthose terms causes it no hardship, and rests on its own undertaking.\n45. In view of the foregoing discussion, the defences raised by the defendant\ndo not justify its continued use of the impugned mark. The plaintiff has\nestablished a prima facie case of infringement and a likelihood of irreparable\ninjury to its goodwill and reputation. The balance of convenience also lies in\nfavour of the plaintiff. Accordingly, the plaintiff is entitled to an interim\ninjunction pending disposal of the suit, confined to the goods and uses identified\nabove.\n\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026 & O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026\nDR.A.D.MARIA CLETE, J.\nAY\n46. In the result O.A. No.778 of 2026 AND O.A. No.779 of 2026 are\nallowed. The ad interim injunction granted on 05.08.2026 is made absolute till\nthe disposal of the suit. A. Nos.3875 and 3876 of 2026, seeking vacation of the\ninterim injunction, are dismissed. There shall be no order as to costs.\n09-10-2026\n(1/2)\nAY\nPRE-DELIVERY ORDER MADE IN\nA. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026\n& O.A. Nos. 778 & 779 of 2026 in\nC.S. (Comm.Div.) No. 223 of 2026\n(1/2)	32	t	HCMA010038752026	Judicial Precedent	2026-10-09	Single Bench	in both	GEF Foods India Private Limited	\N	Mr.Arun C. Mohan	Civil Law	In this judgment under Civil Law, the Court ruled on the dispute between 05 A-3875-3876-2026 and the Respondent.	0	2026 LexGuard (Judg) 796C02	{"court": "Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)", "title": "Annapurna Swadisht Limited v. GEF Foods India Private Limited", "case_id": "796c02b7-3d1b-4e04-b6ac-552262efaf8b", "case_no": "A. Nos. 3875 & 3876 of 2026", "parties": "Annapurna Swadisht Limited v. GEF Foods India Private Limited", "category": "COMMERCIAL_CIVIL", "petitioner": "Annapurna Swadisht Limited", "respondent": "GEF Foods India Private Limited", "legal_domain": "Civil Law", "category_facts": {"category": "COMMERCIAL_CIVIL", "dispute_type": "Trademark Infringement & Passing Off (Section 2(1)(c) Commercial Courts Act)", "statutory_basis": "Commercial Courts Act / Civil Procedure Code", "interim_injunction_granted": true}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "scenario_keywords": [], "procedural_posture": {"remedy"