nbunithi,\nAdditional Public Prosecutor\nFor intervener : Mr.R.Gandhi, Senior Counsel\nfor M/s.Gandhi Association\nPETITION FOR ANTICIPATORY BAIL UNDER SECTION 482 OF BNSS\nPRAYER :-\nFor Anticipatory Bail in Crime no.167 of 2024 on the file of the Respondent\nPolice.\n\n1/5\n\nCRL OP(MD). No.11854 of 2024\nORDER : The Court made the following order :-\nThe petitioner/Accused, who apprehends arrest at the hands of the respondent\npolice for the offences punishable under Sections 406 and 420 I.P.C, in Crime No.167\nof 2024, seeks anticipatory bail.\n2. The case of the prosecution is that the petitioner is running a gold jewellery\nand also pawn broker shop. The defacto complainant along with her sister/Manjula\npledged their gold jewels of 240 grams and availed a loan for a sum of Rs.9,05,000/-\nfrom the petitioner and thereafter, the petitioner having received the total amount of\nRs.9,23,000/- including interest, gave some articles and promised to return the\nbalance articles within a week and thereafter, he did not return the balance articles\nand thereby, cheated the defacto complainant.\n3. The learned Senior Counsel appearing on behalf of the petitioner would\nsubmit that this is a case, where the prosecution is bound to issue a summon under\nSection 41A. He would also submit that the when the petitioner is ready to cooperate with the inquiry by virtue of the dictum of the Hon'ble Supreme Court of\nIndia in the case of Ramesh Kumar V. State of NCT of Delhi [(2023)7 SCC 461], the\nsame should be considered. The learned Senior Counsel would also rely upon the\njudgment in Satender Kumar Antil V. Central Bureau of Investigation [2024-SCCOnline-SC-172], to contend that whether he is to be enlarged on bail, is a matter of\n\n2/5\n\nCRL OP(MD). No.11854 of 2024\nright and the respondents have to mandatorily follow the procedure under Section\n41A.\n4. The learned Senior Counsel would also submit that as per the Form which is\nproduced by the petitioner, it can be seen that the defacto complainant's sister viz.,\nManjula had received the jewels and signed in that column.\n5. I have considered the said submissions made by the learned Senior Counsel\nand perused the material records of the case.\n6.As far as the defacto complaint, viz, Jamuna is concerned, it is the contention\nof the petitioner that her sister has received and accordingly, a printed form in which\nin the relevant column also, the said Manjula has signed, is produced.\n7. But however, it can be seen in the instant case that it is the contention of the\nlearned Public Prosecutor that after the defacto complainant's complaints, three more\npersons have approached with the same complaint. When enquiry was conducted in\nall the four cases, the petitioner appeared before the Investigating Officer and by her\nown handwriting, she has given in writing that she will return the jewels within the\nperiod mentioned by her.\n8. Therefore, prima facie, when the jewels are pledged and loan is taken and\nwhen the parties are repaying the loan, it is expected that the petitioner shall return\nthe said jewels.\n\n3/5\n\nCRL OP(MD). No.11854 of 2024\n9. In that view of the matter, when the serious offences are involved, I am\nunable to accept the contention of the Senior Counsel for the petitioner and it cannot\nbe held that prima facie, this case neither requires a custodial interrogation nor the\ncase can be termed as false and therefore, this is not a fit case for grant of anticipatory\nbail.\n10. Accordingly, this Criminal Original Petition is dismissed.\nsd/-\n06/08/2024\n/ TRUE COPY /\n/ 08 /2024\nSub-Assistant Registrar (C.S. I / II / III / IV)\nMadurai Bench of Madras High Court,\nMadurai - 625 023.\nLS\nTO\n1 THE INSPECTOR OF POLICE\nSOUTH GATE POLICE STATION,\nMADURAI CITY.\n2. THE ADDITIONAL PUBLIC PROSECUTOR,\nMADURAI BENCH OF MADRAS HIGH COURT, MADURAI.\nORDER\nIN\nCRL OP(MD) No.11854 of 2024\nDate :06/08/2024\n\n4/5\n\nCRL OP(MD). No.11854 of 2024\nPSP/GS/SAR /23.08.2024/ 5P/ 3C\nMadurai Bench of Madras High Court is issuing certified\ncopies in this format from 17/07/2023\n\n5/5	5	t	HCMA010118542026	Criminal Original Petition (Bail)	\N	Single Bench	Sole Accused	The Inspector of Police, South Gate Police Station, Madurai City.	Mr.KPS.Palanivel Rajan,	Mr.R.M.Anbunithi, Additional Public Prosecutor	Civil Law	In this judgment under Civil Law, the Court ruled on the dispute between 1N2Etkbldahw8Veognnljo-X6Oxzmxeeb Home   Judgements And Orders, Supreme Court And High Courts Of India and the Respondent.	0	2026 LexGuard (Judg) CCB013	{"court": "Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)", "title": "Indira v. Inspector of Police, South Gate Police Station, Madurai", "case_id": "ccb013c9-3576-485f-8390-ffa1c23705f2", "case_no": "CRL OP(MD). No.11854 of 2024", "parties": "Indira v. Inspector of Police, South Gate Police Station, Madurai", "category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "petitioner": "Indira", "respondent": "Inspector of Police, South Gate Police Station, Madurai", "legal_domain": "Civil Law", "category_facts": {"category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "surety_count": null, "custody_period": null, "application_type": "Anticipatory Bail", "bail_bond_amount": null, "trial_court_fine": null, "reason_for_relief": null, "reporting_condition": null, "trial_court_sentence": null, "trial_court_fine_paid": false}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "scenario_keywords": [], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
