g\nthis, the appellant claiming as assignee sought to have the decree executed against the respondent.\nBy an order, dated 5th September 1893, his application was dismissed. On the 17th September 1894\nthe appellant succeeded in having his name brought on the record and he then launched the present\napplication for the execution of his decree.\n2. There can be no doubt that, as between the original parties to the two decrees, that is, Easwara\nAiyar and the respondent, this application must necessarily have failed nor would it make any\ndifference that one of the decrees had been assigned to a third party. But it is argued that the\nappellant is not an assignee of the decree within the meaning of Sections 233 and 246 of the Code\ninasmuch as it is the decree of the appellate Court that it is sought to execute, and his assignment\ntook place before the appeal was: instituted. It is said that the sections cited apply only to the case,\nof an assignment of an existing decree and in this case the decree was not in existence when the\nassignment was made. As a matter of fact, it appears that it was the original decree which was\nassigned to the appellant and was afterwards confirmed on appeal. We are asked to treat the case as\nif it were one in which an interest in the subject-matter of the suit had been transferred pending the\nsuit, the case for which Section 372 provides.\n3. We are of opinon, however,that, as the appellant was clearly transfereee of the decree in existence\nwhen the transfer took place, he did not cease to be transferee by reason for the fact that an appeal\nwas presented.\n4. In that appeal Easwara Aiyar remained the party on the record, and it was not shown that the\nrespondent had any notice of the assignment to the plaintiff. The same state of things continued up\nto the date of the order of the 31st January 1891. We should be prepared to hold that, as the\nappellant suffered Easwara Aiyar's name alone to remain on the record and proceedings to be taken\naccordingly, he must be bound by the order passed in consequence. But We also think tbat the\nappellant is an assignee of the decree within the meaning of Section 249.\n5. We must dismiss the appeal with costs.\nIndian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/1601668/ 1	1	t	HCMA010018971897	Judicial Precedent	\N	Single Bench	Ramakrishna Aiyar	Narayana Aiyar	\N	\N	Civil Law	In this judgment under Civil Law, the Court ruled on the dispute between Ramakrishna Aiyar and Narayana Aiyar.	0	1897 LexGuard (Judg) 4EB203	{"court": "High Court of Judicature at Madras", "title": "Ramakrishna Aiyar v. Narayana Aiyar", "case_id": "4eb203d0-0771-4a1e-b531-6f7da6010994", "case_no": "(1897) 7 MLJ 227", "parties": "Petitioner / Appellant v. State / Respondent", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Ramakrishna Aiyar", "respondent": "Narayana Aiyar", "legal_domain": "Civil Law", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Writ Petition / Criminal Appeal", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "scenario_keywords": [], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Dismissed", "disposition": "Dismissed"}}
