 the defacto complainant came out of the office, whereupon the accused persons fled the scene leaving behind the gold ornaments. Hence, the complaint. 3.	[]	Principles governing criminal jurisdiction and interim protections.	[]	Disposed	[]	\N	unverified	Good Law	CRL OP(MD). No.23514 of 2026\nBEFORE THE MADURAI BENCH OF MADRAS HIGH COURT\n( Criminal Jurisdiction )\nDate : 14.10.2026\nPRESENT\nThe Hon`ble Mr.Justice K.MURALI SHANKAR\nCRL OP(MD). No.23514 of 2026\n1.Gubendiran\n2.Ramkumar\n... Petitioners/\nAccused 3 & 4\nVs\nThe State of Tamil Nadu rep by\nThe Inspector of Police,\nDindigul Taluk Police Station,\nDindigul District.\n(Crime No.748 of 2026)\n... Respondent/\nComplainant\nFor Petitioners: Mr.M.Suresh,\nAdvocate\nFor Respondent : Mr.C.Susikumar,\nGovernment Advocate (Crl.Side)\nPETITION FOR BAIL Under Section 483 of B.N.S.S.\nPRAYER :-\nFor Bail in Crime No.748 of 2026 on the file of\nthe Respondent Police.\n1/6\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). No.23514 of 2026\nORDER : The Court made the following order :-\nThe petitioners / accused 3 and 4, who were\narrested and remanded to judicial custody on\n03.10.2026 for the offence punishable under Section\n310(2) BNS, in Crime No.748 of 2026, on the file of\nthe respondent police, seek bail.\n2. The case of the prosecution is that the\ndefacto complainant, who is running a finance\ncompany, and the accused persons were known to each\nother, that the defacto complainant had previously\nlent money to the accused persons whenever they\nsought financial assistance, that on 02.10.2026, at\nabout 07.00 p.m., the first petitioner and the\naccused 1 and 2 entered the finance company, while\nthe second petitioner and the accused 5 and 6 stood\noutside the office. Since the finance company was\nrunning successfully, the accused persons demanded\nmoney from the defacto complainant. When he refused\nto comply with their demand, the first accused\nslapped the defacto complainant and snatched his gold\nornaments and at that time, the second petitioner and\n2/6\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). No.23514 of 2026\nthe sixth accused allegedly stood guard outside. On\nraising an alarm, the defacto complainant came out of\nthe office, whereupon the accused persons fled the\nscene leaving behind the gold ornaments. Hence, the\ncomplaint.\n3. The learned counsel appearing for the\npetitioners would submit that the petitioners are\ninnocent and they have not committed any offence as\nalleged by the prosecution. He would further submit\nthat the sixth accused was granted anticipatory bail\nby the Principal Sessions Court in Crl.M.P.No.2947 of\n2026 on 13.10.2026 and that the petitioners are in\njudicial custody from 03.10.2026.\n4. The learned Government Advocate (Criminal\nSide) appearing for the respondent police would\nsubmit that there are totally six accused and the\npetitioners are arrayed as accused 3 and 4 and that\nthe petitioners are not having any previous cases.\n5. Considering the nature of the charges\n3/6\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). No.23514 of 2026\nlevelled against the petitioners and also the facts\nthat the petitioners are in judicial custody from\n03.10.2026 and that the petitioners are not having\nany bad antecedents, this Court is inclined to grant\nbail to the petitioners subject to the following\nconditions:\n6. Accordingly, the petitioners are ordered to\nbe released on bail on their executing a bond for a\nsum of Rs.25,000/- (Rupees Twenty Five Thousand only)\neach with two sureties each for a like sum to the\nsatisfaction of the learned Judicial Magistrate No.1,\nDindigul, and on further conditions that:\n(i)the sureties shall affix their photographs\nand left thumb impression in the surety bond and the\nMagistrate / concerned court may obtain a copy of\ntheir Aadhar card or Bank Pass Book to ensure their\nidentity;\n(ii)the petitioners shall report before the\nrespondent police daily at 10.30 a.m., for a period\nof one month and thereafter, as and when required for\ninterrogation;\n4/6\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). No.23514 of 2026\n(iii)the petitioners shall not tamper with the\nevidence or witness either during investigation or\ntrial;\n(iv)the petitioners shall not abscond either\nduring investigation or trial;\n(v)On breach of any of the aforesaid conditions,\nthe learned Magistrate / Trial Court is entitled to\ntake appropriate action against the petitioners in\naccordance with law as if the conditions have been\nimposed and the petitioners are released on bail by\nthe learned Magistrate / Trial Court himself as laid\ndown by the Hon'ble Supreme Court in P.K.Shaji vs.\nState of Kerala [(2005)AIR SCW 5560];\n(vi)If the accused thereafter abscond, a fresh\nFIR can be registered under Section 269 BNS, 2023.\n(K M S J)\n14.10.2026\nCSM\nTO\n1. The Judicial Magistrate No.1,\nDindigul.\n2. Do through\nThe Chief Judicial Magistrate,\nDindigul.\n3. The Superintendent,\nDistrict Prison,\nDindigul District.\n5/6\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\nCRL OP(MD). No.23514 of 2026\nK.MURALI SHANKAR,J\nCSM\n4. The Inspector of Police,\nDindigul Taluk Police Station,\nDindigul District.\n5. The Additional Public Prosecutor,\nMadurai Bench of Madras High Court,\nMadurai.\nORDER\nIN\nCRL OP(MD) No.23514 of 2026\nDate : 14.10.2026\n6/6\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis	6	t	HCMD010235142026	Criminal Original Petition (Bail)	\N	Single Bench	Gubendiran & Ramkumar	State of Tamil Nadu Rep. by Inspector of Police, Dindigul Taluk	Mr.M.Suresh,	Mr.C.Susikumar, Government Advocate (Crl.Side)	Civil Law	In this criminal original petition under Criminal Law, the Court ruled on the dispute between . No.23514 of 2026 1.Gubendiran 2.Ramkumar and ..	0	2026 LexGuard (Crim) 8307E7	{"court": "Madras High Court (Madurai Bench)", "title": "Gubendiran & Ramkumar v. State of Tamil Nadu Rep. by Inspector of Police, Dindigul Taluk", "case_id": "8307e75f-0794-4574-94c4-26d71bc795ec", "case_no": "CRL OP(MD). No.23514