,WP(MD) No. 16021 of 2021,WP(MD) No. 16020 of 2021\nWP(MD).No.18247 of 2026\nParvathi ...Petitioner\nVs.\n1. The Director General of Police,\nDr.Radhakrishnan Salai,\nChennai.\n2. The Deputy Inspector General of Police,\nMadurai Range,\nMadurai.\n1/31\n\nW.P.(MD).No.31074 of 2024\n3. The Superintendent of Police,\nMadurai District,\nMadurai ... Respondents\nPRAYER: Writ Petition filed under Article 226 of the Constitution of India\nfor issuance of Writ of Certiorarified Mandamus, calling for the records\nrd\nrelating to the impugned orders passed by the 3 respondent in\nC.No.A1/E-1558210/629/2021 dated 01.04.2022 and quash the same as\nillegal and consequently direct the respondents to promote the petitioner as\nInspector of Police with effect from the date on which the petitioner's juniors\nwere promoted .\nFor Petitioner : Mr.S.Chella Pandian\nFor Respondents : Mr.T.Cibi Chakraborthy\nAdditional Government Pleader\nCOMMON O R D E R\nAll the petitioners are similarly placed and are all claiming the very\nsame relief of issuance of a mandamus, directing the respondents to promote\nthem as Inspectors of Police from the date on which their juniors were\npromoted. The factual narrative and all the affidavits are common, and hence,\nthis order is passed as a common order.\n2/31\n\nW.P.(MD).No.31074 of 2024\n2. It is the case of the petitioners that they were recruited as Women\nPolice Constables Grade-II on 21.04.1981 and were sent for training in three\nbatches on three different dates, and the seniority amongst them was fixed on\nthe basis of the marks that they obtained in the examinations conducted for\nthe said purpose. The seniority vis-à-vis the candidates who were sent for\ntraining in the first batch was determined on the basis of the said marks\namongst the candidates in the said batch. Likewise, in the other two batches,\nthe inter se seniority was determined on the basis of marks that were obtained\nby them in the qualifying examination.\n3. It is the petitioners' case that the seniority in respect of the three\nbatches was maintained separately, and the candidates who were sent up for\ntraining in the first batch were placed above the candidates in the second and\nthe third batches. In 1996, they were promoted as Women Head Constables,\nand thereafter, they were promoted as Sub-Inspectors of Police on or around\n25.10.2004. At that point in time a “C-List” was in vogue, and in the said list,\nthe names of the petitioners were listed and maintained based on the initial\nrank that was assigned to them at the time of their enlistment as Grade\nI/Grade-II police constables. The seniority list which was set out in the “C-\n3/31\n\nW.P.(MD).No.31074 of 2024\nList” was maintained and considered for the promotion to the post of women\nSub Inspector of police.\n4. It is the case of the petitioners that one Yashothai, who was similarly\nplaced to the petitioners, approached this court by filing a Writ Petition in\nW.P.No. 451 of 2008 seeking for issuance of a Writ of Mandamus directing\nthe respondents to pass orders on her representation dated 03.12.2007 for\nrevision of her seniority. This court, vide an order dated 07.01.2008, had\ndirected as follows\n"In view of the said submissions, without expressing anything on merits,\nthe respondent is directed to consider the representation of the petitioner\ndated 03.12.2007 seeking revision of seniority and pass orders thereon on\nmerits and in accordance with law, with a period of eight weeks from the date\nof receipt of copy of this order"\n5. In the interregnum, when the matter stood thus, the first respondent,\nvide memo dated 07.08.1996 had directed that the seniority of Women Police\nConstables recruited in the year 1981 (in the three batches) shall be combined\nand fixed based on the marks obtained by them in the final examination in the\nPolice Recruit School (hereinafter referred to as PRS), irrespective of the date\nof their batch of training. Based on the said memo dated 07.08.1996 and in\npursuance of the orders passed by this court dated 07.01.2008, the Deputy\n4/31\n\nW.P.(MD).No.31074 of 2024\nInspector General of Police, Ramnad Range, had revised the seniority of\nWomen Head Constables vide his proceedings dated 12.06.2009. It is averred\nby the petitioner that due to the said exercise, the candidates who were junior\nto the petitioners have moved up in the seniority list and were placed as\nseniors to the petitioners. As narrated above, the seniority was revisited by\ntaking into account the final rank that was obtained by the police recruits in\nthe PRS and not on the basis of their initial rank in the qualifying exam at the\ntime of appointment.\n6. It is therefore the case of the petitioners that their right to be\nconsidered for promotion was deprived, and ultimately the petitioners retired\nin the following years:\nSl. WP(MD). Name DOE as WSI Declare Date of\nNo. No. Gr.II PCV Date of Range Retirement\n''B'' List\n1. 16015/2021 R.Chandra All are 22.05.2006 Dindigul 31.05.2016\nappointed Range\nin the year\n1981\n2. 16016/2021 M.Farish Begum '' 25.05.2003 Dindigul 31.05.2014\nRange\n3. 16017/2021 S.Thenmozhi '' 25.05.2003 Madurai 31.08.2022\nRange\n5/31\n\nW.P.(MD).No.31074 of 2024\n4. 16018/2021 V.Nirmala Devi '' 25.05.2003 Dindigul 31.05.2019\nRange\n5. 16019/2021 R.Senthamarai '' 23.05.2007 Dindigul 30.06.2022\nRange\n6. 16020/2021 C.Santhalakshmi '' 25.05.2003 Dindigul 31.05.2017\nRange\n7. 16021/2021 G.Jothi '' 25.05.2003 Dindigul 31.12.2013\nRange\n8. 16022/2021 K.Subbuthai '' 18.07.2005 Dindigul 31.03.2018\nRange\n9. 16023/2021 Maheswari '' 25.03.2003 Madurai 30.06.2022\nRange\n10. 16024/2021 R.Susila '' 25.05.2003 Dindigul 31.07.2016\nRange VRS\n11. 11844/2022 R.S.Seethammal '' 18.07.2005 Madurai 31.10.2011\nCity\n12. 12502/2022 S.R.Brindha '' 18.07.2005 Madurai 31.07.2006\nCity VRS\n13. 12503/2022 R.Packiathai '' 18.07.2005 Madurai 30.06.2017\nCity\n14. 12504/2022 A.K.Chandra '' 18.07.2005 Madurai 31.12.2014\nCity\n15. 12505/2022 D.Kalamani '' 18.07.2005 Madurai 31.03.2017\nCity\n16. 12506/2022 G.Vijayalakshmi '' 18.07.2005 Madurai 30.09.2015\nCity\n18. 18247/2023 Parvathi '' 01.06.2004 Dindigul 31.03.2017\nRange VRS\n*To be noted all of them were appointed as GR-I WPC on 21.04.1981\n7. It is the case of the petitioners that they sent separate representations\nto the Director-General of Police and to the first respondent, for the\n6/31\n\nW.P.(MD).No.31074 of 2024\nrestoration of seniority. It is further averred in the affidavit filed by the\npetitioners that one Muthulakshmi and other similarly placed persons filed\nwrit petitions before this court in W.P. Nos. 7122 of 2009 and 7332 of 2009\nseeking a Writ of mandamus to dispose of their representations seeking\nrestoration of seniority. By an order dated 03.08.2009, this court was pleased\nto direct the respondents to consider their representations and pass orders.\nThereafter, the first respondent had, vide proceedings dated 29.10.2009,\nrejected the said request. In the rejection order, the respondents contended\nthat the seniority, insofar as Yashothai is concerned, was based on the order of\nthis court passed in W.P. No. 451 of 2008 dated 07.01.2008. However, it is to\nbe noted that this court had merely directed the disposal of the representation\ndated 03.12.2017, and no directions were issued by this court to assign any\nseniority to said Yashothai over and above the others in the order passed in\nW.P. No. 451 of 2008. Therefore, it is contended by the petitioners that the\nreasons recorded in the order dated 29.10.2009 are erroneous.\n8. It is the case of the petitioners that seniority was maintained all\nalong over the period of the last three decades on the basis of the rank\n7/31\n\nW.P.(MD).No.31074 of 2024\nobtained in the qualifying examination, and such seniority cannot be altered\nwithout affording an opportunity to the petitioners. The petitioners have\nfurther contended that Rule 35A of the Tamil Nadu Police Subordinate\nService Rules, contemplates that seniority cannot be revisited or revised\nexcept when the candidate is demoted to a lower post. It is further averred\nthat Yashothai, whose case was directed to be considered on the basis of her\nrepresentation, had also acquiesced to the fact that she had never challenged\nthe seniority for almost two decades and therefore she was estopped from\nagitating the same. Thus, they submitted that the order passed in favour of\nYashothai vide memo dated 07.08.1996 and the consequent order dated\n12.06.2009 were incorrect, and similar persons are being assigned ranks in\nthe seniority batch by clubbing the three batches and granting seniority on the\nbasis of the marks that they obtained in the PRS. It is further contended by\nthe petitioner that similarly placed persons like the petitioners approached\nthis court in A.Muniyammal and Others v. Inspector General of Police and\nOthers, W.P. No. 10989 of 2010 challenging the revision of seniority and\nseeking consequential directions to promote them to the post of Inspector of\nPolice on par with the juniors who were recruited as constables in the year\n1981. This court allowed the aforesaid writ petition by Order dated\n8/31\n\nW.P.(MD).No.31074 of 2024\n11.11.2014 directing as follows:\n“5. This issue relating to re-fixation of the seniority of the respondents\n4 to 12 and 15 is already dealt with by this Court in W.P. (MD) No.\n1542 of 2010. which is filed by similarly placed Women Sub Inspectors\nof Police one Bharathamatha and three others and decided by myself\natter a detailed discussion, I set aside the impugned order of the third\nrespondeat in Proceedings C.No.A.2/11308/2008/R.O.No.\n139/2009,dated 12.06.2009 and the consequential memorandum of the\nrespondents 1 and 2 mainly on the dated 12.06.2009 and the ground of\ndelay and laches and negligence on the part of the individual\nrespondents therein to raise the objections regarding fixation of\nseniority by holding that the fixation of seniority and the revision of\nseniority is without jurisdiction and directed to restore the original\nseniority and while doing so, this Court held that the petitioner\ntherein, who are also similarly placed Sub-Inspectors of Police are\nentitled to be promoted to the next promotional post of Inspector of\nPolice with effect from the date on which their juniors are given\npromotion with all service and monetary benefits.\n6 As the present writ petition is filed challenging the very Same\nproceedings regarding the refixation of seniority and the same\nmemorandum of communication issued by the respondents 1 and 2, the\npresent writ petition shall also be allowed applying the decision in the\nearlier proceeding and the impugned order stands quashed with\nappropriate direction to the respondents to give promotion to the\npetitioner with retrospective effect.\n7. In the result, the impugned order stands quashed with a direction to\nthe respondents 1 to 3 to give promotion to the petitioners with\nconsequential service and monetary benefits with effect from the date\non which their juniors are promoted.\nIn the result, the writ petition is allowed. No costs. Consequently, the\nconnected miscellaneous petition is closed.”\nAgainst the same, the respondents preferred an appeal in W.A. No. 172\nof 2018 Yasothai v. G. Bharathamatha and others batch, which was also\ndismissed by this court on 17.09.2018. The respondents have thereafter partly\n9/31\n\nW.P.(MD).No.31074 of 2024\nrevised the seniority of Muthulakshmi and others and promoted them as\nInspectors of Police from the date on which their juniors were recruited as\npolice constables in the year 1981. The petitioners were also in the fond hope\nthat the benefit which had been extended to Muthulakshmi and others would\nalso ensure to them, and hence they made a representation on 11.08.2021\nrequesting promotion to the post of Inspector of Police on par with their\njuniors.\n9. It is contended by the petitioners that the order dated 17.09.2018\npassed in W.A. No. 172 of 2018 has become final and is therefore a judgment\nin Rem. Consequently, vide proceedings dated 25.02.2021, the Deputy\nInspector General of Police, Madurai Range, as well as the Deputy Inspector\nGeneral of Police, Ramanathapuram Range, accorded deemed promotion to\nthe petitioners covered in A.Muniyammal (supra) W.P. No. 10989 of 2010,\nseeking parity, the petitioners have filed the present mandamus. They also\nrely upon a judgment of the Honourable Supreme Court in State of\nKarnataka and Others v. N. Parameshwarappa and Others reported in\n(2003) 12 SCC 192 and contend that in decisions of courts in matters of\ncommon interest, the same has to be applied equally among the persons who\n10/31\n\nW.P.(MD).No.31074 of 2024\napproached the court as well as those who have not initiated litigation, and\nthus they seek for issuance of a mandamus.\n10. The learned Additional Government Pleader began his submissions\nby firstly adverting to the counter filed by the third respondent submitted that\nthe petitioners were enlisted as women Grade-I police constables on\n21.04.1981, that they had been sent up for training in three different batches\nin the PRS, Vellore, and that the petitioners were upgraded as women head\nconstables with effect from 14.11.1996 as per G.O.Ms. No. 1476, Home\n(Police V) Department, dated 03.10.1996. Later, vide Chief Office\nMemorandum in Rc.No.178383/NGB.VII(1)/04 dated 29.09.2004, the\npetitioners were also promoted as Women Sub-Inspectors after undergoing\nscreening, with effect from 01.12.2004. It is also the case of the respondents\nthat all the petitioners have since retired on various dates, and the dates of\nretirement tabulated above is also not disputed by the respondents.\n11. The learned Additional Government Pleader referred to Rule 35(f)\nof the General Rules of the Tamil Nadu State and Subordinate Service Rules,\n11/31\n\nW.P.(MD).No.31074 of 2024\nwhich corresponds to Section 40(6) of the present Tamil Nadu Government\nServants (Conditions of Service) Act, 2016, and submitted that an application\nfor revision of seniority of a person in service shall be submitted to the\nappointing authority within a period of three years from the date of\nappointment, and any application received after the said period of three years\nhas to be summarily rejected. It is contended by the respondents that the\npetitioners have approached this court by filing a writ petition almost five to\nseven years after their respective superannuation, and hence the writ petition\nis liable to be dismissed on the grounds of delay as well as laches.\n12. He would rely on the judgment of the Hon’ble Supreme Court in\nState of Uttar Pradesh and Others v. Arvind Kumar Srivastava and Others,\nreported in (2015) 1 SCC 347, wherein it was held that persons who did not\nchallenge the wrongful action within a reasonable time and acquiesced in the\nsame cannot subsequently seek the benefit of a judgment rendered in favour\nof similarly situated persons who had approached the Court earlier, and such\npersons would be treated as fence-sitters. He would also rely on the judgment\nof the Hon’ble Supreme Court in C. Jacob v. Director of Geology and\nMining, reported in (2008) 10 SCC 115, to contend that a stale or dead claim\n12/31\n\nW.P.(MD).No.31074 of 2024\ncannot be revived by making a belated representation and that the\nconsideration and rejection of such a representation would not furnish a fresh\ncause of action. It is therefore contended that the petitioners, having failed to\nchallenge the alleged grievance within the prescribed period and having\napproached this Court only after several years of their respective\nsuperannuation, cannot seek to revive a stale claim by relying upon their\nsubsequent representations.\n13. The respondents further contend in the counter that vide G.O.Ms.\nNo. 2655, Home Department, dated 01.11.1974, the seniority of Women\nPolice Constables was fixed with reference to the marks obtained by them in\nthe final examinations in the training institution. Among the police constables\nwho secured the same marks at the final examination, the senior in age was\nplaced above the junior in age. Vide G.O.Ms. No. 1706 dated 03.07.1986, the\nseniority of women police constables upto the rank of Sub-Inspectors of\nPolice was maintained at the respective range levels. Thereafter, vide\nproceedings of the first respondent dated 07.08.1996, addressed to the\nCommissioner of Police, Madurai City, the seniority of women constables\nrecruited in the year 1981 who underwent training in three different batches\n13/31\n\nW.P.(MD).No.31074 of 2024\nwas fixed on the basis of marks obtained by them in the final examination in\nthe PRS, combining all three batches into one, irrespective of their date of\ntraining.\n14. Accordingly, following the aforesaid instructions, the seniority of\nthe 1981 batch women police constables was maintained in all the\nzones/ranges of Tamil Nadu. However, the same had not been followed in the\nRamanathapuram range; instead, seniority was maintained based on the date\nof enlistment, total marks obtained in the PRS, and the date of passing out\nfrom the PRS. It is in the above factual background that Tmt. Yashothai, a\nhead constable working in Virudhunagar district who belonged to the 1981\nbatch and was serving in the Ramanathapuram range, filed a Writ Petition in\nW.P. No. 451 of 2008 seeking for a mandamus to direct the respondents to\nconsider and pass orders on her representation dated 03.12.2007 for the\nrevision of her seniority. This court, by order dated 07.01.2008, directed\nconsideration of her representation. Thereafter, the Deputy Inspector General\nof Police, Ramanathapuram Range, sent a letter to the first respondent\nseeking clarification for revision, and the first respondent, in his\n14/31\n\nW.P.(MD).No.31074 of 2024\nmemorandum dated 11.05.2009, directed that the seniorities of women police\nconstables have to be fixed based on the marks obtained by them in the police\nrecruitment school, following G.O.Ms. No. 1706, Home dated 03.07.1986. In\npursuance of the aforesaid clarifications issued by the first respondent, the\nrepresentation of Yashothai dated 03.12.2007 was considered, and the\nseniority of 24 women Grade-I constables of the Ramanathapuram range was\nrevised by the Deputy Inspector General of Police, Ramanathapuram Range,\nbased on the marks obtained by them in the PRS. Accordingly, because\nYashothai obtained 324 marks in the PRS, she secured the first place in the\nseniority list in the Ramanathapuram range. Consequent to the revision of\nseniority, the dates of promotion of the said 24 women police personnel as\nhead constables were revised on par with their juniors, and thereafter their\ndates of promotion as Sub-Inspectors of Police were fixed as 21.02.1999.\n15. It is contended in the counter that the revision of seniority was\ndone only in the Ramanathapuram range because seniority had not been\nmaintained as per G.O.Ms. No. 2655 dated 01.11.1974, prompting the first\nrespondent to direct the Deputy Inspector General, Ramanathapuram Range,\nto follow the said G.O.Ms. No. 2655 dated 01.11.1974 and the subsequent\n15/31\n\nW.P.(MD).No.31074 of 2024\nG.O.Ms. No. 1706 dated 03.07.1986. He further contended, by referring to\nparagraph 9 of the counter, that insofar as the petitioners are concerned, they\nwere all serving in Madurai city, Madurai Range and Dindigul Range and\ntheir seniority was all through maintained as per G.O.Ms. No. 2655 dated\n01.11.1974 and based on the marks obtained in the PRS. Hence, the question\nof refixing or revisiting their seniority on par with their junior, namely\nYashothai, does not arise at all.\n16. It is further contended that challenging the revision of seniority of\nTmt. Yashothai and 11 others who were promoted with effect from\n21.02.1999, the other aggrieved women officers who, due to the revision,\ncame to the bottom of the seniority list and hence filed writ petitions before\nthis court seeking to set aside the revision of seniority as directed by the\nDeputy Inspector General, Ramanathapuram Range, on 12.06.2009.\nS.No. Name W.P.No Marks obtained in\nPRS Vellore\n1. Tmt.K.Muthulakshmi 7122/2009 261 ¼\n2. Tmt.V.Nagarathinam 7123/2009 258 ½\n3. Tmt.R.Vasantharani 7124/2009 255\n4. Tmt.G.Gnanasoundari 7125/2009 254 ½\n5. Tmt.G.Bharathamatha 7332/2009 258 ½\n16/31\n\nW.P.(MD).No.31074 of 2024\n17. The writ petitioners therein had obtained lower marks when\ncompared to Yashothai and others, and therefore their representations had\nbeen rejected.\n18. Aggrieved by the rejection of representations dated 29.10.2009, Tmt.\nG.Bharathamatha and three others filed W.P. No. 1542 of 2010 seeking for\nissuance of writ of Certiorari and Mandamus to quash the order dated\n12.06.2009 and the consequential memorandum dated 29.10.2009, and to\ndirect the respondents therein to promote the petitioners as Inspectors of\nPolice. It is submitted that the revision of seniority was done only in the\nRamanathapuram range where it was not maintained as per the earlier\ngovernment orders for the 1981 batch police constables.\n19. It is further averred in paragraph 13 of the counter that thereafter\nYashothai, whose seniority was revised, was promoted as a Woman Inspector\nof Police with effect from 01.10.2004 in Virudhunagar district, which comes\nunder the Madurai range. Thereafter, Muniyammal and 20 other women subinspectors filed in A.Muniyammal, W.P. No. 10989 of 2010. The relevant\n17/31\n\nW.P.(MD).No.31074 of 2024\nparagraph is extracted hereunder:\n“ 14. It is respectfully submitted that the Hon'ble Madurai Bench of\nMandras High Court in its order in WP(MD).No.1542 of 2010 dated\n25.02.2014 has made the following findings the revision of the\nseniority has been done only in accordance with the direction of the\nDirector General of Police on the strength of G.O.Ms.No. 1706 dated\n03.07.2006. As rightly pointed out by the learned counsel for the\npetitioners, G.O.Ms.No.1706 cannot be given effect to retrospectively for\nthe seniority fixed during 1981 and the same shall be applied\nprospectively for future course of seniority and the revision of seniority on\nthe basis of the G.O. and on the basis of the instructions issued by DGP as\nper G.O. is arbitrary, without jurisdiction and legally unsustainable.\nViewing from any angle, the impugned order passed in 2009 thereby\nrevising seniority fixed in 1981 is contrary to law and in contravention of\nthe legal principles and in violation of the principles of natural justice and\nsuffers on account of delay and laches and negligence on the part of the\nrespondents to raise the objections regarding the fixation of seniority and\nthe revision of seniority is without jurisdiction and liable to be quashed\nand stands quashed.\nAt this juncture, the learned counsel for the petitioners would bring it to\nthe notice of this Court that out four petitioners, petitioners 1 & 2 are in\nservice and the petitioners 3 & 4 retired on attaining the age of\nsuperannuation. In view of that the petitioners 1 & 2 are entitled to be\npromoted and the petitioners 3 & 4 are entitled to get notional promotion\nin the manner are indicated above.\nIn the result, the impugned orders stand quashed with direction issued to\nthe respondents 1 to 3 to give promotion to the petitioners 1 & 2 and give\nnotional promotion to the petitioners 3 & 4 with all monetary and service\nbenefits with effect from the date on which their juniors are promoted.”\n20. This court, vide order dated 11.11.2014, by quoting the earlier orders\npassed in W.P. No. 1542 of 2010 (G.Bharathamatha and 3 others), allowed\nthe prayer of the writ petitioners, quashed the impugned Order and issued\ndirections to the respondents to promote the petitioners with consequential\n18/31\n\nW.P.(MD).No.31074 of 2024\nservice and monetary benefits. It is further averred that writ appeals were\npreferred in W.A. No. 172 of 2018 by Yashothai and W.A. No. 640 of 2015 by\nthe respondent department against the order dated 25.02.2014 in W.P. No.\n1542 of 2010. Ultimately, this court, vide order dated 17.09.2018 in W.A. No.\n172 of 2018 (and connected matters), dismissed the writ appeals following\nthe earlier orders. The operative part of the order is extracted for ease of\nreference as follows:\n“It is respectfully submitted that in the above said 03 Writ Appeals, the\nHon'ble Madurai Bench of Madras High Court, Madurai has issued a common\norder dated 17.09.2018 and the same is as follows:-\n5. The learned Counsel appearing for the appellants would submit that the\nseniority list was drawn wrongly both on fact and on law. Notwithstanding the\nmarks it was drawn, this Court is unable to accept the said contention. In service\nprudence, the issue qua delay and latches will have to be viewed more seriously.\nThe first seniority list was drawn way back in the year 1981. A representation\nwas made in the year 2009. A mere representation made, notwithstanding the\nnumerous promotions made in the interregnum, cannot and shall not be allowed\nto circumvent delay and latches. It appears that the representations itself has\nbeen given after 10 years. There was no challenge to the subsequent promotion\nas Grade 1, Head Constable, Sub-Inspector of Police and Inspector of Police.\nThe impugned order was also passed without hearing the writ petitioners, whe\nacquired vested rights. Even otherwise promotions made subsequently are not\nchallenged and thus cannot be set aside. Therefore, viewing from any\nperspective, this court does not find any merit in the writ appeals.\n6. In the result, the writ appeals in WA(MD).No.650 of 2015 and 127 of 2017\nare dismissed . In view of the order passed in these writ appeals, W.A.(MD).No.\n172 of 2018 is disposed of . No costs. Consequently, connected miscellaneous\npetitions are closed.”\n21. It is in light of the above fact and to give effect to the order passed\n19/31\n\nW.P.(MD).No.31074 of 2024\nin W.P. No. 1542 of 2010 dated 25.02.2014 and the order passed in the writ\nappeal, the promotions of G.Bharathamatha and 3 others were accorded. It is\nalso averred in the counter that as against the order dated 17.09.2018, in W.A.\nNo.172 of 2018 review applications along with condonation of delay petitions\n(C.M.P. No. 2970 of 2020 in Review Application SR No. 16601 of 2020)\nwere filed, but the C.M.P.s seeking condonation of delay were dismissed by\nthis court. Thus, the orders passed in the writ appeals became final, and\naccordingly, the petitioners therein, namely G. Bharathamatha and 3 others,\nand Muniyammal and 20 others were granted deemed promotion as temporary\ninspectors of police with effect from 01.10.2004.\n22. It is therefore contended that the petitioners cannot seek parity in\nrespect of the orders obtained by G. Bharathamatha and 3 others and\nMuniyammal and 20 others, which arose from writ petitions filed by them\nway back in the year 2010. The petitioners have been fence-sitters and did not\ninitiate any action for the last 12 years, and have filed the present writ petition\nonly after their respective retirements. The seniority position which has been\nsettled cannot be unsettled at this point in time. Moreover, the seniority\npertains to the recruitment of women constables in the year 1981, and 45 long\n20/31\n\nW.P.(MD).No.31074 of 2024\nyears have now passed; to unsettle the position of seniority would be\nincorrect and contrary to the law laid down by the Supreme Court.\n23. The learned Additional Government Pleader also referred to a\ncommon order of the Hon’ble Division Bench of this Court in Yasothai v.\nG.Bharathamatha and 15 Others, W.A.(MD) No.172 of 2018, W.A. (MD)\nNo. 127 of 2017 and, W.A. (MD) No. 650 of 2015 dated 17.09.2018 to\ncontend that unless petitioners approach the authorities within a reasonable\ntime or as stipulated in the statute (in this case, after the coming into force of\nthe Tamil Nadu Government Servants (Conditions of Service) Act, 2016,\nrequiring applications for revision of seniority to be made within three years\nof the date of appointment), such claims cannot be entertained. The relevant\nportions of the judgment are extracted as follows:\n“5. The learned Counsel appearing for the appellants would submit that\nseniority list was drawn wrongly both on fact and on law. Notwithstanding\nthe marks it was drawn, this court is unable to accept the said contention.\nIn service prudence, the issue qua delay and latches will have to be viewed\nmore seriously. The first seniority list was drawn way back in the year\n1981. A representation was made in the year 2009. A mere representation\nmade notwithstanding the numerous promotions made in the interregnum\ncannot and shall not be allowed to circumvent delay and latches. It\nappears that the representations itself has been given after 10 years. There\nwas no challenge to the subsequent promotion as Grade 1, Head\nConstable, Sub-Inspector of Police and Inspector of Police. The impugned\norder was also passed without hearing the writ petitioners, who acquired\nvested rights. Even otherwise promotions made subsequently are not\nchallenged and thus cannot be set aside. Therefore, viewing from any\n21/31\n\nW.P.(MD).No.31074 of 2024\nperspective, this court does not find any merit in the writ appeals.\n6. In the result, the writ appeals in W.A. (MD). No.650 of 2015 and 127 of\n217 are dismissed. In view of the order passed in these writ appeals, W.A.\n(MD). No. 172 of 2018 is disposed of. No costs. Consequently, connected\nmiscellaneous petitions are closed."\n24. The revision of seniority of Yashothai was because of the mistake\nthat was being carried forward in the Ramanathapuram range, while in other\nranges, including Madurai City, seniority was always maintained as per the\nrank obtained by candidates in the final examination at the PRS. Thus, he\nwould contend that the writ petitions filed at this distant point in time cannot\nbe sustained and seek dismissal of the writ petitions.\n25.Upon hearing counsels on either side and having perused all the\nrecords, including the judgements that have been cited by the counsels, the\nfollowing facts emerge.\n26. The petitioners before this court are all mostly from the Dindigul /\nMadurai range, and some of them are also from the Madurai City Police. They\nall seek parity in respect of the promotion granted to one Yashothai, who was\nin the Ramanathapuram range. It is the admitted case of both sides that the\nseniority in respect of Grade-I / Grade-II constables up to the rank of sub-\n22/31\n\nW.P.(MD).No.31074 of 2024\ninspectors was maintained range-wise.\n27. In the present case, Yashothai complained that her seniority had\nbeen overlooked, as she claimed that she had obtained 324 marks in the PRS\nand therefore ought to have been placed on top, whereas the seniority list\ndrawn vide order dated 03.12.1996 by the Ramanathapuram range had put her\nat the 16th position even though she stood first in merit compared to others\nafter undergoing training at the PRS. She preferred a representation and\nthereafter approached this court, and this court disposed of her writ petition in\nW.P. No. 451 of 2008 vide order dated 07.01.2008, to dispose of her\nrepresentation. Her representation pointed out that in all ranges across the\nState of Tamil Nadu, seniority was being maintained on the basis of marks\nobtained in the PRS as covered by government orders and office orders of the\nDGP. However, insofar as Ramanathapuram was concerned it was an\nexception and seniority was not fixed on the said basis.\n28. This court vide the order dated 07.01.2008 directed that\nYashothai's representation be disposed of, and thereafter the Director-General\nof Police (the first respondent herein) passed an order dated 11.05.2009\n23/31\n\nW.P.(MD).No.31074 of 2024\nrevising the seniority of Yashothai and consequently all others in the\nRamanathapuram range on the basis of total marks obtained in the PRS.\nAccordingly, Yashothai was notionally promoted as a Grade-II police\nconstable and thereafter as a woman sub-inspector with effect from\n21.02.1999.\n29. The order dated 07.01.2008 passed in W.P.(MD) No. 10989 of 2010\nfiled by A.Muniyammal. In the said writ petition, Yashothai was not made a\nparty. Therefore, Yashothai alleging that the Order was passed behind her\nback filed W.A. (MD). No. 172 of 2018, which ultimately came to be\ndismissed vide the Order of the Hon'ble Division Bench dated 17.09.2018.\nThe basis of the judgment in W.A.(MD) No. 172 of 2018 and related cases\nwas that the persons whose seniority was affected were neither heard nor\nmade parties to the writ petition. The court observed that Yashothai was guilty\nof laches, and in matters of seniority, laches must be viewed seriously as\norders of such nature unsettle settled issues. It was observed that the the\npromotion made subsequently are not challenged and thus cannot be set aside.\nTherefore, viewing from any perspective, the court did not find any merit\n24/31\n\nW.P.(MD).No.31074 of 2024\nwith the writ appeals. Therefore the writ petition was disposed of in view of\norders passed in W.A.(MD). No. 650 of 2015 and 127 of 2017.\n30. The relief granted to G.Bharathamatha and others was implemented\nvide order of the first respondent dated 25.02.2021 by citing the Order passed\nin W.A. No. 172 of 2018. A batch of writ petitions was also filed by me A.\nMuniyammal and 20 others in W.P. No. 10989 of 2010, and by order dated\n11.11.2014, a single judge of this court allowed the writ petition based on the\nearlier order passed in favour of G.Bharathamatha and others in W.P. No.\n1542 of 2010, which was confirmed in W.A. No. 172 of 2018.\n31. Since the orders passed in favour of G.Bharathamatha and\nMuniyammal were implemented, the petitioners have now come forward\nclaiming parity. However, it must be noted that the petitioners herein are\neither from the Dindigul range, Madurai range, or Madurai City Police, and in\nall these three ranges, seniority was always maintained on the basis of ranks\nobtained in the PRS. This was unlike Ramanathapuram Range, where\nseniority was based on the rank obtained in the selection list and the date of\ntraining (as the 1981 batch women constables were sent in three batches for\n25/31\n\nW.P.(MD).No.31074 of 2024\ntraining, and inter se seniority was based on selection rank). Only in\nRamanathapuram was seniority maintained as per selection rank, whereas in\nall other ranges, seniority positions were maintained on the basis of marks\nobtained in the PRS.\n32. The confusion arose because of the order passed by the first\nrespondent in ROC No. 139 dated 12.06.2009, by virtue of which Yashothai\nwas placed at serial number one while others above her were pushed down.\nThough passed pursuant to a direction to consider her representation, those\naffected by the order (G.Bharathamatha and 3 others) challenged the same,\nleading to the setting aside of the seniority list. By that time, Yashothai had\nalready been promoted, and her promotion was not disturbed. The order in\nfavour of G.Bharathamatha and 3 others was challenged by Yashothai and\nothers in W.A. No. 172 of 2018, which was dismissed, concluding that\nYashothai's alteration of seniority after 18 years though based on the training\nmarks, was barred by laches and delay, and hence seniority was restored.\nConsequently, G.Bharathamatha and three others were promoted as Inspectors\nof Police on par with Yashothai.\n26/31\n\nW.P.(MD).No.31074 of 2024\n33. The petitioners now claim the seniority and promotion on par with\nG.Bharathamatha and Muniyammal. However, those cases arose because\nYashothai (originally a junior) was promoted, and in order to compensate the\nseniors in her list, they were granted promotions. That cannot be the basis for\nseeking promotion in the present case, because in the petitioners' case, the\nseniority list was never disturbed and has all through been maintained on par\nas per their respective marks obtained in the PRS.\n34. Furthermore, at this late stage after having retired from service, the\npetitioners have preferred the writ petition in the year 2021. Most of the\npetitioners retired between 2014 and 2019, barring a few who retired a little\nlater. Thus, the petitioners are seeking to redraw seniority drawn as early as\n1981 based solely on training marks. In this regard, the Hon’ble Supreme\nCourt in State of Uttar Pradesh and Others v. Arvind Kumar Srivastava and\nOthers (Supra), has held that delay, laches and acquiescence constitute wellrecognised exceptions to the general principle of extending the benefit of a\njudgment to similarly situated persons. The relevant portion of the judgment\nis extracted herein:\n"22.2. However, this principle is subject to well-recognised exceptions in the\nform of laches and delays as well as acquiescence. Those persons who did\n27/31\n\nW.P.(MD).No.31074 of 2024\nnot challenge the wrongful action in their cases and acquiesced into the same\nand woke up after long delay only because of the reason that their\ncounterparts who had approached the court earlier in time succeeded in their\nefforts, then such employees cannot claim that the benefit of the judgment\nrendered in the case of similarly situated persons be extended to them. They\nwould be treated as fence-sitters and laches and delays, and/or the\nacquiescence, would be a valid ground to dismiss their claim."\nFurther, in C. Jacob (Supra), the Hon’ble Supreme Court has held that a\nstale or dead claim cannot be revived by a subsequent representation or by an\norder passed thereon. The relevant portion of the judgment is extracted as\nfollows:\n“10. Every representation to the government for relief, may not be replied on merits.\nRepresentations relating to matters which have become stale or barred by\nlimitation, can be rejected on that ground alone, without examining the merits of\nthe claim. In regard to representations unrelated to the department, the reply may\nbe only to inform that the matter did not concern the department or to inform the\nappropriate department. Representations with incomplete particulars may be\nreplied by seeking relevant particulars. The replies to such representations, cannot\nfurnish a fresh cause of action or revive a stale or dead claim.\n11. When a direction is issued by a court/tribunal to consider or deal with the\nrepresentation, usually the directee (person directed) examines the matter on\nmerits, being under the impression that failure to do may amount to disobedience.\nWhen an order is passed considering and rejecting the claim or representation, in\ncompliance with direction of the court or tribunal, such an order does not revive the\nstale claim, nor amount to some kind of `acknowledgment of a jural relationship' to\ngive rise to a fresh cause of action.\n..14. We are constrained to refer to the several facets of the issue only to emphasize\nthe need for circumspection and care in issuing directions for `consideration'. If the\nrepresentation is on the face of it is stale, or does not contain particulars to show\n28/31\n\nW.P.(MD).No.31074 of 2024\nthat it is regarding a live claim, courts should desist from directing `consideration'\nof such claims.”\nRespectfully following the aforesaid decisions on the proposition of delay\nand laches, this Court is of the view that the petitioners, having approached\nthis Court after such a long lapse of time and, in all cases, after their\nretirement, cannot seek to reopen the seniority position which had remained\nsettled from as early as 1981\n35. I see no reason to set aside the seniority or direct the grant of\nnotional or actual promotion to the petitioners, as they have been fence sitters\nand have not satisfactorily explained as to why they have approached this\ncourt after almost 45 long years since seniority was fixed originally in 1981\nand thereafter when promotion was made to the grade of Women Police\nConstable, Grade I in 1996. Moreover, while seniority in the\nRamanathapuram range was erroneously maintained, seniority in the\nMadurai, Dindigul, and Madurai City ranges was rightly drawn as per the\napplicable government orders namely G.O.Ms. No. 2655 dated 01.11.1974,\nG.O.Ms. No. 1706 dated 03.07.1986 and G.O.Ms. No. 1476 Home Police V\nDepartment, dated 03.10.1996.\n29/31\n\nW.P.(MD).No.31074 of 2024\n36. In fine, the writ petitions are dismissed both on the ground of laches\nas well as on the merits of the contention. No cost.\n30.09.2026\nNCC :yes/No\nIndex :yes/No\nInternet:yes/No\nmsrm\nNote: Registry is directed to type the full cause title while issuing the\norder copy.\nTo\n1. The Director General of Police,\nDr.Radhakrishnan Salai,\nChennai.\n2. The Deputy Inspector General of Police,\nMadurai Range,\nMadurai.\n3. The Superintendent of Police,\nMadurai District,\nMadurai\n30/31\n\nW.P.(MD).No.31074 of 2024\nRAJNISH PATHIYIL, J.\nmsrm\nW.P.(MD).No.18247 of 2023 batch cases\n30.09.2026\n31/31	31	t	HCMD010829992023	Writ Petition	2026-09-30	Single Bench	Parvathi ...Petitioner	Director General of Police & Ors.	Mr.S.Chella Pandian	Mr.T.Cibi Chakraborthy Additional Government Pleader	Constitutional Law	In this judgment under Civil Law, the Court ruled on the dispute between 1Ghg2Dhwwk7Uiq5Ucw0Xc Fg4Gmed5Ryd Home   Judgements And Orders, Supreme Court And High Courts Of India and the Respondent.	0	2026 LexGuard (Judg) 9F0D6E	{"court": "Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)", "title": "Parvathi v. Director General of Police & Ors.", "case_id": "9f0d6ee8-74fb-4ff6-a434-22a677b68d03", "case_no": "WP(MD) NO. 18247 of 2023", "parties": "Parvathi v. Director General of Police & Ors.", "category": "QUASHING", "petitioner": "Parvathi", "respondent": "Director General of Polic