rity)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2020) 10 SCC 616", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	https://mhc.tn.gov.in/judis	verified	Good Law	CRL OP(MD). No.12325 of 2026\n\n( Criminal Jurisdiction )\nDate : 24.07.2026\nPRESENT\nTHE HONOURABLE MR.JUSTICE K.RAJASEKAR\nCRL OP(MD)No.12325 of 2026\n1.Dakshinamoorthy\n2.Sivamoorthy ... Petitioners / Accused\nVersus\nThe State of Tamil Nadu,\nRep by the Inspector of Police,\nNIB-CID Police Station,\nNagapattinam District.\n(Crime No.4 of 2024) ... Respondent / Complainant\nPRAYER :-Criminal Original Petition filed under Section 483 of the\nBNSS, 2023, to enlarge the petitioners on bail in Crime No.4 of 2024 on\nthe file of the respondent police.\nFor Petitioners : Mr.T.Lajapathy Roy,\nSenior Counsel\nfor Mr.M.Dhinakar\nAdvocate\nFor Respondent : Mr.T.Lenin Kumar,\nCounsel for State of TN\n(Crl. Side)\n1/6\n\nCRL OP(MD). No.12325 of 2026\nORDER : The Court made the following order :-\nThe petitioners, who were arrested and remanded to judicial\ncustody on 04.07.2024 for the offences punishable under Sections 8(c)\nand 20(b)(ii)(C), 29(1) and 25 of the Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic\nSubstances Act, 1985, in Crime No.4 of 2024 on the file of the\nrespondent police, seek enlargement on bail.\n2. The case of the prosecution is that acting on credible\ninformation, the respondent Police kept surveillance on the\nThirukkuvalai–Mela Pidagai–Thirupoondi Road at about 08.50 hours on\n04.07.2024. During the surveillance, a Tata Sumo vehicle bearing\nRegistration No.CG-04-MZ-7706, followed by a Mercedes-Benz car\nbearing Registration No. TN-09-BA-0033, was intercepted. It was found\nthat the petitioners were travelling in the Tata Sumo vehicle. After\ncomplying with the mandatory provisions of the Narcotic Drugs and\nPsychotropic Substances Act, 1985, the respondent Police conducted a\nsearch and recovered 80 kilograms of ganja from the possession of the\npetitioners. Simultaneously, further contraband was recovered from the\nInnova car occupied by the other accused. Thereafter, after following the\n2/6\n\nCRL OP(MD). No.12325 of 2026\nmandatory procedure, the petitioners were arrested, remanded to judicial\ncustody, and have remained in custody ever since. Hence, the case.\n3. The learned Senior Counsel appearing for the petitioners\nsubmitted that the petitioners have completed 180 days of incarceration\nand that the final report was not filed within the statutory period. Though\nthe respondent subsequently filed an application seeking extension of\ntime to complete the investigation and the Trial Court granted such\nextension, the petitioners were never informed of their indefeasible right\nto seek statutory/default bail upon expiry of the prescribed period. It was\nfurther submitted that, even thereafter, the investigation was not\ncompleted and the respondent obtained two further extensions of time.\nPlacing reliance on the decision of the Hon'ble Supreme Court in\nBikramjit Singh v. State of Punjab, reported in (2020) 10 SCC 616, the\nlearned Senior Counsel contended that the failure to inform the\npetitioners of their right to seek default bail has vitiated the proceedings\nand, therefore, the petitioners are entitled to be released on statutory bail.\n4. Per contra, the learned Counsel for State (Criminal Side)\n3/6\n\nCRL OP(MD). No.12325 of 2026\nreiterated the prosecution case and submitted that the petitioners never\nexercised their right to seek default bail after the expiry of the statutory\nperiod. It was contended that, without having filed an application seeking\ndefault bail, the petitioners cannot now contend that they were deprived\nof such right on the ground that they were not informed of the same. The\nlearned Counsel for State further submitted that the Trial Court had, on\ntwo occasions, extended the period for completion of the investigation\nafter hearing the petitioners and after receiving their written objections.\nTherefore, it cannot be said that the petitioners were denied an\nopportunity to assert their right.\n5. This Court heard both sides and perused the materials available\non record.\n6. It is well settled that the right to statutory/default bail accrues on\nthe expiry of the prescribed period of investigation, provided the accused\navails of such right by filing an application before the filing of the final\nreport. In the present case, admittedly, no application seeking default bail\nwas filed by the petitioners after the expiry of the statutory period.\n4/6\n\nCRL OP(MD). No.12325 of 2026\nThough it is contended that the petitioners were not informed of their\nright to seek default bail, the records disclose that the petitioners were\nheard on every occasion when the respondent sought extension of time\nfor completing the investigation and they had also filed written\nobjections opposing such extensions. In such circumstances, the\ncontention that the petitioners were unaware of their right to seek default\nbail or were prevented from exercising such right cannot be accepted.\nAccordingly, this Court finds no merit in the present petition. The\nCriminal Original Petition is dismissed.\n24.07.2026\npal\nTo\n1.The Inspector of Police,\nNIB-CID Police Station,\nNagapattinam District.\n2.The Additional Public Prosecutor,\nMadurai Bench of Madras High Court,\nMadurai.\n5/6\n\nCRL OP(MD). No.12325 of 2026\nK.RAJASEKAR ,J .\npal\nORDER\nIN\nCRL OP(MD) No.12325 of 2026\nDate : 24.07.2026\n6/6	6	t	HCMA010123252026	Criminal Original Petition (Bail)	\N	Single Bench	Accused	State of Tamil Nadu Rep. by Inspector of Police, NIB-CID	Mr.T.Lajapathy Roy,	Mr.T.Lenin Kumar, Counsel for State of TN	Civil Law	In this judgment under Civil Law, the Court ruled on the dispute between 14Koolo5Lvwr98Wlvh Puac3Vo4Y4Nop9 Home   Judgements And Orders, Supreme Court And High Courts Of India and the Respondent.	0	2026 LexGuard (Judg) B7BD5C	{"court": "Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)", "title": "Dakshinamoorthy & Sivamoorthy v. State o