 India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2015) 1 SCC 347]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2015) 1 SCC 347", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	https://mhc.tn.gov.in/judis	verified	Good Law	WP(MD) NO. 2535 of 2017\n\nReserved on 28.08.2026\nPronounced on 16.10.2026\nCORAM:\nTHE HONOURABLE MR.JUSTICE RAJNISH PATHIYIL\nWP(MD) Nos. 2535 OF 2017\nAND\nWMP(MD) NO. 2107 OF 2017\n1. R. Seenivsan\n2. M.R.Ganesh Babu\n3. S.K. Sree Kumar\n4. G.Jeyaram\n5. K.S.Kubendran Petitioner(s)\nVs\n1. The Secretary,\nState Of Tamilnadu,\nDepartment Of School Education,\nFort.St.George,\nChennai-600 009.\n2. The Director Of School Education,\nCollege Road,\nChennai-600 006.\n3. The Chief Educational Officer\nTuticorin-628 002.\n1/22\n\nWP(MD) NO. 2535 of 2017\n4. The District Educational Officer,\nKovilpatti-628 501\nTuticorin District.\n5. The Correspondent,\nSt.Joseph Higher Secondary School,\nKovilpatti-628 501,\nTuticorin District. Respondent(s)\nPrayer : Writ petiiton is filed under Article 226 of the Constitution of India,\nto issue a Writ of Certiorarified Mandamus calling for the records relating to\nquash the impugned order issued by the 2nd respondent Director of School\nEducation in O.Mu.No.94986/D2/E2/2014 dated 02.01.2015 and the\nconsequential proceedings of the 4th respondent District Educational Officer\nin O.Mu.No.6326/Aa1/2016 dated 25.11.2016, quash the same, and further\nDirect the respondents to approve the appointment of the petitioners as\nMiddle Grade Graduate Teachers either w.e.f. 14.06.2002 in regular scale of\npay as per G.O.Ms.No.79 (Secondary Education – U1) dated 14.06.2002 with\nsalary and all other consequential benefits.\nFor Petitioner(s):\nMr.K.K.Ragatheesh Kumar\nM/s.Isaac Chambers\nFor Respondent(s):\nMr.P. Pethu Rajesh,\nAdditional Government Pleader for Counter for R1 to R4\nNo appearance – R5\n2/22\n\nWP(MD) NO. 2535 of 2017\nORDER\nThis writ petition has been filed seeking for the issuance of a writ\nof certiorarified mandamus to quash the impugned order issued by the second\nrespondent dated 02.01.2015 and the consequential proceedings dated\n25.11.2016 and to direct the respondents to approve the appointment of the\npetitioner as middle grade graduate teachers with effect from 14.06.2002 in\nterms of G.O. Ms. No. 79 dated 14.06.2002.\n2. The facts that have been narrated in the affidavit filed in\nsupport of the writ petition are that the petitioners are working as B.T.\nAssistants in the fifth respondent school at Madurai. The fifth respondent\nschool is a minority educational institution. They were initially appointed as\nsecondary grade teachers against sanctioned vacancies. The fifth respondent\nschool being a minority educational institution, no prior permission of the\ndepartment is required to fill up the sanctioned vacancies. The details of each\nof the petitioners are tabulated hereunder:\n3/22\n\nWP(MD) NO. 2535 of 2017\nS. Date of Initial\nName Qualification Post Details of Vacancy\nNo. Appointment\nBT Due to the promotion\n1. R. Seenivasan B.Sc., B.Ed., Assistant 04.06.1998 of K.\n(Maths) Nagasubramanian\nBT\nM.R. Ganesh Due to the retirement\n2. B.Sc., B.Ed., Assistant 04.06.1998\nBabu of T.K. Kuppusamy\n(Maths)\nBT\nS.K. Sree Due to the promotion\n3. B.Sc., B.Ed., Assistant 25.06.1998\nKumar of K.K. Jenarthanan\n(Maths)\nBT\nM.Sc., M.Ed., Due to the promotion\n4. G. Jeyaram Assistant 01.07.1998\nM.Phil of G.J. Raman\n(Science)\nBT\nDue to the retirement\n5. K.S. Kubendran B.Sc., B.Ed., Assistant 03.06.1999\nof R. Amal Raj\n(Maths)\n3. It is the contention of the petitioners that the appointments\nwere all made against the vacancies of secondary grade teachers in the middle\nschool section with graduate qualifications, i.e., B.Ed. Thereafter, a proposal\nwas submitted for approval of the appointments and for disbursement of\ngrant-in-aid towards the salary. The District Educational Officer, who is the\n4th respondent, had declined to approve the appointments in view of the order\nissued by the Government in G.O.Ms.No.559 dated 11.07.1995 precluding\napproval to the appointments of graduate teachers in secondary grade posts.\n4/22\n\nWP(MD) NO. 2535 of 2017\n4. The said G.O. Ms. No. 559 dated 11.07.1995 was put to\nchallenge by several schools, in a batch of Writ Petition Nos. 6988 of 1998\netc., this Court, by order dated 19.05.1998, dismissed the writ petitions,\nagainst which appeals were filed in W.A.Nos. 991 to 998 of 1998. The\nHon'ble Division Bench, by Order dated 29.06.2001, (Secretary\nCorrespondent Uswathun Hasana Oriental (Arabic) Girls Higher Secondary\nSchool v. State of Tamil Nadu, 2002 Writ LR 173), directed the State\nGovernment to evolve a scheme and approve the Graduate Teachers\nappointed in Secondary Grade posts up to 19.05.1998 i.e. the date on which\nthe learned Single Judge had passed the order.\n5. Accordingly, and in compliance with the directions of the\nHon’ble Division Bench, the State Government evolved a scheme and issued\nG.O. Ms. No. 155, School Education (D2) Department, dated 03.10.2002,\ndirecting to approve the appointments of Graduate Teachers with qualification\nfor B.T. / Tamil Pandit in secondary grade posts up to 19.05.1998. It was\nfurther directed in the said G.O. that the Graduate teachers appointed between\n11.07.1995 and 19.05.1998 should undergo a month’s training in Child\nPsychology, and directed the educational authorities to approve the\n5/22\n\nWP(MD) NO. 2535 of 2017\nappointments after completion of the said training vide G.O.Ms.No.155 dated\n03.10.2002. The G.O. further directed the recovery of salaries paid to those\nteachers who were earlier working on the strength of the Interim Orders of the\nHon'ble Court.\n6. The said G.O.Ms.No.155 dated 03.10.2002 was put to\nchallenge before this Court in a batch of writ petitions. The Hon'ble Division\nBench upheld the aforesaid G.O. in a reported judgment in State of Tamil\nNadu v. Pallivasal Primary School, 2004 (2) LW 591. The Hon'ble Division\nBench, however, directed the Government not to recover the salaries already\npaid to them, and further directed to reckon their services rendered before\ncompletion of one-month Child Psychology for pension purposes. However,\nas the appointment of petitioners were after the cut-off date fixed by this\nHon'ble Court, the Department had not approved the appointments against the\nsanctioned posts, and the school retained the said posts.\n7. In the meantime, sometime in the year 2002, the State\nGovernment noted the fact that Secondary Grade teachers with Teacher\nTraining Certificates / Diplomas in Teacher Education were not qualified\n6/22\n\nWP(MD) NO. 2535 of 2017\nenough to handle the advanced syllabi prescribed for the middle school\nsections and hence, evolved a policy of appointing Graduate Teachers in\nthose posts. Vide G.O.Ms. No. 79 (Secondary Education – U1) dated\n14.06.2002 the said prescription and orders were passed. The posts of\nSecondary Grade teachers were decided to be upgraded into those of\nGraduate Teachers in the regular scale of pay of Rs. 4500-125-7000/-.\n8. In order to protect the interests of the existing Secondary\nGrade teachers, the posts of secondary grade teachers were to be upgraded as\nGraduate posts as and when those posts fell vacant, and thereafter, persons\nwith Graduate qualifications should alone be appointed. Such upgraded posts\ncame to be known as “Middle Grade Graduate Teachers”. In view of the said\nG.O., Graduate Teachers with B.A. / B.Sc., + B.Ed. qualifications came to be\nappointed as Middle Grade Graduate Teachers in the posts of Secondary\nGrade Teachers as and when they fell vacant since 01.06.2002.\n9. The petitioners herein have cited G.O. Ms. No. 79 dated\n14.06.2002 and claimed that they are eligible to be appointed, and that their\nappointments should have been approved. Hence, the fifth respondent\n7/22\n\nWP(MD) NO. 2535 of 2017\nsubmitted a proposal to the fourth respondent to approve their appointments\nin light of G.O. Ms. No. 79 dated 14.06.2002. However, the DEO, the fourth\nrespondent, insisted that the fifth respondent school should issue fresh\nappointment orders in respect of the individuals appointed as Middle Grade\nGraduate teachers. And at the instance of the fourth respondent, the fifth\nrespondent school issued fresh appointment orders on 21.10.2002 appointing\nthem as Middle Grade Graduate teachers vide G.O.Ms.No.79 and submitted\nthe proposal for the approval of the fourth respondent. The fourth respondent,\nhowever, did not pass any orders.\n10. In the meantime, the Government issued two other G.Os,\nnamely G.O.Ms.No.100 (School Education - Budget) dated 27.06.2003 and\nG.O.Ms.No.125 (School Education (X2) Dept.) dated 12.11.2003. The first\nG.O. i.e., G.O.Ms.No.100, pertains to the appointment of Graduate Teachers\nin Panchayat Union Schools, while the second G.O. pertains to private aided\nschools. In the said G.Os, the Government introduced a new system of\npayment namely, 'Consolidated Pay' for a certain period.\n8/22\n\nWP(MD) NO. 2535 of 2017\n11. Thereafter, the Government issued G.O.Ms.No.130, School\nEducation Department dated 27.09.2004, ratifying the appointments of\nvarious Middle Grade Graduate Teachers appointed in the private schools in\nviolation of G.O.Ms.No.79.\n12. The claim of the official respondents is that while appointing\nMiddle Grade Graduate teachers in the Secondary Grade vacancy vide G.O.\nMs. No. 79, the schools had to take prior permission. However, the school\ncontended that the appointments do not require prior permission and most of\nthe appointments were made without obtaining prior permission from the\nconcerned school authorities. However, the Government decided to approve\nthose appointments under consolidated pay of Rs.4,000/- per month, w.e.f.\n01.06.2003.\n13. It is the case of the petitioners that the circumstances that\nwere obtaining then, that is, the lack of enough graduate teachers, were the\nreason for introducing these G.Os and for recruiting teachers on a large scale,\nand also to pay them a consolidated pay.\n9/22\n\nWP(MD) NO. 2535 of 2017\n14. However, the conditions imposed in G.O.Ms.No.79,\nregarding prior permission do not apply to the petitioners, according to them,\nfor two reasons: firstly, that the fifth respondent is a minority educational\ninstitution and there is no necessity to get prior permission; secondly, that the\npetitioners were appointed against sanctioned vacancies much before the\nissuance of G.O. Ms. No. 79, and thus would contend that prior permission is\nnot necessary.\n15. The learned Additional Government Pleader would contend\nthat the petitioners, being degree-qualified teachers, were appointed against\nvacancies in the post of Secondary Grade Teacher, and that their appointments\ncould not be approved in view of G.O.Ms. No.559 dated 11.07.1995. The\nvalidity of said G.O. was put to challenge in a batch of writ petitions and was\ndismissed by a learned Single Judge on 19.05.1998 and the consequent writ\nappeals were also dismissed by a Division Bench on 29.06.2001 (Secretary\nCorrespondent Uswathun Hasana Oriental (Arabic) Girls Higher\nSecondary School v. State of Tamil Nadu, 2002 Writ L.R. 173), subject to the\nconcession that B.Ed. qualified teachers appointed prior to 19.05.1998 would\nbe regularized upon completion of one month's Child Psychology Training,\n10/22\n\nWP(MD) NO. 2535 of 2017\npursuant to which G.O.Ms.No.155 dated 03.10.2002, was issued. Since\npetitioners 1 to 5 were appointed only on 04.06.1998, 04.06.1998,\n25.06.1998, 01.07.1998 and 03.06.1998 respectively, i.e., after the cut-off\ndate, their appointments could not be approved under the said G.O.\n16. He would further contend that G.O.Ms.No.79 dated\n14.06.2002, which provides for the appointment of B.Ed. qualified teachers in\nthe place of Secondary Grade Teachers by classifying them as Middle Grade\nGraduate Teachers, was issued in respect of Government schools and is not\napplicable to aided schools. Hence, the said G.O. cannot be relied upon for\nseeking approval of the petitioners’ appointments w.e.f. 14.06.2002.\n17. Subsequently, the Government issued two G.O. namely,\nG.O.Ms.No.100 dated 27.06.2003 in respect of Government schools and\nG.O.Ms.No.125 dated 12.11.2003 in respect of aided schools, under which,\nvarious categories of teacher posts were converted into Junior Grade Teacher\nposts carrying consolidated pay. Accordingly, the appointments of the\npetitioners were approved w.e.f. 01.06.2003 on consolidated pay and were\nsubsequently brought under the time scale of pay w.e.f. 01.06.2006. Hence,\n11/22\n\nWP(MD) NO. 2535 of 2017\nthe petitioners cannot claim approval of their appointments w.e.f. 14.06.2002\nunder G.O.Ms.No.79, which is applicable only to Government schools.\n18. It was further submitted that, the Government issued\nG.O.Ms.No.144 dated 04.07.2008, wherein he Chief Educational Officer is\nempowered to convert vacant Secondary Grade Teacher posts into subjectwise B.T. Teacher posts, having regard to the requirement of the school and\nthe qualifications of the teachers. The said Government Order provides that\nB.Ed.-qualified teachers appointed to teach Classes VI to VIII in subjects\nsuch as English, Mathematics and Science shall be given preference against\nthe respective vacancies. It is further provided that, since Classes VI to X are\ntreated as a single unit subject-wise, vacant Secondary Grade Teacher posts\nmay be converted into graduate teacher posts according to the requirement of\nthe particular subject and even minority schools are required to obtain\npermission for such subject-wise rotation and that the said requirement does\nnot amount to interference with the right of administration of the minority\ninstitution.\n12/22\n\nWP(MD) NO. 2535 of 2017\n19. It was therefore contended that as the petitioners were\nappointed after the cut-off date i.e., 19.05.1988, their initial appointments\nwere irregular, and their claim can be traced only to G.O.Ms.No.125 dated\n12.11.2003, under which their appointments were approved with effect from\n01.06.2003. Furthermore, the petitioners cannot seek any benefit under\nG.O.Ms.No.79 dated 14.06.2002, as it is applicable only to Government\nschools and not to aided schools.\n20. This Court has considered the submissions made by the\nlearned counsel appearing for the petitioners and the learned Additional\nGovernment Pleader appearing for the respondents and perused the materials\nplaced on record.\n21. The petitioners were appointed between 04.06.1998 and\n03.06.1999. It is seen from the materials placed before this Court that, as\nearly as in the year 2002, the School had submitted a proposal to the\ncompetent authority seeking approval of the petitioners' appointments by\nextending the benefit of G.O.Ms.No.79. The said proposal was not accepted.\nThus, the petitioners were aware, at the earliest, from the year 2002 that the\n13/22\n\nWP(MD) NO. 2535 of 2017\nbenefit claimed by them had not been extended to their appointments.\nHowever, they did not challenge the said decision within a reasonable period.\n22. Instead, after a considerable lapse of time, the petitioners\nsubmitted a representation dated 31.07.2012, seeking extension of the benefit\nallegedly granted to one Padma Sheela Rani, vide proceedings dated\n22.11.2008, and to D.Lily, vide proceedings dated 29.10.2010. The said\nrepresentation came to be rejected by the Director vide proceedings dated\n02.01.2015 on the ground that the benefit extended to the said persons was\npursuant to orders passed by this Court and, therefore, the same could not be\nclaimed as a matter of parity by the petitioners. The rejection was thereafter\nreiterated by the District Educational Officer vide proceedings dated\n25.11.2016, in response to the representation submitted by the School on\n21.10.2016. Aggrieved by the aforesaid proceedings, the present writ petition\nhas been filed.\n23. Eventhough the present writ petition was instituted in the\nyear 2017, shortly after the proceedings dated 25.11.2016, the question of\ndelay and laches cannot be examined merely with reference to the date of the\n14/22\n\nWP(MD) NO. 2535 of 2017\nlast rejection order. The subsequent representation and the order passed\nthereon cannot be treated as giving rise to a fresh cause of action. The claim\nof the petitioners has to be examined with reference to the original cause of\naction, namely, the non-approval of their appointments and the rejection of\nthe proposal seeking such approval in the year 2002. The fact that the\npetitioners approached this Court soon after the rejection of their subsequent\nrepresentation in 2016 would not, by itself, efface the prolonged period of\ninaction from 2002 onwards.\n24. In this context, the principle laid down by the Hon'ble\nSupreme Court in C. Jacob v. Director of Geology and Mining, reported in\n(2008) 10 SCC 115, assumes significance. The Hon'ble Supreme Court has\nheld that representations relating to matters which have become stale or are\nbarred by limitation can be rejected on that ground and that a representation,\nor an order passed upon such representation, does not furnish a fresh cause of\naction or revive a stale or dead claim. The fact that an authority subsequently\nconsiders and rejects a representation cannot, therefore, be construed as\ngiving a fresh lease of life to a claim which had remained unchallenged for a\nconsiderable period. The relevant portions of the said judgment is extracted\n15/22\n\nWP(MD) NO. 2535 of 2017\nhereunder:\n“7. Every representation to the government for relief,\nmay not be replied on merits. Representations relating to\nmatters which have become stale or barred by limitation, can\nbe rejected on that ground alone, without examining the\nmerits of the claim. In regard to representations unrelated to\nthe department, the reply may be only to inform that the\nmatter did not concern the department or to inform the\nappropriate department. Representations with incomplete\nparticulars may be replied by seeking relevant particulars.\nThe replies to such representations, cannot furnish a fresh\ncause of action or revive a stale or dead claim.\n8. When a direction is issued by a court/tribunal to\nconsider or deal with the representation, usually the directee\n(person directed) examines the matter on merits, being under\nthe impression that failure to do may amount to disobedience.\nWhen an order is passed considering and rejecting the claim\nor representation, in compliance with direction of the court or\ntribunal, such an order does not revive the stale claim, nor\namount to some kind of `acknowledgment of a jural\nrelationship' to give rise to a fresh cause of action.”\n25. The above principle is equally applicable to the present case.\nThe petitioners' claim had its genesis in their appointments made between\n1998 and 1999 and, admittedly, the proposal seeking approval of such\n16/22\n\nWP(MD) NO. 2535 of 2017\nappointments under G.O.Ms.No.79 had already been rejected in the year\n2002. The petitioners did not assail the said rejection at that point of time.\nThe subsequent representations made in 2012 and 2016, therefore, cannot\nhave the effect of reviving the original claim or creating a fresh cause of\naction. The proceedings dated 02.01.2015 and 25.11.2016 were only\nconsequential orders passed on the subsequent representations and cannot be\ntreated as fresh orders giving rise to an independent cause of action.\n26. The reliance placed upon the proceedings issued in favour of\nPadma Sheela Rani in the year 2008 and D.Lily in the year 2010 also does not\nalter the position. Those proceedings were not orders granting a general or\nautomatic extension of the benefit under G.O.Ms.No.79 to all similarly\nsituated persons.\n27. The principle laid down by the Hon'ble Supreme Court in\nState of Uttar Pradesh v. Arvind Kumar Srivastava, reported in (2015) 1\nSCC 347, is apposite. The Hon'ble Supreme Court has held that persons who\ndo not challenge the alleged wrongful action in their own cases and remain\nsilent, but subsequently approach the Court only after their counterparts\n17/22\n\nWP(MD) NO. 2535 of 2017\nsucceed, cannot claim the benefit as a matter of right and may be treated as\nfence-sitters. The relevant paragraph is extracted hereunder:\n“22.2. However, this principle is subject to wellrecognised exceptions in the form of laches and delays as well\nas acquiescence. Those persons who did not challenge the\nwrongful action in their cases and acquiesced into the same\nand woke up after long delay only because of the reason that\ntheir counterparts who had approached the court earlier in\ntime succeeded in their efforts, then such employees cannot\nclaim that the benefit of the judgment rendered in the case of\nsimilarly situated persons be extended to them. They would be\ntreated as fence-sitters and laches and delays, and/or the\nacquiescence, would be a valid ground to dismiss their\nclaim.”\n28. In the present case, the petitioners did not challenge the\nrejection of their claim in the year 2002. They remained silent for several\nyears and sought to rely upon the subsequent proceedings issued in favour of\nother individuals in 2008 and 2010. Even thereafter, their representation was\nmade only on 31.07.2012, nearly a decade after the rejection of their claim.\nThe present proceedings of the year 2015 and 2016, and the Government\nOrders issued in respect of other Middle Grade Graduate Teachers, do not\n18/22\n\nWP(MD) NO. 2535 of 2017\ngive them a fresh cause of action. The petitioners, having acquiesced for such\na long time, must be treated as fence-sitters and cannot be permitted to reopen\nthe matter.\n29. The delay in the present case is not a mere technical delay.\nThe petitioners seek to enforce a right arising out of appointments made as far\nback as in 1998-1999, despite the rejection of the proposal seeking approval\nunder G.O.Ms.No.79 in 2002. No satisfactory explanation has been offered\nfor the failure to challenge the said rejection within a reasonable period. The\nsubsequent representations do not erase the intervening period of inaction\nand, applying the principles laid down in C. Jacob and Arvind Kumar\nSrivastava (supra), cannot revive a stale claim.\n30. Accordingly, this Court is of the view that the writ\npetitioners, having approached this Court after an inordinate and unexplained\ndelay, are guilty of laches. The claim is, therefore, liable to be rejected on the\nground of delay and laches alone. In view of the same, it is not necessary for\nthis Court to examine the merits of the petitioners' claim for extension of the\nbenefit of G.O.Ms.No.79.\n19/22\n\nWP(MD) NO. 2535 of 2017\n31. Accordingly, the writ petition stands dismissed. No costs.\nConsequently, connected miscellaneous petition is closed.\n16.10.2026\nNCC :yes/No\nIndex :yes/No\nInternet:yes/No\nKSA\n20/22\n\nWP(MD) NO. 2535 of 2017\nTO\n1. The Secretary,\nState Of Tamilnadu,\nDepartment Of School Education,\nFort.St.George,\nChennai-600 009.\n2. The Director Of School Education,\nCollege Road, Chennai-600 006.\n3. The Chief Educational Officer\nTuticorin-628 002,\nTuticorin District.\n4. The District Educational Officer,\nKovilpatti-628 501\nTuticorin District.\n5. The Correspondent,\nSt.Joseph Higher Secondary School,\nKovilpatti-628 501,\nTuticorin District.\n21/22\n\nWP(MD) NO. 2535 of 2017\nRAJNISH PATHIYIL, J.\nKSA\nOrder in\nWP(MD) NO. 2535 of 2017\n16.10.2026\n22/22	22	t	HCMA010025352026	Writ Petition	\N	Single Bench	R. Seenivasan & Ors.	Secretary, Department of School Education & Ors.	Mr.K.K.Ragatheesh Kumar	Mr.P. Pethu Rajesh, Additional Government Pleader for Counter for R1 to R4	Constitutional Law	In this judgment under Civil Law, the Court ruled on the dispute between 16G98Aeba6Bgnknd-Y8X5Gl1Kqrvzxnzo Home 