le phones were seized under a mahazar at 10.00 hours, and the 	[]	Legal principle affirmed in CASE/B4BD701A/2026	[{"test": "Section 8(c) read with 20(b)(ii)(C) NDPS Act", "mandatory": true}, {"test": "Section 25 NDPS Act", "mandatory": true}, {"test": "Section 29(1) NDPS Act", "mandatory": true}, {"test": "Section 483 BNSS", "mandatory": true}]	Dismissed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2022) 12 SCC 633]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2022) 12 SCC 633", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}, {"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2021) 4 SCC 1]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2021) 4 SCC 1", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}, {"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2010) 14 SCC 496]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2010) 14 SCC 496", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}, {"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2026) 7 SCC 513]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2026) 7 SCC 513", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}, {"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2021 SCC OnLine SC 174]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2021 SCC OnLine SC 174", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}, {"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2025 SCC OnLine Del 293]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2025 SCC OnLine Del 293", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}, {"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2026 SCC OnLine SC 671]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2026 SCC OnLine SC 671", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	https://mhc.tn.gov.in/judis	verified	Good Law	CRL OP No. 26220 of 2026\n\nDATED: 09-10-2026\nCORAM\nTHE HON'BLE MR.JUSTICE N.RAMESH\nCRL OP No. 26220 of 2026\nCNR: {HCMA012161402026}\nYuvaraj\n..Petitioner(s)\nVs\nThe State Rep by\nThe Inspector of Police\nPEW Gummidipoondi,\nTiruvallur District.\nCrime No.120/2025.\n..Respondent(s)\nPRAYER: This criminal original petition filed under Section 482 BNSS Act to\nenlarge the petitioner on bail in CC No.597 of 2026 in connection to the Crime\nNo.120 of 2025 pending on the file of the II Additional Special Judge under\nNDPS Act Cases at Chennai.\nFor Petitioner(s): Mr.P.Muthamizhselvakumar\nFor Respondent(s): Mr.M.M.I.Khaleel\nGovernment Advocate (Crl.Side)\nORDER\nThis is a petition for bail in a case under the NDPS Act (“the Act”), filed\nunder Section 483 of the BNSS. The petitioner is the fifth accused in Crime\nNo.120 of 2025 on the file of the Inspector of Police, Prohibition Enforcement\nWing, Gummidipoondi, Tiruvallur District, now pending as C.C.No.597 of\n2026 on the file of the II Additional Special Court for Exclusive Trial of Cases\n__________\nPage1 of 11\n\nCRL OP No. 26220 of 2026\nunder the NDPS Act, Chennai. The offences charged are under Sections 8(c)\nread with 20(b)(ii)(C), 25 and 29(1) of the Act.\n2. On 09.10.2025 at about 09.00 hours, the respondent police stopped an\nAshok Leyland lorry bearing registration No.TN 52 AB 8935 at the Elavur\ncheck post on the road from Andhra Pradesh to Chennai. In the tool box behind\nthe driver’s seat they found 50 bundles packed in three sacks, each of about 2\nkilograms, in all about 100 kilograms of ganja. The ganja, the lorry and three\nmobile phones were seized under a mahazar at 10.00 hours, and the three men\nin the lorry, Dhanasekaran (A1), Mohanraj (A2) and Rajeshkumar @ Siva (A3),\nwere arrested. The chemical analysis found cannabinoids in the samples. The\npetitioner was not in the lorry and nothing was seized from him. His name\ncomes in the confession statements of A1 and A2 recorded the same day.\nAccording to them, Siva of Brahmapur, Odisha (A4), supplied the ganja, and\ndirected that a part of it be handed over to the petitioner, a resident of Chinna\nNatham village near Gummidipoondi, and the larger part to Ramana of Madurai\n(A6). They also say that on two earlier trips in the same year they had delivered\nganja to the petitioner at Naidupeta in Andhra Pradesh. A mobile number,\n8939861125, is shown against the petitioner in the first information report and\nin the final report.\n3. The whole seizure of about 100 kilograms of Ganjais commercial\nquantity. The share said to have been meant for the petitioner is not stated\nuniformly. A1’s confession speaks of “10 kilo bundles”; A2’s confession speaks\n__________\nPage2 of 11\n\nCRL OP No. 26220 of 2026\nof 10 bundles containing 20 kilograms; the final report in Charge Sheet\nNo.FR-0004/2026 dated 02.04.2026 puts it at 10 kilograms; and the counter\naffidavit, in paragraph 2, at “10 bundles weighing 20 kg”. On either figure the\nshare is not greater than 20 kilograms. By itself it would be an intermediate\nquantity.\n4. The final report was laid on 02.04.2026. It showed the petitioner, along\nwith A4 and A6, as absconding. The case was taken on file on 06.04.2026. The\npetitioner surrendered before the Special Court on 16.04.2026 and was\nremanded to judicial custody. He has been in custody since then. The case is\nnow at the stage of evidence, and according to the counter affidavit it stands\nposted to 12.10.2026 for that purpose. This is the first petition of the petitioner\nbefore this Court. None of the co-accused is shown to have been enlarged on\nbail.\n5. The learned counsel for the petitioner submitted that the petitioner was\nnot at the spot, was not apprehended there, and that no ganja, money, vehicle or\nother article was recovered from him or at his instance. The only material\nagainst him is the confession statements of A1 to A3 and the call records. A\nconfession to a police officer is inadmissible, and without independent material\nit cannot keep a man in prison. The petitioner surrendered on his own, which\nshows his bona fides. The investigation is over and nothing further is to be\nrecovered from him. In paragraph 8 of the petition it is stated that “The\nPetitioner has no other criminal case pending against him and is not required\n__________\nPage3 of 11\n\nCRL OP No. 26220 of 2026\nin any other case.” Reliance was placed on Mallikarjun v. State of\nKarnataka, SLP (Crl.) No.3341 of 2023, dated 22.01.2024; Ragini Dwivedi\n@ Gini @ Rags v. State of Karnataka, 2021 SCC OnLine SC 174; Irshad v.\nState, Crl.O.P.No.27634 of 2024, dated 20.11.2024; Abdul Hameed v. State,\nCrl.O.P.No.21315 of 2022, dated 12.09.2022, affirmed by the Supreme Court\non 06.02.2024; State (by NCB), Bengaluru v. Pallulabid Ahmad Arimutta,\n(2022) 12 SCC 633; and Abdul Rab v. Narcotics Control Bureau, 2025 SCC\nOnLine Del 293.\n6. The learned Government Advocate (Criminal Side), on the strength of\nthe counter affidavit of the Inspector of Police, opposed the petition. The\nquantity is commercial and Section 37 of the Act applies. The surrender was\nmade only after the charge sheet was filed, to avoid custodial interrogation. The\naverment that the petitioner has no other case is false. Paragraph 5 of the\ncounter sets out eight earlier cases against him, four of them under the Act. If\nreleased, he is likely to abscond, to tamper with the evidence and to return to the\ntrade.\n7. I have considered the rival submissions and perused the material placed\nbefore me.\n8. The first question is whether Section 37 of the Act governs this\npetitioner at all. This is not a case of separate recoveries being added together.\nThere was one consignment, in one tool box, seized under one mahazar. But the\npetitioner is not said to have been in possession of any part of it. The whole of\n__________\nPage4 of 11\n\nCRL OP No. 26220 of 2026\nthe 100 kilograms can be put to his account only through Section 29, on the\nfooting that he was party to a conspiracy for the entire consignment and not\nmerely the intended buyer of his share. For that, the material placed before me\nconsists of the confession statements of A1 and A2. A confessional statement\nrecorded by an officer under the Act is inadmissible at the trial, as held in Tofan\nSingh v. State of Tamil Nadu, (2021) 4 SCC 1, and such statements cannot by\nthemselves form the basis for refusing bail, as held in Pallulabid Ahmad\nArimutta (cited supra). Nothing placed before me shows that the call details of\nthe number attributed to the petitioner have been collected, and none of the\neight witnesses listed in the final report is an officer of a service provider.\nWhether the petitioner was a party to the larger agreement is a question for the\ntrial. I do not propose to rest this order on Section 37. I shall take the case on the\nfooting most favourable to the petitioner: that only his share is to be put to his\naccount, that it is an intermediate quantity, and that the petition is to be decided\non the ordinary principles that govern bail.\n9. Those principles are settled. The Court considers, among other things,\nwhether there is a prima facie case, the nature and gravity of the accusation, the\nseverity of the punishment on conviction, the danger of the accused absconding,\nhis character and antecedents, and the likelihood of the offence being repeated,\nas held in Prasanta Kumar Sarkar v. Ashis Chatterjee, (2010) 14 SCC 496.\nWhere antecedents are placed before the Court in a narcotics case, they must be\nconsidered, and a court should be slow to grant even regular bail where the\n__________\nPage5 of 11\n\nCRL OP No. 26220 of 2026\nquantity is large and the accused has criminal antecedents, as held in State by\nthe Inspector of Police v. B. Ramu, 2024 INSC 114.\n10. I accept that the material connecting the petitioner with this\nconsignment is thin. It is the word of the co-accused, recorded by the police,\nand a mobile number. Were that the whole of the case, the petition would stand\non a different footing. It is not the whole of the case. Paragraph 5 of the counter\naffidavit sets out the petitioner’s earlier cases by police station, crime number,\nsections and stage. Four of them are under the Act. Crime No.46 of 2022 of\nArambakkam Police Station, under Sections 8(c) read with 20(b)(ii)(C), 25 and\n29(1) of the Act, involving a commercial quantity, is pending trial. Crime No.97\nof 2022 of the same station, under Sections 8(c) read with 20(b)(ii)(B) of the\nAct, has been charge-sheeted. Crime No.530 of 2024, under Sections 8(c) read\nwith 20(b)(ii)(A) of the Act, Section 25(1A) of the Arms Act and Sections 132,\n296(b) and 351(3) of the BNS, has been charge-sheeted. Crime No.537 of 2023,\nunder Sections 8(c) read with 20(b)(ii)(B) of the Act and Section 328 IPC, is\nstated to be under investigation. Besides these, there is a charge-sheeted case of\nattempt to robbery under Section 393 IPC in Crime No.109 of 2021 of SIPCOT\nPolice Station, and three other cases. These are particulars, not a description. I\ngive little weight to the cases still under investigation. Even leaving them out,\nthree cases under the Act have reached the stage of final report, one of them for\na commercial quantity, and the present case is the fourth such case in which the\n__________\nPage6 of 11\n\nCRL OP No. 26220 of 2026\npetitioner is charge-sheeted. Nothing was placed before me to dispute these\nparticulars.\n11. Against this record, the petitioner swore in paragraph 8 of his petition\nthat he has no other criminal case. That statement is not correct. A court\nconsidering bail under a special statute cannot act on a statement of the\naccused’s record which the papers before it contradict, as held in State of\nPunjab v. Sukhwinder Singh @ Gora, (2026) 7 SCC 513 : 2026 SCC\nOnLine SC 671. The incorrect averment also takes away the weight of the\npetitioner’s assurance that he will abide by any condition.\n12. The surrender does not help the petitioner as much as he claims. He\nwas named in the first information report on 09.10.2025. The final report of\n02.04.2026 records him as absconding. He appeared before the Special Court\nonly on 16.04.2026, ten days after the case was taken on file. I do not accept the\ncounter affidavit’s reading of his motive, which is a matter of inference. But a\nsurrender six months after being named is not a circumstance that tells in his\nfavour.\n13. The decisions cited for the petitioner do not assist him on these facts.\nMallikarjun (cited supra), as presented, concerned separate seizures made on\ndifferent dates; here there is one seizure, and I have not aggregated anything\nagainst the petitioner. Ragini Dwivedi (cited supra) turned on a case of\nconsumption to which Section 27 of the Act applied; no such case is put\nforward here. In Irshad (cited supra), this Court granted bail noting, in\n__________\nPage7 of 11\n\nCRL OP No. 26220 of 2026\nparagraph 6, that there was no previous case pending against the petitioner\nthere. Abdul Hameed (cited supra), as described in the petition, was a case of\na non-commercial quantity with no other material; it does not speak to an\naccused with this record. Pallulabid Ahmad Arimutta (cited supra) was a\npetition for cancellation of bail already granted, and I have applied the principle\nit states on confessions. Abdul Rab (cited supra) is a decision of the Delhi\nHigh Court, persuasive only, on the insufficiency of call records; that is not the\nground on which this petition fails.\n14. Taking the case on the footing most favourable to the petitioner, I find\na real likelihood of the offence being repeated if he is released. He faces three\ncharge-sheeted cases under the Act besides this one, he did not disclose any of\nthem, and he did not come before the Court until six months after he was\nnamed. The period of custody, a little under six months, is not long for an\noffence of this nature, and the trial has reached the stage of evidence. If Section\n37 of the Act were to govern, the position would be no better for him. The twin\nconditions under Section 37(1)(b)(ii) are cumulative, as held in Sukhwinder\nSingh @ Gora (cited supra), and on this record I could not be satisfied that he\nis not likely to commit any offence while on bail.\n15.The observations made in this order are confined to the consideration\nof this petition for bail. They shall not influence the trial, where the evidence,\nincluding the question whether the petitioner was a party to a conspiracy\ncovering the whole consignment, will be appreciated on its own merits.\n__________\nPage8 of 11\n\nCRL OP No. 26220 of 2026\n16. In the result, this Criminal Original Petition is dismissed.\n09-10-2026\nIndex: Yes/No\nSpeaking/Non-speaking order\nNeutral Citation: Yes/No\nsms\nTo\n1.II Additional Special Judge under NDPS Act Cases\nChennai.\n2.The Superintendent of Police\nCentral Prison, Puzhal – II.\n3.The Inspector of Police,\nPEW Gummidipoondi,\nTiruvallur District.\n4.The Public Prosecutor\nHigh Court, Madras.\n__________\nPage9 of 11\n\nCRL OP No. 26220 of 2026\nN.RAMESH, J.\nsms\nCRL OP No. 26220 of 2026\n09-10-2026\n__________\nPage10 of 11\n\nCRL OP No. 26220 of 2026\n__________\nPage11 of 11	11	t	HCMA012161402026	Criminal Original Petition (Bail)	2026-10-09	Single Bench	Yuvaraj	The State Rep by The Inspector of Police PEW Gummidipoondi, Tiruvallur District.	Mr.P.Muthamizhselvakumar	Mr.M.M.I.Khaleel Government Advocate (Crl.Side)	Criminal Law	In this criminal original petition under Civil Law, the Court ruled on the dispute between 17 Crl-Op-26220-2026 and the Respondent.	0	2026 LexGuard (Crim) 52DD98	{"court": "Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)", "title": "Yuvaraj v. State Rep. by Inspector of Police, PEW Gummidipoondi", "case_id": "52dd984d-ae71-4dbf-a983-5d1d8218360e", "case_no": "CRL OP No. 26220 of 2026", "parties": "Yuvaraj v. State Rep. by Inspector of Police, PEW Gummidipoondi", "category": "NDPS", "petitioner": "Yuvaraj", "respondent": "State Rep. by Inspector of Police, PEW Gummidipoondi", "legal_domain": "Criminal Law", "category_facts": {"category": "NDPS", "custody_period": null, "contraband_type": "Ganja", "seized_quantity": "2\\nkilo", "statutory_issue": "Bail / Pre-trial Release under NDPS Act", "quantity_category": "Commercial Quantity", "section_37_embargo": true, "search_compliance_sec_50": "Search procedure evaluated"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "BNSS", "section": "483", "canonical": "Section 483 BNSS", "equivalents": ["Section 439 CrPC"]}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["narcotics_contraband"], "scenario_keywords": ["ndps"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
