rge.	[]	Disposed	[]	\N	verified_authentic	binding	WP.358.2026.doc\nAjay\nIINN TTHHEE HHIIGGHH CCOOUURRTT OOFF JJUUDDIICCAATTUURREE AATT BBOOMMBBAAYY\nCCRRIIMMIINNAALL AAPPPPEELLLLAATTEE JJUURRIISSDDIICCTTIIOONN\nCRIMINAL WRIT PETITION NO. 358 OF 2026\nArnav Manish Mandhana and Anr. .. Petitioners\nVersus\nDirectorate of Enforcement and Anr. .. Respondents\n....................\n Mr. Aabad Ponda, Senior Advocate a/w. Mr. Amit A. Patil and Mr.\nAaditya Nikam, Advocates i/by APLC for Petitioners.\n Mr. Prashant Mishra, Special Counsel a/w. Ms. Purva Birla, Ms.\nShruti Chande, Mr. Krish Kariya, Mr. Akshun Thakur, Ms. Tisha\nThakkar, Mr. Ketan Gavhane, Advocates for Respondent No.1 – ED.\n Ms. Rajeshree V. Newton, APP for Respondent – State.\n......…...........\nCORAM : MILIND N. JADHAV, J.\nDATE : OCTOBER 22, 2026.\nP.C.:\n1. I have heard Mr. Ponda, learned Senior Advocate for\nPetitioners; Mr. Mishra, Special Counsel for Respondent No.1 – ED and\nMs. Newton, learned APP for Respondent – State.\n2. By the present Petition, Petitioners have challenged the order\ndated 30.10.2025 passed below Exhibits 48 and 49 in PMLA case\nNo.101963 of 2024 by the learned Special Court and have sought to\nunfreeze and remove the attachment on Petitioner’s Bank Accounts\nand Demat Accounts as a consequence thereof.\n3. The brief relevant facts necessary for adjudication of the\npresent Petition are as follows:-\n1 of 8\n\nWP.358.2026.doc\n3.1. Petitioners are children of Mr. Manish Mandhana who is\narrayed as an accused person in PMLA Special Case No.101963 of\n2024. Both Petitioners were studying abroad and have now returned\nback to India. Their Bank / Demat accounts are attached and frozen by\nRespondent – ED. Prosecution has registered FIR dated 12.09.2023 for\noffences of cheating and under the PC Act against Mandhana\nIndustries Limited for alleged diversion of funds which were availed by\nthe said Company from various Public Sector Banks during 2014 –\n2017. Both Petitioners are not named as accused persons in the FIR.\nOn the basis of FIR ECIR Case is also lodged on 26.09.2023 under the\nPC Act. Petitioners are not named as accused in the ECIR case as well.\n3.2. On 26.06.2024, Respondent No.1 conducted search at\nPetitioner’s residence under Section 17 of PMLA and under\npanchanama and froze Petitioners’ Bank / Demat accounts. On\n10.07.2024, Authorised Officer of Respondent No.1 passed order\nunder Section 20(1) of PMLA, declaring retention of the Bank / Demat\naccounts for a period not exceeding 180 days from the date of seizure.\nIt is seen that these 180 days expired on 23.12.2024. On 24.07.2024,\nprosecution filed Application before Special Court under Section 17(4)\nof PMLA praying for retention of properties and records and properties\nfrozen during pendency of proceedings, with regard to only those\nproperties stated in paragraph Nos.5 and 6 of the Application.\n2 of 8\n\nWP.358.2026.doc\n3.3. Petitioners’ Bank / Demat accounts were not mentioned in\nparagraph Nos.5 and 6, neither Petitioners were impleaded as parties\nin the Application. Subsequently on 01.10.2024 charge-sheet in\nrelation to ECIR case was filed before Special Court wherein\nPetitioners are not arraigned as Accused persons. On 03.12.2024,\nAdjudicating Authority passed order under Section 8 (3) of PMLA\nallowing the Application under Section 17(4) of PMLA for retention\nand freezing of properties stated in paragraph Nos.5 and 6 of the said\nApplication. This order is appended at Exhibit “G” at page No.155 and\nwith the able assistance of both Advocates, I have perused the same.\n3.4. On 28.01.2025, both Petitioners filed separate Appeals under\nSection 26 of PMLA to challenge the order dated 03.12.2024, wherein\nthey preferred Applications below Exhibits 48 and 49 seeking direction\nto Respondent No.1 to unfreeze and to remove the attachment on their\nBank / Demat Accounts. On 27.08.2026, Petitioners withdrew both\nAppeals since there was no order of continued freezing / retention of\ntheir Bank / Demat Accounts operating against them. Curiously,\nRespondent No.1 resisted Applicatio	8	t	BHCB010003582026	Writ Petition	\N	Single Bench	Arnav Manish Mandhana and Anr. .. Petitioners	Directorate of Enforcement & Anr.	\N	No.1 – ED.	Constitutional Law	In this judgment under Constitutional Law, the Court ruled on the dispute between Arnav Manish Mandhana & Anr. and Directorate of Enforcement & Anr..	0	2026 LexGuard (Judg) 5C2B7B	{"court": "High Court of Judicature at Bombay (Appellate Side)", "title": "Arnav Manish Mandhana & Anr. v. Directorate of Enforcement & Anr.", "case_id": "5c2b7bf1-03f7-499b-b8bb-b8b647c84b4e", "case_no": "WP.358.2026.doc\\nAjay\\nIINN TTHHEE HHIIGGHH CCOOUURRTT OOFF JJUUDDIICCAATTUURREE AATT BBOOMMBBAAYY\\nCCRRIIMMIINNAALL AAPPPPEELLLLAATTEE JJUURRIISSDDIICCTTIIOONN\\nCRIMINAL WRIT PETITION NO. 358 OF 2026", "parties": "Arnav Manish Mandhana & Anr. v. Directorate of Enforcement & Anr.", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Arnav Manish Mandhana & Anr.", "respondent": "Directorate of Enforcement & Anr.", "legal_domain": "Constitutional Law", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Writ Petition / Criminal Appeal", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "21", "canonical": "Section 21 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["constitutional_fundamental"], "scenario_keywords": ["article 21"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Allowed", "disposition": "Allowed"}}
