717a9f06-8bfe-47f5-9fc3-c5239622a9a2	CRL OP No. 30797 of 2026	Mohammad Jafar v. The State Rep. By, The Inspector of Police	Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)	2026	Hon'ble Mr. Justice N.Ramesh	Mohammad Jafar v. The State Rep. By, The Inspector of Police	["Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023", "Information Technology Act", "of BNS"]	["Section 318(4) of BNS", "Section 319(2) of BNS", "Section 336(3) of BNS", "Section 338 of BNS", "Section 61(2) of BNS", "Section 66D Information Technology Act"]	["civil & constitutional law", "mhc", "disposed"]	Judgment in NO. 30797 of 2026 delivered by Hon'ble Bench at Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai). The proceedings concern Civil & Constitutional Law with respect to procedural compliance.	Bail Granted	storage/cases/MHC_2026_NO_30797_of_2026_717a9f06.pdf	810efcb20f5dba05803e57d6ffa950d3c2bae7c01e806e78373a4bf08d9473f9	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 14:51:57.199717+05:30	2026-10-25 14:51:57.199722+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:09.545516+05:30	2. The case of the prosecution is that the de facto complainant was induced by the petitioner to invest money in an online trading platform for higher returns and thereby, the de facto complainant transferred a total sum of Rs.2,11,46,843/- through various bank transactions. Hence, the case. 3.	[]	Regular bail granted in CRL OP No. 30797 of 2026 under applicable statutory provisions, considering the duration of incarceration and absence of flight risk, subject to executing bond and standard reporting conditions.	[]	Allowed	["ble Supreme Court in P.K.Shaji vs. State of Kerala [(2005)13 SCC 283]"]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding Precedent)	CRL OP No. 30797 of 2026\nIN THE HIGH COURT OF JUDICATURE AT MADRAS\nDATED: 23-10-2026\nCORAM\nTHE HON'BLE MR.JUSTICE N.RAMESH\nCRL OP No. 30797 of 2026\nCNR:{HCMA012564032026}\nMohammad Jafar\n..Petitioner\nVs\nThe State Rep. By,\nThe Inspector of Police\nDelta-2, Cyber Crime Police Station,\nCentral Crime Branch,\nChennai.\nCrime No.129 of 2026.\n..Respondent\nPRAYER : Criminal Original Petition filed under Section 483 of Bharatiya\nNagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023, to enlarge the petitioner on bail in Cr.No.129\nof 2026 pending investigation on the file of the Respondent Police.\nFor Petitioner: Mr.P.Cipooral Rose\nFor Respondent: Ms.R.S.Indira\nGovernment Advocate (Crl.Side)\nORDER\nThe petitioner, who was arrested and remanded to judicial custody on\n05.09.2026 for the alleged offences under Sections 318(4), 319(2), 336(3), 338\nand 61(2) of BNS and section 66D of Information Technology Act, 2000 in\nCrime No.129 of 2026 on the file of the respondent police, seeks bail.\n__________\nPage1 of 5\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\nCRL OP No. 30797 of 2026\n2. The case of the prosecution is that the de facto complainant was\ninduced by the petitioner to invest money in an online trading platform for\nhigher returns and thereby, the de facto complainant transferred a total sum of\nRs.2,11,46,843/- through various bank transactions. Hence, the case.\n3. The learned counsel appearing for the petitioner submitted that the\npetitioner has been falsely implicated in the present case and that he has not\ncommitted any offence as alleged by the prosecution. He further submitted that\nthe petitioner's account has already been frozen, having been used as a mule\naccount, and that the petitioner had no knowledge of the transactions. He\nfurther submitted that the petitioner is ready to abide by any stringent conditions\nthat may be imposed by this Court. Hence, he prays for grant of bail to the\nPetitioner.\n4. Per contra, the learned Additional Public Prosecutor appearing for the\nRespondent Police reiterated the prosecution case and submitted that the de\nfacto complainant invested and transferred to the tune of Rs.2,11,46,843/- as 1st\nlayer amount to the petitioner and that the petitioner has received Rs.9,13,890/-\nand the petitioner has been incarcerated since 05.09.2026.\n5. Heard the learned counsel appearing for the petitioner, the learned\nAdditional Public Prosecutor for the respondent Police and perused the\n__________\nPage2 of 5\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\nCRL OP No. 30797 of 2026\nmaterials available on record.\n6. Considering the facts and circumstances of the case; taking note of the\nsubmission made by the learned Additional Public Prosecutor; considering the\nperiod of incarceration of the petitioner and nature of allegation and that there\nare no other complaint against this petitioner and considering the fact that the\npetitioner hails from the State of Maharashtra, this Court is inclined to grant bail\nto the petitioner subject to certain conditions.\n7. Accordingly, the petitioner is ordered to be released on bail on his\nexecuting a bond for a sum of Rs.25,000/- (Rupees Twenty Five Thousand\nonly) with two sureties each (among two sureties, one should be a blood\nsurety and one should be a local surety), for a like sum to the satisfaction of\nthe learned XI MM Court, Saidapet, Chennai, and on further conditions that:\n[a] the sureties shall affix their photographs and Left\nThumb Impression in the Application for Surety ship\n[Judicial Form No.46 annexed to 'The Criminal Rules of\nPractice, 2019']. The learned Magistrate shall obtain a copy\nof any one of the identity proofs to ensure their identity;\n[b] the petitioner shall report before the respondent\npolice everyday at 10.30 a.m., until further orders;\n[c] the petitioner shall not abscond either during\ninvestigation or trial;\n[d] the petitioner shall not tamper with the evidence or\n__________\nPage3 of 5\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\nCRL OP No. 30797 of 2026\nwitness either during investigation or trial;\n[e] On breach of any of the aforementioned conditions,\nthe learned Magistrate/Trial Court is entitled to pass\nappropriate orders against the petitioner in accordance with\nlaw as if the aforementioned conditions have been imposed\nand the petitioner released on bail by the learned\nMagistrate/Trial Court himself as laid down by the Hon'ble\nSupreme Court in P.K.Shaji vs. State of Kerala [(2005)13\nSCC 283];\n[f] If the accused thereafter absconds, a fresh FIR can be\nregistered under Section 269 of B.N.S.\n23-10-2026\nSHL\nNote:\n1. Registry is directed to forthwith upload\nthis order in the Official Website of this\nCourt.\n2. All concerned to act on this order being\nuploaded in Official Website of this Court\nwithout insisting on certified hard copies. To\nbe noted, this order when uploaded in the\nofficial website of this Court will be\nwatermarked and will also have a QR code.\n__________\nPage4 of 5\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\nCRL OP No. 30797 of 2026\nN.RAMESH J.\nSHL\nTo:\n1. The Inspector of Police\nDelta-2, Cyber Crime Police Station,\nCentral Crime Branch,\nChennai.\n2. The XI MM Court, Saidapet,\nChennai\n3. The Central Prison-II,\nPuzhal, Chennai.\n4. The Public Prosecutor\nHigh Court of Madras\nCRL OP No. 30797 of 2026\n23-10-2026\n__________\nPage5 of 5\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis	5	t	HCMA012564032026	Criminal Original Petition (Bail)	2026-10-23	Single Bench	Mohammad Jafar	The State Rep. By, The Inspector of Police	Mr.P.Cipooral Rose	Ms.R.S.Indira Government Advocate (Crl.Side)	Civil & Constitutional Law	In this matter, the Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai) adjudicated issues between Mohammad Jafar and The State Rep. By, The Inspector of Police. The Court rendered a decision disposing of the application under statutory provisions.	0	2026 LexGuard (MHC) 717A9F06	{"court": "Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)", "title": "Mohammad Jafar v. The State Rep. By, The Inspector of Police", "case_id": "717a9f06-8bfe-47f5-9fc3-c5239622a9a2", "case_no": "NO. 30797 of 2026", "parties": "Mohammad Jafar v. The State Rep. By, The Inspector of Police", "category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "petitioner": "Mohammad Jafar", "respondent": "The State Rep. By, The