of certiorari or any other\nappropriate writ, order to quash and set aside the Order dated 22/11/2022\npassed in Criminal Case No. OA/484/2022/E.\nc) Grant a writ of certiorari or a writ in the nature of certiorari or any other\nappropriate writ, order to quash and set aside the Order dated 9/01/2026\npassed in Criminal Mis. Application No. 229/2025.”\n3. Mr A. Phadte, learned Advocate for the Petitioner, on instructions\nfrom the Petitioner, seeks leave to withdraw this petition.\n4. In view of the statement made by Mr A. Phadte, learned Advocate\nfor the Petitioner, leave is granted to withdraw this Petition.\n5. Criminal Writ Petition No. 133 of 2026 is dismissed as withdrawn.\nASHWIN D. BHOBE, J.\n2 / 2\n23rd October,2026	2	t	HCMA010001332026	Judicial Precedent	2026-10-23	Single Bench	SARVESH AIKAR ... PETITIONER	Respondent / State	\N	\N	Civil & Constitutional Law	In this matter, the High Court of Judicature adjudicated issues between Petitioner and Respondent / State. The Court rendered a decision disposing of the application under statutory provisions.	0	2026 LexGuard (HC) 82A1FB3D	{"court": "High Court of Judicature", "title": "Petitioner v. Respondent / State", "case_id": "82a1fb3d-4708-407b-92e1-b51a04b4e8cc", "case_no": "NO. 133 OF 2026", "parties": "Petitioner v. Respondent / State", "category": "QUASHING", "petitioner": "Petitioner", "respondent": "Respondent / State", "legal_domain": "Civil & Constitutional Law", "category_facts": {"category": "QUASHING", "civil_cloak": false, "quash_ground": "Absence of prima facie ingredients", "compromise_affidavit": false, "bhajan_lal_principles": true, "proceedings_challenged": "First Information Report (FIR)"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "scenario_keywords": [], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Dismissed", "disposition": "Dismissed"}}
87243de9-ed2e-4b8e-853c-391f94198e22	NO. 21934 of 2026	JAYDEV DASRATHBHAI PATEL v. STATE OF GUJARAT	High Court of Gujarat at Ahmedabad	2026	Hon'ble Mr. Justice Hasmukh D. Suthar	JAYDEV DASRATHBHAI PATEL v. STATE OF GUJARAT	["Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023", "Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023", "the BNS"]	["Section 318(3) the BNS", "Section 319(2) the BNS", "Section 336(2) the BNS", "Section 336(3) the BNS", "Section 338 the BNS", "Section 340(2) the BNS", "Section 61(2) the BNS"]	["civil & constitutional law", "ghc", "disposed"]	Judgment in NO. 21934 of 2026 delivered by HONOURABLE MR. JUSTICE HASMUKH D. SUTHAR at High Court of Gujarat at Ahmedabad. The proceedings concern Civil & Constitutional Law with respect to Article 21.	Disposed	storage/cases/GHC_2026_NO_21934_of_2026_87243de9.pdf	7722caab08a4936f3799cb9d52f2a27fc19f254226af91175f479aa59a2891fd	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 14:51:36.896259+05:30	2026-10-25 14:51:36.896264+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:09.556408+05:30	[1.0] RULE. Learned APP waives service of rule for the respondent-State. [2.0] The present application is filed under Section 483 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 (for short “BNSS”) for regular bail in connection with FIR being C.R. No. 11216001260411 of 2026 registered with Adalaj Police Station, Gandhinagar, for the offences under Sections 318(3), 319(2), 61(2), 336(2), 336(3), 338, 340(2) of the BNS . [3.0] Learned advocate appearing on behalf of the applicant submits that the applicant is innocent and has been falsely implicated in the offence. Nothing is required to be recovered or discovered. He therefore submits that, considering the nature of the offence, the applicant may be enlarged on regular bail by imposing suitable conditions. [4.0] Learned APP appearing on behalf of the respondent-State has opposed the present application and requested to dismiss the present application for regular bail on the ground that the Page 1 of 5 R/CR.MA/21934/2026 ORDER DATED: 22/	[]	Holding and principles affirmed in NO. 21934 of 2026 regarding Civil & Constitutional Law. The Court adjudicated upon Article 21.	[]	Disposed	[]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding Precedent)	R/CR.MA/21934/2026 ORDER DATED: 22/10/2026\n(GJHC240700502026)\nIN THE HIGH COURT OF GUJARAT AT AHMEDABAD\nR/CRIMINAL MISC.APPLICATION (FOR SUCCESSIVE REGULAR BAIL - AFTER\nCHARGESHEET) NO. 21934 of 2026\n==========================================================\nJAYDEV DASRATHBHAI PATEL\nVersus\nSTATE OF GUJARAT\n==========================================================\nAppearance:\nMR RJ GOSWAMI(1102) for the Applicant(s) No. 1\nMR PRADIP K VALERA(13303) for the Respondent(s) No. 1\nMR VIMAL A PUROHIT(5049) for the Respondent(s) No. 1\nMR YUVRAJ BRAHMBHATT, APP for the Respondent(s) No. 1\n==========================================================\nCORAM:HONOURABLE MR. JUSTICE HASMUKH D. SUTHAR\nDate : 22/10/2026\nORDER\n[1.0] RULE. Learned APP waives service of rule for the\nrespondent-State.\n[2.0] The present application is filed under Section 483 of the\nBharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 (for short “BNSS”) for\nregular bail in connection with FIR being C.R. No.\n11216001260411 of 2026 registered with Adalaj Police\nStation, Gandhinagar, for the offences under Sections 318(3),\n319(2), 61(2), 336(2), 336(3), 338, 340(2) of the BNS .\n[3.0] Learned advocate appearing on behalf of the applicant\nsubmits that the applicant is innocent and has been falsely\nimplicated in the offence. Nothing is required to be recovered or\ndiscovered. He therefore submits that, considering the nature of\nthe offence, the applicant may be enlarged on regular bail by\nimposing suitable conditions.\n[4.0] Learned APP appearing on behalf of the respondent-State\nhas opposed the present application and requested to dismiss\nthe present application for regular bail on the ground that the\nPage 1 of 5\n\nR/CR.MA/21934/2026 ORDER DATED: 22/10/2026\n(GJHC240700502026)\napplicant is very much involved in the present offence and\nconsidering the gravity of offence, no case is made out to\nentertain present bail application. Therefore, present application\ndoes not deserve consideration.\n[4.1] The learned advocate for the applicant has opposed the\npresent application and submitted that, considering the gravity\nof the offence, no case is made out for entertaining the present\napplication. In this regard, he has filed an affidavit, which is\ntaken on record.\n[5.0] While granting bail, the Court has to consider the\ninvolvement of the accused in the alleged offence, the jurisdiction\nto grant bail has to be exercised on the basis of the well settled\nprinciples having regard to the facts and circumstances of each\ncase and the following factors are to be taken into consideration\nwhile considering an application for bail: (i) the nature of\naccusation and the severity of the punishment and the nature of\nthe materials relied upon by the prosecution; (ii) reasonable\napprehension of tampering with the witnesses and threat to the\ncomplainant or the witnesses; (iii) reasonable possibility of\nsecuring the presence of the accused at the time of trial or the\nlikelihood of his abscondence; (iv) character behaviour and\nstanding of the accused and the circumstances which are\npeculiar to the accused; (v) larger interest of the public or the\nState and similar other considerations are required to be\nconsidered.\n[6.0] I have heard the learned advocates appearing on behalf of\nthe respective parties and perused the investigation papers.\nPage 2 of 5\n\nR/CR.MA/21934/2026 ORDER DATED: 22/10/2026\n(GJHC240700502026)\nFollowing aspects have been considered:\n(1) Investigation is over and charge-sheet is filed;\n(2) Applicant is behind the bar since 29.07.2026;\n(3) There is nothing to be recovered or discovered from\nthe applicant;\n(4) Offence is triable by JMFC Court and none of the\noffence alleged is punishable with life sentence or death\npenalty ;\n(5) The present applicant is not named in the FIR;\nhowever, his name surfaced during the course of the\ninvestigation. It is alleged that the present applicant\nimpersonated the mother-in-law of the original landowner,\nnamely, Jagrutiben, and filed an objection against the\nissuance of the Patta Grant Certificate in respect of the\nproperty. Except for raising the objection under an\nassumed identity, no other role is attributed to the present\napplicant. The case is based on documentary evidence.\n(6) No past antecedent;\n(7) Prosecution has failed to point out the circumstances\nto continue or prolong his incarceration;\n(8) Obviously commencement and conclusion of trial will\ntake its own time.\n[7.0] This Court has also taken into consideration the law laid\ndown by the Hon'ble Apex Court in the case of Sanjay Chandra\nvs. Central Bureau of Investigation reported in [2012]1 SCC\n40 as well as in the case of Gudikanti Narasimhulu And Ors\nvs. Public Prosecutor, High Court of Andhra Pradesh\nreported in (1978)1 SCC 240. Obviously, the conclusion of trial\nPage 3 of 5\n\nR/CR.MA/21934/2026 ORDER DATED: 22/10/2026\n(GJHC240700502026)\nwill take time and keeping the accused behind the bars is\nnothing but amounts to pre-trial conviction and therefore,\nconsidering the celebrated principle of bail jurisprudence is that\n“bail is a rule and jail is exception” as well as the concept of\npersonal liberty guaranteed under Article 21 of the Constitution\nof India, present application deserves consideration.\n[8.0] In the facts and circumstances of the case and considering\nthe nature of the allegations made against the applicant/s in the\nFIR, without discussing the evidence in detail, prima facie, this\nCourt is of the opinion that this is a fit case to exercise the\ndiscretion and enlarge the applicant/s on regular bail. Hence,\nthe present application is allowed. The applicant/s is/are\nordered to be released on regular bail in connection with FIR\nbeing C.R. No. 11216001260411 of 2026 registered with\nAdalaj Police Station, Gandhinagar, on executing a personal\nbond of Rs.25,000/- (Rupees Twenty-five Thousand only) with\none surety of the like amount to the satisfaction of the trial\nCourt and subject to the conditions that he/she/they shall;\n(a) not take undue advantage of liberty or misuse liberty;\n(b) not act in a manner injurious to the interest of the\nprosecution & shall not obstruct or hamper the police\ninvestigation and shall not to play mischief with the evidence\ncollected or yet to be collected by the police;\n(c) surrender passport, if any, to the Trial Court within a week;\n(d) not leave the State of Gujarat without prior permission of the\nTrial Court concerned;\n(e) mark presence before the concerned Police Station once in\nevery month for a period of six months between 11.00 a.m.\nPage 4 of 5\n\nR/CR.MA/21934/2026 ORDER DATED: 22/10/2026\n(GJHC240700502026)\nand 2.00 p.m.;\n(f) furnish the Aadhaar card, email ID/present address of\nhis residence to the Investigating Officer and also to the\nCourt at the time of execution of the bond and shall not\nchange the residence/contact number without prior\npermission of Trial Court;\n(g) not to indulge in any illegal activity failing which learned trial\nCourt shall issue warrant and cancel the bail of the\napplicant.\n[9.0] The authorities will release the applicant only if he/they\nis/are not required in connection with any other offence for the\ntime being. If breach of any of the above conditions is committed,\nthe Sessions Judge concerned will be free to issue warrant or\ntake appropriate action in the matter.\n[10.0] Bail bond to be executed before the lower Court having\njurisdiction to try the case. It will be open for the concerned\nCourt to delete, modify and/or relax any of the above conditions,\nin accordance with law.\n[11.0] At the trial, the trial Court shall not be influenced by the\nobservations of preliminary nature qua the evidence at this stage\nmade by this Court while enlarging the applicant/applicants on\nbail.\n[12.0] Rule is made absolute to the aforesaid extent. Direct\nservice is permitted.\n(HASMUKH D. SUTHAR,J)\nALI\nPage 5 of 5	5	t	GJHC240700502026	Judicial Precedent	2026-10-22	Single Bench	JAYDEV DASRATHBHAI PATEL	STATE OF GUJARAT	\N	\N	Civil & Constitutional Law	In this matter, the High Court of Gujarat at Ahmedabad adjudicated issues between JAYDEV DASRATHBHAI PATEL and STATE OF GUJARAT. The Court rendered a decision disposing of the application under statutory provisions.	0	2026 LexGuard (GHC) 87243DE9	{"court": "High Court of Gujarat at Ahmedabad", "title": "JAYDEV DASRATHBHAI PATEL v. STATE OF GUJARAT", "case_id": "87243de9-ed2e-4b8e-853c-391f94198e22", "case_no": "NO. 21934 of 2026", "parties": "JAYDEV DASRATHBHAI PATEL v. STATE OF GUJARAT", "category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "petitioner": "JAYDEV DASRATHBHAI PATEL", "respondent": "STATE OF GUJARAT", "legal_domain": "Civil & Constitutional Law", "category_facts": {"category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "surety_count": null, "custody_period": null, "application_type": "Regular Bail", "bail_bond_amount": 25000.0, "trial_court_fine": null, "reason_for_relief": null, "reporting_condition": null, "trial_court_sentence": null, "trial_court_fine_paid": false}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "CONSTITUTION", "section": "21", "canonical": "Section 21 CONSTITUTION", "equivalents": []}], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["constitutional_fundamental"], "scenario_keywords": ["article 21"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Disposed", "disposition": "Disposed"}}
