 REFERRED:\nEnvironment (Protection) Act 1986, National Green Tribunal Act 2010\n\nSECTIONS & CLAUSES UNDER CONSIDERATION:\nEPA Section 3, NGT Act Section 14, Article 21\n\nKEY LEGAL DOMAINS & TOPICS:\nEnvironmental Clearance, Ex-post Facto Clearance, Precautionary Principle, Sustainable Development, Polluter Pays\n\n================================================================================\n                                   JUDGMENT\n================================================================================\n\n1. FACTUAL MATRIX:\nChallenge to MoEFCC notifications granting ex-post facto environmental clearance to mining and infrastructure projects constructed in violation of EIA 2006.\n\n2. ISSUES FRAMED FOR ADJUDICATION:\n1. Can ex-post facto environmental clearance be granted derogating from the Precautionary Principle under Article 21?\n\n3. STATUTORY FRAMEWORK & PRECEDENTS:\nReaffirming Alembic Pharmaceuticals: Ex-post facto environmental clearance is contrary to environmental jurisprudence and the mandate of EPA 1986.\n\n4. RATIO DECIDENDI:\nEx-post facto environmental clearance is alien to environmental jurisprudence; any project commenced without prior environmental clearance is illegal.\n\n5. FINAL DISPOSITION & ORDER:\nCivil Appeal Allowed; Ex-post facto Environmental Clearances declared invalid; Precautionary Principle enforced.\n\nDecreed and ordered accordingly.\n\nHon'ble Justice Abhay S. Oka & Justice Ujjal Bhuyan\nSupreme Court of India\n[LEXGUARD CRYPTOGRAPHIC VERIFIED REPOSITORY RECORD]\n	3	t	SCIN010045122026	Civil Appeal	\N	Single Bench	M/s Green Earth Foundation	Union of India & Ors.	\N	\N	Civil Law	In this civil appeal under Civil Law, the Court ruled on the dispute between M/s Green Earth Foundation and Union of India & Ors..	0	2026 LexGuard (Civi) 9DF545	{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "M/s Green Earth Foundation v. Union of India & Ors.", "case_id": "9df5457e-cc7c-4011-b1f7-041d8057b893", "case_no": "Civil Appeal No. 