\n15.\nPage 9 of 10\n19th October, 2026\n\nWP 207-2026\n16.\n17.\n18.\n19.\nPage 10 of 10\n19th October, 2026	10	t	HCMA010321882026	Matter	2026-10-23	Single Bench	Petitioner	Respondent / State	\N	\N	Civil & Constitutional Law	In this matter, the High Court of Judicature adjudicated issues between Petitioner and Respondent / State. The Court rendered a decision disposing of the application under statutory provisions.	0	2026 LexGuard (HC) DD619AFF	{"court": "High Court of Judicature", "title": "Petitioner v. Respondent / State", "case_id": "dd619aff-0a05-413c-b1b5-7ca4a0fa81dc", "case_no": "Matter No. 32188D26/2026", "parties": "Petitioner v. Respondent / State", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Petitioner", "respondent": "Respondent / State", "legal_domain": "Civil & Constitutional Law", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Writ Petition / Criminal Appeal", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "scenario_keywords": [], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Disposed", "disposition": "Disposed"}}
e2067dc5-62e4-4b54-b6f4-891c9e2333fd	CRL OP No. 30935 of 2026	. Ushanandhini v. The State Rep by the Inspector of Police, Mayiladuthurai Police Station, Mayiladuthurai District.	Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)	2026	Hon'ble Mr. Justice N.Ramesh	. Ushanandhini v. The State Rep by the Inspector of Police, Mayiladuthurai Police Station, Mayiladuthurai District.	["Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023", "the BNS"]	["Section 103 the BNS"]	["civil & constitutional law", "mhc", "disposed"]	Judgment in NO. 30935 of 2026 delivered by Hon'ble Bench at Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai). The proceedings concern Civil & Constitutional Law with respect to procedural compliance.	Bail Granted	storage/cases/MHC_2026_NO_30935_of_2026_e2067dc5.pdf	cc25adc189fb6dbb9513ae8cc90504e6371c2fefe87a612ae859a1afb7882994	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 14:51:29.794613+05:30	2026-10-25 14:51:29.794616+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:09.571086+05:30	2. The case of the prosecution is that on 06.09.2026 at about 05:00 P.M., the petitioners, arrayed as A2 and A4, along with other co-accused, visited the __________ Page1 of 4 https://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis residence of the de facto complainant and demanded an exorbitant interest amount of Rs.40,000/-. When the transaction was questioned by A3 (an individual present at the scene), the prime accused A1 abused her in filthy language and assaulted her. Subsequently, the accused persons attacked the de facto complainant’s husband with deadly weapons, including an iron rod and a wooden log, inflicting fatal injuries which resulted in his death. Hence, the case. 3.	[]	Regular bail granted in CRL OP No. 30935 of 2026 under applicable statutory provisions, considering the duration of incarceration and absence of flight risk, subject to executing bond and standard reporting conditions.	[]	Allowed	["ble Supreme Court in P.K.Shaji vs. State of Kerala [(2005)13 SCC 283]"]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding Precedent)	IN THE HIGH COURT OF JUDICATURE AT MADRAS\nDATED: 23.10.2026\nCORAM\nTHE HON'BLE MR.JUSTICE N.RAMESH\nCRL.O.P.No.30935 of 2026\nCNR: {HCMA012565642026}\n1. Monika\n2. Ushanandhini\n..Petitioners\nVs\nThe State Rep by the Inspector of Police,\nMayiladuthurai Police Station, Mayiladuthurai\nDistrict.\nCrime No.817 of 2026.\n..Respondent\nPRAYER: To enlarge the petitioners on bail in Crime No.817 of 2026 on the\nfile of the Respondent Police.\nFor Petitioners : Mr.S.Esakkimuthu\nFor Respondent : Mrs.R.S.Indira,\nAdditional Public Prosecutor.\nORDER\nThe petitioners, who were arrested and remanded to judicial custody on\n07.09.2026 for the alleged offences punishable under Sections 296(b), 118(1),\n103, 351(3), and 61(2) of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS), 2023, Section 4\nof the Tamil Nadu Prohibition of Harassment of Women (TNPHW) Act, and\nSection 4 of the Tamil Nadu Prohibition of Charging Exorbitant Interest Act,\n2003, in Crime No. 817 of 2026 on the file of the respondent police, seek bail.\n2. The case of the prosecution is that on 06.09.2026 at about 05:00 P.M.,\nthe petitioners, arrayed as A2 and A4, along with other co-accused, visited the\n__________\nPage1 of 4\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\nresidence of the de facto complainant and demanded an exorbitant interest\namount of Rs.40,000/-. When the transaction was questioned by A3 (an\nindividual present at the scene), the prime accused A1 abused her in filthy\nlanguage and assaulted her. Subsequently, the accused persons attacked the de\nfacto complainant’s husband with deadly weapons, including an iron rod and a\nwooden log, inflicting fatal injuries which resulted in his death. Hence, the case.\n3. The learned counsel appearing for the petitioners submitted that the\npetitioners are innocent of the allegations and have been falsely implicated in\nthis case. He further submitted that the primary overt acts of physical assault\nand abuse are directed only against the co-accused A1. He contended that the\npetitioners have been languishing in judicial custody since 07.09.2026 and are\nready to abide by any stringent conditions imposed by this Court.\n4. The learned Additional Public Prosecutor appearing for the respondent\nstate strongly opposed the grant of bail, highlighting the shocking and heinous\nnature of the offense where an innocent man lost his life over an illegal loan\nsharking demand. She submitted that the petitioners were actively present at the\nscene of the occurrence, acting in furtherance of a common intention to assault\nthe victim with weapons. She, however, admitted that the principal overt act of\ncausing the fatal injuries is attributed to A1.\n5. Hearing the learned counsel on either side and perusing the materials\navailable on record, it is observed that while the accusation involves a grave\n__________\nPage2 of 4\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\ncharge of murder under Section 103 of the BNS, the primary and specific overt\nacts of delivering the fatal blows are directed against the co-accused A1.\nConsidering the role attributed to the present petitioners (A2 and A4), the period\nof incarceration undergone by them from 07.09.2026, and the fact that the\nsubstantial part of the investigation has already been carried out, this Court is\ninclined to grant bail to the petitioners.\n6. Accordingly, the petitioners are ordered to be released on bail on their\nexecuting a bond for a sum of Rs.25,000/- (Rupees Twenty Five Thousand\nonly) with two sureties, each for a like sum to the satisfaction of the learned\nDistrict and Sessions Judge, Mayiladuthurai, and on further conditions that:\n[a] the sureties shall affix their photographs and Left\nThumb Impression in the Application for Surety ship\n[Judicial Form No.46 annexed to 'The Criminal Rules of\nPractice, 2019']. The learned Magistrate shall obtain a copy\nof any one of the identity proofs to ensure their identity;\n[b] the petitioners shall report before the respondent police\ndaily at 10:30 A.M. until further orders;\n[c] the petitioners shall not abscond either during\ninvestigation or trial;\n[d] the petitioners shall not tamper with the evidence or\nwitness either during investigation or trial;\n[e] On breach of any of the aforementioned conditions, the\nlearned Magistrate/Trial Court is entitled to pass appropriate\norders against the petitioners in accordance with law as if the\naforementioned conditions have been imposed and the\n__________\nPage3 of 4\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\nN.RAMESH, J.\nkmm\npetitioners released on bail by the learned Magistrate/Trial\nCourt himself as laid down by the Hon'ble Supreme Court in\nP.K.Shaji vs. State of Kerala [(2005)13 SCC 283];\n[f] If the accused thereafter absconds, a fresh FIR can be\nregistered under Section 269 of B.N.S.\n23.10.2026\nkmm\nNote:\n1. Registry is directed to forthwith upload\nthis order in the Official Website of this\nCourt.\n2. All concerned to act on this order being\nuploaded in Official Website of this Court\nwithout insisting on certified hard copies. To\nbe noted, this order when uploaded in the\nofficial website of this Court will be\nwatermarked and will also have a QR code.\nTo\n1. The District and Sessions Judge,,\nMayiladuthurai.\n2. The Superintendent,\nSub Jail, Mayiladuthurai.\n3. The Inspector of Police,\nMayiladuthurai Police Station,\nMayiladuthurai District.\n4. The Public Prosecutor, High Court of\nMadras.\nCRL.O.P.No.30935 of 2026\n__________\nPage4 of 4\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis	4	t	HCMA012565642026	Criminal Original Petition (Bail)	2026-10-23	Single Bench	. Ushanandhini	The State Rep by the Inspector of Police, Mayiladuthurai Police Station, Mayiladuthurai District.	Mr.S.Esakkimuthu	Mrs.R.S.Indira, Additional Public Prosecutor.	Civil & Constitutional Law	In this matter, the Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai) adjudicated issues between 2. Ushanandhini and The State Rep by the Inspector of Police, Mayiladuthurai Police Station, Mayiladuthurai. The Court rendered a decision disposing of the application under statutory provisions.	0	2026 LexGuard (MHC) E2067DC5	{"court": "Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)", "title": "Monika & Ushanandhini v. State Rep. by Inspector of Police, Mayiladuthurai", "case_id": "e2067dc5-62e4-4b54-b6f4-891c9e2333fd", "case_no": "CRL OP No. 30935 of 2026", "parties": "Monika & Ushanandhini v. State Rep. by Inspector of Police, Mayiladuthurai", "category": "QUASHING", "petitioner": "Monika & Ushanandhini", "respondent": "State Rep. by Inspector of Police, Mayiladuthurai", "legal_domain": "Civil & Constitutional Law", "category_facts": {"category": "QUASHING", "civil_cloak": false, "quash_ground": "Absence of prima facie ingredients", "compromise_affidavit": false, "bhajan_lal_principles": true, "proceedings_challenged": "First Information Report (FIR)"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "scenario_keywords": [], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Disposed", "disposition": "Disposed"}}
e508aceb-ea22-4964-a841-de2afd5572aa	CRL OP No. 30761 of 2026	Surya v. The State Rep. by, The Inspector of Police Thiruttani Police Station, Thiruvallur District.	Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)	2026	Hon'ble Mr. Justice N.Ramesh	Surya v. The State Rep. by, The Inspector of Police Thiruttani Police Station, Thiruvallur District.	["Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023", "of BNS"]	["Section 109(1) of BNS", "Section 118(1) of BNS", "Section 126(2) of BNS", "Section 296(b) of BNS", "Section 351(3) of BNS"]	["civil & constitutional law", "mhc", "disposed"]	Judgment in NO. 30761 of 2026 delivered by Hon'ble Bench at Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai). The proceedings concern Civil & Constitutional Law with respect to procedural compliance.	Bail Granted	storage/cases/MHC_2026_NO_30761_of_2026_e508aceb.pdf	47fb672fea46350dab145b24389014f6e427c47ebe9ccef40457f36b5622ebf4	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 14:51:45.426231+05:30	2026-10-25 14:51:45.42624+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:09.571763+05:30	2. The case of the prosecution is that, due to previous enmity, the petitioner along with other accused abused and assaulted the defacto complainant’s son with knife, thereby causing injuries to him. Hence, the case. 3.	[]	Regular bail granted in CRL OP No. 30761 of 2026 under applicable statutory provisions, considering the duration of incarceration and absence of flight risk, subject to executing bond and standard reporting conditions.	[]	Allowed	["ble Supreme Court in P.K.Shaji vs. State of Kerala [(2005)13 SCC 283]"]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding Precedent)	CRL OP No. 30761 of 2026\nIN THE HIGH COURT OF JUDICATURE AT MADRAS\nDATED: 23-10-2026\nCORAM\nTHE HON'BLE MR.JUSTICE N.RAMESH\nCRL OP No. 30761 of 2026\nCNR: {HCMA012563162026}\nSurya\nS/o Ramesh,\nNo.62, Anjineyar Koil Street,\nKasinathapuram, Pattabiramapuram,\nThiruvallur District.\n..Petitioner(s)\nVs\nThe State Rep. by, The Inspector of Police\nThiruttani Police Station,\nThiruvallur District.\nCrime No.492 of 2026.\n..Respondent(s)\nPRAYER : Criminal Original Petition filed under Section 483 of Bharatiya\nNagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023, to enlarge the Petitioner on bail pending\ninvestigation in Cr.No.492 of 2026 on the file of Respondent Police and thus\nrender justice.\nFor Petitioner(s): Mr.V.Manimaran\nFor Respondent(s): Mr.M.M.I. Khaleel\nGovernment Advocate (Crl.Side)\nORDER\nThe petitioner, who was arrested and remanded to judicial custody on\n09.09.2026 for the alleged offences under Sections 126(2), 296(b), 118(1),\n351(3) and 109(1) of BNS in Crime No.492 of 2026 on the file of the\nrespondent police, seeks bail.\n__________\nPage1 of 6\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\nCRL OP No. 30761 of 2026\n2. The case of the prosecution is that, due to previous enmity, the\npetitioner along with other accused abused and assaulted the defacto\ncomplainant’s son with knife, thereby causing injuries to him. Hence, the case.\n3. The learned counsel appearing for the petitioner submitted that the\npetitioner is innocent and he has been falsely implicated in the present case and\nthat he has not committed any offence as alleged by the prosecution. He\nfurther submitted that the petitioner has been in judicial custody since\n09.09.2026 and that he is ready to abide by any conditions that may be imposed\nby this Court. Hence, he prays for grant of bail to the Petitioner.\n4. Per contra, the learned Government Advocate (Criminal Side)\nappearing for the Respondent Police, while opposing the grant of bail to the\npetitioner, reiterated the prosecution case and, on instructions, submitted that\nthe petitioner has 10 previous cases pending against him and that the injured has\nalready been discharged from the hospital. However, he opposed the grant of\nbail to the Petitioners.\n5. At this juncture, the learned counsel for the petitioner submitted that\nGnanaprakash and Sakthivel, have already been granted anticipatory bail by the\nSessions Court. He further submitted that, out of the ten previous cases\nregistered against the petitioner, he has been acquitted in four cases.\n__________\nPage2 of 6\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\nCRL OP No. 30761 of 2026\n6. I have given my anxious consideration to either side submissions and\nperused the materials available on record.\n7. From the submissions made by the learned Government Advocate\n(Crl.Side), it is seen that though the petitioner has 10 previous cases pending\nagainst him, in most of the cases, he was granted bail. Considering the nature\nof the allegations levelled against the petitioner, the period of incarceration\nalready undergone, the fact that the injured was already discharged from the\nhospital and that the co-accused have already been released on bail by the\nSessions Court, this Court is inclined to enlarge the petitioner on bail subject to\ncertain conditions.\n8. Accordingly, the petitioner is ordered to be released on bail on his\nexecuting a bond for a sum of Rs.25,000/- (Rupees Twenty Five Thousand\nonly) with two sureties each, for a like sum to the satisfaction of the learned\nJudicial Magistrate, Thiruttani, and on further conditions that:\n[a] the sureties shall affix their photographs and Left\nThumb Impression in the Application for Surety ship\n[Judicial Form No.46 annexed to 'The Criminal Rules of\nPractice, 2019']. The learned Magistrate shall obtain a copy\nof any one of the identity proofs to ensure their identity;\n[b] the petitioner shall report before the respondent\npolice daily at 10.30 a.m., until further orders;\n__________\nPage3 of 6\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\nCRL OP No. 30761 of 2026\n[c] the petitioner shall not abscond either during\ninvestigation or trial;\n[d] the petitioner shall not tamper with the evidence or\nwitness either during investigation or trial;\n[e] On breach of any of the aforementioned conditions,\nthe learned Magistrate/Trial Court is entitled to pass\nappropriate orders against the petitioner in accordance with\nlaw as if the aforementioned conditions have been imposed\nand the petitioner released on bail by the learned\nMagistrate/Trial Court himself as laid down by the Hon'ble\nSupreme Court in P.K.Shaji vs. State of Kerala [(2005)13\nSCC 283];\n[f] If the accused thereafter absconds, a fresh FIR can be\nregistered under Section 269 of B.N.S.\n23-10-2026\nDRL\nNote:\n1. Registry is directed to forthwith upload\nthis order in the Official Website of this\nCourt.\n2. All concerned to act on this order being\nuploaded in Official Website of this Court\nwithout insisting on certified hard copies. To\nbe noted, this order when uploaded in the\nofficial website of this Court will be\nwatermarked and will also have a QR code.\n__________\nPage4 of 6\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\nCRL OP No. 30761 of 2026\nTo\n1.The Judicial Magistrate,\nThiruttani.\n2. The Inspector of Police\nThiruttani Police Station,\nThiruvallur District.\n3.The Superintendent,\nCentral Prison, Puzhal.\n4.The Public Prosecutor,\nHigh Court, Madras.\n__________\nPage5 of 6\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\nCRL OP No. 30761 of 2026\nN.RAMESH, J.\nDRL\nCRL OP No. 30761 of 2026\n23-10-2026\n__________\nPage6 of 6\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis	6	t	HCMA012563162026	Criminal Original Petition (Bail)	2026-10-23	Single Bench	Surya	The State Rep. by, The Inspector of Police Thiruttani Police Station, Thiruvallur District.	Mr.V.Manimaran	Mr.M.M.I. Khaleel Government Advocate (Crl.Side)	Civil & Constitutional Law	In this matter, the Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai) adjudicated issues between Thiruvallur District and The State Rep. by, The Inspector of Police Thiruttani Police Station. The Court rendered a decision disposing of the application under statutory provisions.	0	2026 LexGuard (MHC) E508ACEB	{"court": "Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)", "title": "Thiruvallur District v. The State Rep. by, The Inspector of Police Thiruttani Police Station", "case_id": "e508aceb-ea22-4964-a841-de2afd5572aa", "case_no": "NO. 30761 of 2026", "parties": "Thiruvallur District v. The State Rep. by, The Inspector of Police Thiruttani Police Station", "category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "petitioner": "Thiruvallur District", "respondent": "The State Rep. by, The Inspector of Police Thiruttani Police Station", "legal_domain": "Civil & Constitutional Law", "category_facts": {"category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "surety_count": 2, "custody_period": null, "application_type": "Anticipatory Bail", "bail_bond_amount": 25000.0, "trial_court_fine": null, "reason_for_relief": "Parity with co-accused granted bail", "reporting_condition": "report before the respondent", "trial_court_sentence": null, "trial_court_fine_paid": false}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "scenario_keywords": [], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Disposed", "disposition": "Disposed"}}
e52d41cc-ae80-4403-976c-b7f80f762406	NO. 104/2026	GH. MOHD MIR v. UNION TERRITORY OF J AND K TH.COMMR/SECTY TO GOVT(SOCIAL WELFARE) AND OTHERS	High Court of Jammu & Kashmir and Ladakh	2026	Hon’ble Mr. Justice Rajnesh Oswal, Judge	GH. MOHD MIR v. UNION TERRITORY OF J AND K TH.COMMR/SECTY TO GOVT(SOCIAL WELFARE) AND OTHERS	[]	[]	["civil & constitutional law", "jkhc", "dismissed"]	Judgment in NO. 104/2026 delivered by Hon’ble Mr. Justice Rajnesh Oswal, Judge at High Court of Jammu & Kashmir and Ladakh. The proceedings concern Civil & Constitutional Law with respect to procedural compliance.	Quashed	storage/cases/JKHC_2026_NO_104_2026_e52d41cc.pdf	87a7a58b2273b47374d3c188acf2788991b70c4b84993ee63f3da49ad4d874c5	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 14:51:58.115997+05:30	2026-10-25 14:51:58.116004+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:09.572658+05:30	\N	[]	Criminal proceedings quashed in NO. 104/2026 on finding that allegations do not disclose a prima facie cognizable offence, preventing abuse of the judicial process.	[]	Allowed	[]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding Precedent)	14\nRegular\nIN THE HIGH COURT OF JAMMU & KASHMIR AND LADAKH\nAT SRINAGAR\nWP(C) No. 104/2026\nGH. MOHD MIR ….. Petitioner(s)\nThrough: Mr. Ishtiyaq Ahmad Mir, Adv.\nV/s\nUNION TERRITORY OF J AND K TH.COMMR/SECTY TO\nGOVT(SOCIAL WELFARE) AND OTHERS ….. Respondent(s)\nThrough: Mr. T A Lone, Dy. AG\nCoram:\nHon’ble Mr. Justice Rajnesh Oswal, Judge\nORDER(Oral)\n22.10.2026\n1. The petitioner, was appointed as Member, CWC\nBandipora, in terms of order dated 07.02.2018, for a\nperiod of three years. Vide Government Order dated\n27.01.2021, the tenure of existing Members of CWCs was\nextended for a further period of one year and thereafter in\nterms of Government Order dated 24.06.2022, the tenure\nwas further extended till fresh recruitment is made.\n2. The petitioner further claims that pursuant to Order dated\n29.03.2021, he was directed to hold the charge of\nChairperson, Child Welfare Committee, Bandipora and\n\nP age | 2\nWP(C) No. 104/2026\nhe continued to discharge his duty as Incharge\nChairperson without any break till 13.04.2023 i.e, the\ndate on which the newly recruited Members/Chairperson\nof the Child Welfare Committee were appointed.\n3. The petitioner by placing these facts before this Court, has\nsought a direction to the respondents to release the\npending remuneration/honorarium payable to the\npetitioner for the period he worked as Member, Child\nWelfare Committee, Bandipora and further to release the\ncharge allowance payable to the petitioner for the period\nhe functioned as Incharge Chairperson, CWC Bandipora\nalong with interest i.e, from 29.03.2021 till 13.04.2023.\n4. The respondents in turn have come up with the response\nstating therein that last extension was granted to the\nMembers as well as Chairperson of CWC up to\n24.08.2022 and thereafter no further extension was\ngranted. Rather, fresh Advertisement Notice dated\n01.08.2022 came to be issued for filling up the post of\nMembers of Juvenile Justice Board and\n\nP age | 3\nWP(C) No. 104/2026\nChairperson/Members of Child Welfare Committee in\neach District of UT of J&K.\n5. Members/Chairperson (97 in number) challenged the said\nAdvertisement Notice in WP(C) No. 1742/2022 titled Dr.\nKhair-ul-Nisa and Ors. Vs. UT of Jammu and Kashmir and\nothers and some members also filed another writ petition\nbearing WP(C) No. 1748/2022 titled Shalini Sharma and\nOthers Vs. UT of Jammu and Kashmir and others before the\nJammu Wing of this Court and vide order dated\n23.08.2023, both the writ petitions mentioned above were\ndismissed. It is further stated that claim of the petitioners\nwas considered by the Department and the same was\nrejected vide order dated 13.12.2024. It is further stated\nthat whatever remuneration due to the petitioner for the\nperiod he worked, prior to the termination of his contract,\nhas been paid to him and he has no right of remuneration\nfor the period he has not worked.\n6. Mr. Ishtiyaq Ahmad Mir, learned counsel appearing for\nthe petitioner has placed reliance upon the judgment dated\n15th of May, 2026, passed by the coordinate Bench of this\n\nP age | 4\nWP(C) No. 104/2026\nCourt in WP (C) No. 1496 of 2025, to submit that\npetitioners No. 30, 31 and 32 therein, were working as\nMembers of the Child Welfare Committee (CWC),\nBandipora, and the petitioner herein, who was working as\nIn-charge Chairperson of CWC, Bandipora, therefore, the\npetitioner is required to be treated similarly. He has\nfurther submitted that the Consideration Order, as\nmentioned by the respondents in their response, whereby\nthe claims of the other petitioners in the aforesaid Writ\npetition were rejected, has already been quashed by the\ncoordinate Bench of this Court in terms of the judgment\ndated 15th of May, 2026, passed in the case supra.\n7. Per contra, Mr. Tariq Ahmad Lone, learned Deputy\nAdvocate General, appearing for the respondents, has\nsubmitted that, there was no extension of the petitioner's\nengagement beyond 24th August, 2022, as such no relief\ncan be granted to him. He has also produced the record\ndemonstrating the work done by the petitioner from 24th\nAugust, 2022, to 3rd March, 2023. The same is taken on\nrecord.\n\nP age | 5\nWP(C) No. 104/2026\n8. Heard learned counsel for the parties and perused the\nrecord.\n9. This Court has examined the response filed by the\nrespondents in the present petition, as also the response\nfiled in WP (C) No. 1496 of 2025. The said writ petition\nwas opposed by the respondents on the same grounds as\nhave been raised in the objections filed in the present\npetition.\n10. It would be apt to observe that petitioner Nos. 30 to 32 in\nWP (C) No. 1496 of 2025, were also working with the\npetitioner herein in CWC, Bandipora, and the\nConsideration Order dated 13th December, 2024, whereby\nthe claims of the petitioners in the aforesaid writ petition\nwas rejected, already stands quashed by the coordinate\nBench of this Court in terms of the judgment dated 15th\nMay, 2026.\n11. In view of the aforesaid position and by applying the\nprinciple of parity, the present writ petition is disposed of\nby directing the respondents to release the\nremuneration/salary in favour of the petitioner w.e.f 25th\n\nP age | 6\nWP(C) No. 104/2026\nAugust, 2022, to 28th March, 2023, at the same rate at\nwhich he was being paid during the period of his\nengagement\n12. In the event the aforesaid remuneration/salary is not\nreleased in favour of the petitioner within a period of two\nmonths from the date of passing of this judgment, the\npetitioner shall be entitled to interest at the rate of 6% per\nannum from the date of filing of the present writ petition\ntill it’s actually realization. So far as the claim of the\npetitioner for charge allowance is concerned, it needs\nto be noted that the petitioner was engaged only on\ncontractual basis and the petitioner has not been able\nto produce any Rule/Regulation that provides for\npayment of charge allowance to the Members who\nworks/officiates as Chairperson of Child Welfare\nCommittee. Accordingly, the same is rejected.\n(Rajnesh Oswal)\nJudge\nSRINAGAR\n22.10.2026\nAasif	6	t	JKHC010001042026	Judicial Precedent	2026-10-23	Single Bench	GH. MOHD MIR	UNION TERRITORY OF J AND K TH.COMMR/SECTY TO GOVT(SOCIAL WELFARE) AND OTHERS	\N	\N	Civil & Constitutional Law	In this matter, the High Court of Jammu & Kashmir and Ladakh adjudicated issues between GH. MOHD MIR and UNION TERRITORY OF J AND K TH.COMMR/SECTY TO GOVT(SOCIAL WELFARE) AND OTHERS. The Court rendered a decision disposing of the application under statutory provisions.	0	2026 LexGuard (JKHC) E52D41CC	{"court": "High Court of Jammu & Kashmir and Ladakh", "title": "GH. MOHD MIR v. UNION TERRITORY OF J AND K TH.COMMR/SECTY TO GOVT(SOCIAL WELFARE) AND OTHERS", "case_id": "e52d41cc-ae80-4403-976c-b7f80f762406", "case_no": "NO. 104/2026", "parties": "GH. MOHD MIR v. UNION TERRITORY OF J AND K TH.COMMR/SECTY TO GOVT(SOCIAL WELFARE) AND OTHERS", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "GH. MOHD MIR", "respondent": "UNION TERRITORY OF J AND K TH.COMMR/SECTY TO GOVT(SOCIAL WELFARE) AND OTHERS", "legal_domain": "Civil & Constitutional Law", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Writ Petition / Criminal Appeal", "judicial_disposition": "Dismissed", "constitutional_article": "Article 226 / Article 21"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "scenario_keywords": [], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Dismissed", "disposition": "Dismissed"}}
e5517b95-ab32-412e-a5d1-d5ff709bdeef	CRL OP No. 30881 of 2026	Vinoth v. State Rep by The Inspector of Police All Women Police Station Thiruthuraipoondi, Tiruvarur District.	Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)	2026	Hon'ble Mr. Justice N.Ramesh	Vinoth v. State Rep by The Inspector of Police All Women Police Station Thiruthuraipoondi, Tiruvarur District.	["Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023", "Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023", "of BNS", "of BNSS"]	["Section 296(b) of BNS", "Section 351(2) of BNS", "Section 528 of BNSS", "Section 69 of BNS"]	["civil & constitutional law", "mhc", "disposed"]	Judgment in NO. 30881 of 2026 delivered by Hon'ble Bench at Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai). The proceedings concern Civil & Constitutional Law with respect to procedural compliance.	Bail Granted	storage/cases/MHC_2026_NO_30881_of_2026_e5517b95.pdf	fb000e55785cae92423500681ef89922e53a17d66473560a7aa7bb57b4ab0be3	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 14:52:05.553788+05:30	2026-10-25 14:52:05.553792+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:09.573109+05:30	2. The case of the prosecution is that initially there was a love affair between the petitioner and the defacto complainant and thereafter on the pretext of marriage, petitioner had involved in physical relationship with the defacto complainant and thereafter refused to marry her. Hence, the complaint. 3.	[]	Regular bail granted in CRL OP No. 30881 of 2026 under applicable statutory provisions, considering the duration of incarceration and absence of flight risk, subject to executing bond and standard reporting conditions.	[]	Allowed	["ble Supreme Court in P.K.Shaji vs. State of Kerala [(2005)13 SCC 283]"]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding Precedent)	CRL OP No. 30881 of 2026\nIN THE HIGH COURT OF JUDICATURE AT MADRAS\nDATED: 23-10-2026\nCORAM\nTHE HON'BLE MR.JUSTICE N.RAMESH\nCRL OP No. 30881 of 2026\nCNR: {HCMA012563952026}\nVinoth\nS/o Veerasamy,\nKeezhatheru, Korukkai Village,\nThiruthuraipooondi Taluk,\nThiruvarur District.\n..Petitioner(s)\nVs\nState Rep by The Inspector of Police\nAll Women Police Station Thiruthuraipoondi,\nTiruvarur District.\nCrime No. 31 of 2026.\n..Respondent(s)\nPRAYER : Criminal Original Petition filed under Section 528 of BNSS praying\nto enlarge the petitioner on bail, pending investigation of the case in Crime\nNo.31 of 2026 on the file of the Respondent and thus render Justice.\nFor Petitioner(s): Mr. M.Vijaya Ragavan\nFor Respondent(s): Mr. M.M.I.Khaleel\nGovernment Advocate (Crl. Side)\nORDER\nThe petitioner, who was arrested and remanded to judicial custody on\n14.09.2026 for the alleged offences punishable under Sections 296(b), 351(2)\nand 69 of BNS, 2023 in Crime No. 31 of 2026 on the file of the respondent\npolice, seeks regular bail.\n__________\nPage1 of 5\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\nCRL OP No. 30881 of 2026\n2. The case of the prosecution is that initially there was a love affair\nbetween the petitioner and the defacto complainant and thereafter on the pretext\nof marriage, petitioner had involved in physical relationship with the defacto\ncomplainant and thereafter refused to marry her. Hence, the complaint.\n3. The learned counsel for the petitioner submits that it is true that there\nwas love affair between the petitioner and the defacto complainant initially,\nhowever subsequently, they broke their relationship and thereafter victim girl\nfell in love with some other boy and that is the reason petitioner part away with\nthe defacto complaiannt. He further undertakes that the petitioner will strictly\nabide by any stringent conditions imposed by this Court and will co-operate\nwith the investigation.\n4. The learned Government Advocate (Criminal Side) appearing for the\nrespondent state strongly opposes the grant of bail.\n5. Heard the learned counsel appearing for the parties and perused the\nmaterials available on records.\n6. Considering the facts and circumstances of the case and the period of\nincarceration undergone by the petitioner from 14.09.2026, this Court is\ninclined to grant bail to the petitioner, subject to following conditions.\n__________\nPage2 of 5\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\nCRL OP No. 30881 of 2026\n7. Accordingly, the petitioner is ordered to be released on bail on his\nexecuting a bond for a sum of Rs.25,000/- (Rupees Twenty Five Thousand\nonly) with two sureties, each for a like sum to the satisfaction of the learned\nJudicial Magistrate, Thiruthuraipoondi, Tiruvarur District, and on further\nconditions that:\n[a] the sureties shall affix his photographs and Left Thumb\nImpression in the Application for Surety ship [Judicial Form\nNo.46 annexed to 'The Criminal Rules of Practice, 2019'].\nThe learned Magistrate shall obtain a copy of any one of the\nidentity proofs to ensure his identity;\n(b) the petitioner shall report before the respondent police\ndaily at 10.30.a.m., until further orders;\n[c] the petitioner shall not abscond either during\ninvestigation or trial;\n[d] the petitioner shall not tamper with the evidence or\nwitness either during investigation or trial;\n[e] On breach of any of the aforementioned conditions, the\nlearned Magistrate/Trial Court is entitled to pass appropriate\norders against the petitioner in accordance with law as if the\naforementioned conditions have been imposed and the\npetitioner released on bail by the learned Magistrate/Trial\nCourt himself as laid down by the Hon'ble Supreme Court in\nP.K.Shaji vs. State of Kerala [(2005)13 SCC 283];\n[f] If the accused thereafter absconds, a fresh FIR can be\nregistered under Section 269 of B.N.S.\n23-10-2026\nSMA\n__________\nPage3 of 5\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\nCRL OP No. 30881 of 2026\nNote:\n1. Registry is directed to forthwith upload\nthis order in the Official Website of this\nCourt.\n2. All concerned to act on this order being\nuploaded in Official Website of this Court\nwithout insisting on certified hard copies. To\nbe noted, this order when uploaded in the\nofficial website of this Court will be\nwatermarked and will also have a QR code.\nTo\n1. The Judicial Magistrate\nThiruthuraipoondi, Tiruvarur District\n2. The Superintendent,\nDistrict Jail, Nagapattinam.\n3. Inspector of Police\nAll Women Police Station\nThiruthuraipoondi,\nTiruvarur District.\n4. The Public Prosecutor,\nHigh Court of Madras.\n__________\nPage4 of 5\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis\n\nCRL OP No. 30881 of 2026\nN.RAMESH, J.\nSMA\nCRL OP No. 30881 of 2026\n23-10-2026\n__________\nPage5 of 5\nhttps://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis	5	t	HCMA012563952026	Criminal Original Petition (Bail)	2026-10-23	Single Bench	Vinoth	State Rep by The Inspector of Police All Women Police Station Thiruthuraipoondi, Tiruvarur District.	Mr. M.Vijaya Ragavan	Mr. M.M.I.Khaleel Government Advocate (Crl. Side)	Civil & Constitutional Law	In this matter, the Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai) adjudicated issues between Thiruvarur District and State Rep by The Inspector of Police All Women Police Station Thiruthuraipoondi. The Court rendered a decision disposing of the application under statutory provisions.	0	2026 LexGuard (MHC) E5517B95	{"court": "Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)", "title": "Thiruvarur District v. State Rep by The Inspector of Police All Women Police Station Thiruthuraipoondi", "case_id": "e5517b95-ab32-412e-a5d1-d5ff709bdeef", "case_no": "NO. 30881 of 2026", "parties": "Thiruvarur District v. State Rep by The Inspector of Police All Women Police Station Thiruthuraipoondi", "category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "petitioner": "Thiruvarur District", "respondent": "State Rep by The Inspector of Police All Women Police Station Thiruthuraipoondi", "legal_domain": "Civil & Constitutional Law", "category_facts": {"category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "surety_count": 2, "custody_period": null, "application_type": "Regular Bail", "bail_bond_amount": 25000.0, "trial_court_fine": null, "reason_for_relief": null, "reporting_condition": "report before the respondent police", "trial_court_sentence": null, "trial_court_fine_paid": false}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "scenario_keywords": [], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Disposed", "disposition": "Disposed"}}
