or grant\nof similar interim order of protection by the Appellate\nAuthority by filing necessary application.\n(Sashikanta Mishra)\nVacation Judge\nSipra\nPage 2 of 2.	2	t	ODHC010831192026	Writ Petition	2026-10-23	Single Bench	– Mr. Niranjan Lenka	State Of Odisha & others ..... Opposite Parties Represented by Adv	\N	\N	Civil & Constitutional Law	In this matter, the High Court of Orissa at Cuttack adjudicated issues between – Mr. Niranjan Lenka and State Of Odisha & others ..... Opposite Parties Represented by Adv. The Court rendered a decision disposing of the application under statutory provisions.	0	2026 LexGuard (OHC) B5044B2B	{"court": "High Court of Orissa at Cuttack", "title": "– Mr. Niranjan Lenka v. State Of Odisha & others ..... Opposite Parties Represented by Adv", "case_id": "b5044b2b-41ab-4a5b-94f8-a43cde3a4fd4", "case_no": "NO. 34921", "parties": "– Mr. Niranjan Lenka v. State Of Odisha & others ..... Opposite Parties Represented by Adv", "category": "QUASHING", "petitioner": "– Mr. Niranjan Lenka", "respondent": "State Of Odisha & others ..... Opposite Parties Represented by Adv", "legal_domain": "Civil & Constitutional Law", "category_facts": {"category": "QUASHING", "civil_cloak": false, "quash_ground": "Amicable settlement and joint compromise between parties", "compromise_affidavit": true, "bhajan_lal_principles": true, "proceedings_challenged": "FIR / Investigation"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "scenario_keywords": [], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Disposed", "disposition": "Disposed"}}
bbff4997-ed77-4230-941f-2d5ecd38818b	NO. 2964 OF 2026	Applicant v. The State of Maharashtra	High Court of Judicature at Bombay	2026	Hon'ble MADHAV J. JAMDAR, J	Applicant v. The State of Maharashtra	["Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023", "Food Safety and Standards Act", "Indian Penal Code, 1860", "of BNS", "of IPC"]	["Section 123 of BNS", "Section 26(2)(i) Food Safety and Standards Act", "Section 26(2)(iv) Food Safety and Standards Act", "Section 328 of IPC", "Section 59 Food Safety and Standards Act"]	["civil & constitutional law", "bhc", "disposed"]	Judgment in NO. 2964 OF 2026 delivered by Hon'ble MADHAV J. JAMDAR, J at High Court of Judicature at Bombay. The proceedings concern Civil & Constitutional Law with respect to procedural compliance.	Disposed	storage/cases/BHC_2026_NO_2964_OF_2026_bbff4997.pdf	5fd06a44eec2c9b5e803f75c7c3472899558561b02375720edfad11fea1baa95	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 14:51:53.194784+05:30	2026-10-25 14:51:53.194793+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:09.827578+05:30	\N	[]	Holding and principles affirmed in NO. 2964 OF 2026 regarding Civil & Constitutional Law. The Court adjudicated upon applicable judicial standards.	[]	Disposed	[]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding Precedent)	916 ABA-2964-2026.DOC\nskt\nIN THE HIGH COURT OF JUDICATURE AT BOMBAY\nCRIMINAL APPELLATE JURISDICTION\nANTICIPATORY BAIL APPLICATION NO. 2964 OF 2026\nMangilal Bhalaram Jaat …Applicant\nVersus\nThe State of Maharashtra …Respondent\nMr. Rajesh More, for the Applicant.\nMs. Savita M. Yadav, APP, for the Respondent-State.\nCORAM: MADHAV J. JAMDAR, J.\nDATE : 22nd OCTOBER 2026\nPC:-\n1. Heard Mr. More, learned Counsel appearing for the\nApplicant and Ms. Yadav, learned APP, for the Respondent-State of\nMaharashtra.\n2. By the present Anticipatory Bail Application, the Applicant is\nseeking pre-arrest bail in connection with C.R. No.310 of 2026\nregistered on 12th September 2026 with Kharadi Police Station,\nPune City, for the offences punishable under Sections 123, 223,\n274 and 275 read with Section 3(5) of the Bharatiya Nyaya\nSanhita, 2023 (“BNS”) and Sections 26(2)(i), 26(2)(iv) and 59 of\nthe Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006 and Sections 7(2), 20(2)\nof the Cigarettes and Other Tobacco Products (Prohibition of\nPage 1/ 5\n22nd October 2026\n\n916 ABA-2964-2026.DOC\nskt\nAdvertisement and Regulation of Trade and Commerce,\nProduction, Supply and Distribution) Act, 2003 (“COTPA Act”).\n3. As per the prosecution case, the Applicant was procuring\ncontraband material which is unsafe and dangerous for the health\nof people.\n4. It is the submission of Mr. More, learned Counsel, that\nexcept the offence under Section 123 of BNS, all other offences are\nbailable offences. He submits that as far as Section 123 of BNS is\nconcerned, the Supreme Court is considering the applicability of\nSection 123 (i.e. Section 328 of IPC) to the similar cases. He\ntherefore, states that the anticipatory bail be granted.\n5. On the other hand, Ms. Yadav, learned APP, strongly opposes\nthe Anticipatory Bail Application. He submits that the offence is\nvery serious and, therefore, the Anticipatory Bail Application be\nrejected. On instructions, learned APP submits that there is one\nantecedent against the Applicant.\n6. Learned APP submits that if this Court is inclined to grant\nanticipatory bail then the Applicant be directed to give undertaking\nPage 2/ 5\n22nd October 2026\n\n916 ABA-2964-2026.DOC\nskt\nin terms of Order of the Supreme Court in the case of Abhijit\nJitendra Lolage v. State of Maharashtra 1 and more particularly\nrelies on following condition imposed by the Supreme Court in\ncase of similar offences:\n“It is deemed appropriate to impose the\ncondition – “I, Abhijeet Jitendra Lolage do\nundertake to not deal with Gutkha, that is, Pan\nMasala with Tobacco”. In case, the appellant –\nAbhijeet Jitendra Lolage violates the terms and\nconditions of bail, including the undertaking\nimposed, it would be open to the prosecution to\nseek cancellation of bail.”\n(Emphasis added)\nMr. More, learned Counsel appearing for the Applicant, after\ntaking instructions, submits that the Applicant will give the\nundertaking.\n7. The Supreme Court in the said order has imposed the above\ncondition in case of similar offence.\n8. Perusal of the record shows that except the offence under\nSection 123 of BNS the other offences are bailable offences. The\nissue whether Section 123 of BNS will apply to similar cases is\npending before the Supreme Court. As the Applicant is ready to\n1 SLP (Crl.) Diary No.2806/2023\nPage 3/ 5\n22nd October 2026\n\n916 ABA-2964-2026.DOC\nskt\ngive the undertaking that he will not deal with Gutkha and\nTobacco products in any manner whatsoever, the case is made out\nfor grant of anticipatory bail.\n9. In view thereof, the following order is passed:\nO R D E R\n(a) In the event of arrest of the Applicant-Mangilal\nBhalaram Jaat in connection with C.R. No.310 of\n2026 registered with Kharadi Police Station, Pune City,\nthe Applicant is directed to be released on bail on his\nfurnishing PR Bond in the sum of Rs.1,00,000/- with\none or two solvent sureties in the like amount.\n(b) The Applicant shall attend the concerned Police\nStation once in a month, i.e., first Sunday of each\nmonth, till filing of the Charge-sheet and shall\ncooperate with the investigation.\n(c) The Applicant to furnish an undertaking that he will\nnot deal with banned products i.e. Gutkha, Pan\nMasala with Tobacco products, etc., in any manner\nwhatsoever. Such undertaking is to be filed in this\nCourt within a period of two weeks from today. The\nPage 4/ 5\n22nd October 2026\n\n916 ABA-2964-2026.DOC\nskt\nApplicant to strictly comply with the said undertaking.\n(d) The Applicant shall furnish his cell phone number and\nresidential address to the Investigating Officer and\nshall keep the same updated, in case of any change\nthereto.\n(e) The Applicant shall not directly or indirectly make any\ninducement, threat, or promise to any person\nacquainted with the facts of the case so as to dissuade\nsuch a person from disclosing the facts to the Court or\nto any Police personnel.\n(f) The Applicant shall not tamper with the prosecution\nevidence and shall not contact or influence the\nComplainant or any witness in any manner.\n(g) The Applicant shall not leave India without prior\npermission of the Court.\n(h) In case, the Applicant violates the terms and\nconditions of bail, including the undertaking, it would\nbe open to the prosecution to seek cancellation of bail.\n10. The Anticipatory Bail Application is disposed of accordingly.\n[MADHAV J. JAMDAR, J.]\nPage 5/ 5\n22nd October 2026	5	t	BHCB010029642026	Judicial Precedent	2026-10-23	Single Bench	Applicant	The State of Maharashtra	\N	\N	Civil & Constitutional Law	In this matter, the High Court of Judicature at Bombay adjudicated issues between skt Mangilal Bhalaram Jaat and The State of Maharashtra. The Court rendered a decision disposing of the application under statutory provisions.	0	2026 LexGuard (BHC) BBFF4997	{"court": "High Court of Judicature at Bombay", "title": "skt Mangilal Bhalaram Jaat v. The State of Maharashtra", "case_id": "bbff4997-ed77-4230-941f-2d5ecd38818b", "case_no": "NO. 2964 OF 2026", "parties": "skt Mangilal Bhalaram Jaat v. The State of Maharashtra", "category": "NDPS", "petitioner": "skt Mangilal Bhalaram Jaat", "respondent": "The State of Maharashtra", "legal_domain": "Civil & Constitutional Law", "category_facts": {"category": "NDPS", "custody_period": null, "contraband_type": "Contraband / Narcotics", "seized_quantity": null, "statutory_issue": "Bail / Pre-trial Release under NDPS Act", "quantity_category": "Intermediate Quantity", "section_37_embargo": false, "search_compliance_sec_50": "Search procedure evaluated"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": [], "sce