gov.in/judis	verified	Good Law	CRL OP No. 26284 of 2026\n\nDATED: 09-10-2026\nCORAM\nTHE HON'BLE MR.JUSTICE N.RAMESH\nCRL OP No. 26284 of 2026\nCNR: {HCMA012140432026}\nSilu Chhinchani\n..Petitioner(s)\nVs\nState Rep.by,\nThe Inspector of Police\nPEW - Tambaram Police Station,\nTambaram, Cr.No.52 of 2025.\n..Respondent(s)\nPRAYER: This criminal original petition filed under Section 482 BNSS Act to\nenlarge on bail in CC.No.758 of 2025 in Cr.No.52 of 2025 pending on the file\nof the I Additional Special Judge, Special Court for EC and NDPS Act,\nChennai.\nFor Petitioner(s): Mr.S.Karthikeyan\nfor Mr.K.Subburaj\nFor Respondent(s): Mr.M.M.I.Khaleel,\nGovt.Advocate (Crl.Side)\nORDER\nThis is a petition for bail, under Section 483 of BNSS, in a case under the\nNDPS Act ('the Act'). The petitioner is the sole accused in Crime No.52 of 2025\non the file of the Inspector of Police, Prohibition Enforcement Wing,\nTambaram, registered on 08.04.2025 for offences under Section 8(c) read with\nSection 20(b)(ii)(C) of the Act. The final report has been taken on file as\n__________\nPage1 of 11\n\nCRL OP No. 26284 of 2026\nC.C.No.758 of 2025 by the learned I Additional Special Judge, Special Court\nfor EC and NDPS Act Cases, Chennai. This is the sixth petition for bail by the\npetitioner before this Court.\n2. The case of the prosecution is that on 08.04.2025, on information that a\nperson from Odisha would bring ganja for sale near the service road at K.R.B.\nNagar, Anakaputhur, a police party of the respondent waited at that place and, at\nabout 17.15 hours, intercepted the petitioner, a native of Gajapati District,\nOdisha. He was carrying two trolley bags. The first information report states\nthat a black plastic cover in the maroon bag held about 12 kilograms of dry\nganja, and a black plastic cover in the sky-blue bag about 10 kilograms, in all\nabout 22 kilograms. The contraband and a mobile phone were seized under a\nmahazar between 17.30 and 18.00 hours, and the petitioner was arrested at\n18.05 hours.\n3. The quantity alleged, 22 kilograms, exceeds the commercial quantity\nby 2 kilograms, or one part in eleven. If it is a commercial quantity, the offence\nis punishable with rigorous imprisonment for not less than ten years, which may\nextend to twenty years, and Section 37 of the Act governs the petition. Samples\nS1 and S3 reached the Forensic Sciences Department, Chennai, on 16.06.2025,\nsixty-nine days after the seizure. The report in T.No.6749 NAR 880/2025 dated\n17.06.2025, which the respondent has annexed to its counter affidavit, describes\neach sample as dry greenish brown broken leaves, leaf stalks, flowering tops\n__________\nPage2 of 11\n\nCRL OP No. 26284 of 2026\nand seeds, weighing 61.9 grams and 59.8 grams net, and records that\ncannabinoids were detected in both.\n4. The petitioner has been in custody since 08.04.2025, that is, for one\nyear and six months. The final report was filed on 01.08.2025 and was taken on\nfile on 04.09.2025. The case status of the Special Court, as on 03.09.2026,\nshows that the case has been listed repeatedly since 20.11.2025, each time for\n"Remand", and stood posted to 07.09.2026. Charges have not been framed. The\ncounter affidavit does not say otherwise.\n5. The earlier petitions before this Court are Crl.O.P.No.23040 of 2025,\ndismissed on 20.08.2025; Crl.O.P.No.29736 of 2025, dismissed on 02.12.2025;\nCrl.O.P.No.34975 of 2025, dismissed on 05.01.2026; Crl.O.P.No.6843 of 2026,\ndismissed on 02.04.2026; and Crl.O.P.No.12160 of 2026, dismissed on\n17.06.2026. The petition and the counter affidavit list the same five. Each of\nthose orders proceeded on the figure of 22 kilograms as a commercial quantity.\nThe second and third orders also took note of the custody and of the delay in\nframing charges, and held that these did not overcome Section 37. The fourth\norder, while dismissing the petition, requested the Special Court to dispose of\nthe case as expeditiously as possible. None of the orders refers to the report of\nthe laboratory or to what the 22 kilograms consisted of.\n6. Learned counsel for the petitioner submits that the report of the\nlaboratory shows that the seized material contained leaves, leaf stalks and seeds;\n__________\nPage3 of 11\n\nCRL OP No. 26284 of 2026\nthat these were weighed with the flowering tops; and that, if the parts which the\ndefinition in Section 2(iii)(b) of the Act does not take in are left out, the ganja\nwould fall below the commercial quantity. He relies on Kunal Dattu Kadu v.\nUnion of India, 2022 SCC OnLine Bom 1770 and on Rajesh Sahu v. State of\nChhattisgarh, order of the Supreme Court dated 01.09.2025. He further\nsubmits that the arrest intimation was only a printed proforma, in breach of\nArticle 22(1) of the Constitution; that the option offered to the petitioner under\nSection 50 of the Act included a third option not known to law, relying on State\nof Punjab v. Baldev Singh, (1999) 6 SCC 172 and State of Himachal\nPradesh v. Surat Singh, 2026 SCC OnLine SC 376; that no application under\nSection 52A(2) was made, that the samples were not forwarded within the time\nstipulated by Rule 14 of the NDPS (Seizure, Storage, Sampling and Disposal)\nRules, 2022, relying on Rambabu v. State of Rajasthan, order of the Supreme\nCourt dated 13.08.2025 in SLP (Crl.) No.5648 of 2025; and that the seizure was\nnot video recorded. The definition of ganja and Rule 14 are said to be new\ngrounds.\n7. Learned Government Advocate (Criminal Side), on the basis of counter\naffidavit of Inspector of Police, opposed the petition. The counter affidavit\nstates that about 22 kilograms of ganja, a commercial quantity, was recovered;\nthat this is the sixth petition and the petitioner cannot circumvent the effect of\nearlier bail orders by merely describing previously available legal grounds as\n__________\nPage4 of 11\n\nCRL OP No. 26284 of 2026\n“new grounds”; that Kunal Dattu Kadu (cited supra) and Rajesh Sahu (cited\nsupra) turned on their own facts; and, in paragraph 13, that the petitioner has\nnot demonstrated from the prosecution records that the entire alleged quantity\nof 22 kilograms consists only of excluded seeds/leaves/stalks. It answers the\nremaining grounds by saying that Section 50 does not apply to the search of a\nbag, that no prejudice from the form of the arrest intimation has been shown,\nand that delay in compliance with Section 52A and the Rules does not satisfy\nSection 37. Reliance is placed on Union of India v. Shiv Shanker Kesari,\n(2007) 7 SCC 798, State of Kerala v. Rajesh, (2020) 12 SCC 122 and\nNarcotics Control Bureau v. Mohit Aggarwal, (2022) 18 SCC 374.\n8. I have considered the rival submissions and perused the material placed\nbefore me.\n9. Ganja is defined by Section 2(iii)(b) of the Act as the flowering or\nfruiting tops of the cannabis plant (excluding the seeds and leaves when not\naccompanied by the tops). The bar in Section 37(1)(b)(ii) is attracted only\nwhere the offence involves a commercial quantity. It is for the prosecution,\nwhich invokes the bar, to place before the Court the material from which it can\nbe seen that the threshold has been crossed. On the parts of the plant, a learned\nJudge of this Court in Ganesan v. The Deputy Superintendent of Police,\nCrl.A.(MD)No.212 of 2020 and batch, dated 15.10.2025, held that\n__________\nPage5 of 11\n\nCRL OP No. 26284 of 2026\nIf the flowering or fruiting tops of cannabis plant are not separately\nrecovered and recovered with stalks and stem, the legal requirement\nis to exclude the weight of the stalks and stem.\nand that\nThe said compliance is legally required to be followed in the border\nline cases of the recovered contraband having weight between 21 and\n25 kgs.\n10. I have applied that decision in Crl.O.P.No.17678 of 2026, dated\n28.08.2026, where the report described the sample from a seizure of 21.5\nkilograms in much the same terms, and in Crl.O.P.No.24478 of 2026, where\nthe sample from a seizure of 21 kilograms contained leaf stalks. In Rajesh Sahu\n(cited supra), the Supreme Court granted bail where the seized quantity was a\nlittle above the commercial quantity and was found to contain leaves, seeds and\ndry vegetative floral parts, which, it observed, needed to be excluded while\ncomputing the actual quantity of ganja.\n11. This case falls within the band Ganesan (cited supra) speaks of. Each\nof the two samples is described by the laboratory as containing leaf stalks. The\nfirst information report gives each lot as "about" 12 kilograms and "about" 10\nkilograms of dry ganja, each weighed in bulk at the spot. Nothing on the record\nshows that the stalks were separated or weighed apart, or how much of the 22\nkilograms consisted of flowering or fruiting tops with their accompanying\nleaves and seeds. The answer in paragraph 13 of the counter affidavit asks the\n__________\nPage6 of 11\n\nCRL OP No. 26284 of 2026\nwrong question. The petitioner does not have to show that the whole of the 22\nkilograms consists of excluded parts. The question is whether the ganja, as\ndefined, is shown to exceed 20 kilograms, and on a margin of 2 kilograms the\nrespondent's own report leaves that open. I do not hold that the quantity is\nbelow the commercial quantity. That is a matter on which evidence will be led,\nand I leave it entirely to the Special Court. I hold only that there is a real\npossibility that the ganja, excluding the stalks, is below the commercial\nquantity. On the material as it stands, I am satisfied that there are reasonable\ngrounds for believing that the petitioner is not guilty of an offence involving a\ncommercial quantity. The counter affidavit does not attribute any previous case\nto the petitioner, and I am satisfied that he is not likely to commit any offence\nwhile on bail.\n12. The other grounds do not need to be decided. The arrest intimation\nhas not been placed before me, the plea on it is stated in a single sentence, and it\nwas available in each of the earlier petitions; I leave it open. Whether Section\n50 of the Act was attracted to the search of the two trolley bags, and whether the\noption offered complied with it, are questions for the trial; Surat Singh (cited\nsupra) was decided in an appeal after a full trial. A lapse or delay in compliance\nwith Section 52A by itself neither vitiates the trial nor entitles the accused to\nbail, as held in Narcotics Control Bureau v. Kashif, 2024 INSC 1045, and\nRambabu (cited supra) is a short order on its own facts. The decision relied on\n__________\nPage7 of 11\n\nCRL OP No. 26284 of 2026\nfor video recording, as extracted in the petition, turned on directions of the High\nCourt at Calcutta. I rest nothing on any of these points.\n13. The decisions relied on by the respondent state the rigour of Section\n37 where it applies. They do not assist where the prosecution's own material\ndoes not show that the commercial quantity has been crossed.\n14. The investigation is complete, and the prosecution's material is\ndocumentary and in its own hands. The apprehension recorded in the first order,\nthat the petitioner, who hails from Odisha, may abscond, is real, and it is to be\nmet by conditions that fix his address and his attendance, and that allow the\nSpecial Court to act on any breach.\n15. The observations in this order are prima facie and are confined to the\ndisposal of this petition. They are not an expression of opinion on the weight or\ncomposition of the contraband, or on compliance with Sections 42, 50 and 52A\nof the Act and the Rules of 2022. The Special Court shall decide every question\non the evidence let in before it, uninfluenced by anything stated here.\n16. Accordingly, this Criminal Original Petition is allowed and the\npetitioner is ordered to be released on bail on his executing a bond for a sum of\nRs.25,000/- (Rupees Twenty Five Thousand only) with two sureties, out of the\ntwo sureties, one shall be a local surety from Tamil Nadu and one from his\nblood relative, each for a like sum, to the satisfaction of the learned I Additional\nSpecial Judge, Special Court for EC and NDPS Act Cases, Chennai, in\n__________\nPage8 of 11\n\nCRL OP No. 26284 of 2026\nC.C.No.758 of 2025, on the following conditions:\n[a] the sureties shall affix his photographs and Left Thumb\nImpression in the Application for Surety ship [Judicial Form\nNo.46 annexed to 'The Criminal Rules of Practice, 2019'].\nThe learned Magistrate shall obtain a copy of any one of the\nidentity proofs to ensure his identity;\n[b] before his release, the petitioner shall file before the\nSpecial Court an affidavit stating his permanent address in\nOdisha, the address at which he will reside while on bail\nand his mobile number, with proof of each, and shall not\nchange that address or number without prior intimation in\nwriting to the Special Court;\n[c] the petitioner shall appear before the Special Court on\nevery date of hearing, unless his appearance is dispensed\nwith by that Court.\n[d] the petitioner shall not abscond either during\ninvestigation or trial;\n[e] the petitioner shall not tamper with the evidence or\nwitness either during investigation or trial;\n[f] On breach of any of the aforementioned conditions, the\nlearned Magistrate/Trial Court is entitled to pass appropriate\norders against the petitioner in accordance with law as if the\naforementioned conditions have been imposed and the\npetitioner released on bail by the learned Magistrate/Trial\nCourt himself as laid down by the Hon'ble Supreme Court in\nP.K.Shaji vs. State of Kerala [(2005)13 SCC 283];\n__________\nPage9 of 11\n\nCRL OP No. 26284 of 2026\n[g] If the accused thereafter absconds, a fresh FIR can\nbe registered under Section 269 of B.N.S.\n09-10-2026\nIndex: Yes/No\nSpeaking/Non-speaking order\nNeutral Citation: Yes/No\nsms\nNote:\n1. Registry is directed to forthwith upload\nthis order in the Official Website of this\nCourt.\n2. All concerned to act on this order being\nuploaded in Official Website of this Court\nwithout insisting on certified hard copies. To\nbe noted, this order when uploaded in the\nofficial website of this Court will be\nwatermarked and will also have a QR code.\nTo\n1.I Additional Special Judge,\nSpecial Court for EC and NDPS Act,\nChennai.\n2.The Superintendent of Police\nCentral Prison, Puzhal.\n3.The Inspector of Police\nPEW - Tambaram Police Station,\nTambaram, Cr.No.52 of 2025.\n4.The Public Prosecutor\nHigh Court, Madras.\n__________\nPage10 of 11\n\nCRL OP No. 26284 of 2026\nN.RAMESH, J.\nsms\nCRL OP No. 26284 of 2026\n09-10-2026\n__________\nPage11 of 11	11	t	HCMA012140432026	Criminal Original Petition (Bail)	2026-10-09	Single Bench	Silu Chhinchani	State Rep. by Inspector of Police, PEW Tambaram	Mr.S.Karthikeyan	Mr.M.M.I.Khaleel, Govt.Advocate (Crl.Side)	Civil Law	In this criminal original petition under Civil Law, the Court ruled on the dispute between 06 Crl-Op-26284-2026 and the Respondent.	0	2026 LexGuard (Crim) FB9671	{"court": "Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)", "title": "Silu Chhinchani v. State Rep. by Inspector of Police, PEW Tambaram", "case_id": "fb9671c0-074c-4dcf-83e9-f5bb47b8cab9", "case_no": "CRL OP No. 26284 of 2026", "parties": "Silu Chhinchani v. State Rep. by Inspector of Police, PEW Tambaram", "category": "NDPS", "petitioner": "Silu Chhinchani", "respondent": "State Rep. by Inspector of Police, PEW Tambaram", "legal_domain": "Civil Law", "category_facts": {"category": "NDPS", "custody_period": null, "contraband_type": "Ganja", "seized_quantity": "12 kilo", "statutory_issue": "Bail / Pre-trial Release under NDPS Act", "quantity_category": "Commercial Quantity", "section_37_embargo": true, "search_compliance_sec_50": "Search from vehicle/bag (Section 50 personal search held inapplicable)"}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [{"act": "BNSS", "section": "483", "canonical": "Section 483 BNSS", "equivalents": ["