nal Safety and Health Code 2020\n\nSECTIONS & PROVISIONS:\nArticle 21, Article 19(1)(g), TN Shops Act Section 14\n\nKEY TOPICS & ISSUES:\nRight to Disconnect, Digital Burnout, Workplace Mental Health, Article 21, Working Hours\n\n================================================================================\n                                   JUDGMENT\n================================================================================\n\n1. FACTS OF THE CASE:\nPetition seeking guidelines against mandatory off-hours electronic communication by employers causing psychological distress.\n\n2. ISSUES FRAMED:\nDoes continuous digital connectivity outside contracted working hours violate the right to health under Article 21?\n\n3. LEGAL RATIO & FINDINGS:\nUnregulated electronic intrusions outside working hours violate employees' fundamental right to health and dignity under Article 21.\n\n4. FINAL ORDER:\nWrit Petition Allowed; Right to disconnect recognized as facet of mental health under Art. 21.\n\nOrdered and decreed accordingly.\n\nHon'ble Justice N. Anand Venkatesh\nMadras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)\n[LEXGUARD REPOSITORY CRYPTOGRAPHIC SEAL]\n	3	t	HCMA010201142026	Writ Petition	\N	Single Bench	Chennai IT Employees Union	Union of India & 3 Ors.	\N	\N	Constitutional Law	In this writ petition under Constitutional Law, the Court ruled on the dispute between Chennai IT Employees Union and Union of India & 3 Ors..	0	2026 LexGuard (Writ) C85618	{"court": "Madras High Court (Principal Seat, Chennai)", "title": "Chennai IT Employees Union v. Union of India & 3 Ors.", "case_id": "c8561883-0d4a-4bcb-9eaa-0d9cfbf5709e", "case_no": "W.P. No. 20114 of 2026", "parties": "Chennai Information Technology Employees Union", "category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "petitioner": "Chennai IT Employees Union", "respondent": "Union of India & 3 Ors.", "legal_domain": "Constitutional Law", "category_facts": {"category": "GENERAL_CONSTITUTIONAL", "remedy_type": "Writ Petition / Criminal