nd 21st July, 2019\nincluding a request for appointment of an arbitrator. The Applicant\nalso filed a criminal compliant with the Vikhroli police station at\nMumbai and initiated criminal proceedings before the Court of the\nChief Judicial Magistrate at Kargil, which are stated to be pending.\nDespite this, the Respondent did not make payment and engaged in\ndilatory conduct to defeat the claim of the Applicant. Finally, on 4th\nNovember 2019, the Applicant invoked Clause 32 of the Work Order\nand called upon the Respondent to appoint an independent and\nimpartial arbitrator. By letter dated 5th December 2019, the\nRespondent rejected that request. The Applicant thereafter filed\nArbitration Petition No. 21 of 2021 before the High Court of Jammu\nand Kashmir and Ladakh at Jammu under Section 11(6) of the\nArbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996. By an order dated 13th\nFebruary 2026, that petition was dismissed for want of territorial\njurisdiction, with liberty to approach the competent Court. The present\n2/9\n901 CARAP-359-2026.doc bdpsps\n\nApplication has been filed pursuant to that liberty.\n3. Mr. Dalia submits that although the Applicant had signed No\nClaim Certificate on 20th April, 2013, the subsequent correspondence\ndemonstrates that disputes continued to subsist. It is contended that\nwhether the claims are barred by limitation or constitute dead claims\nought to be decided by the arbitral tribunal. As regards the time taken\nto approach this Court, Mr. Dalia submits that the period during which\nthe Applicant prosecuted the Section 11 proceedings before the\nJammu and Kashmir High Court should be excluded under Section 14\nof the Limitation Act, 1963. He submits that those proceedings were\npursued bona fide and that the order dismissing them expressly\ngranted liberty to approach the competent Court. The Applicant\naccordingly seeks appointment of a sole arbitrator for adjudication of\ndisputes and differences that have arisen under the Work Order dated\n25th July, 2011.\n4. On the other hand, Mr. Lalan, learned counsel for the\nRespondent, opposes the Application on the ground that the claims are\nex facie barred by limitation. The Petitioner has averred various facts\nand material documents in this Application. He submits that on 20th\n3/9\n901 CARAP-359-2026.doc bdpsps\n\nApril 2013, the Applicant accepted the final bill and executed an\nunconditional No Claim Certificate. On the same date, the Applicant\nalso issued a letter on its own letterhead accepting the measurements,\nmaterials, requisitions and quality of the work and 	9	t	BHCB010003592026	Judicial Precedent	\N	Single Bench	.Applicant.	Hindustan Construction Company Ltd.	\N	\N	Banking & Negotiable Instruments	In this judgment under Commercial & Arbitration, the Court ruled on the dispute between M/s. S. D. Bhatt and Hindustan Construction Company Ltd..	0	2026 LexGuard (Judg) DB67EC	{"court": "High Court of Judicature at Bombay (Ordinary Original Civil Jurisdiction)", "title": "M/s. S. D. Bhatt v. Hindustan Construction Company Ltd.", "case_id": "db67ec49-36cb-48ae-89a1-5fa1c21e0fa9", "case_no": "Commercial Arbitration Application No. 359 of 2026", "parties": "M/s. S. D. Bhatt v. Hindustan Construction Company Ltd.", "category": "COMMERCIAL_CIVIL", "petitioner": "M/s. S. D. Bhatt", "respondent": "Hindustan Construction Company Ltd.", "legal_domain": "Banking & Negotiable Instruments", "category_facts": {"category": "COMMERCIAL_CIVIL", "dispute_type": "Arbitration & Conciliation Challenge", "statutory_basis": "Commercial Courts Act / Civil Procedure Code", "interim_injunction_granted": false}, "activity_events": [], "profile_version": "2.5", "statutory_matrix": [], "activity_clusters": [], "scenario_clusters": ["financial_commercial"], "scenario_keywords": ["statutory notice"], "procedural_posture": {"remedy": "Dismissed", "disposition": "Dismissed"}}
