f7894482-7fef-4313-bcbe-2017ccea3ae9	WA No. 25 of 2026	MRS. SHUBHA M v. Respondent / State	High Court of Karnataka	2026	Hon'ble Mr. Justice D K Singh	MRS. SHUBHA M v. Respondent / State	["Constitution of India", "ID Act", "INDUSTRIAL DISPUTES ACT", "Industrial Disputes Act"]	["Section 10 ID Act", "Section 12 INDUSTRIAL DISPUTES ACT", "Section 18(1) ID Act", "Section 18(1) Industrial Disputes Act", "Section 2(oo) ID Act", "Section 2(s) ID Act"]	["civil & constitutional law", "khc", "dismissed"]	Judgment in NO. 25 of 2026 delivered by Hon'ble THE HON'BLE MR. JUSTICE D K SINGH at High Court of Karnataka. The proceedings concern Civil & Constitutional Law with respect to Article 226.	Disposed	storage/cases/KHC_2026_NO_25_of_2026_f7894482.pdf	67640dcfb4d794d1fcdcf8e71f60e28419365bf793229f016e1a5d87349c20a4	\N	\N	\N	published	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	657396f3-5181-4922-8323-72d78b963be0	2026-10-25 14:51:42.627679+05:30	2026-10-25 14:51:42.627687+05:30	2026-10-26 10:38:09.85122+05:30	DATED 03.12.2025 PASSED BY THE LEARNED SINGLE JUDGE IN WRIT PETITION No. 18958/2021 AND CONSEQUENTLY DISMISS THE WRIT PETITION FILED BY THE 1ST RESPONDENT, TO MEET THE ENDS OF JUSTICE. THESE APPEALS HAVING BEEN HEARD AND RESERVED FOR JUDGMENT ON 07.09.2026 AND COMING ON FOR PRONOUNCEMENT THIS DAY, HON'BLE MR. JUSTICE H.SHANTHI BHUSHAN DELIVERED THE FOLLOWING: CORAM: HON'BLE MR. JUSTICE D K SINGH AND HON'BLE MR. JUSTICE H.SHANTHI BHUSHAN - 7 - WA No. 25 of 2026 C/W WA No. 20 of 2026 WA No. 29 of 2026 AND 2 OTHERS CAV JUDGMENT (PER: HON'BLE MR. JUSTICE H.SHANTHI BHUSHAN) A “golden handshake”, as a voluntary retirement scheme is commonly described, is intended to bring the employer–employee relationship to an end on the terms mutually accepted by the parties. Having opted for voluntary retirement, acknowledged the terms of the scheme in writing and received the benefits thereunder, the petitioner’s subsequent challenge must be examined in the light of her earlier conduct and the material 	[]	Holding and principles affirmed in NO. 25 of 2026 regarding Civil & Constitutional Law. The Court adjudicated upon Article 226.	[]	Disposed	[{"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [1979) 1 SCC 1]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "1979) 1 SCC 1", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}, {"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [1989) 3 SCC 271]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "1989) 3 SCC 271", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}, {"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2003) 4 SCC 27]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2003) 4 SCC 27", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}, {"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2014) 10 SCC 301]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2014) 10 SCC 301", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}, {"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [2015) 15 SCC 1]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "2015) 15 SCC 1", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}, {"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [AIR 2003 SC 2189]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "AIR 2003 SC 2189", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}, {"court": "Supreme Court of India", "title": "Judicial Precedent [AIR 2006 SC 2795]", "status": "Good Law (External Authority)", "case_id": null, "citation": "AIR 2006 SC 2795", "treatment": "Cited / Considered"}]	\N	unverified	Good Law (Binding Precedent)	- 1 -\nWA No. 25 of 2026\nC/W WA No. 20 of 2026\nWA No. 29 of 2026\nR\nAND 2 OTHERS\nIN THE HIGH COURT OF KARNATAKA AT BENGALURU\nDATED THIS THE 15TH DAY OF OCTOBER 2026\nPRESENT\nTHE HON'BLE MR. JUSTICE D K SINGH\nAND\nTHE HON'BLE MR. JUSTICE H.SHANTHI BHUSHAN\nWRIT APPEAL NO. 25 OF 2026 (L-RES)\nC/W\nWRIT APPEAL NO. 20 OF 2026 (L-RES)\nWRIT APPEAL NO. 29 OF 2026 (L-RES)\nWRIT APPEAL NO. 30 OF 2026 (L-RES)\nWRIT APPEAL NO. 56 OF 2026 (L-RES)\nIN WA No. 25/2026:\nBETWEEN:\nMRS. SHUBHA M\nAGE ABOUT 48 YEARS,\nD/O MUKUNDA, NO.59, AECS LAYOUT, 16TH CROSS,\n4TH MAIN ROAD, GEDDALHALLI,\nBANGALORE-560 094.\n…APPELLANT\n(BY SRI. S.B.MUKKANNAPPA, ADVOCATE)\nAND:\n1. MANAGEMENT OF\nM/S SURETEX PROPHYLACTICS (INDIA)\nLIMITED & ANOTHER,\nPLOT NO.74 KIADB INDUSTRIAL ESTATE,\nJIGANI 2ND PHASE, ANEKAL TALUK,\nBENGALURU -560 105.\nREPRESENTED BY ITS\nSENIOR MANAGER HR\n2. THE SECRETARY\nDEPARTMENT OF LABOUR\nVIKASA SOUDHA, DR AMBEDKAR VEEDHI,\nBENGALURU-560 001. .. RESPONDENTS\n(BY SMT. ROOPA SRI.S.,ADVOCATE FOR R1;\nSRI. B.RAVINDRANATH, AGA FOR R2)\n\n- 2 -\nWA No. 25 of 2026\nC/W WA No. 20 of 2026\nWA No. 29 of 2026\nAND 2 OTHERS\nTHIS WRIT APPEAL FILED U/S 4 OF THE KARNATAKA HIGH\nCOURT ACT PRAYING TO SET ASIDE THE ORDER DATED 03/12/2025\nPASSED BY THE LEARNED SINGLE JUDGE IN WP NO.7675/2022 AND\nCONSEQUENTLY DISMISS THE WRIT PETITION FILED BY THE 1ST\nRESPONDENT.\nIN WA NO. 20/2026:\nBETWEEN:\nSRI. NAGARAJU\nAGED ABOUT 49 YEARS,\nS/O SIDDEGOWDA,\nNO.285, MARUTHI NAGAR,\nSONNENAHALLI, ULLALA UPANAGAR POST,\nBANGALORE-560 056.\n...APPELLANT\n(BY SRI. S.B.MUKKANNAPPA.,ADVOCATE)\nAND:\n1. MANAGEMENT OF\nM/S SURETEX PROPHYLACTICS\nINDIA PVT.LTD. AND ANR\nPLOT NO.74,KIADB INDUSTRIAL ESTATE,\nJIGANI 2ND PHASE, ANEKAL TALUK,\nBANGALORE-560 105,\nREPRESENTED BY ITS SENIOR MANAGER-HR\n2. THE SECRETARY,\nDEPARTMENT OF LABOUR,\nVIKASA SOUDHA, DR.AMBEDKAR VEEDHI,\nBANGALORE-560 001.\n...RESPONDENTS\n(BY SMT. ROOPA SRI.S., ADVOCATE FOR R1;\nSRI. B.RAVINDRANATH, AGA FOR R2)\nTHIS WRIT APPEAL FILED U/S 4 OF THE KARNATAKA HIGH\nCOURT ACT PRAYING TO SET ASIDE THE ORDER DATED\n03/12/2025 PASSED BY THE LEARNED SINGLE JUDGE IN WP\nNO.7893/2022 AND CONSEQUENTLY DISMISS THE WRIT\nPETITION FILED BY THE 1ST RESPONDENT.\n\n- 3 -\nWA No. 25 of 2026\nC/W WA No. 20 of 2026\nWA No. 29 of 2026\nAND 2 OTHERS\nIN WA NO. 29/2026:\nBETWEEN:\n1. SRI. BHIMAPPA,\nAGED ABOUT 51 YEARS,\nS/O. LATE SRI.MARIYAPPA,\nRESIDING AT. NO.264, GULBARGA COLONY,\n1ST BLOCK, JAYANAGAR,\nBANGALORE – 560 011.\n2. SRI. V.CHANDRASHEKAR,\nAGED ABOUT 49 YEARS,\nS/O. LATE. L.VENKATARAMAIAH,\nRESIDING AT NO.17, NEW NO.97,\n8TH MAIN, 13TH CROSS, MALLESHWARAM,\nBANGALORE - 560 003.\n3. SRI. NAGARAJAPPA\nAGED ABOUT 50 YEARS,\nS/O. NILAGIRIYAPPA,\nRESIDING AT SRIRAMAGONDANAHALLI,\nDAVANGERE DISTRICT. -577 005.\n4. SRI.SHIVAMADEGOWDA,\nAGED ABOUT 50 YEARS,\nS/O HONNEGOWDA,\nRESIDING AT NO. MUTHUNILAYA,\n2ND CROSS, NEAR SHANTHI COLLEGE,\nMYSORE MAIN ROAD, MALAVALLI,\nMANDYA DISTRICT 571 430.\n5. SRI. RAMESH. K.S.\nS/O. LATE. SIDDAIAH,\nAGED ABOUT 50 YEARS,\nC/O SRI VENUGOPALAREDDY,\nRESIDING AT NO.40, KEMPANAYAKANA HALLI,\nBANNERGHATTA,BANGALORE 560 083.\n6. SMT. SEETHALAKSHMI,\nAGED ABOUT 51 YEARS,\nW/O SRI. RAVIKUMAR P PATIL,\nRESIDING AT NO.237, SRINANDHI,\n1ST MAIN, SRI DWARAKA NAGARA,\nCHIKKABANAWARA POST,\n\n- 4 -\nWA No. 25 of 2026\nC/W WA No. 20 of 2026\nWA No. 29 of 2026\nAND 2 OTHERS\nHESARAGHATTA MAIN ROAD,\nBANGALORE 560 090.\n...APPELLANTS\n(BY SRI. S.B.MUKKANNAPPA.,ADVOCATE)\nAND:\n1. MANAGEMENT OF\nM/S SURETEX PROPHYLACTICS INDIA PVT.LTD.\nPLOT NO.74, KIADB INDUSTRIAL ESTATE,\nJIGANI 2ND PHASE, ANEKAL TALUK,\nBANGALORE-560 105,\nREPRESENTED BY ITS\nSENIOR MANAGER-HR\n2. THE ASSISTANT LABOUR COMMISSIONER\nDIVISION IV, LABOUR DEPARTMENT,\nBANNERGHATTA ROAD,\nBANGALORE - 560 029,\n...RESPONDENTS\n(BY SMT. ROOPA SRI.S.,ADVOCATE FOR R1;\nSRI. B.RAVINDRANATH, AGA FOR R2)\nTHIS WRIT APPEAL FILED U/S 4 OF THE KARNATAKA HIGH\nCOURT ACT PRAYING TO SET ASIDE THE ORDER DATED\n03.12.2025 PASSED BY THE LEARNED SINGLE JUDGE IN WRIT\nPETITION No. 2459/2023 AND CONSEQUENTLY DISMISS THE\nWRIT PETITION FILED BY THE 1ST RESPONDENT WITH A\nFURTHER DIRECTION TO THE 2ND RESPONDENT TO HOLD THE\nCONCILIATION PROCEEDINGS IN ACCORDANCE WITH SECTION\n12 OF THE INDUSTRIAL DISPUTES ACT, 1947, TO MEET THE\nENDS OF JUSTICE.\nIN WA NO. 30/2026:\nBETWEEN:\nSRI. R. LOKESH\nAGED ABOUT 47 YEARS,\nS/O RAMAKRISHNASHETTY,\nRESIDING AT NO.61, 7TH CROSS,\nMALLESHWARAM, BENGALURU 560 003.\n...APPELLANT\n\n- 5 -\nWA No. 25 of 2026\nC/W WA No. 20 of 2026\nWA No. 29 of 2026\nAND 2 OTHERS\n(BY SRI. S.B.MUKKANNAPPA.,ADVOCATE)\nAND:\n1. MANAGEMENT OF\nM/S SURETEX PROPHYLACTICS INDIA\nPVT.LTD. AND ANR\nPLOT NO.74, KIADB INDUSTRIAL ESTATE,\nJIGANI 2ND PHASE, ANEKAL TALUK,\nBANGALORE-560 105,\nREPRESENTED BY ITS\nSENIOR MANAGER-HR\n2. THE SECRETARY,\nDEPARTMENT OF LABOUR,\nVIKASA SOUDHA,\nDR.AMBEDKAR VEEDHI,\nBANGALORE-560 001. …RESPONDENTS\n(BY SMT. ROOPA SRI.S.,ADVOCATE FOR R1;\nSRI. B.RAVINDRANATH, AGA FOR R2)\nTHIS WRIT APPEAL FILED U/S 4 OF THE KARNATAKA HIGH\nCOURT ACT PRAYING TO SET ASIDE THE ORDER DATED\n03.12.2025 PASSED BY THE LEARNED SINGLE JUDGE IN WRIT\nPETITION No. 7803/2022 AND CONSEQUENTLY DISMISS THE\nWRIT PETITION FILED BY THE 1ST RESPONDENT, TO MEET THE\nENDS OF JUSTICE.\nIN WA NO. 56/2026:\nBETWEEN:\nSRI. RAMAKRISHNAIAH\nS/O LATE VEERABHADRAIAH,\nAGED ABOUT 47 YEARS\nR/A DODDABOOLI, MANCHANABELE POST,\nMAGADI TALUK,\nRAMANAGARA DISTRICT-562 159.\n...APPELLANT\n(BY SRI. S.B. MUKKANNAPPA.,ADVOCATE)\n\n- 6 -\nWA No. 25 of 2026\nC/W WA No. 20 of 2026\nWA No. 29 of 2026\nAND 2 OTHERS\nAND:\n1. MANAGEMENT OF\nM/S SURETEX PROPHYLACTICS (INDIA)\nPVT LTD & ANR.\nPLOT NO.74, KIADB INDUSTRIAL ESTATE,\nJIGANI 2ND PHASE, ANEKAL TALUK,\nBENGALURU-560 105.\nREPRESENTED BY ITS SENIOR MANAGER – HR\n2. THE SECRETARY\nDEPARTMENT OF LABOUR,\nVIKAS SOUDHA, DR AMBEDKAR VEEDHI,\nBENGALURU-560 001.\n...RESPONDENTS\n(BY SMT. ROOPA SRI.S.,ADVOCATE FOR R1;\nSRI. B.RAVINDRANATH, AGA FOR R2)\nTHIS WRIT APPEAL FILED U/S 4 OF THE KARNATAKA\nHIGH COURT ACT PRAYING TO SET ASIDE THE ORDER\nDATED 03.12.2025 PASSED BY THE LEARNED SINGLE\nJUDGE IN WRIT PETITION No. 18958/2021 AND\nCONSEQUENTLY DISMISS THE WRIT PETITION FILED BY\nTHE 1ST RESPONDENT, TO MEET THE ENDS OF JUSTICE.\nTHESE APPEALS HAVING BEEN HEARD AND RESERVED\nFOR JUDGMENT ON 07.09.2026 AND COMING ON FOR\nPRONOUNCEMENT THIS DAY, HON'BLE MR. JUSTICE\nH.SHANTHI BHUSHAN DELIVERED THE FOLLOWING:\nCORAM: HON'BLE MR. JUSTICE D K SINGH\nAND\nHON'BLE MR. JUSTICE H.SHANTHI BHUSHAN\n\n- 7 -\nWA No. 25 of 2026\nC/W WA No. 20 of 2026\nWA No. 29 of 2026\nAND 2 OTHERS\nCAV JUDGMENT\n(PER: HON'BLE MR. JUSTICE H.SHANTHI BHUSHAN)\nA “golden handshake”, as a voluntary retirement scheme\nis commonly described, is intended to bring the\nemployer–employee relationship to an end on the terms\nmutually accepted by the parties. Having opted for\nvoluntary retirement, acknowledged the terms of the\nscheme in writing and received the benefits thereunder,\nthe petitioner’s subsequent challenge must be examined\nin the light of her earlier conduct and the material\navailable on record. Such an arrangement cannot\nordinarily be repudiated merely by a subsequent\nassertion of coercion, in the absence of sufficient\nmaterial substantiating the allegation. The acceptance of\nretirement benefits and the unexplained delay in raising\nthe dispute are relevant circumstances in determining\nwhether the concluded employment arrangement\nwarrants reconsideration.\nAll these intra-court appeals have been preferred by the\npetitioner/Management of M/s.Suretex Prophylactics (India)\nLimited, against the order dated 03.12.2025, passed by the\n\n- 8 -\nWA No. 25 of 2026\nC/W WA No. 20 of 2026\nWA No. 29 of 2026\nAND 2 OTHERS\nlearned Single Judge of this Court in W.P.No.7675/2022 (L-\nRES), W.P.No.7893/2022 (L-RES), W.P.No.2459/2023 (L-RES),\nW.P.No.7803/2022 (L-RES) and W.P.No.18958/2021 (L-RES),\nwherein the learned Single Judge of this Court had allowed the\nWrit Petitions by quashing the notice/reference impugned\ntherein.\n2. These connected writ appeals though arising out of\nthe separate orders passed by the learned Single Judge, but\nemanate from substantially similar facts and circumstances and\ninvolve common questions of law and fact, we have, therefore,\nconsidered the matters together and dispose of them by this\ncommon order. W.A.No.25 of 2026 is treated as the lead\nappeal for the purpose of reference and disposal.\n3. For the sake of convenience and to avoid confusion,\nthe parties are referred to by their respective rankings before\nthe writ court.\nFacts of the Writ petitioner:\n4. The writ petitioner, Management of M/s. Suretex\nProphylactics (India) Limited, is engaged in the manufacture of\nrubber contraceptives at its factory situated at Plot No.74,\n\n- 9 -\nWA No. 25 of 2026\nC/W WA No. 20 of 2026\nWA No. 29 of 2026\nAND 2 OTHERS\nKIADB Industrial Estate, Jigani, Second Phase, Anekal Taluk,\nBengaluru. Respondent No.1, Smt.Shubha M., joined the\nservices of the petitioner-company as a Trainee on 11.09.1999.\nAccording to the petitioner, with a view to reducing surplus\nmanpower, the management introduced a Voluntary Retirement\nScheme in October 2009 after holding discussions with the\npermanent workmen. Under the scheme, a sum of\nRs.1,56,160/- was payable towards voluntary retirement, apart\nfrom the other admissible dues and incentives.\n5. The petitioner contended that 86 workmen\nsubmitted applications under the scheme between 05.10.2009\nand 10.10.2009. The applications were accepted by the\nmanagement, and the workmen were relieved from service\nafter their accounts were settled. The payments included the\nvoluntary retirement amount, gratuity, early-bird incentive,\nseniority incentive, leave encashment and bonus, as applicable.\nIn the case of respondent No.1, the petitioner asserted that she\nreceived a total sum of Rs.3,31,528/-, comprising\nRs.1,56,160/- towards voluntary retirement and Rs.1,75,368/-\ntowards other benefits. The amounts were paid through\nindividual account-payee cheques. Following the acceptance of\n\n- 10 -\nWA No. 25 of 2026\nC/W WA No. 20 of 2026\nWA No. 29 of 2026\nAND 2 OTHERS\nvoluntary retirement and settlement of accounts, the\nmanagement and the concerned workmen entered into\nsettlements under Section 18(1) of the Industrial Disputes Act,\n1947 (‘the ID Act’).\n6. The petitioner further pleaded that respondent No.1\nhad submitted her application for voluntary retirement on\n10.10.2009 and was relieved after the settlement of her dues.\nHowever, by a petition dated 12.07.2017, registered as\nPTN/CR/84/2017-18, she approached the Labour and\nConciliation Officer, Division VI, Bengaluru, alleging that her\nresignation had been obtained by force and that her cessation\nfrom service was, in substance, a termination of employment.\nAccording to the petitioner, respondent No.1 had accepted the\nentire settlement and encashed the cheque before raising the\ndispute, and no satisfactory explanation had been furnished for\nthe intervening delay of nearly eight years.\n7. The management appeared before the conciliation\nofficer and filed its objections, specifically relying upon the\nvoluntary retirement application, the settlement executed\nunder Section 18(1) of the ID Act and the payment of the\nentire amount due under the scheme. It maintained that the\n\n- 11 -\nWA No. 25 of 2026\nC/W WA No. 20 of 2026\nWA No. 29 of 2026\nAND 2 OTHERS\ndispute was stale and that the allegation of forced resignation\nhad been raised only after respondent No.1 had accepted the\nbenefits of voluntary retirement.\n8. Upon failure of conciliation, the Government, by\norder bearing No.LD-IDM/197/2021/LD.DO.6.L.S dated\n19.04.2021, referred the dispute for adjudication. The\nreference was registered as Reference No.30/2021 before the I\nAdditional District and Sessions Judge, Bengaluru Rural,\nBengaluru. Aggrieved by the reference, the management\ninstituted Writ Petition No.7675/2022 under Article 226 of the\nConstitution of India, seeking a writ of certiorari to quash the\norder dated 19.04.2021.\nDefence of the respondents:\n9. Respondent No.1 opposed the writ petition,\ncontending that the voluntary retirement was not the result of a\nfree and voluntary decision on her part. Her specific case was\nthat the management had insisted upon her accepting the\nscheme and had obtained the retirement application by\ncoercion, thereafter relieving her from service and transferring\nthe compensation amount without her genuine consent. She\n\n- 12 -\nWA No. 25 of 2026\nC/W WA No. 20 of 2026\nWA No. 29 of 2026\nAND 2 OTHERS\nrelied upon the terms of the Voluntary Retirement Scheme, the\ndates appearing on the applications and the documents\nproduced by the management to dispute the contention that\nthe retirement was voluntary.\n10. It was contended that the scheme was stated to\nremain in force from 05.10.2009 to 09.10.2009, whereas\ncertain applications, including the application relied upon in her\ncase, bore dates falling outside that period. Respondent\nNo.1also questioned the manner in which the applications had\nbeen processed and contended that Clause VII of the scheme\nhad not been given effect to. According to her, these\ncircumstances required examination in the industrial\nadjudication proceedings and could not be brushed aside\nmerely because monetary benefits had been paid.\n11. The respondents further submitted that the dispute\nhad arisen out of an allegation of forced resignation, which, if\nestablished, would amount to an involuntary termination of\nservice. They contended that the management's denial of\ncoercion and the workwoman's allegation to the contrary\ndisclosed a disputed question of fact requiring adjudication on\nevidence. It was argued that the ID Act did not prescribe a\n\n- 13 -\nWA No. 25 of 2026\nC/W WA No. 20 of 2026\nWA No. 29 of 2026\nAND 2 OTHERS\nspecific period of limitation for raising such a dispute and that\nthe mere lapse of time could not, by itself, justify quashing the\nreference at the threshold. The learned Additional Government\nAdvocate supported the action of the Government in referring\nthe dispute for adjudication and sought dismissal of the writ\npetition.\nConsideration of the writ court:\n12. The writ court, by order dated 03.12.2025 in Writ\nPetition No.7675/2022, considered the rival submissions and\nthe records placed before it. The principal question addressed\nwas whether the Government was justified in referring the\ndispute for adjudication despite the considerable lapse of time\nbetween the alleged forced resignation and the raising of the\nindustrial dispute, considering that the relevant payments\npursuant to voluntary retirement had already been made.\n13. The writ court noticed that respondent No.1 had\njoined the petitioner-company on 11.09.1999 and that the\nmanagement had introduced the voluntary retirement scheme\nin 2009. It took note of the petitioner's case that the\nworkwoman had received Rs.3,31,528/- towards the voluntary\n\n- 14 -\nWA No. 25 of 2026\nC/W WA No. 20 of 2026\nWA No. 29 of 2026\nAND 2 OTHERS\nretirement package and other admissible benefits, that her\naccounts had been settled and that a settlement had been\nexecuted under Section 18(1) of the ID Act. It also noticed that\nthe allegation of forced resignation had been raised before the\nconciliation officer only after a lapse of nearly eight years.\n14. The writ court observed that voluntary retirement\nordinarily brings the employer–employee relationship to an end\nupon acceptance of the scheme and the associated benefits. It\ninvoked the principle of approbate and reprobate, holding that\na person who accepts the benefits flowing from a transaction\ncannot ordinarily turn around at a later stage and challenge the\nvery transaction or assert a position inconsistent with the\nacceptance of those benefits.\n15. On the question of delay, the writ court relied upon\nthe principle that, although the ID Act does not prescribe a\nfixed period of limitation for making a reference under Section\n10, the appropriate Government must nevertheless consider\nwhether a live and existing industrial dispute survives. In its\nview, a claim raised after a long and unexplained interval,\nparticularly after acceptance of a full and final settlement under\na voluntary retirement scheme, cannot be treated as an\n\n- 15 -\nWA No. 25 of 2026\nC/W WA No. 20 of 2026\nWA No. 29 of 2026\nAND 2 OTHERS\nexisting industrial dispute warranting reference. The court also\nconsidered the need to avoid unsettling an employer's financial\narrangements and disturbing industrial peace by entertaining\nstale claims without satisfactory explanation for the delay.\n16. The writ court rejected the contention that the\nproceedings were premature merely because the Government\nhad already made a reference and the question of delay could\nbe examined by the adjudicating forum. It held that the\nadministrative authority was required, at the stage of making\nthe reference, to examine whether a bona fide industrial\ndispute continued to exist or whether the claim had become\nstale with the passage of time. The court concluded that the\nGovernment had failed to have regard to the relevant\nconsiderations before making the reference and that the\nreference, in the circumstances of the case, was unsustainable.\n17. Accordingly, by its order dated 03.12.2025, the writ\ncourt allowed the writ petition, issued a writ of certiorari and\nquashed the order of reference dated 19.04.2021 bearing No.\nLD-IDM/197/2021/LD.DO.6.L.S.\n\n- 16 -\nWA No. 25 of 2026\nC/W WA No. 20 of 2026\nWA No. 29 of 2026\nAND 2 OTHERS\nWrit appeal by respondent No. 1:\n18. Aggrieved by the order dated 03.12.2025 passed in\nWrit Petition No.7675/2022, respondent No.1 has preferred\nWrit Appeal No.25.2026 seeking to set aside the order of the\nwrit court and consequently dismiss the writ petition instituted\nby the petitioner.\n19. Respondent No.1 contends that the writ court failed\nto properly appreciate the factual circumstances surrounding\nthe introduction and implementation of the voluntary\nretirement scheme and erroneously proceeded on the footing\nthat receipt of monetary benefits necessarily established\nvoluntary retirement. She asserts that her specific allegation\nwas that the application had been obtained by coercion and\nthat the circumstances attending the submission and\nacceptance of the application required examination in the\nindustrial adjudication proceedings.\n20. It is urged that the writ court failed to give due\nconsideration to the objections and materials relied upon by\nrespondent No.1, particularly the dates of the voluntary\nretirement applications, the period during which the scheme\n\n- 17 -\nWA No. 25 of 2026\nC/W WA No. 20 of 2026\nWA No. 29 of 2026\nAND 2 OTHERS\nwas stated to remain in force and the alleged non-compliance\nwith Clause VII of the scheme. According to respondent No.1,\nthese matters could not have been conclusively determined\nagainst her merely on the basis of the payment of the\nretirement benefits.\n21. Respondent No.1 further contends that the\nGovernment's power under Section 10 of the ID Act is\nexercisable where an industrial dispute exists or is apprehended\nand that the absence of a prescribed limitation period does not\npermit the High Court to interfere with a reference solely on the\nground of delay, without due consideration of the nature of the\ndispute and the circumstances in which it was raised. It is her\ncase that the allegation of coercion, having been disputed by\nthe management, ought to have been examined by the\ncompetent adjudicating forum on the basis of the evidence\nadduced by the parties.\n22. Respondent No.1 also relies upon the following\ndecisions:\n(i) Avon Services Production Agencies (P) Ltd. v.\nIndustrial Tribunal, Haryana1,\n1(1979) 1 SCC 1.\n\n- 18 -\nWA No. 25 of 2026\nC/W WA No. 20 of 2026\nWA No. 29 of 2026\nAND 2 OTHERS\n(ii) Telco Convoy Drivers Mazdoor Sangh v. State of\nBihar2,\n(iii) S.M. Nilajkar v. Telecom District Manager,\nKarnataka3,\n(iv) Raghubir Singh v. General Manager, Haryana\nRoadways4,\n(v) Prabhakar v. Joint Director, Sericulture\nDepartment5,\n(vi) M/s. Bata India Limited v. State of Karnataka6,\n(vii) M/s. Bata India Limited v. State of Karnataka.7\nShe contends that the principles emerging from the said\ndecisions were not properly applied by the writ court to the\nfacts of the present case.\n23. A further ground urged in the appeal is that the\norder of reference itself had identified the delay as a matter\nrequiring consideration in the industrial dispute. According to\nrespondent No.1, once the dispute had been referred for\nadjudication, the parties ought to have been afforded an\nopportunity to place their respective evidence on record,\nincluding evidence concerning the alleged coercion and the\ncircumstances surrounding the voluntary retirement.\n2(1989) 3 SCC 271.\n3(2003) 4 SCC 27.\n4(2014) 10 SCC 301.\n5(2015) 15 SCC 1.\n6W.P. No.47002/2015, disposed of on 22.02.2021\n7W.A. No.504/2021, disposed of on 29.07.2021\n\n- 19 -\nWA No. 25 of 2026\nC/W WA No. 20 of 2026\nWA No. 29 of 2026\nAND 2 OTHERS\nRespondent No.1 therefore maintains that the writ court erred\nin quashing the reference at the threshold instead of permitting\nthe dispute to be adjudicated in accordance with law.\n24. On these grounds, respondent No.1 seeks setting\naside of the order dated 03.12.2025 and dismissal of Writ\nPetition No.7675/2022, thereby restoring the reference for\nadjudication.\n25. We have carefully considered the submissions\nadvanced on behalf of respondent No.1, the grounds urged in\nthe memorandum of appeal, the records placed before us and\nthe order dated 03.12.2025.\n26. Having heard Sri S.B.Mukkannappa, learned\ncounsel appearing for the appellant/respondent No.1,\nSmt.Roopasri. S, learned counsel appearing for respondent\nNo.1, and Sri.B.Ravindranath, learned Additional Government\nAdvocate appearing for respondent No.2, and having\nconsidered the decisions cited and the submissions advanced,\nthe principal question that arises for our consideration is,\n“Whether the appropriate Government was\njustified in making the reference dated 19.04.2021,\n\n- 20 -\nWA No. 25 of 2026\nC/W WA No. 20 of 2026\nWA No. 29 of 2026\nAND 2 OTHERS\nnotwithstanding respondent No.1’s acceptance of\nvoluntary retirement and receipt of the benefits\npayable under the scheme in October 2009, and the\ndelay of nearly eight years in raising the dispute\nalleging forced retirement?”\nWe answer the above point in the negative for the reasons\nhereinbelow:\nReasons for dismissal of the writ appeal:\n27. Respondent No.1 joined the services of the\npetitioner-management on 11.09.1999. Upon introduction of\nthe Voluntary Retirement Scheme in October 2009, respondent\nNo.1 submitted her application dated 08.10.2009, stating that\nshe had gone through the scheme dated 05.10.2009,\nunderstood its implications and accepted its terms. She further\nrequested the management to accept her application and settle\nher accounts, acknowledging that she had read and signed\neach page of the scheme as a token of her acceptance. The\nmanagement’s case is that the application was accepted,\nrespondent No.1 was relieved from service and a sum of\nRs.3,31,528/- was paid to her, comprising Rs.1,56,160/-\ntowards voluntary retirement and Rs.1,75,368/- towards other\nbenefits, including gratuity and incentives. The management\n\n- 21 -\nWA No. 25 of 2026\nC/W WA No. 20 of 2026\nWA No. 29 of 2026\nAND 2 OTHERS\nalso contended that the settlement was concluded under\nSection 18(1) of the ID Act. The dispute alleging that\nrespondent No.1 had been compelled to opt for voluntary\nretirement was raised only on 12.07.2017. The relevant portion\nof the aforementioned letter dated 08.10.2009 whereby\nrespondent No.1 accepted voluntary retirement under the\nscheme is produced hereinbelow:\n“08/10/2009\nFrom,\nEmp. Name: SHUBHA M.\nEmp. Code:\nTo,\nM/s. Suretex Prophylactics (I) Ltd.,\nPlot No. 74 to 91, KIADB Industrial Estate,\nJigani II Phase, Anekal Taluk,\nBangalore – 560 105.\nDear Sirs,\nI have gone through the Voluntary Retirement Scheme,\ndated 5th October 2009 announced by the company. I\nhave also gone through the benefits that I would be\nentitled to in case of my opting to retire under the\nScheme. After fully understanding the implications of\nretiring under the Voluntary Retirement Scheme, I\nhereby opt for retirement under the scheme. Kindly\naccept my application for retirement and settle my\naccounts.\nI have also gone through a copy of the Voluntary\nRetirement Scheme furnished to me by the\nmanagement, and after going through the Scheme, I\nhave signed on each page of the Scheme in token of my\nacceptance of the terms of the Scheme. Along with this\n\n- 22 -\nWA No. 25 of 2026\nC/W WA No. 20 of 2026\nWA No. 29 of 2026\nAND 2 OTHERS\napplication, I am forwarding the said copy for your\nrecords.\nThanking you,\nYours faithfully “\n28. The question requires examination not merely of\nwhether respondent No.1was a workman during the\nsubsistence of her employment, but also of the legal\nconsequences flowing from the cessation of the employer–\nemployee relationship pursuant to the accepted voluntary\nretirement scheme. Section 2(s) of the ID Act defines a\n‘workman’ and includes, for the purposes of proceedings under\nthe Act, persons who have been dismissed, discharged or\nretrenched in connection with, or as a consequence of, an\nindustrial dispute. Section 2(oo) of the ID Act defines\n‘retrenchment’ and expressly excludes voluntary retirement of\na workman. The cessation of employment pursuant to an\naccepted voluntary retirement scheme therefore raises a\nquestion as to whether respondent No.1 continued to fall within\nthe definition of ‘workman’ at the time of reference.\n29. However, even assuming that respondent No.1\ncontinued to qualify as a ‘workman’, such status would not, by\n\n- 23 -\nWA No. 25 of 2026\nC/W WA No. 20 of 2026\nWA No. 29 of 2026\nAND 2 OTHERS\nitself, establish that a live industrial dispute continued to\nsubsist after her retirement. The existence of an industrial\ndispute, or one which is apprehended, must be examined in the\nlight of the facts and circumstances of the particular case.\n30. At this juncture, we may place reliance on the\njudgment of the Supreme Court in A.K. Bindal v. Union of\nIndia8, wherein the court observed:\n“34. This shows that a considerable amount is to be paid\nto an employee ex-gratia besides the terminal benefits in\ncase he opts for voluntary retirement under the Scheme\nand his option is accepted. The amount is paid not for\ndoing any work or rendering any service. It is paid in lieu\nof the employee himself leaving the services of the\ncompany or the industrial establishment and forgoing all\nhis claims or rights in the same. It is a package deal of\ngive and take. That is why in business world it is known\nas ‘Golden Handshake’. The main purpose of paying this\namount is to bring about a complete cessation of the\njural relationship between the employer and the\nemployee. After the amount is paid and the employee\nceases to be under the employment of the company or\nthe undertaking, he leaves with all his rights and there is\nno question of his again agitating for any kind of his past\nrights, with his erstwhile employer including making any\nclaim with regard to enhancement of pay scale for an\n8AIR 2003 SC 2189.\n\n- 24 -\nWA No. 25 of 2026\nC/W WA No. 20 of 2026\nWA No. 29 of 2026\nAND 2 OTHERS\nearlier period. If the employee is still permitted to raise a\ngrievance regarding enhancement of pay scale from a\nretrospective date, even after he has opted for Voluntary\nRetirement Scheme and has accepted the amount paid\nto him, the whole purpose of introducing the Scheme\nwould be totally frustrated.”\n31. The Supreme Court explained that a voluntary\nretirement scheme constitutes an arrangement intended to\nbring about the cessation of the legal relationship between the\nemployer and employee upon fulfillment of the stipulated\nterms. The principle underlying such a scheme cannot be\ndefeated by permitting an employee to accept the benefits\nthereunder and subsequently reopen the concluded\narrangement without sufficient grounds.\n32. The Supreme Court in Gyanendra Sahay v. M/s.\nTata Iron & Steel Co. Ltd.9 has observed as under:\n“14. We have also perused the Memo of Appeal and\nother representation made by the 1st respondent. The 1st\nrespondent has made a vague allegation that he was\nforced to take retirement. Neither he has made it specific\nnor had given the name of any officer who compelled him\nto write the letter dt.1st April, 1995 or exercised undue\nand excessive pressure to sign the letter of\n9AIR 2006 SC 2795.\n\n- 25 -\nWA No. 25 of 2026\nC/W WA No. 20 of 2026\nWA No. 29 of 2026\nAND 2 OTHERS\npremature/voluntary retirement. Though the Labour\nCourt has come to the conclusion that the 1st respondent\nwas compelled to submit the letter of resignation, the\nsame is not supported by any acceptable evidence. It is\nsettled law that suspicion and doubt cannot take the place\nof evidence. No finding of fact can be given on mere\ndoubt and suspicion or on the basis of baseless\nallegations. The 1st respondent having written letter of\nvoluntary retirement and after having accepted the retiral\nbenefits without any protest cannot now turn round and\nsay that he was compelled to submit his\npremature/voluntary retirement. The appeal has\nabsolutely no merits and we, therefore, have no\nhesitation to dismiss the same and to affirm the order\npassed by the learned Judges of the Division Bench of the\nHigh Court. No order as to costs.”\n33. Further, the principle emerging from the above\njudgment requires the circumstances surrounding the\nemployee’s departure from service to be examined to\ndetermine whether the retirement was voluntary or whether\nthe employer had, in substance, dispensed with the employee’s\nservices without authority. Thus, the allegation of coercion\nmust be assessed on the basis of the material on record and\ncannot be accepted merely because it is subsequently asserted.\n\n- 26 -\nWA No. 25 of 2026\nC/W WA No. 20 of 2026\nWA No. 29 of 2026\nAND 2 OTHERS\n34. In the present case, respondent No.1’s application\nacknowledging her understanding and acceptance of the\nvoluntary retirement scheme, the payment and receipt of\nRs.3,31,528/- towards retirement and other benefits, and the\nabsence of any contemporaneous protest assume considerable\nsignificance. The allegation that respondent No.1 was\ncompelled to opt for retirement cannot be accepted as\nestablished merely because it was subsequently raised. The\nmaterial placed before us does not disclose specific particulars\nidentifying the person who allegedly exerted pressure, the\nnature of the alleged coercion or any contemporaneous\ncircumstance sufficient to prima facie substantiate the assertion\nthat the application was not submitted of her free will. Equally,\nthe mere submission of an application for voluntary retirement\nwould not, by itself, foreclose an inquiry into a properly\nsubstantiated allegation of coercion. The relevant\ncircumstances must be considered cumulatively.\n35. The delay in raising the dispute is, in this context, a\nmaterial consideration. Respondent No.1 accepted voluntary\nretirement in October 2009, whereas the allegation of forced\nretirement was raised only on 12.07.2017, after nearly eight\n\n- 27 -\nWA No. 25 of 2026\nC/W WA No. 20 of 2026\nWA No. 29 of 2026\nAND 2 OTHERS\nyears. No satisfactory explanation for this prolonged interval is\nforthcoming from the material placed before us. The absence of\na contemporaneous protest, when considered alongside the\nacceptance of the retirement benefits, assumes particular\nimportance in determining whether the claim disclosed a\nsubsisting industrial grievance warranting reference. Although\nthe ID Act does not prescribe a fixed period of limitation for a\nreference under Section 10, the absence of a prescribed\nlimitation period does not relieve the appropriate Government\nof its obligation to consider whether a live industrial dispute\nexists or is apprehended. The power of reference cannot be\nexercised mechanically merely because a claim has been\nraised, irrespective of the circumstances in which it is made or\nthe unexplained delay in pursuing it.\n36. The contention that all questions relating to delay\nand coercion ought to have been left entirely to the industrial\nadjudicating forum cannot be accepted in the facts of the\npresent case. While the appropriate Government is not\nexpected to conduct a full-fledged adjudication of disputed\nquestions of fact, it is nevertheless required to apply its mind to\nthe relevant material and determine whether the proposed\n\n- 28 -\nWA No. 25 of 2026\nC/W WA No. 20 of 2026\nWA No. 29 of 2026\nAND 2 OTHERS\nreference concerns an existing or apprehended industrial\ndispute. The Government was, therefore, required to consider\nthe terms of respondent No.1’s retirement application, the\nacceptance of the scheme, the receipt of the benefits, the\nabsence of contemporaneous protest and the unexplained delay\nin raising the dispute. Respondent No.1’s reliance on alleged\nirregularities in the implementation of the scheme, including\ndiscrepancies in the dates appearing on certain applications and\nalleged non-compliance with Clause VII, does not alter this\nconclusion, in the absence of sufficient material demonstrating\nhow those alleged irregularities established coercion in her\nindividual case or explained the delay in raising the grievance.\n37. The writ court, by its order dated 03.12.2025,\nexamined the relevant circumstances and concluded that the\nappropriate Government had failed to address the staleness of\nthe claim and the essential question as to whether a live\nindustrial dispute existed or was apprehended before making\nthe reference dated 19.04.2021. We find no perversity or error\nin the writ court’s conclusion warranting interference in the\npresent appeal. Respondent No.1 has failed to demonstrate any\nsufficient ground for restoring the reference or for holding that\n\n- 29 -\nWA No. 25 of 2026\nC/W WA No. 20 of 2026\nWA No. 29 of 2026\nAND 2 OTHERS\nthe writ court exceeded the permissible limits of judicial review.\nIn the circumstances, permitting the reference to proceed\nwould have the effect of reopening a concluded employment\narrangement after an unexplained lapse of nearly eight years,\nnotwithstanding the acceptance of the retirement benefits and\nthe absence of adequate material supporting the allegation of\ncoercion.\n38. It is a settled principle of law that a litigant who\napproaches the Court must do so with clean hands and make\nfull disclosure of all material facts. This principle applies not\nonly to proceedings under Articles 226 of the Constitution of\nIndia, but also to proceedings before other Courts and judicial\nforums. Every Court is duty-bound to protect its process from\nabuse by litigants who seek to secure relief by suppressing\nmaterial particulars, making misleading statements or\nwithholding facts having a bearing on the adjudication. The\njudicial process cannot be permitted to become an instrument\nfor reopening concluded transactions through belated and\nunsubstantiated assertions. The equitable jurisdiction of the\nCourt cannot be invoked by a party who seeks to approbate\nand reprobate, thereby gaining an advantage contrary to the\n\n- 30 -\nWA No. 25 of 2026\nC/W WA No. 20 of 2026\nWA No. 29 of 2026\nAND 2 OTHERS\nposition previously accepted by such party. The sanctity of\njudicial proceedings demands fairness and candour and no\nlitigant can claim equitable relief as a matter of right while\nabusing the very process through which such relief is sought.\n39. In view of the decisions discussed hereinabove, the\ndecisions relied upon by the petitioner do not advance her case\nor assist in sustaining the grounds urged in the present appeal.\n40. For the foregoing reasons, we find no merit in the\nwrit appeal.\n41. The grounds urged by respondent No.1 do not\ndisclose any error warranting interference with the order dated\n03.12.2025 passed by the writ court in W.P. No.7675/2022.\nAccordingly, Writ Appeal No.25/2026 is dismissed.\nIn view of the dismissal of W.A.No.25/2026 and for the\nreasons stated herein, we find no ground warranting a different\nconclusion in the connected appeals. Accordingly, all other\nconnected appeals are also dismissed.\nThe impugned orders dated 03.12.2025, passed by the\nlearned Single Judge of this Court in W.P.No.7675/2022 (L-\n\n- 31 -\nWA No. 25 of 2026\nC/W WA No. 20 of 2026\nWA No. 29 of 2026\nAND 2 OTHERS\nRES), W.P.No.7893/2022 (L-RES), W.P.No.2459/2023 (L-RES),\nW.P.No.7803/2022 (L-RES) and W.P.No.18958/2021 (L-RES)\nare hereby confirmed.\nWhile we are inclined to impose exemplary costs on\nrespondent No.1, having regard to the circumstances of the\ncase, we are mindful of the fact that respondent No.1 had\nopted for voluntary retirement. We, therefore, refrain from\nimposing exemplary costs and direct the parties to bear their\nown costs.\nSd/-\n(D K SINGH)\nJUDGE\nSd/-\n(H.SHANTHI BHUSHAN)\nJUDGE\nKNM	31	t	HCMA010000252026	Writ Appeal	2026-10-23	Single Bench	MRS. SHUBHA M	Respondent / State	\N	\N	Civil & Constitutional Law	In this matter, the High Court of Karnataka adjudicated issues between Petitioner and Respondent / State. The Court rendered a decision disposing of the application under statutory provisions.	0	2026 LexGuard (KHC) F7894482	{"court": "High Court of Karnataka", "title": "Petitioner v. Respondent / State", "case_id": "f7894482-7fef-4313-bcbe-2017ccea3ae9", "case_no": "NO. 25 of 2026", "parties": "Petitioner v. Respondent / State", "category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "petitioner": "Petitioner", "respondent": "Respondent / State", "legal_domain": "Civil & Constitutional Law", "category_facts": {"category": "BAIL_SUSPENSION", "surety_count": null, "custody_period": null, "applicat