COPY public.draft_sessions (id, user_id, title, draft_text, citations_inserted, gap_analysis_result, created_at, updated_at) FROM stdin;
dd4f98e2-0fce-4b71-b9da-ea48e866e820	0eae320e-4ffb-450c-9856-0da43c4f8c90	Petition Draft v1 b8e0fdb2	The Petitioner respectfully submits before this Hon'ble Court that...	[{"case_id": "7112db29-627b-4aec-abd2-4931c533534c", "citation": "2026 SC 101"}]	{"missing_arguments": ["Article 21 delay"], "score": 0.85}	2026-10-12 05:44:10.563727+05:30	2026-10-12 05:44:10.563729+05:30
2b2168dd-33be-433e-b966-1e49f629ed23	a376615f-2de1-47ae-b81e-66f687fd3b0b	Untitled Petition Draft	\n\n[Precedent Citation: Biman Krishna Bose v. United India Insurance Co. Ltd. (Biman Krishna Bose v. United India Insurance Co. Ltd. (2001) Supreme Court of India) - Re: Section 14 Consumer Protection]\n"Held that public sector insurance companies cannot arbitrarily refuse to renew a medical insurance policy merely because previous claim disputes were raised."\n\n\n[Precedent Citation: Arun Kumar Agrawal v. National Insurance Co. Ltd. (Arun Kumar Agrawal v. National Insurance Co. Ltd. (2010) Supreme Court of India) - Re: Section 166 Motor Vehicles Act]\n"Affirmed that the contribution of a deceased homemaker is invaluable and must be properly recognized in motor accident awards rather than dismissed on bare technicalities."\n\n\n[Precedent Citation: Kusum Sharma v. Batra Hospital & Medical Research Centre (Kusum Sharma v. Batra Hospital & Medical Research Centre (2010) Supreme Court of India) - Re: Section 14 Consumer Protection]\n"Laid down comprehensive 11-point guidelines distinguishing between a bonafide clinical error of judgment and actionable medical negligence."\n	[]	\N	2026-10-12 11:43:40.572937+05:30	2026-10-12 11:43:40.572942+05:30
e3785441-6d1e-40bf-98d3-58795f0f0679	a376615f-2de1-47ae-b81e-66f687fd3b0b	Untitled Petition Draft	[Precedent Citation: Biman Krishna Bose v. United India Insurance Co. Ltd. (Biman Krishna Bose v. United India Insurance Co. Ltd. (2001) Supreme Court of India) - Re: Section 14 Consumer Protection]\n"Held that public sector insurance companies cannot arbitrarily refuse to renew a medical insurance policy merely because previous claim disputes were raised."\n\n\n[Precedent Citation: Arun Kumar Agrawal v. National Insurance Co. Ltd. (Arun Kumar Agrawal v. National Insurance Co. Ltd. (2010) Supreme Court of India) - Re: Section 166 Motor Vehicles Act]\n"Affirmed that the contribution of a deceased homemaker is invaluable and must be properly recognized in motor accident awards rather than dismissed on bare technicalities."\n\n\n[Precedent Citation: Kusum Sharma v. Batra Hospital & Medical Research Centre (Kusum Sharma v. Batra Hospital & Medical Research Centre (2010) Supreme Court of India) - Re: Section 14 Consumer Protection]\n"Laid down comprehensive 11-point guidelines distinguishing between a bonafide clinical error of judgment and actionable medical negligence."\n\nFOR THAT the impugned actions/order of the Respondent run directly contrary to the binding law and ratio decidendi laid down by the Hon'ble Supreme Court of India in Rajneesh Kumar v. Union of India [Civil Appeal No. 6798 of 2019], wherein the Court categorically held that: "The Court held that: Reaffirmed reservation for persons with disabilities in public employment promotions, mandating reasonable accommodation as a facet of substantive equality. It was unequivocally established as binding law that statutory prerequisites and constitutional safeguards cannot be circumvented, and non-compliance invalidates the impugned proceedings.". The Respondent's failure to adhere to the mandate of Section 33 PwD Act, Article 16 renders the entire proceedings manifestly illegal, void ab initio, and liable to be quashed.	[]	\N	2026-10-12 11:44:21.842412+05:30	2026-10-12 11:44:21.842419+05:30
0e61838a-fefe-49ea-b99e-6dfa7cc51bfc	500f8fcc-7c3a-48db-ad75-8442bd16a886	Test Session 1	Petition text under Section 482 CrPC	[]	\N	2026-10-23 02:01:01.40671+05:30	2026-10-23 02:01:01.406716+05:30
